    7                /       TAKEN FROM A 1976 P?S/8 SYSTEM TAPE IMAGE [KNOWN TO NOT BE A FINAL RELEASE].
    8   
    9                /       CONTAINED WITHIN THIS FILE IS THE COMPLETE DEFINITION OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE
   10                /       HANDLER AND ALL DEVICE-RELATED COMPONENTS THAT DISTINGUISH ONE P?S/8 BOOTABLE
   11                /       SYSTEM FROM ANOTHER.  THIS IS THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE-SPECIFIC SET OF SYSTEM
   12                /       COMPONENTS WHICH INCLUDES THE FOLLOWING:
   13   
   14                /       1) THE P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  THIS IS THE BASIC READ/WRITE
   15                /          DEVICE HANDLER FOR ALL OPERATIONS THROUGHOUT THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND ALL
   16                /          SYSTEM COMPONENTS.
   17   
   18                /          THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER INCLUDES THE ABILITY TO RELOAD THE HANDLER AND
   19                /          BASE OPERATING SYSTEM BY RESTART AT 07600.  THIS IS THE CODE NORMALLY
   20                /          RESIDENT IN 07600-07777 WITH VARIOUS INTERNAL WORDS RESERVED FOR SPECIFIC
   21                /          PURPOSES.  [THIS IS EXPLAINED IN DETAIL BELOW.]
   22   
   23                /          WHEN THE SYSTEM IS STARTED AT 07600, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CODE AND THE
   24                /          SYSTEM KEYBOARD MONITOR ROUTINES ARE RELOADED USING A STRICTLY READ-ONLY
   25                /          PROCESS.  IF DESIRED, USER PROGRAMS CAN BE WRITTEN TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE BY
   26                /          INVOKING A VARIETY OF COMMANDS RELATED TO LOADING BINARY FILES AND MEMORY
   27                /          SAVE OPERATIONS; HOWEVER, THIS SELDOM-NEEDED FUNCTION IS NOT PERFORMED
   28                /          DURING ROUTINE SYSTEM RESTARTS.  THIS DESIGN WAS CHOSEN TO ALLOW SAFER
   29                /          OPERATION AS WELL AS HIGHER PERFORMANCE; GENERALLY, TRANSIENT SYSTEM DEVICE
   30                /          PROBLEMS CANNOT CORRUPT THE DEVICE-RESIDENT OPERATING SYSTEM IF THE DRIVE
   31                /          IS WRITE-LOCKED.  BY DESIGN, SYSTEM DEVICE FAILURES CAN BE SAFELY RETRIED.
   32                /          THE USER IS ENCOURAGED TO WRITE-ENABLE THE SYSTEM DRIVE ONLY WHEN
   33                /          PERFORMING DELIBERATE WRITE OPERATIONS.
   34   
   35                /          VARIOUS STAND-ALONE TC01/TC08 STANDARD BOOTSTRAP ROUTINES EXIST, EACH
   36                /          GENERALLY DESIGNED TO MINIMIZE THE EFFORT TO START THE SYSTEM FROM
   37                /          UNINITIALIZED MEMORY.  [NOTE: VARIOUS EFFORTS HAVE BEEN MADE OVER THE YEARS
   38                /          LEADING TO SUCCESSIVELY SMALLER ROUTINES AND/OR LESS MANUAL ENTRY EFFORT.]
   39                /          THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER FOR TC01/TC08 IS COMPATIBLE WITH ALL KNOWN
   40                /          BOOTSTRAPS [INCLUDING THOSE KNOWN TO ONLY SUPPORT P?S/8 AND A FEW OTHER
   41                /          OPERATING SYSTEMS WHILE MINIMIZING THE MANUAL EFFORT].  [NOTE: ONCE P?S/8
   42                /          IS STARTED UP, SECONDARY PROGRAMS COULD BE DEPLOYED TO BOOT ANY PDP-8
   43                /          OPERATING SYSTEM INCOMPATIBLE WITH THESE SHORTER BOOTSTRAP ROUTINES].
   44   
   45                /          APPENDIX "A" CONTAINS DOCUMENTATION OF WHAT IS PURPORTED TO BE THE SHORTEST
   46                /          KNOWN MANUAL BOOTSTRAP FOR TC01/TC08 DECTAPE SYSTEMS.  THIS METHOD CAN BE
   47                /          USED TO BOOTSTRAP P?S/8 AS WELL AS SEVERAL OTHER PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS.
   48   
   49                /          UNLIKE THE OS/8 OPERATING SYSTEM, BOTH THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND
   50                /          INITIALIZATION CODE RESIDE IN BLOCK 0000 OF THE DECTAPE; THERE IS NO BOOTUP
   51                /          "HELPER" BLOCK TO ASSIST LOADING A SEPARATE HANDLER.  OVER THE LONG TERM,
   52                /          THIS HAS PROVEN TO BE A SUPERIOR METHOD FOR ALL SUPPORTED DEVICES.  [NOTE:
   53                /          CERTAIN LOCATIONS WITHIN THE BLOCK ARE DEFINED AS LIST ELEMENTS DURING
   54                /          PROGRAM EXECUTION ONCE THE SYSTEM IS FULLY LOADED.  DURING THE BOOTSTRAP
   55                /          OPERATION THESE WORDS ARE USED AS ONCE-ONLY INITIALIZING INSTRUCTIONS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 2

   57                /       2) A STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP FOR THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM.  THIS IS A ROUTINE
   58                /          DESIGNED TO BE LOADED AND STARTED AT 07600 WHEN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
   59                /          IS DELIBERATELY DISPLACED FROM MEMORY; THIS OCCURS WHEN ONE OF TWO BINARY
   60                /          LOADING ALTERNATIVES IS INVOKED BY RUNNING THE P?S/8 "BIN" UTILITY PROGRAM
   61                /          WITH VARIOUS OPTION SWITCHES SET.  THE REST OF THE LAST PAGE OF FIELD 0 IS
   62                /          FILLED WITH SPECIFIC LOADER CODE AND PARAMETERS APPROPRIATE TO THE
   63                /          PARTICULAR BINARY LOADING FUNCTION CHOSEN [A) P?S/8 BINARY FILES, B) BINARY
   64                /          PAPER-TAPES IN BIN OR RIM FORMAT].
   65   
   66                /          THE MAXIMUM LENGTH PERMITTED FOR THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP ALLOWS
   67                /          COMPATIBILITY WITH THE PAPER-TAPE BIN AND RIM LOADERS; HOWEVER, THE ACTUAL
   68                /          TC01/TC08 BOOTSTRAP CODE IS SHORTER THAN THIS MAXIMUM AMOUNT [WHICH MAY BE
   69                /          IN EFFECT FOR OTHER P?S/8 SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS].  AS CURRENTLY DEFINED,
   70                /          THE ENTIRETY OF THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP MUST LOAD INTO 07600-07642.
   71                /          [NOTE: THIS MEMORY RANGE MAY BE SLIGHTLY MODIFIED IN FUTURE RELEASES TO
   72                /          IMPLEMENT ADDITIONAL FEATURES WITHIN THE VARIOUS BINARY LOADERS.]
   73   
   74                /       3) SLIGHTLY MODIFIED VERSIONS OF THE DEC STANDARD BIN AND RIM LOADERS.  THE
   75                /          BIN AND RIM FORMAT PAPER-TAPE LOADERS CAN BE PLACED IN MEMORY WHEN LOADING
   76                /          DIAGNOSTIC OR OTHER PROGRAMS NOT RELATED TO ANY STORAGE-BASED OPERATING
   77                /          SYSTEM.  THIS PROVIDES BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY WITH LEGACY SYSTEMS HAVING NO
   78                /          OPERATING CAPABILITY BEYOND BASIC BINARY PAPER-TAPE LOADING.
   79   
   80                /          CERTAIN KNOWN PROGRAMS MUST BE RUN FROM THIS MORE FUNDAMENTAL ENVIRONMENT
   81                /          DUE TO VIOLATION OF VARIOUS PROGRAMMING CONVENTIONS ASSOCIATED WITH ALL
   82                /          STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEMS.  [NOTE: IN SOME INSTANCES, P?S/8 MAY BE
   83                /          MORE ACCOMMODATING THAN OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS WITH REGARD TO EXPLICIT
   84                /          LOADING BOUNDARIES; HOWEVER, CERTAIN PROGRAMS ARE KNOWN TO CLEARLY VIOLATE
   85                /          STORAGE-BASED LOADING LIMITS.  A BRIEF DISCUSSION IS GIVEN BELOW REGARDING
   86                /          POTENTIAL WORK-AROUNDS PARTICULAR TO P?S/8 FOR THESE "ROGUE" PROGRAMS.]
   87   
   88                /          A SLIGHTLY MODIFIED BIN LOADER [ALONG WITH THE STANDARD RIM LOADER] LOADS
   89                /          INTO 07643-07777 ALLOWING SEEMLESS COEXISTENCE WITH THE STAND-ALONE
   90                /          BOOTSTRAP LOADING INTO 07600-07642.  AS LONG AS ALL PROGRAMS LOADED BY THE
   91                /          BIN OR RIM BINARY PAPER-TAPE LOADERS [OR MANUAL FRONT-PANEL ENTRY] DO NOT
   92                /          VIOLATE THE DEFINED BOOTSTRAP MEMORY RANGE, P?S/8 CAN BE RELOADED BY
   93                /          RESTARTING AT 07600.
   94   
   95                /          WHILE THE BIN AND RIM PAPER-TAPE LOADERS ARE NOT SPECIFIC TO THE TC01/TC08
   96                /          P?S/8 CONFIGURATION, THEY ARE INCLUDED IN THIS DOCUMENT TO ILLUSTRATE THE
   97                /          INTERACTION BETWEEN THE STAND-ALONE P?S/8 BOOTSTRAP [AND THE SLURP LOADER]
   98                /          AND THE BIN AND RIM LOADERS.  THE BIN LOADER HAS BEEN SLIGHTLY MODIFIED TO
   99                /          FACILITATE COEXISTENCE WITH THE BOOTSTRAP; DUE TO ITS SIMPLISTIC NATURE,
  100                /          THE RIM LOADER IS UNCHANGED.  [NOTE: FURTHER MODIFICATIONS MAY BE MADE TO
  101                /          THE BIN LOADER IN FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN FOR THE PURPOSE OF
  102                /          ELIMINATION OF KNOWN TRADE-OFFS THAT AFFECT ALL RELEASES OF THE BIN LOADER.
  103                /          ASSUMING NO FURTHER OPTIMIZATION OF THE CODE IS POSSIBLE, IT MAY BE
  104                /          FEASIBLE TO SHORTEN THE MAXIMUM LENGTH OF THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP TO
  105                /          ACCOMPLISH THIS.  A DISCUSSION OF THIS ISSUE IS INCLUDED BELOW.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 3

  107                /       4) THE P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATION OF RICHARD LARY'S UNIQUE "SLURP" LOADER FOR
  108                /          TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS.  THE SLURP LOADER WAS FIRST IMPLEMENTED WITHIN THE
  109                /          ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM [SOMETIMES KNOWN AS "MS/8"].  BINARY FILES ON
  110                /          THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE [AND/OR RELATED LOGICAL UNITS] CAN BE LOADED FASTER
  111                /          BY THE SLURP LOADER THAN ANY OTHER METHOD; THE SYSTEM DEVICE CAN REMAIN
  112                /          WRITE-LOCKED DURING THE LOADING PROCESS.  [NOTE: MAJOR DETAILS OF THE BASIC
  113                /          SLURP LOADER ARE DISCUSSED BELOW.  FOR A COMPLETE LIST OF AVAILABLE
  114                /          LOADING OPTIONS, INCLUDING THE ALTERNATIVE "VIRTUAL" LOADER, CONSULT
  115                /          DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 "BIN" UTILITY PROGRAM.]
  116   
  117                /          [NOTE: THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY PROGRAM SHOULD NOT BE CONFUSED WITH THE
  118                /          PAPER-TAPE BIN LOADER.  TO MAINTAIN CLARITY, THE PAPER-TAPE BIN LOADER IS
  119                /          ALWAYS CLEARLY REFERENCED AS NEEDED.  ALL OTHER REFERENCES TO "BIN" REFER
  120                /          TO THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY PROGRAM.  P?S/8 BIN CAN PERFORM VARIOUS FUNCTIONS
  121                /          RELATED TO BINARY FILES AND BINARY PAPER-TAPES INCLUDING LAUNCHING THE
  122                /          PAPER-TAPE BIN AND RIM LOADERS; THE PRIMARY FUNCTION OF P?S/8 BIN IS TO
  123                /          LOAD BINARY FILES ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE (AND RELATED LOGICAL DRIVE UNITS)
  124                /          INTO MEMORY.  FOR MORE INFORMATION ON THE VARIOUS OPERATING MODES OF P?S/8
  125                /          BIN, CONSULT ADVANCED DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY PROGRAM.]
  126   
  127                /          THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP DESCRIBED IN 2) ABOVE IS ALSO INTEGRATED WITH THE
  128                /          SLURP LOADER; THE TWO COMPONENTS WORK TOGETHER TO LOAD BINARY FILES LOCATED
  129                /          ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE [AND RELATED LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS] INTO USER MEMORY
  130                /          WITHOUT USING A SEPARATE BUFFER.
  131   
  132                /          [NOTE: MOST SYSTEMS DO NOT CONCEPTUALLY SUPPORT THIS FORM OF ADVANCED
  133                /          BINARY FILE LOADER; CONVENTIONAL WISDOM DICTATES IT CANNOT EXIST.  DESPITE
  134                /          WIDESPREAD IGNORANCE OF THIS NOVEL DESIGN, SOME FORM OF SLURP LOADER HAS
  135                /          BEEN PRESENT IN SEVERAL SUPPORTED DEC PRODUCTS.  DEC MANAGEMENT PERSONNEL
  136                /          OFTEN ASSUMED THE PROGRAMMING STAFF KNEW WHAT THEY WERE DOING BASED ONLY ON
  137                /          ERRATIC HUNCHES; FEW IF ANY OF THEM WERE COMPETENT TO UNDERSTAND THIS AND
  138                /          OTHER PDP-8 SOFTWARE ISSUES; HOWEVER, WHEN PUT IN A POSITION TO PASS
  139                /          JUDGEMENT ON CERTAIN SOFTWARE PROJECTS, THEY MADE ARBITRARY DECISIONS NOT
  140                /          BASED ON TECHNICAL MERIT; THIS SOMETIMES RESULTED IN NEGATIVE CONSEQUENCES,
  141                /          SOME OF WHICH ARE DISCUSSED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.]
  142   
  143                /       5) A SPECIAL "FILE" IMAGE USED TO RELOAD THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER DIRECTLY
  144                /          FROM THE SLURP LOADER WITHOUT WRITING ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  THE RELEVANT
  145                /          OPTIONS OF THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY ARE DESCRIBED IN SUFFICIENT DETAIL TO
  146                /          EXPLAIN HOW THIS TRULY NOVEL SYSTEM COMPONENT FUNCTIONS.  [NOTE: THIS
  147                /          MODULE WAS NEVER PART OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM OR THE FEW OTHER SYSTEMS
  148                /          THAT SUPPORT SLURP BINARY LOADING.  AS BEYOND CONVENTIONAL WISDOM THE SLURP
  149                /          LOADER IS, THE CONCEPT OF A FILE THAT CAN RELOAD THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
  150                /          OVER THE SLURP LOADER (WHICH DOESN'T USE A BUFFER) IS BEYOND THE
  151                /          UNDERSTANDING OF MANY PEOPLE.  THIS DOCUMENT WILL ATTEMPT TO EXPLAIN
  152                /          EXACTLY WHAT IS ACCOMPLISHED WHEN USING THIS OPTION.  SUFFICE TO SAY, AT
  153                /          LEAST THE AUTHORS OF P?S/8 UNDERSTAND HOW THIS WORKS.)
  154   
  155                /       6) P?S/8 BOOTUP STARTS A COMBINATION KEYBOARD MONITOR AND IMBEDDED EDITOR FOR
  156                /          TEXT FILES; SOME OF THE AVAILABLE COMMANDS ARE DISCUSSED IN THIS DOCUMENT.
  157                /          WHILE ANY COMMAND IS BEING ENTERED, TWO KEYBOARD CHARACTERS ARE RESERVED TO
  158                /          INITIATE REWIND AND UNLOAD OPERATIONS ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  THE TC01/TC08
  159                /          VERSION OF THESE OPTIONAL DEVICE-DEPENDENT COMMANDS IS DOCUMENTED BELOW.
  160                /          AS APPLICABLE, ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS BASED ON TAPES OR OTHER
  161                /          DISMOUNTABLE MEDIA SHOULD IMPLEMENT EQUIVALENT ROUTINES.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 4

  163                /       DOCUMENT LAYOUT DETAILS.
  164   
  165                /       THIS DOCUMENT CONTAINS SECTIONS REGARDING ISSUES FAR REMOVED FROM THE PRIMARY
  166                /       PURPOSE OF MERELY DEFINING THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND RELATED
  167                /       DEVICE-SPECIFIC COMPONENTS AS OUTLINED ABOVE.
  168   
  169                /       TOPICS OF DISCUSSION INCLUDE CERTAIN ASPECTS OF OVERALL SYSTEM DESIGN,
  170                /       DIRECTORY STRUCTURE TRADE-OFFS AND RELATED ISSUES.  A GLOSSARY OF TERMS IS
  171                /       PRESENT AT THE END OF THE DOCUMENT FOR THE BENEFIT OF ALL READERS NOT FULLY
  172                /       VERSED IN THE SUBJECT OF PDP-8 SYSTEM DEVELOPMENT.  BY INCLUDING THIS
  173                /       ADDITIONAL MATERIAL, A BETTER UNDERSTANDING OF HOW P?S/8 OPERATES, BOOTS UP
  174                /       AND OTHER CONSIDERATIONS OF ITS HISTORICAL DEVELOPMENT CAN BE OBTAINED.
  175   
  176                /       SOME OF THE MATERIAL CONCERNS THE RELEVANT HISTORY AND DEVELOPMENT OF THE R-L
  177                /       MONITOR SYSTEM, AS WELL AS SOME DETAILS ABOUT KEY PEOPLE WHO DESIGNED AND
  178                /       IMPLEMENTED THIS PREDECESSOR OPERATING SYSTEM.  ONE OF THE MISSIONS OF THE
  179                /       P?S/8 OPERATING SYSTEM IS TAKING THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM CONCEPTS TO THEIR
  180                /       LOGICAL CONCLUSION, A PROCESS STILL UNFOLDING AS OF THE TIME OF THIS WRITING. 
  181   
  182                /       INCLUDED IS A DISCUSSION OF FUTURE DIRECTIONS FOR P?S/8 INCLUDING RECENT WORK
  183                /       EXPRESSED IN THE CONTEXT OF THE SYSTEM AS IT PRESENTLY EXISTS.  [NOTE: MANY
  184                /       ASPECTS OF THIS REPRESENT A LEVEL OF DEVELOPMENT SOMEWHAT LATER THAN THE
  185                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND RELATED CODING AS DESCRIBED IN THIS DOCUMENT; FUTURE
  186                /       VERSIONS WILL UPDATE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CODE TO INCLUDE SUPPORT FOR
  187                /       ADDITIONAL WORK NOT YET FULLY DEFINED; CERTAIN CHANGES ARE CURRENTLY UNDER
  188                /       CONSIDERATION FOR FUTURE RELEASES.]
  189   
  190                /       WHERE APPLICABLE, NEW WORK IS INDICATED IN TERMS OF MINOR MODIFICATIONS THAT
  191                /       ARE REQUIRED THAT DO NOT MATERIALLY AFFECT CURRENT PERFORMANCE OF WHAT IS NOW
  192                /       DEFINED AS THE "BASIC" P?S/8 SYSTEM.  SOME OF THESE CHANGES ARE REQUIRED TO
  193                /       ELIMINATE KNOWN ISSUES WITHIN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM OR ADD CERTAIN
  194                /       ENHANCEMENTS THAT APPLY TO POTENTIAL SYSTEM EXTENSIONS.
  195   
  196                /       SOME OF THESE MODIFICATIONS ARE KNOWN TO BE PART OF MORE RECENT RELEASES OF
  197                /       P?S/8; IT IS HOPED THAT THE CONTENT OF THESE NEWER PROGRAMS CAN BE INTEGRATED
  198                /       INTO A FUTURE RELEASE OF THIS DOCUMENT.
  199   
  200                /       ADDITIONAL MODIFICATIONS ARE CONCERNED WITH THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE P?S/8
  201                /       "SHELL" OVERLAY.  THIS IS AN OPTIONAL COMPONENT IN THE EARLY STAGES OF
  202                /       DEVELOPMENT; THE OVERALL GOAL IS TO PROVIDE A POWERFUL SOFTWARE BASE WITH
  203                /       LEVELS OF IMPLEMENTATION SUITABLE FOR ALL USERS FROM MODEST CONFIGURATIONS
  204                /       THROUGH ELABORATE SYSTEMS LARGER THAN WERE AVAILABLE DURING THE ORIGINAL
  205                /       ECONOMIC LIFETIME OF THE PDP-8.  [NOTE: IN THEORY, STORAGE DEVICES THAT
  206                /       AGGREGATE AS MUCH AS 1.0 TB ARE SUPPORTABLE.]
  207   
  208                /       DEVELOPMENT WILL BE OPEN-ENDED TO ALLOW ADDITIONAL CHANGES IN THE FUTURE;
  209                /       EXPERIENCE SHOWS THAT ONLY AFTER CERTAIN FEATURES ARE AVAILABLE WILL THE
  210                /       NEED FOR ADDITIONAL FUNCTIONALITY BE REALIZED; AS SUCH, THE IMPLEMENTATION
  211                /       WILL ALLOW EASY EXPANSION AT ANY LEVEL OF DEVELOPMENT.  UNLIKE CERTAIN OTHER
  212                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS, NO NOVEL FEATURES WILL BE CONCEPTUALLY LOCKED OUT DUE TO
  213                /       SHORT-SIGHTED DESIGN.


