  257                /       IN-MEMORY SYSTEM GENERATION DEFINITIONS.
  258   
  259                /       GENERATION ADDRESSES ARE WHERE IN MEMORY THE STATED COMPONENT LOADS DURING THE
  260                /       INITIAL GENERATION OF P?S/8.  THIS IS UNRELATED TO THE ACTUAL MECHANISM USED
  261                /       TO PERFORM THE WRITING OUT OF THE COMPONENT WHICH IS NOT DEFINED HERE.  IN
  262                /       ACTUAL SYSTEM GENERATION SITUATIONS, A PRE-EXISTING P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE
  263                /       HANDLER IS RESIDENT IN 07600 AND AVAILABLE FOR THE WRITE OPERATIONS.  [NOTE:
  264                /       IT IS BEYOND THE SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT TO EXPLAIN ANY ADDITIONAL DETAILS OF
  265                /       SYSTEM GENERATION, CERTAIN OF WHICH ARE STILL EVOLVING AS OF THIS WRITING.]
  266   
  267                /       THE GENERATION FIELD IS EXPRESSED IN BITS[6-8] FOR THE PURPOSES OF GENERATION
  268                /       MEMORY EXTENSION INSTRUCTIONS SUCH AS "CDF SDHFLD".  FIELD SETTINGS SHOULD USE
  269                /       THIS PARAMETER DIVIDED BY 10 SINCE THE FIELD DIRECTIVE IS EXPRESSED IN THE
  270                /       RANGE OF 0 THROUGH 7.
  271   
  272                /       ALL COMPONENTS WILL BE SUITABLY RELOCATED TO ENSURE THE INTENDED INSTRUCTIONS
  273                /       ARE CREATED.  THE NOPUNCH AND ENPUNCH DIRECTIVES WILL BE USED TO RELOCATE THE
  274                /       GENERATION FIELD; THE RELOC DIRECTIVE WILL BE USED TO RELOCATE THE 12-BIT
  275                /       ADDRESS.
  276   
