/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 8

  356                /       BACKGROUND MATERIAL.
  357   
  358                /       THE P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND RELATED DEVICE-SPECIFIC
  359                /       COMPONENTS REPRESENT THE CULMINATION OF A SURPRISINGLY LARGE AMOUNT OF
  360                /       PROGRAMMING EFFORT ON THE PART OF A SERIES OF INDIVIDUALS.  SOMETIMES ACTING
  361                /       ALONE OR IN SMALL GROUPS, THE CODE PRESENTED BELOW WAS DEVELOPED VIA A LONG
  362                /       AND CIRCUITOUS PATH OVER THE COURSE OF SEVERAL DECADES.
  363   
  364                /       VARIOUS RELATED TOPICS WILL BE INTRODUCED TO ATTEMPT TO EXPLAIN THE UNUSUAL
  365                /       CIRCUMSTANCES THAT APPLY TO THIS DEVELOPMENT; THE APPLICATION OF VARIOUS
  366                /       INSPIRATIONAL NOTIONS LED TO THE PRESENT DESIGN AS PRESENTED IN THIS DOCUMENT.
  367   
  368                /       SECTION 0: THE HISTORY OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM.
  369   
  370                /       THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM IS THE FIRST VIABLE PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEM FOR THE
  371                /       PDP-8 WITH TC01/TC08 DECTAPE.  IT WAS CREATED MANUALLY OVER THE COURSE OF
  372                /       SEVERAL MONTHS WITH LITTLE, IF ANY, PRIOR PLANNING; THE HARDWARE BECAME
  373                /       AVAILABLE AND A GROUP OF FIVE INDIVIDUALS [LATER KNOWN AS THE "WILLIAM CLAUDE
  374                /       FIELDS MEMORIAL PROGRAMMING GROUP" (WCFMPG) ESSENTIALLY JUST DECIDED TO WRITE
  375                /       IT EVEN THOUGH NO DEVELOPMENT RESOURCES WHATSOEVER WERE AVAILABLE.
  376   
  377                /       THE SYSTEM WAS INCREMENTALLY DESIGNED AND INDIVIDUAL SECTIONS OF CODE WERE
  378                /       HAND ASSEMBLED FROM NOTES WRITTEN ON PAPER [INCLUDING NAPKINS FROM LOCAL
  379                /       CHINESE RESTAURANTS].  THE CODE WAS WRITTEN DOWN IN OCTAL NOTATION AS WELL AS
  380                /       PRIMITIVE [AND GENERALLY UNCOMMENTED] SOURCE CODE, THEN MANUALLY ENTERED INTO
  381                /       MEMORY BY EXTRAORDINARY USE OF THE PDP-8 FRONT PANEL ON THE FIRST PDP-8 SYSTEM
  382                /       AVAILABLE [AT BROOKLYN POLYTECHNIC INSTITUTE WHERE THEY WERE ALL STUDENTS AT
  383                /       THE TIME].
  384   
  385                /       DESPITE THE INCREDIBLE NATURE OF THE DEVELOPMENT PROCESS, THE R-L MONITOR WAS
  386                /       QUITE SUCCESSFUL.  A SIGNIFICANT PERCENTAGE OF THE EVENTUAL PDP-8 PROGRAMMING
  387                /       GROUP BOTH WITHIN DEC AND IN THE REST OF THE WORLD STARTED THEIR PDP-8 CAREERS
  388                /       ON THIS SYSTEM.  IN MANY WAYS IT IS THE STARTING POINT OF THE DEVELOPMENT OF
  389                /       P?S/8, WHICH IS IN PART DESIGNED TO TAKE THE PRINCIPLES OF THE R-L MONITOR TO
  390                /       THEIR LOGICAL CONCLUSION.
  391   
  392                /       SINCE THE R-L MONITOR WAS AVAILABLE TO THE "NEXT GENERATION" OF PDP-8
  393                /       PROGRAMMERS AT BROOKLYN POLY, ATTENTION COULD FOCUS MORE ON IMPROVING THE
  394                /       SYSTEM THAN MERELY CREATING IT FROM LITERALLY NOTHING.
  395   
  396                /       MANY OF THE ORIGINAL CONCEPTS WERE RETAINED IN P?S/8; OTHERS WERE REFINED.
  397                /       MANY ADDITIONAL CONCEPTS WERE EVENTUALLY ADDED AS WELL AS A LARGE NUMBER OF
  398                /       NEW PROGRAMS OVER THE COURSE OF SEVERAL DECADES.  P?S/8 IS STILL EVOLVING AS
  399                /       OF THE TIME OF THIS WRITING AND WILL EVENTUALLY INCLUDE ADDITIONAL FEATURES.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 9

  401                /       R-L SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND LIMITATIONS.
  402   
  403                /       THE SENSE OF EXPEDIENCY INHERENT IN THE CREATION OF THE R-L MONITOR IS
  404                /       UNDERSTANDABLE; HOWEVER, P?S/8 IS BASED ON MANY OF THE SAME CONCEPTS ONLY
  405                /       TAKEN TO A "BETTER PLACE" OVER TIME.  P?S/8 WAS NOT WORKED ON WITH A
  406                /       SCHEDULE IN MIND.  SPORADICALLY, THE SYSTEM WAS GRADUALLY IMPROVED; AT ANY
  407                /       GIVEN TIME, OVERALL VIABILITY WAS ALWAYS MAINTAINED; A CURRENTLY WORKING
  408                /       SYSTEM IS REQUIRED TO EVOLVE INTO A NEWER WORKING SYSTEM.  [IN SOME CASES,
  409                /       THIS MEANT PUTTING ON HOLD CERTAIN NEW DESIGN COMPONENTS BECAUSE OF THE LACK
  410                /       OF A PREREQUISITE FOUNDATION.  NO OTHER SIGNIFICANT SOFTWARE PROJECT HAS EVER
  411                /       IMPLEMENTED IN THIS FASHION.]
  412   
  413                /       THE "WEAKEST LINK" OF THE R-L MONITOR IS THE COMPLEX OF COMPONENTS NEEDED TO
  414                /       CARRY OUT THE VARIOUS LOW-LEVEL FUNCTIONS NEEDED TO SUPPORT PDP-8 PROGRAMS
  415                /       THAT CAN OCCUPY NEARLY THE ENTIRE MEMORY OF THE SYSTEM.  THIS LEAVES ONLY A
  416                /       SMALL RESIDENT AREA CAPABLE OF RELOADING THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND SUPPLYING
  417                /       CERTAIN SYSTEM SERVICES [IF POSSIBLE].
  418   
  419                /       THE ORIGINAL R-L SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WAS NOT A GOOD EXAMPLE OF ADVANCED
  420                /       PDP-8 PROGRAMMING OF THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER; HOWEVER, IT WAS
  421                /       ADEQUATE TO THE TASK AS DEFINED IN THE ORIGINAL SYSTEM.
  422   
  423                /       THE R-L SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS ENTERED VIA ONE OF TWO ENTRY POINTS [ONE FOR
  424                /       READING, THE OTHER FOR WRITING].  THE HANDLER IS CALLED AS FOLLOWS:
  425   
  426                /               CIF CDF 00              /THE R-L SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
  427                /                                       /ONLY WORKS WITH FIELD 0.
  428   
  429                /               JMS I   PHNDLER         /"PHNDLER" CONTAINS THE ENTRY POINT THAT
  430                                                        /APPLIES; EITHER THE READ OR WRITE
  431                                                        /ENTRY POINT AS APPROPRIATE.
  432                /               CORE                    /USER'S MEMORY BUFFER STARTING ADDRESS
  433                                                        /IN THE RANGE OF 00000-07400.  [THE LAST
  434                                                        /ADDRESS TRANSFERRED MUST BE LESS THAN 07600.]
  435                /               -BLOCKS                 /TWO'S COMPLEMENT OF THE DESIRED NUMBER OF
  436                                                        /BLOCKS TO BE TRANSFERRED AS A CONTIGUOUS
  437                                                        /GROUP IN A SINGLE SEARCH THEN READ [OR
  438                                                        /WRITTEN] IN ONE SMOOTH MOTION OF THE DECTAPE.
  439                /               BLKNUM                  /THE STARTING BLOCK NUMBER FOR THE TRANSFER;
  440                /                                       /ANY VALUE FROM 0000-2701 IS ALLOWED; THE LAST
  441                                                        /BLOCK TRANSFERRED MUST BE 2701 OR LESS.
  442   
  443                /       THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS RESIDENT IN MEMORY WHEN THE PRIMARY SYSTEM
  444                /       COMPONENT, THE KEYBOARD MONITOR AND INTEGRATED EDITOR, IS MEMORY RESIDENT.
  445                /       THE SYSTEM CAN BE RESTARTED BY ANY MEANS OF EXIT TO 07600.  CALLS FOR THE
  446                /       PURPOSE OF READING ARE LIMITED TO A MAXIMUM OF 31 BLOCKS; ATTEMPTING TO
  447                /       TRANSFER OVER LOCATIONS 07600-07777 WILL CAUSE HANDLER SELF-DESTRUCTION.
  448                /       CALLS FOR THE PURPOSE OF WRITING CAN BE PERFORMED UP TO 32 BLOCKS AT A TIME;
  449                /       HOWEVER, THE IMAGE OF THE SYSTEM HANDLER ITSELF WOULD BE PART OF THE WRITTEN
  450                /       DATA.  THUS, WITHOUT ANY SUPPORT FOR EXTENDED MEMORY, PRACTICAL READ AND WRITE
  451                /       OPERATIONS ARE LIMITED TO AT MOST 31 CONTIGUOUS BLOCKS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 10

  453                /       THERE IS NO ERROR RETURN; ALL CALLS ALWAYS RETURN TO THE CALLER PAST THE THREE
  454                /       IN-LINE ARGUMENTS.
  455   
  456                /       IF AN ACTUAL DEVICE ERROR OCCURS, THE HANDLER HALTS THE CPU; THE AC CONTAINS
  457                /       ERROR-DEPENDENT CONTENTS WHICH CORRESPONDS TO THE TC01/TC08 CONTROLLER
  458                /       STATUS REGISTER "B" CONTENTS [WHICH CONTAINS THE CURRENT ERROR STATUS AND THE
  459                /       MEMORY TRANSFER FIELD, THE LATTER ALWAYS BEING 000].  THE OPERATOR CAN ALWAYS
  460                /       SAFELY PRESS "CONTINUE" ON THE FRONT PANEL TO RETRY THE OPERATION.  AS LONG AS
  461                /       THE CALL TO THE HANDLER IS COMPLETELY LEGITIMATE [MEANING NO ATTEMPT TO ACCESS
  462                /       NONEXISTENT BLOCKS], THE ERROR WILL LIKELY CLEAR AS LONG AS THE HARDWARE IS IN
  463                /       GOOD WORKING ORDER.  TRANSIENT READ ERRORS WILL GENERALLY SUCCEED WITHIN ONE
  464                /       OR TWO RETRY ATTEMPTS.
  465   
  466                /       MOST DETECTED ERRORS ARE CAUSED BY FAILURE TO MAKE THE DRIVE READY.  IN THE
  467                /       CASE OF WRITING, THE DRIVE MUST NOT BE WRITE-LOCKED.  MANY USERS PREFERRED TO
  468                /       DELIBERATELY WRITE-LOCK THE TAPE DRIVE; WHEN A WRITE WAS INITIATED, THE HALT
  469                /       WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WAS EXPECTED;  THE WRITE-LOCK CONDITION WAS
  470                /       CLEARED THEN "CONTINUE" WAS PRESSED AND THE WRITE OPERATION SUCCEEDED AS
  471                /       INTENDED.  ADEPT USERS NEVER CAUSED ANY ACCIDENTAL HARM TO THEIR PERSONAL
  472                /       DECTAPE SYSTEM BY ALWAYS USING THIS PRUDENT METHOD.
  473   
  474                /       THE R-L SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ONLY SUPPORTS DEVICE UNIT 0.  [NOTE: THE PDP-8
  475                /       AT BROOKLYN POLY ONLY HAD ONE DECTAPE DRIVE.]  BY A GRADUAL PROCESS INVOLVING
  476                /       MANY TAPE CHANGES, IT IS POSSIBLE TO EVENTUALLY TRANSFER AN ENTIRE DECTAPE IF
  477                /       NEED BE, 31 BLOCKS AT A TIME; SINCE THE DRIVE WAS ONLY WRITE-ENABLED WHEN THE
  478                /       TARGET TAPE WAS MOUNTED, USERS COULD PERIODICALLY CREATE BACKUPS OF THE SYSTEM
  479                /       AND THEIR DATA [TIME PERMITTING; IT WAS A VERY BUSY SHARED ENVIRONMENT].
  480   
  481                /       THE R-L SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER INCLUDES THE ABILITY TO RESTART THE SYSTEM AT
  482                /       07600.  THE BOOTSTRAP CODE RELOADS THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER OVER ITSELF BY
  483                /       THREE-CYCLE DATA-BREAK [DMA].  WHILE THE TRANSFER IS OCCURRING, THE PDP-8
  484                /       STANDARD WAIT LOOP FOR TC01/TC08 DECTAPE [07616-07617] IS IN CONTROL WAITING
  485                /       FOR THE DECTAPE DONE FLAG TO SET.  WHEN THE FLAG RAISES, THE NEW CONTENTS OF
  486                /       MEMORY TAKE CONTROL BECAUSE KEY WORDS IN MEMORY ARE OVERLAID BY THE
  487                /       CORRESPONDING CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000, REPLACING THE WORDS IN MEMORY THAT WERE
  488                /       SETUP FOR THIS OPERATION.  THIS INCLUDES REPLACEMENT VALUES FOR OVERLAY OF THE
  489                /       TC01/TC08 WORD COUNT [07754] AND CURRENT ADDRESS [07755] REGISTERS FOR PROPER
  490                /       TRANSFER.  [NOTE: WHEN THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 OVERLAY SIMILAR CODE IN
  491                /       MEMORY, THE WORD COUNT IS EVENTUALLY OVERLAID BY THE COPY ON THE DECTAPE; THIS
  492                /       CAN CHANGE THE OUTCOME OF THE TRANSFER TO A NEW VALUE DETERMINED BY THE
  493                /       INTERNAL BOOTSTRAP OPERATION ITSELF.  THE CURRENT ADDRESS MUST ALSO HAVE THE
  494                /       PROPER VALUE SO AS TO CONTINUE THE DMA OPERATION AS INTENDED BY THE BOOTSTRAP
  495                /       CODE ON THE DECTAPE.]
  496   
  497                /       AT THIS POINT, THE FRESH COPY OF THE BOOTSTRAP BLOCK CODE IS IN CONTROL.
  498                /       ADDITIONAL TRANSFERS ARE SETUP TO READ IN THE KEYBOARD MONITOR AND THE TINY
  499                /       FILE SYSTEM [TFS] DIRECTORY BLOCKS BY SEVERAL INDEPENDENT CALLS TO THE SYSTEM
  500                /       DEVICE HANDLER THAT ARE LOCATED IN THE LAST WORDS OF BLOCK 0000.  [NOTE: THESE
  501                /       LOCATIONS ARE LATER USED TO PASS A LIST OF FILE POINTERS; AT THE TIME OF
  502                /       BOOTING UP, THEY ARE AVAILABLE FOR THE INSTRUCTIONS THAT CARRY OUT THE BOOTUP
  503                /       PROCESS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 11

  505                /       AFTER ALL OF THESE OPERATIONS HAVE COMPLETED, THE SYSTEM IS RUNNING AND
  506                /       CAPABLE OF PROCESSING COMMANDS.  THE AVAILABLE COMMANDS CAN BE BROKEN DOWN BY
  507                /       CATEGORY:
  508   
  509                /       1) FILE MANAGEMENT COMMANDS.  FILES CAN BE ADDED TO THE DIRECTORY OR DELETED
  510                /          FROM THE DIRECTORY; THE DIRECTORY CAN BE LISTED OUT ON THE SYSTEM CONSOLE.
  511                /          SINCE THE ENTIRE TFS DIRECTORY IS CACHED IN MEMORY, ONLY OPERATIONS THAT
  512                /          CHANGE THE DIRECTORY CONTENTS CAUSE IT TO BE WRITTEN OUT; IF A FILE IS
  513                /          LOADED INTO THE EDITOR BUFFER AND UPDATED, A FILE SAVE COMMAND WILL WRITE
  514                /          TO THE SAME DIRECTORY SLOT; NO DIRECTORY OPERATIONS ARE PERFORMED IN SUCH
  515                /          CIRCUMSTANCES.  [NOTE: THE PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENT OF THIS DIRECTORY ACCESS
  516                /          METHOD OVER MORE CONVENTIONAL SYSTEMS IS QUITE PROFOUND.]
  517   
  518                /       2) EDITING COMMANDS.  WHEN A FILE IS LOADED INTO THE EDITING BUFFER, VARIOUS
  519                /          COMMANDS EXIST TO EITHER ADD OR REMOVE RANGES OF LINES.  THE USER CAN ADD
  520                /          NEW LINES BY SPECIFYING THE LINE NUMBER APPROPRIATE TO POSITIONING THE
  521                /          LINE IN THE FILE.  AN AUTO-SEQUENCING MODE EXISTS TO ALLOW MORE FREE-FORM
  522                /          TEXT ENTRY.  [NOTE: ALL LINE NUMBERS ARE KEPT IN THE FILE.  COMMANDS EXIST
  523                /          TO RENUMBER LINES BY AN ORDERED RESEQUENCE.  AS NECESSARY THE REPLACEMENT
  524                /          LINE NUMBERS WILL SUPERSEDE THE PREVIOUS LINE NUMBERS; THE TEXTUAL CONTENTS
  525                /          OF ALL AFFECTED LINES IS NOT CHANGED.]  [NOTE: THE LINE NUMBERS ARE PLACED
  526                /          INTO A DIFFERENT AREA WITHIN THE FILE; THIS ALLOWS SYSTEM PROGRAMS TO
  527                /          ACCESS THE TEXT WITHOUT PROCESSING THE LINE NUMBERS.]
  528   
  529                /          EACH TFS FILE IS EXACTLY 2048 WORDS LONG.  COMMANDS EXIST TO INDICATE HOW
  530                /          FULL A FILE IS; IF A FILE IS GETTING CLOSE TO FULL, IT MAY BE SPLIT INTO
  531                /          TWO FILES AS NECESSARY BY USING APPROPRIATE COMMANDS TO DELETE CERTAIN
  532                /          LINE RANGES WHILE RETAINING OTHERS.  THIS PROCESS IS NOT AS CLUMSY AS IT
  533                /          MIGHT SOUND; MANY USERS HAVE ADAPTED TO THIS FORM OF "RANDOM ACCESS" FILE
  534                /          EDITING OVER THE YEARS.  [NOTE: INDEFINITE LENGTH FILE SYSTEMS GENERALLY
  535                /          REQUIRE THAT THE ENTIRETY OF ANY ONE FILE BE AVAILABLE DURING EDITING PLUS
  536                /          ADDITIONAL SPACE EQUAL TO OR GREATER THAN THE CURRENT LENGTH; THE EXISTING
  537                /          FILE MUST BECOME A BACKUP COPY WHILE A NEW COPY IS BEING CREATED.  ON A
  538                /          LOW-PERFORMANCE DEVICE SUCH AS DECTAPE, THIS CAN BE EXTREMELY FRUSTRATING
  539                /          TO ANY USER DUE TO THE EXTREME OVERHEAD INVOLVED.  BY ALLOWING THE ABILITY
  540                /          TO WORK ON ANY PROJECT SPANNING MULTIPLE FILES, ONLY A SINGLE 2048 WORD
  541                /          SECTION IS UPDATED AT ANY GIVEN TIME.  ANYONE WHO ROUTINELY EDITS TEXT FILES
  542                /          CAN EASILY GET ACCUSTOMED TO LOCATING WHICH OF SEVERAL RELATED FILES
  543                /          CONTAINS THE SECTION OF INTEREST AT ANY GIVEN MOMENT; THE REST OF THE TEXT
  544                /          FILES ARE NOT EVEN READ, MUCH LESS WRITTEN.]
  545   
  546                /       3) EXECUTION [RUN] COMMANDS.  SYSTEM PROGRAMS [SUCH AS THE PAL III ASSEMBLER
  547                /          MODIFIED TO TAKE INPUT FROM TFS TEXT FILES AND WRITE OUTPUT TO DESIGNATED
  548                /          AREAS ON THE DECTAPE] CAN BE BROUGHT INTO MEMORY AND PASSED A LIST OF TEXT
  549                /          FILES TO PROCESS.  THE RESULTANT BINARY FILE[S] CAN SUBSEQUENTLY BE LOADED
  550                /          INTO MEMORY FOR EXECUTION.  [NOTE: THE R-L "SLURP" BINARY LOADER LOADS
  551                /          BINARY FILES WITHOUT WRITING TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  PROTECTING THE SYSTEM
  552                /          DEVICE IS HIGHLY RECOMMENDED ESPECIALLY DURING PROGRAM DEVELOPMENT.]
  553   
  554                /          SEVERAL OTHER SYSTEM PROGRAMS ARE AVAILABLE FOR SYSTEM MAINTENANCE.
  555                /          ADDITIONAL LANGUAGES ARE SUPPORTED INCLUDING AN ADAPTATION OF FOCAL, 1969
  556                /          MODIFIED TO ACCEPT INPUT FROM THE CONTENTS OF MULTIPLE TFS FILES.  [NOTE:
  557                /          THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF FILES THAT CAN BE PASSED TO MOST PROGRAMS IS 17.  A
  558                /          12-BIT COMMAND-LINE PARAMETER EXPRESSED AS A FOUR-DIGIT OCTAL VALUE CAN
  559                /          OPTIONALLY BE LOADED INTO 07756 WHEN THE SYSTEM PROGRAM IS STARTED.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 12

  561                /       R-L MONITOR LIMITATIONS.
  562   
  563                /       WHILE THE R-L MONITOR WAS QUITE USEFUL FOR PROGRAM DEVELOPMENT AND EXECUTION,
  564                /       THERE ARE MANY SEVERE LIMITATIONS THAT WERE EVENTUALLY OVERCOME.  P?S/8 AS
  565                /       DESCRIBED IN THIS DOCUMENT IS THE CULMINATION OF ALL OF THE IMPROVEMENTS MADE
  566                /       OVER THE YEARS; CERTAIN CONCEPTS REMAIN UNCHANGED, AND IN SOME CASES THE
  567                /       FUNCTIONS ARE FULLY BACKWARD-COMPATIBLE.  MANY PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENTS WERE
  568                /       MADE INCREMENTALLY OVER THE COURSE OF DECADES.
  569   
  570                /       DURING THE LONG PROCESS OF GRADUAL DEVELOPMENT, CERTAIN LEVELS OF ABSOLUTE
  571                /       COMPATIBILITY WERE LOST, GENERALLY REPLACED BY EQUIVALENT OR BETTER SYSTEM
  572                /       FUNCTIONS OR COMPLETE REPLACEMENT PROGRAMS.
  573   
  574                /       WITHIN THIS DOCUMENT, TOPICS OF DISCUSSION WILL BE LIMITED TO THOSE ASSOCIATED
  575                /       WITH THE TC01/TC08-ORIENTED COMPONENTS OF THE R-L MONITOR AND P?S/8.  THE
  576                /       LARGER ISSUES OF OVERALL DESIGN CHANGES, ADD-ONS AND IMPROVEMENTS IS BEYOND
  577                /       THE SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT; HOWEVER, SEVERAL ISSUES WILL BE DISCUSSED IN
  578                /       DETAIL.
  579   
  580                /       R-L MONITOR PROGRAM EXECUTION.
  581   
  582                /       EXECUTION OF CORE-IMAGE PROGRAMS UNDER THE R-L MONITOR IS NOT A
  583                /       STRAIGHTFORWARD PROCESS.  THE KEYBOARD MONITOR PARSES A RUN COMMAND FOR THE
  584                /       DESIRED SYSTEM PROGRAM.  [NOTE: ONLY THE FIRST TWO CHARACTERS OF THE PROGRAM
  585                /       NAME ARE SIGNIFICANT; ANY ADDITIONAL CHARACTERS ARE IGNORED.]  THE
  586                /       CORRESPONDING PROGRAM IS LOCATED IN A SPECIAL-PURPOSE SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY
  587                /       TOTALLY DISTINCT FROM THE TFS FILE DIRECTORY.  APPROPRIATE ENTRIES ARE LOCATED
  588                /       AND USED FOR THE LOADING PROCESS OF THE PROGRAM SUCH AS STARTING BLOCK, LOWEST
  589                /       LOADING ADDRESS, NUMBER OF BLOCKS TO LOAD, AND THE PROGRAM STARTING ADDRESS.
  590   
  591                /       THESE PARAMETERS CANNOT BE APPLIED TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, WHICH CAN
  592                /       ONLY PERFORM THE THREE FUNCTIONS STATED ABOVE [READ, WRITE, AND RESTART AT
  593                /       07600].  THE KEYBOARD MONITOR LOADS IN THE CONTENTS OF A DESIGNATED BLOCK
  594                /       WHICH IS USED AS A DEDICATED LOADER OF THE SYSTEM PROGRAM IMAGE.
  595   
  596                /       THE R-L SYSTEM LOADER IS NOT A GENERAL-PURPOSE DEVICE HANDLER.  ITS SOLE
  597                /       FUNCTION IS TO PERFORM A SINGLE READ OPERATION TO LOAD THE PROGRAM IMAGE INTO
  598                /       MEMORY AND THEN START AT THE ADDRESS PASSED BY THE KEYBOARD MONITOR.  ALL OF
  599                /       THE PARAMETERS FROM THE SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY ARE PLUGGED INTO DESIGNATED
  600                /       LOCATIONS WITHIN THE SYSTEM LOADER FOR EVENTUAL EXECUTION.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 13

  602                /       THE KEYBOARD MONITOR PARSES FILE NAMES USED IN THE COMMAND-LINE; THESE ARE
  603                /       TRANSLATED INTO A SERIES OF STARTING BLOCKS FOR EACH FILE, ALL OF WHICH ARE
  604                /       PASSED IN A LIST STARTING AT 07757.  IF THERE ARE NO FILES, 07757 IS LOADED
  605                /       WITH A SENTINAL 0000.  AS MANY AS 17 FILE HANDLES CAN BE PASSED IN THE
  606                /       ADDRESSES 07757-07777.  WITH THE EXCEPTION OF THE MAXIMUM SIZE LIST, THE END
  607                /       OF THE LIST ALWAYS CONTAINS A WORD OF 0000 TO SIGNIFY THE END OF ALL INPUT
  608                /       FILES.  [NOTE: IT IS NOT NECESSARY TO HAVE A SENTINAL 0000 ON A FULL-LENGTH
  609                /       LIST; THIS IS BECAUSE THE PROGRAMMING USED TO PROCESS THE LIST AUTOMATICALLY
  610                /       ENDS SHOULD LOCATION 07777 BE REFERENCED.  AN EXAMPLE OF FILE LIST HANDLING IS
  611                /       AS FOLLOWS:
  612   
  613                /       ISZ     FILPTR                  /BUMP TO NEXT FILE
  614                /       TAD I   FILPTR                  /GET THE NEXT ENTRY
  615                /       SNA CLA                         /SKIP IF ANOTHER FILE
  616                /       JMP     FILDONE                 /JUMP IF ALL FILES PROCESSED.
  617   
  618                /       FOR ALL LESSER ENTRIES, THE SENTINEL TEST ENCOUNTERS 0000 AS THE END OF THE
  619                /       LIST; FOR A MAXIMUM LENGTH LIST, THE "ISZ FILPTR" INSTRUCTION SKIPS THE NEXT
  620                /       INSTRUCTION.  THUS, THE LIST IS SELF-TERMINATING WHEN THE MAXIMUM FILE LIST IS
  621                /       PASSED.
  622   
  623                /       THE LIST IS NOT INITIALLY PLACED IN THE LOCATIONS INDICATED ABOVE.  THE LIST
  624                /       IS CREATED AT THE END OF THE SYSTEM LOADER IMAGE READ IN BY THE KEYBOARD
  625                /       MONITOR.  WHEN ALL PARAMETERS ARE PROCESSED, THE SYSTEM LOADER IMAGE IS MOVED
  626                /       TO 07600-07777 AND STARTED AT THE INTERNAL ADDRESS OF THE SYSTEM LOADER TO
  627                /       READ IN THE SYSTEM PROGRAM INTO AT MOST LOCATIONS 00000-07577.
  628   
  629                /       ONCE THE SYSTEM LOADER HAS FINISHED ALL LOADING OPERATIONS, THE SYSTEM PROGRAM
  630                /       IS STARTED WITH THE =XXXX VALUE [IF ANY] PASSED IN LOCATION 07756, THE LIST OF
  631                /       FILES PASSED IN 07757-07777.  [NOTE: THE SYSTEM LOADER ALSO INCLUDES A
  632                /       TC01/TC08 BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE STARTING AT 07600.]
  633   
  634                /       THERE IS A SEVERE PROBLEM AT THIS POINT AS THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS NO
  635                /       LONGER IN MEMORY.  NO MEANS IS PROVIDED BY THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM TO ALLOW THE
  636                /       JUST-LOADED SYSTEM PROGRAM TO PERFORM ANY READ OR WRITE OPERATIONS.  [THE
  637                /       INPUT FILE HANDLES (JUST BARE BLOCK NUMBERS IN THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM) ARE
  638                /       AVAILABLE TO SETUP A READ OPERATION; HOWEVER, THERE IS NO MEANS PROVIDED TO
  639                /       ACTUALLY IMPLEMENT THIS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 14

  641                /       TO WORK AROUND THIS, EVERY R-L SYSTEM PROGRAM MUST PROVIDE ITS OWN MEANS OF
  642                /       ACCESSING THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  THIS GENERALLY CONSISTS OF A GENERIC READ AND
  643                /       WRITE SUBROUTINE PATTERNED AFTER THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; HOWEVER, THIS
  644                /       IS AN INTERNAL SUBROUTINE WITHIN EACH SYSTEM PROGRAM.  THERE ARE SEVERAL
  645                /       ISSUES WITH THIS APPROACH:
  646   
  647                /       1) ALL R-L SYSTEM PROGRAMS THAT REQUIRE ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE
  648                /          [PRESUMABLY TO READ TFS FILES AND/OR WRITE TO DESIGNATED BLOCKS ON THE
  649                /          SYSTEM DEVICE SET ASIDE FOR WRITING OUT BINARY] MUST INCLUDE THIS CODE;
  650                /          THIS CAUSES ALL SUCH PROGRAMS TO BE HARDWARE-DEPENDENT.  IF THE R-L SYSTEM
  651                /          DEVICE IS RECONFIGURED FOR AN ALTERNATE STORAGE DEVICE, ALL AFFECTED
  652                /          PROGRAMS MUST PROVIDE AN APPROPRIATE SET OF ALTERNATE READ/WRITE
  653                /          SUBROUTINES; SUFFICIENT CODE SPACE MUST BE SET ASIDE; CERTAIN STORAGE
  654                /          DEVICES REQUIRE MORE CODE SPACE TO SUPPORT COMPARABLE READ/WRITE ROUTINES.
  655   
  656                /       2) SINCE THE TYPICAL READ/WRITE SUBROUTINES USED WERE NEARLY AN ENTIRE PAGE OF
  657                /          PDP-8 BINARY CODE, THE CONSTRUCTIVE MAXIMUM SIZE OF SYSTEM PROGRAMS IS NOT
  658                /          THE ENTIRE RANGE OF 00000-07577; CERTAIN PROGRAMS WERE MODIFIED WHEN
  659                /          ADAPTED TO THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM TO LIMIT THE FORMER MEMORY RANGE
  660                /          SUFFICIENTLY TO ALLOW THE INCLUSION OF THE INTERNAL SUBROUTINES DESPITE THE
  661                /          ORIGINAL DESIGN EXPECTATIONS OF FULL ACCESS TO THE ENTIRE MEMORY RANGE OF
  662                /          00000-07577; ONLY PROGRAMS HAVING NO EXPECTATION OF ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM
  663                /          DEVICE CAN BE ACCOMMODATED WITHOUT COMPROMISE [SUCH AS THE DEC STANDARD
  664                /          DECTAPE FORMATTING PROGRAM WHICH WAS SAVED AS AN IMAGE PROGRAM].
  665   
  666                /       WHILE THERE ARE MANY OTHER SPECIFIC LIMITATIONS WITHIN THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM,
  667                /       MOST OF WHICH WERE EVENTUALLY OVERCOME DURING THE DEVELOPMENT OF P?S/8, THE
  668                /       MOST IMPORTANT WAS ELIMINATION OF THE PROBLEM DESCRIBED ABOVE.  AFTER MANY
  669                /       YEARS OF SPORADIC DEVELOPMENT, P?S/8 EMERGED AS A DEVICE-INDEPENDENT SYSTEM;
  670                /       ALL PROGRAMS WILL WORK ON ANY P?S/8 CONFIGURATION, AND NO MEMORY NEED BE
  671                /       RESERVED FOR INTERNAL HARDWARE-SPECIFIC READ/WRITE SUBROUTINES.
  672   
  673                /       A DISCUSSION OF SPECIFIC PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENTS WILL BE FOUND LATER IN THIS
  674                /       DOCUMENT REGARDING DETAILS OF THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  [NOTE: THE
  675                /       READER IS REFERRED TO EXTERNAL DOCUMENTATION FOR THE MANY OTHER IMPROVEMENTS
  676                /       FOUND IN P?S/8 THAT DO NOT PERTAIN TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER OR RELATED
  677                /       HARDWARE-SPECIFIC COMPONENTS.]
  678   
  679                /       THE OTHER COMPONENTS OF P?S/8 SPECIFIC TO THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE HARDWARE WILL
  680                /       BE DISCUSSED BELOW.  IN GENERAL, THEY ARE IMPROVEMENTS OVER THEIR COUNTERPARTS
  681                /       IN THE R-L MONITOR; ADEQUATE DESCRIPTIONS WILL BE PROVIDED IN EACH SECTION TO
  682                /       GIVE SOME INSIGHT AS TO HOW THE R-L MONITOR WAS GRADUALLY REPLACED WITH NEW
  683                /       P?S/8 EQUIVALENTS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 15

  685                /       SECTION 1: THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
  686   
  687                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CALLING SEQUENCE.
  688   
  689                /       CDF     MYFLD                   /SET THE DATA FIELD TO THE CALLING FIELD.
  690                /       CIF     00                      /THE HANDLER IS IN FIELD 0.
  691                /       JMS I   (SYSIO)/(7640)          /CALL THE HANDLER IN 07640.
  692                /       BUFFER                          /12-BIT READ/WRITE BUFFER ADDRESS.
  693                /       FUNCTION                        /FUNCTION WORD; SEE BELOW.
  694                /       BLOCK                           /STARTING LOGICAL BLOCK NUMBER. [NOTE: ALL
  695                                                        /LOGICAL BLOCKS ARE 128 WORDS EACH.]
  696   
  697                /       THE CALL ALWAYS RETURNS HERE WITH THE DATA FIELD SET TO THE CALLING FIELD, THE
  698                /       AC CLEAR AND THE LINK UNDEFINED.  [NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
  699                /       RETURNS WITH THE LINK CLEAR AS RECOMMENDED.]
  700   
  701                /       FUNCTION WORD BREAKDOWN.
  702   
  703                /       BIT[0]:         READ=0, WRITE=1.
  704   
  705                /       BITS[1-5]:      BLOCK/PAGE COUNT 1 THROUGH 31, 00000=32 PAGES [AN ENTIRE MEMORY
  706                /                       FIELD].  NOTE: IF A TRANSFER CROSSES 7777 IN ANY MEMORY FIELD,
  707                /                       THE TRANSFER GENERALLY CONTINUES IN 0000 OF THE SAME MEMORY
  708                /                       FIELD, NOT THE NEXT HIGHER FIELD.  HOWEVER, THIS BEHAVIOR IS
  709                /                       HARDWARE-DEPENDENT AND NOT TO BE ATTEMPTED OUTSIDE OF SPECIFIC
  710                /                       WELL-DOCUMENTED CIRCUMSTANCES.  [NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM
  711                /                       DEVICE HANDLER PERFORMS THIS SPECIFIC TRANSFER INTERNALLY
  712                /                       DURING THE NORMAL BOOTUP PROCESS TO OPTIMIZE PERFORMANCE.]
  713   
  714                /       BITS[6-8]:      READ/WRITE BUFFER MEMORY FIELD EXTENSION.
  715   
  716                /       BITS[9-11]:     THE LOGICAL UNIT FOR THE CALL IN THE RANGE OF 0-7 [SEE BELOW].
  717   
  718                /       NOTE: ALL CALLS TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER HAVE NO SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS
  719                /       REGARDING THE AC, LINK OR INTERRUPT STATE.  CERTAIN DEVICE HANDLERS FOR
  720                /       OMNIBUS DEVICES, SUCH AS THE TD8E, WILL INHIBIT INTERRUPTS WITHIN THE HANDLER;
  721                /       THE PREVIOUS INTERRUPT STATE WILL BE RESTORED UPON EXIT.  THE SYSTEM DEVICE
  722                /       MUST NOT GENERATE INTERRUPTS, GENERALLY ACHIEVED BY CLEARING INTERRUPT-ENABLE
  723                /       WITHIN THE DEVICE; THIS ALLOWS DEVICE FLAGS TO REMAIN RAISED IF NECESSARY
  724                /       [AS IS IMPLEMENTED FOR THE RX DISKETTE CONTROLLERS].  INTERRUPT-DRIVEN
  725                /       PROGRAMS SHOULD INCLUDE CUSTOM PATCHING SECTIONS TO HANDLE STRAY INTERRUPTS.
  726   
  727                /       THE TC01/TC08 CONTROLLER CAN BE CONNECTED TO THE EARLIEST COMPATIBLE SYSTEMS
  728                /       SUCH AS THE CLASSIC PDP-8 COMPUTER; NO MECHANISM TO LIMIT INTERRUPTS EXISTS
  729                /       WITHIN THIS DEVICE HANDLER.  AS LONG AS WORST-CASE INTERRUPT-HANDLING LATENCY
  730                /       IS WITHIN REASON, DECTAPE PERFORMANCE SHOULD NOT BE COMPROMISED WHILE ALSO
  731                /       MAINTAINING GOOD OVERLAP WITH OTHER DEVICES [SUCH AS THE SYSTEM CONSOLE].
  732   
  733                /       DECTAPES ARE BASED ON THE EARLIER LINCTAPES AS USED IN VARIOUS SYSTEMS.
  734                /       APPENDIX "B" CONTAINS ADDITIONAL DETAILS ABOUT VARIOUS LINCTAPE AND DECTAPE
  735                /       ISSUES INCLUDING IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 16

  737                /       LOGICAL UNIT CONSIDERATIONS.
  738   
  739                /       THE ASSIGNMENT OF LOGICAL UNITS VERSUS PHYSICAL MEDIA IS HARDWARE-DEPENDENT.
  740                /       ON THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER THIS REFERS TO THE PHYSICAL TU55/TU56
  741                /       DRIVE UNITS ATTACHED TO THE CONTROLLER.  THE ACTUAL UNIT ASSIGNMENT IS
  742                /       USER-CONTROLLED BY SETTING DRIVE-RESIDENT ADJUSTABLE SELECT SWITCHES.  SIMILAR
  743                /       CONSIDERATIONS APPLY TO ALL DECTAPE AND LINCTAPE SYSTEMS AS WELL AS CERTAIN
  744                /       DISKETTE SYSTEMS; THE ABILITY TO CHANGE DRIVE SELECTION VARIES WITH THE EXACT
  745                /       CONTROLLER HARDWARE.  FOR CERTAIN HARDWARE CONFIGURATIONS, LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS
  746                /       MAY [PARTIALLY] BE IMPLEMENTED BY SOME FORM OF DRIVE MAPPING SCHEME.
  747   
  748                /       APPENDIX "C" CONTAINS ADDITIONAL INFORMATION ABOUT THE P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATION OF
  749                /       LOGICAL UNITS ON VARIOUS SUPPORTED DEVICES.
  750   
  751                /       LOGICAL BLOCKSIZE CONSIDERATIONS.
  752   
  753                /       REGARDLESS OF UNDERLYING HARDWARE, ALL P?S/8 SUPPORTED STORAGE DEVICES
  754                /       ARE TREATED AS AN ORDERED SERIES OF LOGICAL BLOCKS, EACH 128 12-BIT
  755                /       WORDS LONG.  THIS SIZE WAS CHOSEN AS A GOOD COMPROMISE BETWEEN PHYSICAL
  756                /       SIZE AND LOGICAL SIZE GIVEN THE MANY ISSUES THAT APPLY.
  757   
  758                /       A LOGICAL BLOCKSIZE THAT MATCHES THE EXACT SIZE OF A SINGLE PDP-8 PAGE
  759                /       OF MEMORY IS A CONVENIENT UNIT ALLOWING A SIMPLE CORRESPONDANCE OF
  760                /       IMAGES OF MEMORY TO STORAGE BLOCKS WITHOUT REQUIRING ANY FORM OF MAPPING
  761                /       BE PERFORMED.
  762   
  763                /       ONE IMPORTANT CONSIDERATION REGARDING BLOCKSIZE IS THE REQUIREMENT FOR
  764                /       DEDICATED BUFFERS WHEN ACCESSING THE BLOCKS.  SHOULD THE BLOCKSIZE BE
  765                /       LARGER, THE MINIMUM BUFFER WOULD ALSO NECESSARILY BECOME LARGER.  FOR AN
  766                /       OPERATING SYSTEM THAT REQUIRES AS LITTLE AS 32 PAGES OF MEMORY, IF THE
  767                /       BLOCKSIZE IS MADE LARGER, CERTAIN PROGRAMS WOULD BECOME IMPOSSIBLE TO
  768                /       IMPLEMENT.  [NOTE: CERTAIN P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS CHECK FOR EXTENDED
  769                /       MEMORY; IF AVAILABLE, LARGER BUFFERS WILL BE ALLOCATED.  BY ALLOWING THE
  770                /       MINIMUM BLOCKSIZE OF 128 WORDS, ANY MULTIPLE BUFFERS AVAILABLE CAN BE
  771                /       ALLOCATED UP TO A MAXIMUM OF 32, THE UPPER LIMIT OF A PDP-8 MEMORY FIELD
  772                /       WHICH IS GENERALLY A HARDWARE CONSTRAINT OF MOST PDP-8 DMA DEVICES.]
  773   
  774                /       APPENDIX "D" CONTAINS ADDITIONAL INFORMATION REGARDING LOGICAL BLOCKSIZE
  775                /       CONSIDERATIONS, ESPECIALLY AS IT PERTAINS TO VARIOUS SUPPORTED DEVICES.
  776   
  777                /       ADDITIONAL DECTAPE PERFORMANCE CONSIDERATIONS.
  778   
  779                /       WHILE P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLERS FOR DECTAPE ACHIEVE A REASONABLE RESULT CARRYING
  780                /       OUT THE VARIOUS READ, WRITE AND BOOTUP OPERATIONS REQUIRED, THERE ARE
  781                /       ADDITIONAL ISSUES THAT PERTAIN EXCLUSIVELY TO DECTAPES [AND LINCTAPES].  DUE
  782                /       TO THE DYNAMICS OF THE DEVICE, THERE ARE OTHER POTENTIAL ACCESS SCHEMES THAT
  783                /       MIGHT BE BETTER SUITED [BUT ONLY TO CERTAIN APPLICATIONS].
  784   
  785                /       THE DESIGN OF P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS INCLUDED CONSIDERATION OF SOME OF
  786                /       THESE ISSUES, EVEN IF NOT ACTUALLY USED.  WITH THE ARRIVAL OF THE P?S/8 SHELL,
  787                /       SOME OF THESE ISSUES MAY BE REVISITED FOR NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS.
  788                 
  789                /       APPENDIX "E" CONTAINS ADDITIONAL INFORMATION REGARDING MANY OF THE DESIGN
  790                /       CONSIDERATIONS THAT GO INTO PRODUCING A PRACTICAL HANDLER [INCLUDING SOME OF
  791                /       THE SPECIFIC ISSUES THAT COULD BE APPLIED IN THE FUTURE].


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 17

  793                /       P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLER ERROR ISSUES.
  794   
  795                /       BY DESIGN, EVERY CALL TO THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS MADE WITH AN
  796                /       EXPECTATION OF SUCCESS; THERE IS NO MECHANISM FOR THE CALLING PROGRAM TO
  797                /       INTERACT WITH POTENTIAL ERRORS WHICH ARE ALWAYS DEVICE-DEPENDENT.  BY
  798                /       ISOLATING INTERNAL ERROR HANDLING FROM THE CALLING PROGRAM, PROGRAMS CAN BE
  799                /       WRITTEN IN A DEVICE-INDEPENDENT MANNER.
  800   
  801                /       ACTUAL ERROR HANDLING IS DEPENDENT ON THE OVERALL SYSTEM CONFIGURATION; THE
  802                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY INTERCEPTS ALL SYSTEM DEVICE ERRORS PRESENTING THE
  803                /       USER WITH SEVERAL ALTERNATIVES REGARDING THE DETECTED ERROR.  IN EVERY
  804                /       CONFIGURATION THERE IS ALWAYS A MECHANISM AVAILABLE TO RETRY THE LATEST
  805                /       OPERATION.
  806   
  807                /       MOST REAL-WORLD PROBLEMS WITH STORAGE DEVICES ARE CAUSED BY FAILING TO ALLOW
  808                /       THE DEVICE TO FUNCTION PROPERLY, SUCH AS HAVING THE DEVICE OFF-LINE, OR THE
  809                /       DEVICE IS WRITE-PROTECTED [IF APPLICABLE]; CORRECTING THE CONDITION WILL ALLOW
  810                /       THE LATEST OPERATION TO SUCCEED ON THE NEXT RETRY.  FOR CERTAIN DEVICES, THERE
  811                /       IS A SMALL POSSIBILITY OF A SOFT ERROR SITUATION, GENERALLY CLEARED BY
  812                /       RETRYING THE OPERATION.  IF ERRORS ARE OCCURRING OFTEN, THIS IS LIKELY A SIGN
  813                /       OF HARDWARE FAILURE REQUIRING MAINTENANCE BEYOND THE CONTROL OF HANDLER
  814                /       SOFTWARE.  THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER AND DECTAPE DRIVES ARE SUBJECT TO
  815                /       ALL OF THESE POTENTIAL ISSUES.
  816   
  817                /       THE P?S/8 SYSTEM LOADER [AN IMBEDDED CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER LOCATED
  818                /       WITHIN THE RESIDENT SYSTEM KERNEL] AVOIDS ERROR HANDLING COMPLICATIONS BY THIS
  819                /       DESIGN PHILOSOPHY.  APPENDIX "F" DISCUSSES P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ERROR
  820                /       HANDLING IN MORE DETAIL, INCLUDING COMPARISON WITH OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS
  821                /       THAT HAVE CONCEPTUAL FLAWS IN CORRESPONDING INTERNAL SITUATIONS.
  822   
  823                /       P?S/8 CODE OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES.
  824   
  825                /       THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER DESCRIBED IN THIS DOCUMENT IS THE PRODUCT OF
  826                /       TREMENDOUS EFFORT ON THE PART OF A RELATIVELY SMALL GROUP OF INDIVIDUALS.
  827                /       DESPITE THE OBJECTIVE OF CREATING A FUNCTIONAL DEVICE HANDLER THAT FITS IN
  828                /       AT MOST 128 WORDS, THE EFFORT INVOLVED EXCEEDS THAT OF MOST OTHER PDP-8
  829                /       SOFTWARE PROJECTS OVER 100 TIMES AS LARGE.  MOST OF THIS EFFORT CONSISTS OF
  830                /       APPLYING VARIOUS OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES; ONLY A FEW PDP-8 EXPERTS ARE
  831                /       FAMILIAR WITH ALL OF THE "TRICKS" ALLOWED [AS WELL AS CERTAIN OTHER TECHNIQUES
  832                /       NOT ALLOWED].  HAD THIS EFFORT NOT BEEN APPLIED, P?S/8 WOULD STILL LARGELY
  833                /       RESEMBLE THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM INTERNALLY [WITH ALL THE ATTENDANT
  834                /       DRAWBACKS]; MOST OF THE OVERALL IMPROVEMENTS OF P?S/8 OVER THE R-L SYSTEM
  835                /       DEPEND ON THE PROGRESS MADE WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
  836   
  837                /       A COMPLETE DISCUSSION OF ALL RELEVANT TECHNIQUES BOTH ACTUAL AND THEORETICAL
  838                /       IS AVAILABLE WITHIN APPENDIX "G".  WHERE RELEVANT, CERTAIN DISCUSSIONS
  839                /       REGARDING TENTATIVE CONTENTS OF SPECIFIC MEMORY LOCATIONS WITHIN THE SYSTEM
  840                /       DEVICE HANDLER ARE RETAINED FOR FUTURE REFERENCE.  [NOTE: ANY PRACTICAL
  841                /       HANDLER IS ALWAYS THE PRODUCT OF VARIOUS TRADE-OFFS OF POTENTIAL OPTIMIZATION
  842                /       TECHNIQUES AS CERTAIN METHODS ARE BY NATURE IN DIRECT CONFLICT WITH EACH
  843                /       OTHER.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 18

  845                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM GENERATION CONSIDERATIONS.
  846   
  847                /       SINCE P?S/8 IS A COMPLETE AND INDEPENDENT OPERATING SYSTEM, MEANS MUST BE
  848                /       AVAILABLE TO CREATE AN ENTIRE SYSTEM FROM SYSTEM COMPONENTS LOADED INTO MEMORY
  849                /       BY ANOTHER OPERATING SYSTEM.
  850   
  851                /       APPENDIX "H" DISCUSSES DETAILS ABOUT THE SYSTEM GENERATION PROCESS AND VARIOUS
  852                /       METHODS USED TO ACCOMPLISH THIS.  SINCE THERE ARE SEVERAL POTENTIAL WAYS TO
  853                /       GO ABOUT THIS [NOT ALL OF WHICH HAVE BEEN IMPLEMENTED TO DATE], A COMPLETE
  854                /       DISCUSSION IS AVAILABLE TO GIVE SOME INSIGHT ON THIS IMPORTANT PROCESS.
  855                /       HISTORIC METHODS ARE DETAILED AS WELL AS SOME TENTATIVE FUTURE METHODS TO
  856                /       ALLOW SYSTEM GENERATION BY USERS INSTEAD OF SYSTEM GURUS.  CERTAIN AUXILIARY
  857                /       ISSUES ARE DISCUSSED THAT HAVE AFFECTED THE PERFORMANCE OF OTHER OPERATING
  858                /       SYSTEMS [IN SOME CASES CAUSING NEEDLESS OVERHEAD DUE TO EITHER INADEQUATE
  859                /       PLANNING OR IMPLEMENTATION SHORTCOMINGS].  IN ONE PARTICULAR CASE, REPURPOSING
  860                /       AN OBSCURE FEATURE OF THE PAL LANGUAGE MAY LEAD TO AN ELEGANT GENERATION
  861                /       METHOD IN FUTURE SYSTEM BUILDING UTILITIES.
  862   
  863                /       ADDITIONAL SYSTEM COMPONENTS.
  864   
  865                /       APART FROM SYSTEM PROGRAMS AND OTHER UTILITIES, CERTAIN SYSTEM COMPONENTS ARE
  866                /       ESSENTIAL TO P?S/8 DEPENDING ON THE CONFIGURATION.  WHILE NOT DISCUSSED IN
  867                /       DETAIL IN THIS DOCUMENT, CERTAIN REFERENCES TO THE FOLLOWING WILL BE MADE:
  868   
  869                /       1) SYSTEM HANDLER DEVICE EXTENSION.  WHILE NOT RELEVANT TO THE TC01/TC08
  870                /          CONFIGURATION, CERTAIN P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS REQUIRE ANOTHER SECTION
  871                /          OF CODE TO BE LOADED INTO THE LAST QUARTER OF THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD.
  872   
  873                /       2) LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY.  ALL P?S/8 SYSTEMS NEWER THAN VERY EARLY RELEASES
  874                /          SUPPORT AN ALTERNATE CONSOLE AND LINEPRINTER INTERFACE TO ALLOW LOGICAL
  875                /          CONFIGURATIONS INSTEAD OF THE HARD-WIRED DEVICE 03/04 CONSOLE AND DEVICE 66
  876                /          LINEPRINTER DEVICES.  ALL SYSTEM DEVICES, INCLUDING THE TC01/TC08
  877                /          CONFIGURATION, INTERFACE WIH THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY FOR SPECIFIC
  878                /          PURPOSES [HANDLER PERFORMANCE ENHANCEMENT, ENHANCED ERROR RECOVERY].
  879   
  880                /       3) P?S/8 SHELL OVERLAY FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA.  THIS IS PART OF THE
  881                /          OPTIONAL SHELL OVERLAY CURRENTLY UNDER DEVELOPMENT.  ALL SYSTEM DEVICE
  882                /          HANDLERS MUST INCLUDE CERTAIN STATUS INFORMATION REGARDING THE DYNAMICS
  883                /          OF THIS FACILITY.
  884   
  885                /       IMAGE OF BLOCK 0000 FOR THE P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM.
  886   
  887                /       A COMPLETE IMAGE OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS DEFINED BELOW; IT IS INTENDED
  888                /       TO BE WRITTEN OUT AS THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE.
  889   
  890                /       THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER PROVIDES AN EXIT ROUTINE AT 07600 TO RELOAD
  891                /       THE OPERATING SYSTEM.  ANY PROGRAM CAN TAKE THIS STANDARD EXIT EVEN IF NOT
  892                /       EXPRESSLY WRITTEN FOR P?S/8; MANUAL RESTART AT 07600 IS ALSO SUPPORTED.
  893   
  894                /       A FULLY DEVICE-INDEPENDENT LOGICAL READ/WRITE INTERFACE IS PROVIDED TO GIVE
  895                /       ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE; ALL P?S/8 SYSTEMS USE THE IDENTICAL CALLING
  896                /       CONVENTION REGARDLESS OF UNDERLYING SYSTEM DEVICE HARDWARE.  SUBROUTINE CALLS
  897                /       CAN BE MADE BY ANY P?S/8 SYSTEM UTILITY OR FULLY COMPLIANT USER PROGRAM.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 19

  899                /       A LOADING TEMPLATE IS PROVIDED TO FINALIZE ALL EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS THAT
  900                /       LOAD OVER THE OPERATING SYSTEM [OR SHELL PROGRAM].  THIS INTERNAL CALL
  901                /       MECHANISM ALLOWS ALL USER MEMORY TO BE AVAILABLE TO ANY EXECUTABLE PROGRAM
  902                /       WITHOUT RESTRICTION; IT IS ALSO USED TO IMPLEMENT OVERLAY STRUCTURES WITHIN
  903                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS AS REQUIRED.
  904   
  905                /       [NOTE: FOR HISTORICAL PURPOSES, THE INTERNAL CALL IS REFERRED TO AS THE
  906                /       "SYSTEM LOADER".  THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM CONTAINS A VAGUELY ANALOGOUS
  907                /       ROUTINE THAT WAS IMPLEMENTED IN A VERY AWKWARD MANNER WITH MANY SERIOUS
  908                /       LIMITATIONS.  CERTAIN READERS OF THIS DOCUMENT ARE MORE FAMILIAR WITH THE R-L
  909                /       MONITOR SYSTEM AND ITS ORIGINAL EXECUTABLE PROGRAM LOADING MECHANISM; AS SUCH,
  910                /       THE SIGNIFICANT DIFFERENCES WILL BE DETAILED ELSEWHERE IS THIS DOCUMENT.]
  911   
  912                /       NUMEROUS RESERVED WORDS EXIST FOR A VARIETY OF SYSTEM PARAMETERS USED BY
  913                /       VARIOUS PORTIONS OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND ITS UTILITIES.  MOST NOTABLY,
  914                /       THIS INCLUDES THE ABILITY TO PASS INPUT AND OUTPUT FILES TO EXECUTABLE
  915                /       PROGRAMS, OPTION SWITCHES, AN =XXXX PARAMETER [WHERE XXXX IS ANY FOUR-DIGIT
  916                /       OCTAL VALUE], ACCESS TO THE CURRENT DATE AND VARIOUS STATUS WORDS AFFECTING
  917                /       THE PRESENCE OR STATUS OF CERTAIN OPTIONAL SYSTEM COMPONENTS.  [NOTE: SOME OF
  918                /       THESE VALUES ARE DEPENDENT ON OPTIONAL COMPONENTS OF THE SYSTEM NOT YET
  919                /       [RE-]WRITTEN, BUT VIRTUALLY ALL OF THE RESERVED WORDS HAVE BEEN DEFINED FOR
  920                /       OVER THREE DECADES WHILE VARIOUS COMPONENTS WERE IN THE PROCESS OF
  921                /       DEVELOPMENT].
  922   
  923                /       LOADING CONSIDERATIONS FOR SYSTEM GENERATION.
  924   
