/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 24

 1155                /       MULTI-PURPOSE DECTAPE DONE FLAG WAIT ROUTINE ["SYSWAIT"].
 1156   
 1157                /       ALL CALLERS ENTER HERE.  WITH ONE EXCEPTION [SEE BELOW] THE ROUTINE ALWAYS
 1158                /       RETURNS DIRECTLY TO THE CALLER.
 1159   
 1160                /       THIS SUBROUTINE IS CALLED FROM SEVERAL DIFFERENT CODE SEGMENTS FOR A VARIETY
 1161                /       OF PURPOSES:
 1162   
 1163                /       1) INITIATING A SEARCH IN A DIRECTION DETERMINED BY THE CALLING PROGRAM.
 1164                /          DETECTED BLOCK MARKS WILL BE USED BY THE CALLER TO CALCULATE THE CORRECT
 1165                /          DIRECTION FOR SUBSEQUENT SEARCH OPERATIONS [AS REQUIRED; GENERALLY SEVERAL
 1166                /          SEARCH OPERATIONS ARE NECESSARY FOR THE CURRENT OVERALL DEVICE HANDLER
 1167                /          CALL UNTIL THE TAPE IS PROPERLY POSITIONED].  THE GOAL IS TO LOCATE THE
 1168                /          DESIRED BLOCK FROM A TAPE POSITION JUST BEFORE THE BLOCK WHILE MOVING
 1169                /          FORWARD.  WHEN NECESSARY, REVERSE SEARCHES MUST BE PERFORMED UNTIL A BLOCK
 1170                /          EXACTLY TWO HIGHER THAN THE TARGET BLOCK IS DETECTED BEFORE TURNING THE
 1171                /          TAPE AROUND.
 1172   
 1173                /          [NOTE: FOR LOW-NUMBERED BLOCKS THE REVERSE END ZONE WILL BE ENCOUNTERED
 1174                /          WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE; THIS WILL NECESSITATE AN IMMEDIATE CHANGE TO
 1175                /          FORWARD MOTION; BLOCK 0000 OR BLOCK 0001 MUST BE READ OR WRITTEN FORWARD
 1176                /          COMING OUT OF THE REVERSE END ZONE.]
 1177   
 1178                /       2) CHANGE A SUCCESSFUL SEARCH INTO A READ OR WRITE.  [BY DESIGN, CHANGING THE
 1179                /          CURRENT MODE FROM SEARCH TO READ OR WRITE JUST AFTER DETECTING THE REAR
 1180                /          BLOCK MARK IS THE INTENDED METHOD.]
 1181   
 1182                /       3) CONTINUING A MULTI-BLOCK READ OR WRITE TRANSFER IN PROGRESS.  [NOTE: ONLY
 1183                /          THE FIRST OF A SET OF CONTIGUOUS BLOCKS IS EXPLICITLY SEARCHED FOR.  IT IS
 1184                /          NEITHER NECESSARY NOR EVEN POSSIBLE TO SEARCH FOR EVERY BLOCK IN A
 1185                /          MULTI-BLOCK TRANSFER AS THE SEARCH FUNCTION WOULD OF NECESSITY CAUSE THE
 1186                /          NEED TO TURN THE TAPE AROUND TWICE.  WHILE THE OPERATING MODE CAN BE
 1187                /          CHANGED FROM SEARCH TO READ OR WRITE, IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO CHANGE A READ
 1188                /          OR WRITE TO A SEARCH AND IMMEDIATELY DETECT THE NEXT CONTIGUOUS BLOCK.]
 1189   
 1190                /       IF AN END ZONE ERROR IS DETECTED DURING ANY CALL, THE SUBROUTINE DOES NOT
 1191                /       RETURN TO THE CALLER.  INSTEAD, A DEDICATED ROUTINE "STURNIT" [07673] IS
 1192                /       ENTERED TO REVERSE THE CURRENT TAPE DIRECTION AND REWIND OUT OF THE END ZONE.
 1193                /       THIS IS FOLLOWED BY A NEW SEARCH OPERATION FOR THE LATEST BLOCK.
 1194   
 1195                /       ALL CALLS SHOULD HAVE AC[9-11] CLEAR.  SETTING AC[9] INVERTS DECTAPE
 1196                /       INTERRUPT ENABLE, GENERALLY ALREADY CLEAR; SETTING AC[10] WOULD PRESERVE THE
 1197                /       ERROR FLAG FROM THE PREVIOUS OPERATION; SETTING AC[11] WOULD PRESERVE THE
 1198                /       DECTAPE DONE FLAG FROM THE PREVIOUS OPERATION.  ALL OF THESE CONDITIONS SHOULD
 1199                /       BE AVOIDED IN THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER FOR PROPER OPERATION.
 1200   
 1201                /       ANY BITS SET IN AC[0-9] WILL INVERT THE CORRESPONDING BITS IN STATUS REGISTER
 1202                /       "A".  CALLS WITH THE AC CLEAR ARE MADE TO CONTINUE PREVAILING SEARCH, READ OR
 1203                /       WRITE OPERATIONS.  THE WORD COUNT REGISTER IS ALWAYS SET TO TRANSFER THE FIRST
 1204                /       128 DATA WORDS OF THE CURRENT BLOCK IN CASE THE CALL IS FOR A READ OR WRITE
 1205                /       OPERATION. [NOTE: WORD COUNT REGISTER SETTINGS ARE IRRELEVANT DURING NORMAL
 1206                /       MODE SEARCH OPERATIONS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 25

 1208                /       DURING THE BOOTUP OVERLAY THE RETURN ADDRESS FROM THE READ NEXT BLOCK CALL
 1209                /       "SBLKRET" [07727] IS STORED OVER THE SUBROUTINE HEADER FROM THE PERMANENT COPY
 1210                /       ON BLOCK 0000; WHEN THE DECTAPE FLAG RAISES AT THE END OF THE INITIAL BOOTUP
 1211                /       READ OF BLOCK 0000, CONTROL WILL BE PASSED TO "SBLKRET" [07727].  ADDITIONAL
 1212                /       SEQUENTIAL READ OPERATIONS WILL BE IMMEDIATELY PERFORMED [WITHOUT SEARCHING]
 1213                /       TO LOAD IN THE KEYBOARD MONITOR [AND INTEGRATED LINE EDITOR], THE TFS
 1214                /       DIRECTORY AND THE KEYBOARD MONITOR TRANSIENT AREA FROM BLOCKS 0001-0017 IN ONE
 1215                /       CONTINUOUS READ OPERATION.  ALL RELEVANT PARAMETERS IN MEMORY ARE SET TO
 1216                /       APPROPRIATE VALUES THAT ARE THE PERMANENT CONTENTS OF THE CORRESPONDING WORDS
 1217                /       ON BLOCK 0000.  THIS WILL CONTINUE THE OVERALL READ OPERATION ALREADY IN
 1218                /       PROGRESS.  THE NET EFFECT IS EQUIVALENT TO THE [ACTUALLY IMPOSSIBLE] DEVICE
 1219                /       HANDLER CALL TO READ BLOCKS 0000-0017 INTO 07600-03577 EXITING TO "KBDMON"
 1220                /       [02530]. ["KBDMON" [02530] IS THE ADDRESS WITHIN THE KEYBOARD MONITOR WHERE
 1221                /       FINALIZATION IS PERFORMED TO ALLOW USER INPUT FROM THE CONSOLE OR FROM THE
 1222                /       CONTENTS OF BATCH FILES IF BATCH IS IN EFFECT.]
 1223   
 1224   07612* 7727  SYSWAIT,SBLKRET                         /UPDATE AND WAIT ROUTINE; PRELOADED WITH PROPER
 1225                                                        /RETURN ADDRESS NEEDED FOR BOOTUP OPERATIONS.
 1226   
 1227                /       SPECIFIC VALUES ARE PASSED IN THE AC TO MODIFY STATUS REGISTER "A" IN VARIOUS
 1228                /       WAYS.  AC[10-11] MUST BE CLEAR TO RESET THE DECTAPE AND ERROR FLAGS.  THE REST
 1229                /       OF THE AC IS SETUP FOR VARIOUS FUNCTION AND MOTION BIT UPDATES.  THE PRIOR
 1230                /       CONTENTS OF STATUS REGISTER "A" MUST BE KNOWN; ALL CHANGES TO STATUS REGISTER
 1231                /       "A" [OTHER THAN BITS[10-11] ARE ACCOMPLISHED BY A BIT-WISE .XOR. BETWEEN
 1232                /       STATUS REGISTER "A" AND THE AC [WHICH IS THEN CLEARED].
 1233   
 1234                /       IF THE LATEST OPERATION IS SEARCH, STATUS REGISTER "A" WILL BE SET FOR NORMAL
 1235                /       MODE OPERATION WHICH INHIBITS WORD COUNT OVERFLOW [WHILE INCREMENTING THE WORD
 1236                /       COUNT REGISTER]; THE CURRENT ADDRESS REGISTER IS NOT INCREMENTED TO ALLOW A
 1237                /       ONE WORD SEARCH BUFFER FOR DETECTED BLOCK MARKS.
 1238   
 1239                /       IF THE LATEST OPERATION IS A REVERSAL OF TAPE DIRECTION [AFTER AN END ERROR],
 1240                /       AC[4] MUST BE SET BECAUSE ALL ERRORS CLEAR TAPE MOTION.  AC[3] WILL ALSO BE
 1241                /       SET TO REVERSE THE CURRENT DIRECTION [WITHOUT KNOWING WHAT IT WAS PRIOR TO THE
 1242                /       END ZONE ERROR].  THIS CONDITION CAN ONLY COME ABOUT DURING THE SEARCH PHASE
 1243                /       OF DEVICE HANDLER OPERATION; THE SEARCH ROUTINE WILL LATER RESOLVE ALL MOTION
 1244                /       AND BLOCK POSITION ISSUES BEFORE MOVING ON TO A READ OR WRITE OPERATION.
 1245   
 1246                /       THE WORD COUNT WILL BE SET FOR A 128 WORD DATA TRANSFER.  THIS IS REQUIRED
 1247                /       SHOULD THE LATEST OPERATION BE A READ OR WRITE [AND OTHERWISE IGNORED]. [AS
 1248                /       SUCH, THE 129TH WORD IS ALWAYS IGNORED IN MEMORY; IT WILL BE USED FOR CHECKSUM
 1249                /       CALCULATION PURPOSES ONLY.]
 1250   
 1251                /       THEORETICALLY, IT COULD BE NECESSARY TO INCLUDE THE NEXT INSTRUCTION [OR
 1252                /       EQUIVALENT] TO PREVENT ACCIDENTALLY SETTING BITS THAT MUST REMAIN CLEAR.
 1253   
 1254                /       AND     (7770)                  /MASK OFF POTENTIALLY PRESENT UNWANTED BITS
 1255   
 1256   07613* 6764          DTXA                            /INVERT STATUS REGISTER "A" BITS PER CONTENTS OF
 1257                                                        /AC[0-9]; IF AC-10-11] ARE CLEAR RESET ERROR
 1258                                                        /AND DONE FLAGS; CLEAR THE AC.
 1259   
 1260   07614* 1201          TAD     S7600/(-200)            /SETUP THE WORD COUNT REGISTER FOR A 128 WORD 
 1261   07615* 3354          DCA     WC                      /DATA TRANSFER IN CASE READING OR WRITING.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 26

 1263                /       BOOTSTRAP COMPATIBILITY ISSUES.
 1264   
 1265                /       THIS CODE MUST BE CAREFULLY LOCATED FOR BOOTSTRAP COMPATIBILITY WITH THE
 1266                /       DIRECT OVERLAY FORM OF BOOTSTRAP [AS OPPOSED TO OTHER METHODS THAT READ IN THE
 1267                /       ENTIRE BLOCK THEN START THE CODE FROM AN EXTERNAL ADDRESS].  THE WAIT LOOP
 1268                /       MUST BE PLACED AT EXACTLY 07616 AND 07617 BECAUSE MOST BOOTUP SEQUENCES DEPEND
 1269                /       ON THE GRADUAL OVERLAY OF THE CODE WAITING FOR THE READ OPERATION TO COMPLETE.
 1270                /       LOCATION 07616 MAY CONTAIN [OR BE OVERLAID] WITH A VARIANT INSTRUCTION SUCH AS
 1271                /       "DTSF" [6771] AS OPPOSED TO "DTSF!DTRB" [6773] AS USED HERE.  FULL BOOTSTRAP
 1272                /       COMPATIBILITY EXISTS WHETHER P?S/8 IS ATTEMPTING TO OVERLAY THIS OR SIMILAR
 1273                /       CODE [OR CONVERSELY, SIMILAR CODE IS ATTEMPTING TO OVERLAY P?S/8].
 1274   
 1275                /       NOTE: AT THE POINT IN TIME 07616 IS OVERLAID, LOCATION 07617 IS STILL
 1276                /       "JMP SWAITLP" [5216].
 1277   
 1278                /       THE NEXT DATA BREAK TRANSFER WILL OVERLAY LOCATION 07617.  THE REPLACEMENT
 1279                /       CONTENTS MUST BE A "JMP" [OR "JMS"] INSTRUCTION TO AN APPROPRIATE ADDRESS IN
 1280                /       THE RANGE OF 07600-07616 ["JMP 07600-07616" OR "JMS 07600-07615" DUE TO THE
 1281                /       WAY THE "JMS" INSTRUCTION OPERATES]; AT THIS POINT IN TIME, ONLY THESE FEW
 1282                /       LOCATIONS CONTAIN THE DATA TRANSFERRED FROM BLOCK 0000 OF THE BOOTING UP
 1283                /       DECTAPE.  [NOTE: MOST SYSTEMS USE THE "JMP SWAITLP" [5216] [OR EQUIVALENT] AS
 1284                /       IMPLEMENTED WITHIN THIS DEVICE HANDLER.]
 1285   
 1286                /       STANDARD TC01/TC08 DECTAPE WAIT LOOP.
 1287   
 1288                        IFNZRO  7616-.  <ERROR .>       /ENSURE COMPATIBILITY WITH ADDRESS STANDARDS.
 1290   
 1291   07616* 6773  SWAITLP,DTSF!DTRB                       /SKIP ON DECTAPE FLAG OR ERROR FLAG SET,
 1292                                                        /.OR. TRANSFER STATUS REGISTER "B" INTO AC.
 1293   07617* 5216          JMP     SWAITLP                 /FLAG NOT UP YET, KEEP WAITING FOR IT.
 1294   
 1295                /       THE WAIT LOOP EXITS WHEN EITHER THE DECTAPE DONE FLAG OR THE ERROR FLAG
 1296                /       RAISES; THE AC NOW CONTAINS THE CONTENTS OF STATUS REGISTER "B".
 1297   
 1298                /       DETERMINE IF AN END ZONE ERROR OCCURRED. [NOTE: IT IS IMPOSSIBLE FOR THE MARK
 1299                /       TRACK, TIMING TRACK OR SELECT ERROR STATUS BITS TO BE SET IF THE END ZONE
 1300                /       ERROR BIT IS SET.]
 1301   
 1302   07620* 7006          RTL                             /MOVE END ZONE ERROR STATUS BIT TO AC[0]
 1303   
 1304                /       NOTE: THE AC IS GUARANTEED TO BE NON-ZERO AS AC[9] NOW CONTAINS THE DONE FLAG
 1305                /       STATUS BIT AND AC[11] NOW CONTAINS THE ERROR FLAG STATUS BIT.  THIS ALLOWS THE
 1306                /       TEST FOR THE END ZONE ERROR TO BE PERFORMED BY EITHER AN "SPA CLA" [7710] OR
 1307                /       "SPA SNA CLA" [7750] INSTRUCTION.
 1308   
 1309   07621* 7750  S7750,  SPA SNA CLA                     /SKIP IF NOT END ZONE ERROR; ALSO USED
 1310                                                        /ELSEWHERE AS THE CONSTANT 7750.
 1311   07622* 5273          JMP      STURNIT                /TURN THE TAPE AROUND IF WE ARE IN THE END ZONE.
 1312   07623* 6772          DTRB                            /GET CONTENTS OF STATUS REGISTER "B" AGAIN.
 1313   07624* 7500          SMA                             /SKIP IF ANY OTHER ERROR[S] SET.
 1314   07625* 5612          JMP I   SYSWAIT                 /RETURN WITH DIRTY AC [BUT NO ERROR BITS SET].
 1315   07626* 7402  SHALT,  HLT                             /HALT SHOWING STATUS REGISTER "B" IN THE AC.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 27

 1317                /       ERROR RECOVERY CONSIDERATIONS.
 1318   
 1319                /       AT THIS POINT THE MACHINE HAS HALTED DUE TO SOME FORM OF ERROR [OTHER THAN END
 1320                /       ZONE ERROR].  IT IS EXPECTED THE OPERATOR WILL PRESS "CONTINUE" ON THE CPU
 1321                /       FRONT PANEL AFTER PERHAPS PERFORMING SOME CORRECTIVE STEPS [DEPENDING ON THE
 1322                /       SPECIFICS OF THE ERROR CONDITION].  A COMPLETE RETRY OF THE LATEST DECTAPE
 1323                /       OPERATION WILL THEN BE PERFORMED [IF POSSIBLE].
 1324   
 1325                /       USERS FAMILIAR WITH STATUS REGISTER "B" BIT ASSIGNMENTS CAN OBSERVE THE EXACT
 1326                /       ERROR CONDITION CAUSING THE CURRENT PROBLEM DISPLAYED IN THE AC INDICATOR
 1327                /       LIGHTS [OR THE EQUIVALENT STATUS LAMPS ON THE TC01 OR TC08 STATUS PANEL].
 1328                /       THERE ARE SEVERAL CATEGORIES OF ERRORS; CERTAIN ERROR CONDITIONS CAN BE CAUSED
 1329                /       BY SEVERAL POSSIBLE FAILURE SITUATIONS.
 1330   
 1331                /       SELECT ERROR CONSIDERATIONS.
 1332   
 1333                /       SELECT ERROR CAN BE CAUSED BY ANY OF THE FOLLOWING:
 1334   
 1335                /       1) DRIVE SELECT - NO DRIVES ARE SET TO THE CURRENT DRIVE UNIT.  ALL DRIVE
 1336                /          SELECT LIGHTS ARE OFF.
 1337   
 1338                /       2) DRIVE SELECT - TOO MANY DRIVES ARE SET TO THE CURRENT DRIVE UNIT.  ALL
 1339                /          DRIVES SET TO THE CURRENT UNIT WILL HAVE LIT SELECT LIGHTS.
 1340   
 1341                /          A UNIQUE TAPE DRIVE MUST BE SET TO THE CURRENT DRIVE UNIT TO AVOID A SELECT
 1342                /          ERROR.  FUNCTIONS THAT CAUSE TAPE MOTION ADDITIONALLY REQUIRE A MOUNTED
 1343                /          DECTAPE; WITHOUT A TAPE, ONE OR MORE REEL MOTORS WILL SPIN. [NOTE: THIS
 1344                /          ALSO DEPENDS ON THE SPECIFIC DRIVE MODEL; TU56 DRIVE MOTORS WILL ALWAYS
 1345                /          SPIN WHEN THERE IS NO TAPE TENSION UNLESS THE DRIVE IS OFFLINE].
 1346   
 1347                /       3) WRITE-PROTECT - A TAPE IS CORRECTLY SELECTED AND CAPABLE OF MOTION.  THE
 1348                /          DRIVE-MOUNTED WRITE-ENABLE SWITCH IS SET TO THE WRITE-LOCKED POSITION.
 1349                /          WHERE RELEVANT, THE CORRESPONDING WRITE-ENABLE INDICATOR LAMP WILL BE OFF.
 1350                /          ONCE INITIATED, TAPE MOTION OCCURS UNTIL THE DESIRED BLOCK IS LOCATED WHILE
 1351                /          THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARD.  WHEN THE FUNCTION CHANGES FROM NORMAL SEARCH
 1352                /          TO WRITE, THE TAPE STOPS DUE TO THE WRITE-PROTECT ERROR.
 1353   
 1354                /       4) FORMAT SWITCH - THE WRITE TIMING AND MARK TRACK ENABLE SWITCH MUST BE OFF
 1355                /          DURING NORMAL TAPE OPERATIONS. [NOTE: WITHIN THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE
 1356                /          FORMATTING PROGRAM, SELECT ERROR OCCURS IF THE SWITCH IS NOT THROWN WHEN
 1357                /          DIRECTED.  LATER IN THE PROGRAM THE OPERATOR IS INSTRUCTED TO TURN THE
 1358                /          SWITCH OFF TO CONTINUE THE TAPE FORMATTING OPERATION.]
 1359   
 1360                /          A FORMAT-RELATED SELECT ERROR HAPPENS WHEN THE SWITCH IS THROWN AND NORMAL
 1361                /          OPERATIONS ARE ATTEMPTED, AS WELL AS WHEN FORMATTING OPERATIONS ARE
 1362                /          ATTEMPTED WITH THE SWITCH OFF.  [THE LATTER IS ONLY USED IN THE VARIOUS
 1363                /          TC01/TC08 FORMATTING AND DIAGNOSTIC PROGRAMS.]
 1364   
 1365                /       5) DIAGNOSTIC READ - THIS READ ALL FUNCTION REQUIRES THE FORMAT SWITCH TO BE
 1366                /          THROWN TO THE READ MARK POSITION.  ALL OTHER SETTINGS CAUSE A SELECT ERROR.
 1367                /          [NOTE: THIS FUNCTION IS NOT USED WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.]
 1368   
 1369                /       6) OTHER - ISSUING COMMANDS WITH THE RESERVED FUNCTION CODE OF 111 CAUSES A
 1370                /          SELECT ERROR.  [NOTE: THIS FUNCTION IS NOT USED WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 1371                /          HANDLER.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 28

 1373                /       TIMING AND MARK ERROR CONSIDERATIONS.
 1374   
 1375                /       THEORETICALLY, TIMING ERRORS OCCUR ONLY WHEN DMA CYCLES CANNOT BE PERFORMED IN
 1376                /       THE WINDOW OF TIME ALLOTTED PER WORD [3 CYCLES EVERY 133 MICROSECONDS].  THIS
 1377                /       CAN HAPPEN IF THE CPU HALTS WHILE THE OPERATION IS IN EFFECT.  THE ERROR HALT
 1378                /       WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SHOWS THE STATUS WHILE THE MACHINE WAS
 1379                /       RUNNING; THUS, THIS NORMALLY CANNOT HAPPEN UNLESS THE BUSS IS BUSIED OUT BY
 1380                /       EXTREME LOADING FROM ANOTHER DMA DEVICE ALSO IN USE AT THE TIME.  THIS
 1381                /       SITUATION, AS UNLIKELY AS IT IS, CAN BE PREVENTED BY SETTING THE DATA BREAK
 1382                /       PRIORITY FOR THE DECTAPE CONTROLLER HIGHER THAN FOR OTHER DEVICES. [NOTE:
 1383                /       TIMING ERRORS CAN ALSO OCCUR IF IMPROPERLY WRITTEN SOFTWARE IS USED: THE DONE
 1384                /       FLAG MUST BE CLEAR BEFORE IT SETS TO AVOID A TIMING ERROR; THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 1385                /       HANDLER PERFORMS ALL TAPE CONTROL FUNCTIONS IN THE PROPER ORDER STARTING WITH
 1386                /       CLEARING THE DONE AND ERROR FLAGS. THUS, THAT SITUATION CANNOT OCCUR WITHIN
 1387                /       THIS DEVICE HANDLER.]
 1388   
 1389                /       HOWEVER, WHEN THERE ARE READ ERRORS ON THE TIMING AND MARK TRACKS, THE TIMING
 1390                /       ERROR CAN HAPPEN FOR UNEXPECTED REASONS.  TIMING PULSES MUST BE CONSISTENT AND
 1391                /       REGULAR [TO WITHIN A DEFINED TOLERANCE FACTOR]; TIMING TRACK READ ERRORS CAN
 1392                /       AFFECT THE INTERVAL SUFFICIENTLY TO CAUSE AN OTHERWISE UNACCOUNTABLE ERROR.
 1393   
 1394                /       MARK ERRORS OCCUR WHEN THE SIX BIT MARK WINDOW DETECTS CERTAIN ILLEGAL CODES
 1395                /       OR UNEXPECTED CODES DEPENDING ON THE CURRENT OPERATION AND DECODING LOGIC
 1396                /       WITHIN THE CONTROLLER.  SINCE THE MARK WINDOW IS DEPENDENT ON THE TIMING
 1397                /       TRACKS FOR PROPER SYNCHRONIZATION, THE INDICATED MARK ERROR COULD BE THE
 1398                /       INDIRECT RESULT OF TIMING TRACK READ PROBLEMS AS WELL AS MARK TRACK READ
 1399                /       ERRORS.
 1400   
 1401                /       GENERALLY, TIMING AND MARK ERRORS SHOULD NOT OCCUR ON PROPERLY OPERATING
 1402                /       SYSTEMS.  POTENTIAL ISSUES INCLUDE THE CONDITION AND SKEW [ALIGNMENT] OF THE
 1403                /       TAPE HEAD, TAPE GUIDES, THE OVERALL CONDITION AND [PROPER] FORMATTING OF THE
 1404                /       DECTAPE MEDIA AND THE OVERALL CLEANLINESS OF ALL EQUIPMENT.  AS COMPONENTS AGE
 1405                /       AND/OR WEAR, IT IS ESSENTIAL THAT PROPER MAINTENANCE BE PERFORMED.
 1406   
 1407                /       OFTEN THE FIRST SIGN OF A PROBLEM IS THE DETECTION OF TIMING AND MARK ERRORS
 1408                /       SOMEWHERE ON THE TAPE.  AS SUCH, IT IS FORTUITOUS THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 1409                /       ERROR ROUTINE DETECTS THESE PROBLEMS AS THEY OCCUR.  OFTEN THE LATEST TAPE
 1410                /       OPERATION SUCCEEDS AFTER A SERIES OF RETRY ATTEMPTS.
 1411   
 1412                /       THIS SHOULD BE TAKEN AS A WARNING THE TAPE HARDWARE REQUIRES MAINTENANCE
 1413                /       AND/OR THE DECTAPE NEEDS TO BE EITHER REFORMATTED [PERHAPS AFTER CLEANING] OR
 1414                /       [IN INSTANCES OF SEVERE WEAR OR DAMAGE] DISCARDED.  DUE TO THE INHERENTLY SAFE
 1415                /       DEVICE HANDLER RECOVERY TECHNIQUES EMPLOYED, THE CURRENT OPERATION WILL LIKELY
 1416                /       [EVENTUALLY] SUCCEED AS DESCRIBED ABOVE.
 1417   
 1418                /       THE DESIGN PRINCIPLES OF THIS DEVICE HANDLER SHOULD BE CONTRASTED WITH OTHER
 1419                /       MORE EXPEDIENTLY DESIGNED SYSTEMS SUCH AS OS/8 WHERE ALL SUCH ERRORS ARE
 1420                /       TOTALLY IGNORED.  BY THE TIME A TAPE EXHIBITS ANY ERRORS USING OS/8, IT IS
 1421                /       OFTEN TOO LATE TO RECOVER DATA.  [NOTE: IT HAS BEEN SUGGESTED THAT THIS ASPECT
 1422                /       OF THE OS/8 DECTAPE HANDLERS IS THE RESULT OF AN EDICT FROM A MISGUIDED
 1423                /       PRODUCT MANAGER.  CLEARLY THE HANDLER OPERATES AS IF WRITTEN WITH NO ACTUAL
 1424                /       UNDERSTANDING OF THE PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTICS OF DECTAPE.  A HANDLER THAT
 1425                /       TAKES AN ERROR RETURN FOR THESE SERIOUS CONDITIONS CAN BE WRITTEN FOR OS/8;
 1426                /       HOWEVER, THIS WAS NEVER PART OF THE DEC STANDARD RELEASE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 29

 1428                /       BY BEING MORE DIAGNOSTIC IN NATURE, THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER GREATLY
 1429                /       BENEFITS THE USER: DATA LOSS WILL BE EITHER ELIMINATED OR AT LEAST SERIOUSLY
 1430                /       REDUCED.  HARDWARE ERRORS ARE DETECTED WHEN THEY FIRST OCCUR BEFORE DATA LOSS
 1431                /       IS LIKELY.  SUBSEQUENT MAINTENANCE WILL ELIMINATE THE CAUSE OF THE ERRORS LONG
 1432                /       BEFORE THE PROBLEM ESCALATES TO POTENTIALLY PERMANENT DATA LOSS.
 1433   
 1434                /       THIS DESIGN PHILOSOPHY HAS SUCCESSFULLY BEEN USED FROM 1966 [STARTING WITH THE
 1435                /       ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM] ONWARD WITH VIRTUALLY NO LOST DATA.  AS
 1436                /       NECESSARY, SPECIFIC DECTAPES ARE TAKEN OUT OF SERVICE AND REPLACED [AND
 1437                /       POSSIBLY RECYCLED]; COPYING THE CONTENTS OF MARGINAL TAPES TO FRESHLY
 1438                /       FORMATTED MEDIA ELIMINATES DATA LOSS.  ALL RELEASES OF THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE
 1439                /       HANDLER USE THIS PROVEN METHOD OF ERROR DETECTION.
 1440   
 1441                /       READ PARITY ERROR CONSIDERATIONS.
 1442   
 1443                /       PARITY ERRORS ONLY OCCUR WHEN ATTEMPTING TO READ THE CURRENT BLOCK[S].  RETRY
 1444                /       OPERATIONS GENERALLY CLEAR THE PROBLEM [BUT NOT ALWAYS].  REASONABLE EFFORT
 1445                /       SHOULD BE MADE TO ATTEMPT DATA RECOVERY IF POSSIBLE. [NOTE: THE ROOT CAUSE OF
 1446                /       PARITY ERRORS IS GENERALLY THE SAME SET OF ISSUES THAT TEND TO CAUSE TIMING
 1447                /       AND MARK TRACK ERRORS AS DISCUSSED ABOVE.]
 1448   
 1449   07627* 5257          JMP     SRETRY                  /"CONTINUE" WAS PRESSED; RETRY THE ENTIRE CALL.
 1450   
 1451                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY MODIFICATIONS.
 1452   
 1453                /       IF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT, THE PREVIOUS TWO INSTRUCTIONS ARE
 1454                /       REPLACED AS FOLLOWS:
 1455   
 1456                /       *SHALT                          /OVER THE HLT INSTRUCTION
 1457   
 1458                /       CIF     XX                      /WILL BE "CIF" TO THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL FIELD;
 1459                /       JMP     ERRTRAP                 /NOTE: "ERRTRAP" IS ON PAGE ZERO OF THE
 1460                                                        /CONSOLE OVERLAY THUS DIRECTLY ADDRESSABLE.
 1461   
 1462                /       THE ERROR TRAP HANDLES ALL ERROR PARTICULARS AS GUIDED BY A TABLE OF
 1463                /       INFORMATION SPECIFIC TO THIS SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WRITTEN OUT TO A SPECIFIC
 1464                /       BLOCK RESERVED FOR THE PURPOSE. [NOTE: THE EXACT BLOCK NUMBER IS DEPENDENT ON
 1465                /       THE PARTICULAR RELEASE OF P?S/8 AND IS SUBJECT TO CHANGE.]
 1466   
 1467                /       THE ERROR INTERCEPTION IS GENERALLY DISPLAYED ON THE CURRENT LOGICAL CONSOLE
 1468                /       DISPLAY DEVICE IN THE FORM OF AN ERROR MESSAGE WITH COMPLETE PARTICULARS OF
 1469                /       THE CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND EXPLICIT STRINGS OF CHARACTERS TO
 1470                /       DESCRIBE THE PARTICULAR ERROR.  THIS CAN INCLUDE SUCH INFORMATION AS THE
 1471                /       CALLING ADDRESS, WHETHER THE CALL WAS A READ OR WRITE OPERATION AND THE
 1472                /       LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT, ETC. [NOTE: THE EXACT NATURE OF THE ERROR HANDLING IS
 1473                /       CUSTOMIZABLE WITHIN THE CONSOLE OVERLAY CONFIGURATION.  SLOWER CONSOLE DEVICES
 1474                /       CAN BE CONFIGURED TO USE SHORTER ERROR MESSAGES.  DEVICES SUCH AS THE VT8E AND
 1475                /       THE PDP-12 VR12/VR14 DISPLAY THAT ARE ESSENTIALLY TERMINAL EMULATORS GENERALLY
 1476                /       CAN PROVIDE ENTIRE SCREENS DEVOTED TO THE PARTICULARS OF ERROR RECOVERY.  WHEN
 1477                /       THE ERROR CLEARS, THE NORMAL CONSOLE SCREEN WILL BE DISPLAYED.]
 1478   
 1479   07630* 0010  S10,    10                              /CONSTANT 0010
 1480   07631* 6203  S6203,  CIF CDF 00                      /CONSTANT 6203


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 30

 1482                /       NOTE: THE FOLLOWING SECTION WILL BE REMOVED IN THE NEXT RELEASE OF THE
 1483                /       TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
 1484   
 1485                /       ******************************************************************************
 1486                /       *                                                                            *
 1487                /       * NOTE: AS STATED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT, THIS IS NOT THE FINAL VERSION  *
 1488                /       * OF THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  THERE IS A KNOWN THEORETICAL BUG  *
 1489                /       * IN THIS CODE THAT CAN ONLY SURFACE IN A HIGHLY UNLIKELY SCENARIO:          *
 1490                /       *                                                                            *
 1491                /       * ASSUME THAT SOMEHOW AN END ZONE ERROR OCCURS DURING A SUBSEQUENT READ OR   *
 1492                /       * WRITE OPERATION INVOLVING A MULTIPLE BLOCK TRANSFER ALSO INVOLVING THE USE *
 1493                /       * OF EXTENDED MEMORY FOR THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER.  THIS WOULD MEAN AN END   *
 1494                /       * ZONE ERROR WOULD HAVE UNEXPECTEDLY OCCURRED WHICH WOULD THEN LEAD TO       *
 1495                /       * RECOVERY ATTEMPTS TO ALLOW THE LATEST BLOCK TO TRANSFER.  THIS WOULD       *
 1496                /       * INVOLVE A NEW SEARCH OPERATION TO LOCATE THE CURRENT BLOCK MOVING FORWARD. *
 1497                /       * HOWEVER, IN THIS ODD SCENARIO [WHICH IMPLIES THERE IS SOME FORM OF FAILURE *
 1498                /       * IN THE CONTROLLER, TAPE OR DRIVE], IT IS POSSIBLE FOR THE DEVICE HANDLER   *
 1499                /       * TO FAIL TO PROPERLY SEARCH FOR THE NEXT BLOCK ON ALL RETRY OPERATIONS.     *
 1500                /       *                                                                            *
 1501                /       * AN ALTERNATE SCENARIO INVOLVES AN IMPROPER CALL FOR MULTIPLE BLOCKS WHERE  *
 1502                /       * AT LEAST ONE [BUT NOT EVERY ONE] IS NONEXISTENT [SUCH AS AN ATTEMPT TO     *
 1503                /       * TRANSFER BLOCK 2702 AFTER BLOCK 2701 ON A STANDARD LENGTH DECTAPE.  [NOTE: *
 1504                /       * LONGER TAPES MERELY MOVE THE APPLICATION POINT OF THE PROBLEM.]            *
 1505                /       *                                                                            *
 1506                /       * THE ROOT CAUSE OF THE BUG IS THE IMPROPER PLACEMENT OF A "DTLB"            *
 1507                /       * INSTRUCTION TO CAUSE DATA BREAK TRANSFER OF THE BLOCK MARKS TO FIELD 0.    *
 1508                /       * DURING THE READ OR WRITE OPERATIONS THAT FOLLOW THE FIRST SUCCESSFUL       *
 1509                /       * SEARCH, THE NEXT DATA BREAK FIELD SETTING OPERATION SETS DATA TRANSFERS TO *
 1510                /       * THE CALLER'S BUFFER FIELD.  IF THE SEARCH IS RESTARTED AFTER AN END ZONE   *
 1511                /       * ERROR, THE FIELD WILL NOT BE RESET TO 0.                                   *
 1512                /       *                                                                            *
 1513                /       * A TRIVIAL FIX IS AVAILABLE:  MERELY MOVE THE "DTLB" INSTRUCTION [CURRENTLY *
 1514                /       * LOCATED AT 07666] TO "STURNIT" [WHICH WOULD CHANGE TO 07672 AS A RESULT].  *
 1515                /       * THE AC IS KNOWN TO BE 0000 AT THAT POINT; THIS WILL CORRECTLY RESET THE    *
 1516                /       * DATA BREAK FIELD FOR PROPER SEARCH OPERATIONS.  THE CODE CAN BE CORRECTED  *
 1517                /       * BY PATCHING TAPE BLOCK 0000 USING THE P?S/8 DUMP UTILITY.  NOTE: ALL       *
 1518                /       * AFFECTED LOCATIONS MUST BE PATCHED SIMULTANEOUSLY BEFORE THE NEXT BOOTUP.  *
 1519                /       *                                                                            *
 1520                /       * SUBSEQUENT RELEASES OF THIS DEVICE HANDLER WILL INCLUDE FIXING THIS HIGHLY *
 1521                /       * IMPROBABLE BUG; OTHER MORE IMPORTANT IMPROVEMENTS WILL BE INCLUDED THAT    *
 1522                /       * WERE ORIGINALLY CREATED SEVERAL YEARS AFTER THIS VERSION WAS RELEASED.     *
 1523                /       *                                                                            *
 1524                /       * THE SOURCE CODE HAS BEEN MODIFIED TO ALLOW ELIMINATION OF THE BUG OR       *
 1525                /       * MAINTAINING IT FOR HISTORICAL PURPOSES [AS PART OF THE CIRCA 1976 RELEASE  *
 1526                /       * OF P?S/8 AVAILABLE AS AN IMAGE FILE].  FUTURE RELEASES OF THIS FILE WILL   *
 1527                /       * ELIMINATE THIS SUPPORT AS PART OF AN EFFORT TO INCLUDE ADDITIONAL WORK     *
 1528                /       * PERFORMED AS MUCH AS A DECADE AFTER THIS RELEASE; ADDITIONAL NEWER CHANGES *
 1529                /       * MAY BE INCLUDED AS PART OF A NEWER OVERALL RELEASE OF P?S/8 IN THE FUTURE. *
 1530                /       *                                                                            *
 1531                /       * THE PROGRAM CAN BE ASSEMBLED FOR EITHER CASE BY APPROPRIATE DEFINITION OF  *
 1532                /       * THE SYMBOL "ASMBUG" IN THE DEFINITIONS SECTION ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT. *
 1533                /       *                                                                            *
 1534                /       ******************************************************************************


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 31

 1536                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAM LOADER.
 1537   
 1538                /       PERHAPS THE SINGLE MOST SIGNIFICANT IMPROVEMENT UPON THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR
 1539                /       SYSTEM, THE P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAM LOADER [THE "SYSTEM LOADER" AS IT WAS FIRST
 1540                /       IDENTIFIED WITHIN THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM] IS THE MECHANISM BY WHICH IMAGE
 1541                /       BINARY PROGRAMS ARE LOADED INTO USER MEMORY [00000-07577] WITHOUT RESTRICTION
 1542                /       AND THEN STARTED.  IN GENERAL, LOADING RULES ARE FULLY COMPATIBLE WITH THE
 1543                /       BINARY PAPER TAPE LOADERS.  THUS, OTHER THAN OPTIONALLY INTERFACING WITH
 1544                /       ADDITIONAL FEATURES OF P?S/8, GENERAL PROGRAMMING RULES FOR THE PDP-8 ARE
 1545                /       FULLY SUPPORTED WITHOUT RESTRICTION AS DOCUMENTED WITHIN BIN LOADER
 1546                /       SPECIFICATIONS.
 1547   
 1548                /       APPENDIX "K" DISCUSSES THE MANY ISSUES SURROUNDING THE SYSTEM LOADER, WHICH IS
 1549                /       USED IN SEVERAL CONTEXTS THROUGHOUT P?S/8.  ADDITIONALLY, THERE IS A DETAILED
 1550                /       DISCUSSION OF HOW THE ORIGINAL CONVOLUTED R-L MONITOR SYSTEM EQUIVALENT
 1551                /       FUNCTION WAS CARRIED OUT [WITH INVOLVES A SERIOUS AMOUNT OF OVERHEAD AND MANY
 1552                /       LIMITATIONS] THAT, IF THIS HAD NOT COMPLETELY REDESIGNED, WOULD HAVE GREATLY
 1553                /       CRIPPLED THE OVERALL P?S/8 SYSTEM.
 1554   
 1555                /       IT IS ONLY BECAUSE OF THE TREMENDOUS EFFORT ON THE PART OF MANY INDIVIDUALS
 1556                /       OPTIMIZING THE ENTIRE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CODE IN VARIOUS WAYS OVER THE
 1557                /       YEARS THAT THE SYSTEM LOADER AS NOW DEFINED HERE BECAME POSSIBLE.  [NOTE: VERY
 1558                /       EARLY RELEASES OF P?S/8 WERE FULLY COMPATIBLE WITH ALL OF THE R-L MONITOR
 1559                /       CALLS REPLETE WITH SYSTEM OVERHEAD; THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER DESCRIBED IN
 1560                /       THIS DOCUMENT IS THE FIRST GENERATION THAT REMOVED ALL OF THIS OVERHEAD ALL AT
 1561                /       ONCE; FUTURE RELEASES OF THE HANDLER ARE MERELY REFINEMENTS IN OTHER WAYS TO
 1562                /       ADD ADDITIONAL FUNCTIONALITY WITHOUT ADDING ANY STRUCTURAL CHANGES.]
 1563   
 1564                /       THIS ABSOLUTELY *MUST* BE AT LOCATION 07632 UNLESS MANY PROGRAMS ARE ALTERED.
 1565   
 1566                        IFNZRO  7632-.  <ERROR  .>      /ENSURE COMPATIBILITY WITH DESIGN STANDARDS.
 1568   
 1569                /       NOTE: THE PRELOADED CONTENTS OF THE SYSTEM LOADER ARE USED TO SAVE ALL OF
 1570                /       FIELD 0 BEFORE RESTARTING THE SYSTEM AT 07600; THIS IS USED BY VARIOUS
 1571                /       MEMORY-SAVING OPERATIONS THROUGHOUT P?S/8; OTHER SYSTEM COMPONENTS WILL LOAD
 1572                /       ALTERNATE PARAMETERS INTO THE MEMORY-RESIDENT COPY AS REQUIRED.
 1573   
