/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 58

 2909                /       BINARY LOADER IMPLEMENTATION CONSIDERATIONS:
 2910   
 2911                /       1) APPLYING CODE OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES TO THE BINARY [BIN] LOADER.
 2912   
 2913                /          THE DEC BINARY LOADER PROGRAM EVOLVED OVER SEVERAL GENERATIONS OF
 2914                /          DEVELOPMENT.  IT IS APPARENT THAT EXTENDED MEMORY LOADING AND OTHER
 2915                /          CONSIDERATIONS CAUSED INCREMENTAL CHANGES TO THE CODING THAT, IN HINDSIGHT,
 2916                /          ARE SHOWN TO NOT BE WELL ORGANIZED.  [IT IS LIKELY THERE WAS LITTLE IMPETUS
 2917                /          TO MODIFY THE CODE BEYOND DIRECT REPAIR OF SPECIFIC PROBLEMS AS THEY
 2918                /          SURFACED.]  THE P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATION ADDS THE ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENT OF
 2919                /          USING MORE EFFICIENT CODING TECHNIQUES.  THIS TENDS TO ACHIEVE A SMALLER
 2920                /          OVERALL CODE SPACE TO MAXIMIZE THE SIZE OF THE SYSTEM RESTART AT 07600
 2921                /          REQUIRED FOR CERTAIN DEVICES; THE TWO ROUTINES MUST COEXIST WITHIN
 2922                /          07600-07777.  [NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM RESTART CODE IS DESCRIBED
 2923                /          ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT; IT IS SOMEWHAT SMALLER THAN THE CURRENT
 2924                /          DEFINITION OF MAXIMUM SIZE ALLOWED.]
 2925   
 2926                /          TO MINIMIZE THE CODE SPACE REQUIRMENTS OF THE BINARY LOADER, CERTAIN SLOPPY
 2927                /          "SPAGHETTI CODE" SEQUENCES HAVE BEEN REPLACED BY MORE COHERENT SECTIONS
 2928                /          THAT ARE ACTUALLY MORE STRAIGHTFORWARD THAN THE ORIGINAL CODING IN THE DEC
 2929                /          RELEASES.  [NOTE: THE P?S/8 REPLACEMENT CODE DEPENDS ON QUIRKS OF THE PDP-8
 2930                /          INSTRUCTION SET CLEARLY NOT FULLY UNDERSTOOD BY THE ORIGINAL PROGRAMMERS OF
 2931                /          THE BINARY LOADER.  THOSE INTERESTED IN SEEING HOW THE ORIGINAL CODING WAS
 2932                /          REWORKED ARE REFERRED TO THE DOCUMENTATION OF THE ORIGINAL RELEASE OF THE
 2933                /          BINARY LOADER DEC-08-LBAA-D, MAY 10, 1967.]  [NOTE: THERE MAY HAVE BEEN AN
 2934                /          EARLIER RELEASE OF THE BINARY LOADER KNOWN TO BE INCOMPATIBLE WITH THE
 2935                /          TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER; THERE ARE HAND-WRITTEN CHANGES TO THE
 2936                /          DOCUMENTATION INDICATING CORRECTIONS MADE TO BE COMPATIBLE WITH THE TC01
 2937                /          DECTAPE CONTROLLER.]
 2938   
 2939                /          THE ORIGINAL VERSION OF THE BIN LOADER USES SEVERAL VALID SELF-REFERENTIAL
 2940                /          PROGRAMMING TECHNIQUES OF CONTRIVING INSTRUCTIONS TO FORM USEFUL CONSTANTS
 2941                /          THAT TEND TO MAKE THE OVERALL PROGRAM SIZE SMALLER.
 2942   
 2943                /          A CASE IN POINT IS THE NEED TO COMPARE THE LATEST PAPER-TAPE FRAME TO THE
 2944                /          VALUE 377.  AN INITIAL DESIGN PASS MIGHT START BY COMPARING THE LATEST
 2945                /          CHARACTER TO 377 BY INVERTING THE LATEST CHARACTER AND ADDING THE CONSTANT
 2946                /          0377, THEN CHECK FOR A ZERO ACCUMULATOR.  AN OBVIOUS OPTIMIZATION IS TO
 2947                /          INSTEAD COMPARE THE CHARACTER TO THE SIGNED CONSTANT -377 [7401] WHICH
 2948                /          ELIMINATES THE INHERENT INVERTING INSTRUCTION ["CIA" 7041].  HOWEVER, THE
 2949                /          BIN LOADER INCLUDES THE "HLT" [7402] INSTRUCTION WHICH IS ALSO THE SIGNED
 2950                /          CONSTANT -376.  BY CHANGING THE COMPARISON TO SKIP ON STRICTLY POSITIVE,
 2951                /          ALL FRAME CODES LESS THAN 377 ARE SEPARATED FROM THE DETECTION OF THE 377
 2952                /          FRAME CODE USING EQUIVALENT LOGIC; A MEMORY LOCATION IS SAVED BY
 2953                /          REFERENCING THE "HLT" [7402] INSTRUCTION AS A CONSTANT.
 2954   
 2955                /          IN ANOTHER PORTION OF THE PROGRAM, AN INSTRUCTION IS NEEDED TO CLEAR THE
 2956                /          ACCUMULATOR; THE "CLA" [7200] GROUP I OPR INSTRUCTION IS GENERALLY USED TO
 2957                /          ACCOMPLISH THIS FUNCTION.  INSTEAD, THE GROUP II INSTRUCTION 7600 IS USED
 2958                /          TO CLEAR THE ACCUMULATOR; THE CONSTANT 7600 IS USED ELSEWHERE IN THE
 2959                /          PROGRAM THUS SAVING AN ADDITIONAL CODE SPACE WORD.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 59

 2961                /          THE P?S/8 MODIFICATIONS TAKE THIS CONCEPT A FEW STEPS FURTHER.  THE BINARY
 2962                /          LOADER CODING REQUIRES A CONSTANT 0300 AND A CONSTANT 0070.  BY REQUIRING
 2963                /          THE "FMASK" CONSTANT [0070] TO BE LOCATED AT LOCATION 7700 WITHIN THE BIN
 2964                /          LOADER PROGRAM, THE LOCATION THAT REFERENCES THE CONSTANT, THE INSTRUCTION
 2965                /          "AND FMASK", CAN BE USED AS THE CONSTANT 0300 THUS SAVING AN ADDITIONAL
 2966                /          CODE SPACE WORD.
 2967   
 2968                /          THE CODING OF THE RUBOUT PROTECTION SUPPORT WAS SHORTENED BY USE OF AN
 2969                /          ALTERNATE METHOD WHICH PERFORMS ARITHMETIC OPERATIONS ON AN INSTRUCTION
 2970                /          WITHIN THE CODING ITSELF.
 2971   
 2972                /          ALTHOUGH UNUSUAL, THESE TECHNIQUES ARE VALID WAYS TO MINIMIZE THE OVERALL
 2973                /          CODE SPACE OF THE PROGRAM.  IN THEORY, ADDITIONAL TECHNIQUES COULD BE USED
 2974                /          TO IMPLEMENT FURTHER CODE SPACE OPTIMIZATION IN THE FUTURE.
 2975   
 2976                /       2) EXTENDED MEMORY PROGRAMMING CONSIDERATIONS.
 2977   
 2978                /          DUE TO THE WAY THE BIN LOADER DETECTS FIELD SETTINGS [IGNORING THEM FOR
 2979                /          CHECKSUM PURPOSES], A "CDF <NEW DATA FIELD" INSTRUCTION IS CREATED AND
 2980                /          BUFFERED FOR USE WITH LATER DATA TO BE LOADED INTO THE NEW FIELD.  A
 2981                /          DEFECTIVE VERSION OF DEC'S BIN LOADER WAS RELEASED KNOWN AS THE
 2982                /          "SELF-STARTING BINARY LOADER".  THIS RELEASE HAS A FLAWED DESIGN AS IT
 2983                /          LACKS THE REQUIRED BUFFERING; IT WAS APPARENTLY WRITTEN BY SOMEONE IGNORANT
 2984                /          OF THE NUANCES OF BINARY PAPER-TAPE FORMAT AS IT APPLIES TO LOADING DATA
 2985                /          INTO EXTENDED MEMORY.
 2986   
 2987                /          THE ONLY REASON THIS SERIOUS PROBLEM WAS NOT DETECTED UNTIL POST-RELEASE IS
 2988                /          MOST FIELD SETTINGS IN PRACTICAL PDP-8 CODE ARE FOLLOWED BY AN ORIGIN
 2989                /          SETTING; THE PREMATURE FIELD CHANGE IS UNABLE TO AFFECT AN ORIGIN SETTING
 2990                /          IMMEDIATELY FOLLOWING THE MISHANDLED FIELD SETTING; LOADING AFTER THE
 2991                /          ORIGIN SETTING WILL BE CORRECTLY STORED INTO THE NEW FIELD.  HOWEVER, THERE
 2992                /          IS NO RULE REGARDING MANDATORY ORIGIN SETTINGS IMMEDIATELY AFTER A FIELD
 2993                /          SETTING.  CERTAIN PAL ASSEMBLERS DO NOT AUTOMATICALLY CREATE A "PROTECTIVE"
 2994                /          ORIGIN SETTING AFTER A FIELD SETTING.  AS IS THE CASE IN ALL OTHER VERSIONS
 2995                /          OF THE BINARY LOADER, THE P?S/8 RELEASE IMPLEMENTS EXTENDED MEMORY LOADING
 2996                /          AS ORIGINALLY DEFINED.
 2997   
 2998                /       3) THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK ADDRESS CONSIDERATIONS.
 2999   
 3000                /          STANDARD PDP-8 PERIPHERALS INCLUDE THREE DEVICES THAT RESERVE HARD-WIRED
 3001                /          ADDRESS PAIRS USED TO CONTROL DATA TRANSFERS BY THE METHOD OF THREE CYCLE
 3002                /          DATA BREAK [DMA].  ANY ACTIVITY ON THESE DEVICES WILL WRITE ON THE
 3003                /          CORRESPONDING MEMORY LOCATIONS.  THE FIRST IS USED AS A WORD COUNT REGISTER
 3004                /          TO REGULATE THE SIZE OF THE TRANSFER; THE SECOND IS USED AS THE CURRENT
 3005                /          ADDRESS REGISTER TO LOCATE THE DATA TRANSFER IN MEMORY.  THE FOLLOWING
 3006                /          ADDRESS RESERVATIONS ARE DEFINED:
 3007   
 3008                /          A) 07750-07751 IS RESERVED FOR THE DF32 OR RF08.  ONLY ONE OF THESE IS
 3009                /             ALLOWED ON ANY SYSTEM.
 3010   
 3011                /          B) 07752-07753 IS RESERVED FOR THE TC58 SERIES OF .5" MAGNETIC TAPE
 3012                /             CONTROLLERS.
 3013   
 3014                /          C) 07754-07755 IS RESERVED FOR THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER.  THE USE
 3015                /             OF THESE LOCATIONS IS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 60

 3017                /          WHILE THE RIM LOADER IS NOT AFFECTED BY RUNNING PROGRAMS FOR THESE DEVICES,
 3018                /          THERE ARE CONSIDERATIONS AFFECTING PROGRAMS SUCH AS THE BIN LOADER THAT
 3019                /          CAN BE DESTROYED IN MEMORY ONCE A PROGRAM ADDRESSING THESE LOCATIONS IS
 3020                /          STARTED; THE BIN LOADER WILL BECOME NONFUNCTIONAL UNTIL MEMORY LOCATIONS
 3021                /          AFFECTED BOTH DIRECTLY AND INDIRECTLY ARE RESTORED.  THE GENERAL
 3022                /          RECOMMENDATION IS TO RELOAD THE BIN LOADER USING THE RIM LOADER IF
 3023                /          DIAGNOSTIC OR OTHER PROGRAMS FOR THESE DEVICES ARE EXECUTED.  [NOTE: ANY
 3024                /          DOCUMENTED REMEDY FOR THIS PROBLEM OUTSIDE OF P?S/8 DOES NOT CONSIDER THE
 3025                /          PRESENCE OF A STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM.]
 3026   
 3027                /          ASSUMING THE P?S/8 STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AT 07600 IS NOT CORRUPTED, THE
 3028                /          OPERATING SYSTEM CAN BE RELOADED; THE BIN AND RIM LOADERS CAN THEN BE
 3029                /          LOADED INTO MEMORY USING THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY [PRESUMABLY BY REPEATING THE
 3030                /          LAST COMMAND ENTERED INTO THE KEYBOARD MONITOR].  MORE ELABORATE BATCH
 3031                /          FILES CAN BE CREATED TO HANDLE KNOWN TROUBLESOME CIRCUMSTANCES.
 3032   
 3033                /          THE ORIGINAL RELEASE OF THE BIN LOADER WAS PRONE TO CORRUPTION BY THE USE
 3034                /          OF ANY OF THESE THREE DEVICES; THE MAY 10, 1967 RELEASE [DEC-08-LBAA]
 3035                /          ELIMINATES INTERACTION WITH THE TC01/TC08 THREE CYCLE DATA BREAK LOCATIONS
 3036                /          [07754-07755] BUT CAN STILL BE DESTROYED BY INTERACTION WITH PROGRAMS FOR
 3037                /          RF08/DF32 OR THE TC58 MAGNETIC TAPE CONTROLLER.
 3038   
 3039                /          AS CURRENTLY DEFINED, THE P?S/8 VERSION OF THE BINARY LOADER CAN ONLY BE
 3040                /          CORRUPTED BY THE USE OF PROGRAMS FOR THE TC58 MAGNETIC TAPE CONTROLLER.
 3041                /          FUTURE RELEASES OF THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AT 07600 OR THE BINARY LOADER
 3042                /          MAY ELIMINATE THIS PROBLEM; ONLY ONE ADDITIONAL MEMORY LOCATION IS REQUIRED
 3043                /          TO REMEDY THE SITUATION.
 3044   
 3045                /       BY ALLOWING THE AVAILABLE CODE SPACE FOR THE BIN LOADER TO INCREASE BY ONE
 3046                /       WORD, IT IS POSSIBLE TO ELIMINATE ALL CORRUPTION ASSOCIATED WITH THREE-CYCLE
 3047                /       DATA BREAK DEVICES WITHOUT OTHERWISE CHANGING THE DESIGN AS PRESENTED IN THIS
 3048                /       DOCUMENT.  OVERALL BINARY LOADER ROBUSTNESS CAN BE IMPROVED STILL FURTHER IF
 3049                /       SEVERAL MORE LOCATIONS ARE MADE AVAILABLE.
 3050   
 3051                /       OVER THE LONG COURSE OF DECADES OF P?S/8 SYSTEM DESIGN, CERTAIN STAND-ALONE
 3052                /       BOOSTRAP ROUTINES HAVE BEEN WRITTEN ATTEMPTING TO COMPLETELY EXIST IN FIELD 0.
 3053                /       IN THE CASE OF CERTAIN DEVICES, EXTENDED MEMORY IS ALWAYS REQUIRED TO RUN
 3054                /       P?S/8.  ASSUMING NO PORTION OF RESERVED MEMORY IS CORRUPTED BY IMPROPER
 3055                /       OVERLAY BY USER PROGRAMS, THERE SHOULD NOT BE ANY MEANINGFUL DIFFERENCE
 3056                /       BETWEEN THE FIELD 0 STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AND AN EXTENDED MEMORY-BASED
 3057                /       STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP.  [NOTE: ARGUABLY, THE STAND-ALONE BOOSTRAP LOCATED IN
 3058                /       EXTENDED MEMORY IS LESS LIKELY TO BE CORRUPTED THAN THE LONGER FIELD 0-BASED
 3059                /       BOOTSTRAP FOR CERTAIN OF THESE DEVICES; CORRUPTION CAN ONLY BE CAUSED BY
 3060                /       EITHER IMPROPER USE OF THE FRONT PANEL OR ATTEMPTING TO LOAD KNOWN "ROGUE"
 3061                /       DIAGNOSTIC PROGRAMS THAT VIOLATE STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM LOADING
 3062                /       CONVENTIONS (YET BARELY FIT IN THE SLIGHTLY MORE ACCOMMODATING ENVIRONMENT OF
 3063                /       A PARTICULAR VERSION OF THE BINARY LOADER).  ISSUES REGARDING ROGUE PROGRAMS
 3064                /       ARE DISCUSSED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.]
 3065   
 3066                /       AS SUCH, THE SUBJECT OF LOWERING THE MAXIMUM STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP SIZE IS
 3067                /       CURRENTLY BEING RESEARCHED.  IT IS CONCEIVABLE THAT THE CURRENT DEFINITION CAN
 3068                /       BE LOWERED IF IT TURNS OUT THE CURRENT MAXIMUM SIZE WAS DEFINED FOR A DEVICE
 3069                /       THAT REQUIRES EXTENDED MEMORY FOR THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 61

 3071                /       THE OVERALL SIZE OF THE EXTENDED MEMORY EXTENSION FOR A SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 3072                /       WAS INCREASED AFTER ALL EXISTING HANDLERS WERE WRITTEN.  THIS WAS DONE TO
 3073                /       FORMALIZE THE DEFINITIONS OF THE MAXIMUM SIZE OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY
 3074                /       AND THE OPTIONAL SHELL OVERLAY FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA; THIS WOULD ALSO
 3075                /       FACILITATE WRITING SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS IN THE FUTURE FOR MORE PROBLEMATIC
 3076                /       DEVICES SUCH AS 8-BIT MODE DISKETTE INTERFACES.  AS SUCH, A SHORT STUB OF
 3077                /       CODE IN FIELD 0 [WITH PERHAPS SEVERAL SHORT AUXILIARY ROUTINES] CAN REPLACE
 3078                /       THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP FOR THE LARGEST DEVICE DEFINED PRIOR TO THE INCREASE
 3079                /       IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY EXTENSION.  [NOTE: FOR ALL SUCH SYSTEM DEVICES, THE
 3080                /       FIELD 0 CODE WOULD NOT ONLY BE QUITE SHORT, IT WOULD LIKELY BE GENERIC TO ALL
 3081                /       SUCH DEVICES; THE BULK OF THE CODE WOULD BE RESIDENT IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY
 3082                /       SECTION ENTERED INDIRECTLY FROM THE FIELD 0 STUB DEFINED AT 07600 WHICH WOULD
 3083                /       BE ONLY TWO INSTRUCTIONS LONG: A "CIF" INSTRUCTION TO THE HIGHEST FIELD OF
 3084                /       EXTENDED MEMORY FOLLOWED BY A "JMP" INSTRUCTION TO THE REST OF THE RESTART
 3085                /       CODE RESIDENT IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY SECTION.  THE MAIN ISSUE IS SUCH SUPPORT
 3086                /       CODE WOULD BE FAR SMALLER THAN THE TC01/TC08 STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP CODE OR ANY
 3087                /       FUTURE VARIATION.]
 3088   
 3089                /       ONCE AN EXHAUSTIVE STUDY IS CONCLUDED, THERE MAY BE A SHORTER DEFINITION OF
 3090                /       STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP MAXIMUM SIZE; THIS WOULD ALLOW THE BIN LOADER TO BE
 3091                /       DEFINED AS A LONGER PROGRAM TO ADD ADDITIONAL FEATURES.
 3092   
 3093                /       RIM LOADER IMPLEMENTATION CONSIDERATIONS.
 3094   
 3095                /       THE P?S/8 RIM LOADER IS NOT 100% IDENTICAL TO THE DEC VERSION DEFINED IN
 3096                /       DEC-08-LRAA-D, FEBRUARY 23, 1965.  MODIFICATIONS HAVE BEEN MADE TO SIMPLIFY
 3097                /       SUPPORT FOR THE HIGH-SPEED READER.
 3098   
 3099                /       1) THE OFFICIAL LOW-SPEED RIM LOADER USES THE "KRB" [6036] INSTRUCTION IN
 3100                /          "BEG3" [07761] TO READ IN POTENTIAL LEADER FRAMES.  AS SUCH, WHEN IGNORING
 3101                /          THE INPUT, THE PROGRAM LOOPS BACK TO "BEG1" [07757] SINCE THE "KRB" [6036]
 3102                /          INSTRUCTION HAS ALREADY STARTED THE READER FOR THE NEXT CHARACTER; FOR EACH
 3103                /          DATA FRAME, THE PRIOR CONTENTS OF THE ACCUMULATOR CAN BE IGNORED BECAUSE
 3104                /          THE "KRB" [6036] INSTRUCTION LOADS THE ACCUMULATOR DIRECTLY.  THE "KCC"
 3105                /          [6032] INSTRUCTION AT "BEG" [07756] IS ONLY USED ONCE TO CLEAR THE INPUT
 3106                /          FLAG WHICH INITIATES A READ OPERATION FOR THE FIRST DATA FRAME.  THE "RFC"
 3107                /          [6014] INSTRUCTION REPLACES THE CONTENTS OF "BEG" [07756] TO INITIATE A
 3108                /          READ ON THE HIGH-SPEED READER.
 3109   
 3110                /       2) THE OFFICIAL HIGH-SPEED RIM LOADER USES THE "RFC RRB" [6016] INSTRUCTION
 3111                /          IN "BEG3" [07761] TO READ IN POTENTIAL LEADER FRAMES; THE ACCUMULATOR MUST
 3112                /          ALREADY BE CLEAR BECAUSE THE "RFC RRB" [6016] INSTRUCTION TRANSFERS THE
 3113                /          LATEST FRAME DATA BY INCLUSIVE-OR INSTEAD OF LOADING THE ACCUMULATOR AS
 3114                /          IN THE LOW-SPEED VERSION.  WHILE IGNORING THE LEADER FRAMES, THE PROGRAM
 3115                /          INSTRUCTION LOADED AT "BEG3+4" [07765] JUMPS TO "CLRAC" [07774] TO CLEAR
 3116                /          THE ACCUMULATOR, AND THEN JUMPS TO "BEG1" [07757] TO EFFECT THE SAME
 3117                /          OVERALL PROGRAM FLOW AS IN THE LOW-SPEED VERSION.
 3118   
 3119                /       3) THE P?S/8 VERSION OF THE LOW-SPEED RIM LOADER USES THE SAME INSTRUCTION AS
 3120                /          THE HIGH-SPEED VERSION IN "BEG3+4" [07765] THUS USES "CLRAC" [07774] TO
 3121                /          ACCOMPLISH THE SAME RESULT; HOWEVER, THE INSTRUCTION AT "CLRAC+1" [07775]
 3122                /          REMAINS AS "JMP BEG" [5356] INSTEAD OF THE CORRESPONDING VALUE IN THE
 3123                /          OFFICIAL VERSION INSTRUCTION "JMP BEG1" [5357] IN THIS POSITION.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 62

 3125                /          DUE TO THE WAY THE LOW-SPEED TELETYPE READER MECHANISM FUNCTIONS, IT IS
 3126                /          COMPLETELY SAFE TO START THE READER MULTIPLE TIMES WITHIN THE FIRST FEW
 3127                /          MICROSECONDS ONCE INITIATED.  SINCE THE RIM LOADER CAN BE STARTED MANUALLY,
 3128                /          THE READER WILL BE STARTED BOTH BY THE CPU POWER CLEAR AND THE KCC
 3129                /          INSTRUCTION THAT STARTS THE PROGRAM DURING ROUTINE USAGE.  UNLESS THE
 3130                /          PROGRAM IS EITHER MANUALLY STEPPED OR PERHAPS RUN ON THE PDP-12 USING THE
 3131                /          VARIABLE CPU CLOCK OPTIONS, THIS SHOULD NOT BE OF ANY CONSEQUENCE.
 3132   
 3133                /       4) ASSUMING THE INSTRUCTION AT "CLRAC+1" [07775] IS "JMP BEG" [5356] AND NOT
 3134                /          "JMP BEG1" [5357], THE HIGH-SPEED RIM LOADER WILL REPEATEDLY START THE
 3135                /          READER TWICE FOR EACH FRAME AFTER THE INITIAL FRAME.  IT WOULD APPEAR THE
 3136                /          SAME CONSIDERATION WOULD APPLY AS IN THE LOW-SPEED RIM LOADER AS DESCRIBED
 3137                /          IN 3) ABOVE: STARTING THE READER MORE THAN ONCE WITHIN A FEW MICROSECONDS
 3138                /          DOES NOT AFFECT THE READER OPERATION.  [NOTE: AN EARLIER RELEASE OF THE
 3139                /          P?S/8 HIGH-SPEED RIM LOADER IMPLEMENTED THIS SPECIFIC CODING.]
 3140   
 3141                /          HOWEVER, THIS IS NOT THE ACTUAL CODE IN THIS RELEASE.  THE GOAL WAS TO
 3142                /          CREATE A COMPOSITE VERSION OF THE RIM LOADER MINIMIZING THE DIFFERENCES
 3143                /          WHEN SWITCHING FROM THE LOW-SPEED RIM LOADER TO THE HIGH-SPEED RIM LOADER
 3144                /          IN-PLACE JUST BEFORE RELOCATING THE CODE TO 07756-07776.  THE INSTRUCTION
 3145                /          PATCHED INTO "BEG13" [07771] IS ACTUALLY "RRB" [6012] AND NOT "RFC RRB"
 3146                /          [6016] AS IN THE OFFICIAL VERSION; THE NEXT CHARACTER IS NOT INITIATED AT
 3147                /          THIS PART OF THE PROGRAM AS IN THE OFFICIAL VERSION.
 3148   
 3149                /          AS SUCH, THIS REQUIRES THE "JMP BEG" [5356] INSTRUCTION BE LOCATED AT
 3150                /          "CLRAC+1" [07775] TO START THE READER FOR THE NEXT CHARACTER.  HAD THIS
 3151                /          MINOR CHANGE BEEN MADE TO THE OFFICIAL VERSION, THE LOW-SPEED AND
 3152                /          HIGH-SPEED VERSIONS WOULD DIFFER BY ONE LESS WORD OVERALL.  [NOTE: IT IS
 3153                /          NOT LIKELY SUCH CONSIDERATIONS WERE PART OF THE ORIGINAL DESIGN; IT WAS
 3154                /          ACCEPTABLE THAT BOTH VERSIONS MERELY HAVE THE SAME OVERALL LENGTH.  THE
 3155                /          DESIGN OF THE P?S/8 BIN IMPLEMENTATION WHICH DYNAMICALLY TRANSFORMS A
 3156                /          LOW-SPEED VERSION INTO A HIGH-SPEED VERSION WAS INFLUENCED BY TECHNIQUES TO
 3157                /          MINIMIZE ONCE-ONLY INITIALIZATION CODE.]
 3158   
 3159                /       IT IS OUT OF BOUNDS FOR A PROGRAM TO EXPECT THE RIM LOADER'S PARTICULAR
 3160                /       INSTRUCTIONS TO BE PRECISELY AS EXPECTED; THIS IS DOCUMENTED IN THE ORIGINAL
 3161                /       RELEASE [DEC-08-LRAA-D, FEBRUARY 23, 1965] WHICH SUGGESTS THE EXISTENCE OF
 3162                /       ALTERNATE RIM LOADERS FOR ADDITIONAL DEVICES [SUCH AS PT08-BASED ASR-33
 3163                /       TELETYPES].  LOADED PROGRAMS MUST NOT REFERENCE THE ACTUAL CODE ACCOMPLISHING
 3164                /       THE LOADING.  [NOTE: THERE ARE KNOWN PROGRAMS THAT VIOLATE THIS PRINCIPLE WITH
 3165                /       REGARD TO THE BIN LOADER WITH POTENTIAL TO CORRUPT THE CONTENTS OF MEMORY.]
 3166   
 3167                /       THE UNDERLYING DESIGN ISSUE LEADING TO THIS DISCUSSION IS INHERENT IN THE
 3168                /       NATURE OF THE RIM LOADER.  UNLIKE THE BIN LOADER, RIM TAKES ADVANTAGE OF THE
 3169                /       ABILITY TO TRANSFER THE LATEST DATA FRAME BY AN INCLUSIVE-OR TRANSFER TO JOIN
 3170                /       THE LATEST FRAME DATA WITH THE PREVIOUS FRAME DATA TO FORM A 12-BIT WORD.
 3171                /       THIS REQUIRES THE "KRS" [6034] INSTRUCTION IN THE LOW-SPEED VERSION WHICH DOES
 3172                /       NOT CLEAR THE FLAG [OR START THE READER FOR THE NEXT FRAME].  AS SUCH, THE
 3173                /       LOGICAL FLOW FOR DATA TRANSFER IN THE RIM LOADER DIFFERS FROM MOST OTHER
 3174                /       CONSOLE DEVICE INPUT PROGRAMMING [INCLUDING THE BIN LOADER].
 3175   
 3176                /       FOR FULL AUTHENTICITY AND TO ELIMINATE ANY POTENTIAL PROBLEMS WITH SYSTEMS
 3177                /       THAT RUN EITHER FASTER OR SLOWER THAN EXPECTED, FUTURE RELEASES OF THE P?S/8
 3178                /       "BIN" UTILITY WILL CHANGE THE PARTICULARS SUCH THAT THE AUTHENTIC VERSIONS OF
 3179                /       BOTH THE LOW-SPEED AND HIGH-SPEED RIM LOADERS WILL BE SUPPORTED.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 63

 3181                /       SECTION 4: THE P?S/8 SLURP LOADER FOR TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS.
 3182   
 3183                /       THIS IS AN IMAGE OF THE TC01/TC08 HARDWARE-SPECIFIC SLURP LOADER AS LOADED
 3184                /       INTO 07600-07777 BY THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY.  THE CODE IS MOVED THROUGH MEMORY
 3185                /       [FROM AN INTERNAL IMAGE WITHIN P?S/8 BIN] WHEN P?S/8 BIN IS INVOKED FOR THE
 3186                /       PURPOSE OF LOADING BINARY FILES ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE OR RELATED LOGICAL DEVICE
 3187                /       UNITS.  [NOTE: BY DEFAULT, P?S/8 BIN LOADS BINARY FILES AS DESCRIBED HERE;
 3188                /       HOWEVER, THERE ARE ADDITIONAL UTILITY FUNCTIONS AVAILABLE FOR BINARY
 3189                /       PAPER-TAPE MANAGEMENT.  USING APPROPRIATE OPTION SWITCHES, P?S/8 BIN CAN READ
 3190                /       OR PUNCH BINARY PAPER-TAPES; THE BIN AND RIM LOADERS CAN BE PLACED INTO MEMORY
 3191                /       ALONG WITH THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.]
 3192   
 3193                /       THE CONTENTS OF BINARY FILES ARE LOADED INTO USER MEMORY WITHOUT USING A
 3194                /       GENERAL-PURPOSE DEVICE HANDLER; AN INTERNAL ONE-WORD BUFFER IS USED TO LOAD
 3195                /       THE FILE CONTENTS IN REAL-TIME BY DMA OPERATION UPDATED AFTER EACH INDIVIDUAL
 3196                /       THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK TRANSFER.  DETECTION INVOLVES MONITORING THE WORD COUNT
 3197                /       [OR CURRENT ADDRESS] REGISTER FOR THE INCREMENT THAT OCCURS EVERY 133
 3198                /       MICROSECONDS [NOMINALLY].  THE LATEST WORD TRANSFERRED IS THEN USED AS THE
 3199                /       NEXT DATA WORD TO BE PROCESSED IN SLURP FORMAT [WHICH IS DESCRIBED IN DETAIL
 3200                /       ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT] FROM THE LATEST BINARY INPUT FILE.
 3201   
 3202                /       SLURP BINARY FORMAT IS DESIGNED FOR EFFICIENT HIGH-SPEED LOADING; ONCE
 3203                /       INITIATED, THE SLURP LOADER PROGRAM EASILY MAINTAINS DATA SYNCHRONIZATION WITH
 3204                /       THE DMA TRANSFER; THE FILES ARE TRANSFERRED WITHOUT ANY ADDITIONAL BUFFERING
 3205                /       AND THE DECTAPE DRIVES MAY BE WRITE-LOCKED DURING THE ENTIRE LOADING PROCESS.
 3206   
 3207                /       ALL FILES PASSED TO THE SLURP LOADER MUST BE IN SLURP BINARY FORMAT.  SLURP
 3208                /       BINARY FILES ARE GENERALLY CREATED DURING SOURCE FILE ASSEMBLY USING THE P?S/8
 3209                /       PAL ASSEMBLY PROGRAM WITH APPROPRIATE OUTPUT OPTIONS SET.  SLURP FILES CAN
 3210                /       ALSO BE CREATED BY P?S/8 BIN WHEN CONVERTING BINARY PAPER-TAPES TO P?S/8
 3211                /       BINARY OUTPUT FILES.  [FOR FURTHER DETAILS, REFER TO DOCUMENTATION OF P?S/8
 3212                /       PAL OR P?S/8 BIN AS NECESSARY.]
 3213   
 3214                /       THE P?S/8 SLURP LOADER INCLUDES THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AT 07600, WHICH
 3215                /       MAINTAINS SEEMLESS COMPATIBILITY WITH THE CORRESPONDING P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE
 3216                /       HANDLER.  THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS DISPLACED BY THE STAND-ALONE BOOSTRAP
 3217                /       AND THE SLURP LOADER AS DESCRIBED HERE; HOWEVER, THE REST OF THE PAGE PAST THE
 3218                /       STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP MAY CONTAIN THE BIN AND RIM LOADERS IF APPROPRIATE
 3219                /       COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS ARE USED.  P?S/8 CAN BE RESTARTED AT 07600 AS LONG AS
 3220                /       THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP IS NOT CORRUPTED.
 3221   
 3222                /       NOTE: THE BOOTSTRAP FUNCTION AS AN INDEPENDENT ENTITY IS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE
 3223                /       IN THIS DOCUMENT; THIS ALLOWS FOCUS ON THE INTERACTION BETWEEN THE STAND-ALONE
 3224                /       BOOTSTRAP AT 07600 AND THE PAPER-TAPE RIM AND BIN LOADERS WHEN ALL ARE LOADED
 3225                /       INTO THE LAST PAGE OF FIELD 0.  ALL INTERNAL ASPECTS OF THE STAND-ALONE
 3226                /       BOOTSTRAP ARE DISCUSSED HERE IN GREATER DETAIL; THIS INCLUDES INTEGRAL
 3227                /       SECTIONS THAT ARE SHARED WITH THE SLURP LOADER.
 3228   
 3229                /       WHEN THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY IS INVOKED FOR SLURP LOADING, A LIST OF FILE
 3230                /       HANDLES IS PASSED IN LOCATIONS 07757-07777.  A SENTINEL VALUE OF 0000 IS
 3231                /       PLACED AFTER THE LAST PASSED FILE HANDLE.  IF THE LIST ENDS AT 07777, THERE IS
 3232                /       NO NEED FOR A SENTINEL ELEMENT; THE PROGRAM EXPLOITS A QUIRK OF THE PDP-8
 3233                /       INSTRUCTION SET TO SELF-TERMINATE LISTS ENDING AT LOCATION 7777.  [NOTE: THIS
 3234                /       METHOD OF PROCESSING FILE HANDLES APPLIES TO ALL BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS;
 3235                /       PROGRAMMING PARTICULARS ARE DISCUSSED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 64

 3237                /       FOR USE WITH THE SLURP LOADER, P?S/8 FILE HANDLE LISTS MAY START WITH
 3238                /       SUPERFLUOUS PASSED OUTPUT FILES FOLLOWED BY INPUT FILES.  WHEN THE SLURP
 3239                /       LOADER AND ALL PASSED PARAMETERS ARE PLACED INTO 07600-07777, THE LIST POINTER
 3240                /       "SLFLPTR" [07753] WILL BE ADJUSTED TO POINT TO THE FIRST INPUT FILE AS
 3241                /       NECESSARY; ALL OUTPUT FILES ARE IGNORED.  [NOTE: FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN
 3242                /       MAY ISSUE ERROR MESSAGES IF OUTPUT FILES ARE PASSED WITH BINARY INPUT FILES.]
 3243   
 3244                /       [NOTE: DUE TO THE WAY P?S/8 BIN FUNCTIONS, CERTAIN FILE HANDLE ELEMENTS MAY BE
 3245                /       SUPPLIED BY P?S/8 BIN BASED ON COMMAND-LINE SWITCHES INSTEAD OF FILENAMES AS
 3246                /       USUALLY PROCESSED BY THE KEYBOARD MONITOR EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS.  FOR
 3247                /       EXAMPLE, THE KEYBOARD MONITOR COMMAND "BIN", WHICH HAS NO ARGUMENTS, MAY
 3248                /       RESULT IN AN INPUT FILE HANDLE ELEMENT FOR THE "%" FILE [BLOCK 0020-0037]
 3249                /       BEING CREATED IN THE PASSED FILE HANDLE LIST.  THE COMMANDS "BIN /B" OR
 3250                /       "BIN %" WILL ALSO PASS THE SAME FILE HANDLE INFORMATION DUE TO THE INTERNAL
 3251                /       OPERATIONAL FLOW OF P?S/8 BIN.  IN CERTAIN CASES, THE FILE HANDLE LIST MAY BE
 3252                /       EXPANDED INTO A COMPOSITE OF FILE HANDLES PASSED EXPLICITLY BY THE KEYBOARD
 3253                /       MONITOR AND FILE HANDLE ENTRIES SUPPLIED BY P?S/8 BIN; WHEN USED TO INITIATE
 3254                /       THE SLURP LOADER, THERE WILL ALWAYS BE AT LEAST ONE PASSED INPUT FILE.  THE
 3255                /       DEGREE OF INTEGRITY CHECK WITH REGARD TO POINTLESS CASES OF OPTION SWITCH
 3256                /       USAGE AND DEFAULT ACTIONS VARIES WITH THE RELEASE OF P?S/8 BIN; FUTURE
 3257                /       RELEASES ARE EXPECTED TO BE COMPLETELY CONSISTENT INCLUDING ISSUING ERROR
 3258                /       MESSAGES WHEN IMPROPER OPTION "BLENDING" IS ATTEMPTED.  ALL PURPOSEFUL
 3259                /       COMBINATION OF OPTIONS ARE SUPPORTED BY THE 1976 RELEASE OF P?S/8 BIN.]
 3260   
 3261                /       STARTING FIELD INFORMATION IS DERIVED FROM COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS; THE DEFAULT
 3262                /       IS FIELD 0.  [NOTE: CERTAIN RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN MAY ALLOW ANOMALY OF
 3263                /       CERTAIN SWITCH OPTIONS; NEWER RELEASES WILL ISSUE ERROR MESSAGES WHEN MORE
 3264                /       THAN ONE FIELD OPTION SWITCH ["/0" THROUGH "/7"] IS ATTEMPTED.]
 3265   
 3266                /       THE STARTING ADDRESS IS GIVEN BY THE "=XXXX" COMMAND-LINE OPTION [WHERE XXXX
 3267                /       IS ANY VALID VALUE BETWEEN 0000 AND 7776]; THIS IS PASSED IN LOCATION
 3268                /       "SEQUALS" [07756] CONSISTENT WITH ALL P?S/8 PROGRAM EXECUTION.  WHEN THE
 3269                /       "=XXXX" COMMAND-LINE OPTION IS NOT GIVEN, P?S/8 BIN WILL DETECT THE DEFAULT
 3270                /       VALUE OF 7777.  AS REQUIRED, THE ADDRESS OF A SAFE HALT OPERATION WILL BE
 3271                /       PASSED AS THE STARTING ADDRESS BY INITIALIZATION CODE WITHIN P?S/8 BIN BEFORE
 3272                /       THE SLURP LOADER IS MOVED TO 07600-07777.
 3273   
 3274                /       WHEN P?S/8 BIN IS USED TO INVOKE ANY OF THE AVAILABLE LOADER FUNCTIONS [SLURP
 3275                /       BINARY FILE LOADER, RIM AND BIN PAPER-TAPE LOADERS, VIRTUAL BINARY FILE
 3276                /       LOADER], VARIOUS OPTION SWITCHES ARE AVAILABLE TO PREPARE USER MEMORY BEFORE
 3277                /       THE LOADER STARTS.  THE VALUE 0000 [THE LINC "HLT" INSTRUCTION] OR 7402 [THE
 3278                /       PDP-8 "HLT" INSTRUCTION] MAY BE CHOSEN USING THE APPROPRIATE OPTION SWITCHES.
 3279                /       [NOTE: THIS FEATURE IS ALWAYS RECOMMENDED TO PREVENT RUNAWAY PROGRAMS IN THE
 3280                /       EARLY STAGES OF DEVELOPMENT.]  [NOTE: FOR MORE INFORMATION ON HOW P?S/8 BIN
 3281                /       OPERATES INTERNALLY WITH REGARD TO THE VARIOUS OPERATING MODES AND A COMPLETE
 3282                /       LIST OF  OPTION SWITCHES, REFER TO ADVANCED DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 BIN
 3283                /       UTILITY.]
 3284   
 3285                /       THE CODE USED HERE IS TAKEN FROM A 1976 RELEASE OF P?S/8 FOR TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS
 3286                /       AND IS KNOWN TO NOT BE THE FINAL RELEASE; CHANGES WERE MADE IN BOTH THE SLURP
 3287                /       LOADER CODE AND THE BOOTSTRAP CODE FOR VARIOUS REASONS.  VARIOUS FUTURE
 3288                /       DIRECTIONS FOR THE SLURP LOADER IN GENERAL AND THE TC01/TC08 VERSION ARE
 3289                /       DISCUSSED BELOW.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 65

 3291                /       SLURP LOADER IMBEDDED BOOTSTRAP CODE SIZE CONSIDERATIONS.
 3292   
 3293                /       AS IMPLEMENTED IN THE 1976 RELEASE OF P?S/8, THE DEFINED SIZE OF THE
 3294                /       STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP IS 07600-07642, ALTHOUGH THE TC01/TC08 VERSION IS
 3295                /       SOMEWHAT SHORTER.  FUTURE RELEASES COULD CHANGE THE MAXIMUM SIZE TO ALLOW
 3296                /       PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENT IN EITHER THE SLURP LOADER FOR A SPECIFIC DEVICE, OR
 3297                /       THE PAPER-TAPE BIN AND RIM LOADERS AS USED ON ANY P?S/8 CONFIGURATION.
 3298   
 3299                /       WHEN THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY LOADS THE PAPER-TAPE BIN AND RIM LOADERS INTO
 3300                /       07643-07777, THE SAME BOOTSTRAP CODE THAT IS THE INITIAL PORTION OF THE SLURP
 3301                /       LOADER WILL BE LOADED INTO 07600-07642; THE THREE COMPONENTS ARE DESIGNED TO
 3302                /       COEXIST IN 07600-07777.  [NOTE: SPECIFIC COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS TO P?S/8 BIN
 3303                /       WILL LOAD AND START THE BIN OR RIM LOADERS INSTEAD OF THE SLURP LOADER.]
 3304   
 3305                /       FOR CERTAIN CONFIGURATIONS, THE ABILITY TO REDEFINE ONE OF THESE COMPONENTS
 3306                /       MAY BE AT ODDS WITH ANOTHER COMPONENT.  LOWERING THE MAXIMUM SIZE OF THE
 3307                /       STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP MAY BE FEASIBLE; THIS WOULD ALLOW ENHANCEMENT OF THE
 3308                /       BIN LOADER AS DISCUSSED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.
 3309   
 3310                /       SPECIFIC MEMORY ALLOCATION FOR THE BOOTSTRAP-ONLY PORTION OF THE SLURP LOADER
 3311                /       MAY ALSO VARY WITH OVERALL RELEASE OF P?S/8.  CONTRADICTORY FACTORS INCLUDE:
 3312   
 3313                /       1) THE LARGEST SIZE CONTIGUOUS CODE REQUIRED TO BOOTSTRAP THE MOST CUMBERSOME
 3314                /          DEVICE.  WHILE THE TC01/TC08 BOOTSTRAP SIZE IS NOT AT ISSUE, THE ANALOGOUS
 3315                /          CODING FOR OTHER DEVICES MAY REQUIRE MORE MEMORY.
 3316   
 3317                /       2) IMPLEMENTATION RESTRICTIONS WITHIN THE P?S/8 VERSION OF THE BIN LOADER ARE
 3318                /          SUBJECT TO CHANGE; CERTAIN CONTEMPLATED MODIFICATIONS COULD LENGTHEN OR
 3319                /          SHORTEN THE PROGRAM DEPENDING ON SPECIFIC GOALS.  THE CURRENT P?S/8
 3320                /          IMPLEMENTATION OF THE BINARY LOADER IS SOMEWHAT LESS PRONE TO INTERNAL
 3321                /          CORRUPTION PROBLEMS CAUSED BY THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK DEVICES THAN THE DEC
 3322                /          STANDARD RELEASE [DEC-08-LBAA, 1967].  SHORTENING THE BOOTSTRAP LENGTH BY
 3323                /          ONE WORD WOULD ELIMINATE THE ONLY REMAINING DMA-RELATED PROBLEM WHILE
 3324                /          MAINTAINING THE CURRENT FEATURE SET.  [NOTE: FOR MORE INFORMATION, REFER TO
 3325                /          DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATION OF THE BIN LOADER WITHIN THE
 3326                /          P?S/8 BIN UTILITY.]
 3327   
 3328                /       3) THE TC01/TC08 BOOSTRAP DESCRIBED HERE IS A STAND-ALONE PROGRAM LOADED
 3329                /          ENTIRELY IN FIELD 0.  BY DESIGN, CERTAIN SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS REQUIRE
 3330                /          EXTENDED MEMORY TO OPERATE.  IN MANY SUCH CASES, MOST OF THE SYSTEM RESTART
 3331                /          CODE CAN BE MADE RESIDENT IN EXTENDED MEMORY, REQUIRING ONLY A SHORT
 3332                /          LINKAGE ROUTINE IN FIELD 0 TO INITIATE THE STANDARD RESTART.  AS SUCH,
 3333                /          FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN COULD SUPPORT A UNIVERSAL RESTART CONVENTION
 3334                /          FOR ALL SYSTEMS REQUIRING EXTENDED MEMORY.
 3335   
 3336                /          THE FIELD 0 SEGMENT WOULD BE EXACTLY TWO WORDS LONG: A) A "CIF" INSTRUCTION
 3337                /          TO THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL FIELD OF MEMORY [62X3], B) A "JMP 7777" [5377] OR
 3338                /          "JMP 7600" [5200] INSTRUCTION; THE HANDLER EXTENSION IS RESPONSIBLE FOR ALL
 3339                /          ADDITIONAL RESTART CONSIDERATIONS.
 3340   
 3341                /          WITH THIS SECONDARY CONVENTION IN PLACE, IT IS CONCEIVABLE THE LARGEST
 3342                /          STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP WOULD BE THE TC01/TC08 VERSION AS DESCRIBED HERE
 3343                /          [PERHAPS WITH CERTAIN MODIFICATIONS, DEPENDING ON SLURP LOADER INTERACTION]
 3344                /          AND OTHER CONSIDERATIONS.  SHOULD THIS BE THE CASE, ALL KNOWN PROBLEMS OF
 3345                /          THE PAPER-TAPE BIN LOADER CAN BE ELIMINATED.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 66

 3347          0001          FIELD    SLUFLD%10              /GENERATION FIELD FOR SLURP LOADER.
              *0200 
 3348   
 3349                        NOPUNCH                         /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 3350   
 3351          0000          FIELD   0                       /WHERE THIS LOADS WHEN EXECUTED.
              *0200 
 3352   
 3353                        ENPUNCH                         /UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 3354   
 3355         *7000          *SLUADR                         /GENERATION ADDRESS FOR SLURP LOADER.
 3356   
 3357         *7600          RELOC   SBOOT                   /WHERE THIS IS MOVED TO FOR EXECUTION.
 3358   
 3359                /       THE SYSTEM RESTARTS HERE USING THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP PLACED IN 07600; THIS
 3360                /       ALLOWS THE UNIVERSAL EXIT CONVENTION TO APPLY IN NEARLY ALL SITUATIONS OF
 3361                /       CONFORMING PROGRAMS AND CONFORMING OPERATING SYSTEMS.  [NOTE: NEVER ASSUME ANY
 3362                /       PARTICULAR OPERATING SYSTEM IS FULLY COMPATIBLE WITHOUT PROPER DOCUMENTATION;
 3363                /       HOWEVER, ALL RELEASES OF P?S/8 AND THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM ARE COMPATIBLE.]
 3364   
 3365                /       RESTART CONSIDERATIONS:
 3366   
 3367                /       1) THE DATA FIELD MUST BE SET TO 0.
 3368   
 3369                /       2) INTERRUPTS SHOULD BE DISABLED AS THE P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR LOADS OVER THE
 3370                /          PRIMARY INTERRUPT LOCATIONS [00000-00001].
 3371   
 3372                /       3) THE AC AND LINK CONTENTS ARE IGNORED.
 3373   
 3374                /       NOTE: ALL PROGRAMMING ON THIS PAGE IS SPECIFIC TO THE TC01/TC08 CONFIGURATION;
 3375                /       BOOTUP ROUTINES FOR OTHER DEVICES MUST FIT INTO THE SAME [OR GENERALLY LESS]
 3376                /       CODE SPACE.
 3377   
 3378                /       TC01/TC08 HARDWARE-SPECIFIC BOOTSTRAP DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS.
 3379   
 3380                /       1) WHEN THE BOOTSTRAP CODE IS INITIATED, THE DECTAPE IS REWOUND INTO THE
 3381                /          REVERSE END ZONE.  THIS IS FOLLOWED BY A FORWARD READ OPERATION WHICH
 3382                /          INITIATES DATA TRANSFER OF THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 INTO MEMORY USING
 3383                /          THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK [DMA].
 3384   
 3385                /       2) DURING THE TAPE BLOCK DMA TRANSFER, THE CONTENTS OF TAPE BLOCK 0000
 3386                /          GRADUALLY REPLACES THE CODE DEFINED BELOW; THE ONLY REQUIREMENT IS THE
 3387                /          REPLACEMENT WAIT LOOP CODE MUST BE SUFFICIENTLY COMPATIBLE WITH THE
 3388                /          BOOTSTRAP-PROVIDED WAIT LOOP TO ALLOW THE DMA TO COMPLETE PROPERLY (SUBJECT
 3389                /          TO POSSIBLE DATA TRANSFER LENGTH ALTERATION ONCE THE WORD COUNT AND CURRENT
 3390                /          ADDRESS LOCATIONS [07754-07755] ARE OVERLAID BY THE CORRESPONDING DATA ON
 3391                /          THE DECTAPE BLOCK).
 3392   
 3393                /       3) CONFORMING TO THE TC01/TC08 BOOTSTRAP CONVENTION REQUIRES THE LOOP WAITING
 3394                /          FOR THE DONE FLAG BE PLACED IN MEMORY LOCATIONS 07616-07617.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 67

 3396                /       4) TRADE-OFFS EXIST WITH REGARD TO DECTAPE STATUS REGISTER "A" ACCESS.  THE
 3397                /          "DTLA" [6766] OPERATION REQUIRES NO PRIOR KNOWLEDGE OF STATUS REGISTER "A".
 3398                /          THE "DTXA" [6764] OPERATION UPDATES THE EXISTING STATUS REGISTER DATA VIA
 3399                /          .XOR. TRANSFER BETWEEN THE ACCUMULATOR AND STATUS REGISTER "A".  END-ZONE
 3400                /          HANDLING IS GENERALLY EASIER TO IMPLEMENT USING THE "DTXA" [6764]
 3401                /          INSTRUCTION; THE DIRECTION IS MERELY INVERTED USING THE CONSTANT 0400 [OR
 3402                /          EQUIVALENT] WHEN THE END-ZONE ERROR IS DETECTED.
 3403   
 3404                /       5) UNLIKE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, THE WAIT ROUTINE WITHIN THE STAND-ALONE
 3405                /          BOOTSTRAP IS ONLY USED TO RESTART P?S/8 [OR ANY OTHER OPERATING SYSTEM
 3406                /          SUFFICIENTLY COMPATIBLE WITH THIS BOOTSTRAP CONVENTION].  AS SUCH, THE
 3407                /          WAIT ROUTINE USED HERE DOES NOT CHECK FOR ERRORS; WHEN USED TO REWIND THE
 3408                /          OPERATING SYSTEM DECTAPE, THE ERROR FLAG IS RAISED DUE TO CONTROLLER-BASED
 3409                /          DETECTION OF THE REVERSE END-ZONE.  WHEN USED TO READ BLOCK 0000 WHILE
 3410                /          MOVING FORWARD COMING OUT OF THE REVERSE END-ZONE, THE WAIT LOOP WILL BE
 3411                /          OVERLAID BY THE CODE CONTAINED IN BLOCK 0000 AT THE DESIGNATED WAIT
 3412                /          ROUTINE LOCATIONS.
 3413   
 3414                /       6) AS A RESULT OF THE READ OPERATION, ALL MEMORY LOCATIONS FROM 07600 ONWARD
 3415                /          [OTHER THAN THE WAIT LOOP] ARE EXPECTED TO BE REPLACED WITH VALUES OBTAINED
 3416                /          FROM THE CONTENTS OF THE BOOT BLOCK [0000]; ALL SUBSEQUENT ACTIONS ARE
 3417                /          DETERMINED BY THE CONTENTS OF THE BOOT-BLOCK.  [NOTE: IT IS LIKELY THE
 3418                /          BOOT-BLOCK CODE DIFFERS GREATLY FROM THE REST OF THE CODE LOADED PREVIOUSLY
 3419                /          ALONG WITH THE BOOTSTRAP CODE; THE FORMER CODE IS PRIMARILY INTERNAL
 3420                /          ROUTINES OF THE SLURP LOADER WHEN USED AS DEFINED HERE; ALTERNATIVELY THE
 3421                /          REST OF THE CODE COULD BE PRIMARILY THE RIM AND BIN LOADERS.  THE BOOTSTRAP
 3422                /          ROUTINE IS MEANT TO BE LOADED WITH EITHER LOADING PROGRAM AS REQUIRED.]
 3423   
 3424                /       7) NEWER RELEASES OF THIS BOOTSTAP SHOULD EXIST THAT HAVE BEEN WRITTEN FOR
 3425                /          LATER RELEASES OF P?S/8.  THE DISTINGUISHING FEATURE IS SUPPORT FOR DRIVES
 3426                /          OTHER THAN UNIT 0.  THIS IS ACCOMPLISHED BY EXTENDING THE STANDARD
 3427                /          BOOTSTRAP TO INCLUDE DRIVE UNIT SUPPORT WITHIN THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP
 3428                /          WHEN USED AS DEFINED HERE, AS WELL AS SUPPORT CODE ON THE DECTAPE
 3429                /          BOOT-BLOCK USED DURING THE INITIAL BOOTUP PHASE THAT DETERMINES THE DRIVE
 3430                /          UNIT IN EFFECT.  AS SUCH, THIS FEATURE WILL LIKELY REMAIN A UNIQUE FEATURE
 3431                /          OF P?S/8.  [NOTE: MOST OF THE MANUAL BOOT PROCEDURES FOR TC01/TC08 CAN BE
 3432                /          EXTENDED TO SUPPORT DRIVES OTHER THAN UNIT 0 BY SLIGHT MODIFICATION.]
 3433   
 3434                /          IMPLEMENTING EXTENDED UNIT SUPPORT WITHIN THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP
 3435                /          REQUIRES SUPPLYING A UNIT SELECT VALUE IN THE RANGE OF 0000-7000 DEPENDING
 3436                /          ON THE DESIRED DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT; THE TC01/TC08 HARDWARE REQUIRES UNIT
 3437                /          SELECT INFORMATION BE PASSED IN AC[0-2] DURING A "DTLA" [6766] OR "DTXA"
 3438                /          [6764] OPERATION.  THERE MAY BE ADDITIONAL CONSIDERATIONS AFFECTING OVERALL
 3439                /          PROGRAM OPERATION AND/OR CODE EFFICIENCY; THESE ISSUES WILL BE DEALT WITH
 3440                /          IN FUTURE RELEASES OF THIS DOCUMENT.


