/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 77

 3873                /       SECTION 5: THE P?S/8 HANDLER RELOADING "FILE" IMAGE FOR TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS.
 3874   
 3875                /       WHEN THE USER PROGRAM BINARY FILES ARE LOADED BY THE SLURP LOADER, THE
 3876                /       CONTENTS OF THE LAST PAGE OF MEMORY CONSISTS OF THE STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AT
 3877                /       07600 AND THE SLURP LOADER.  AS SUCH, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS NOT
 3878                /       AVAILABLE TO THE LOADED PROGRAM.  BY INCLUDING AN ADDITIONAL "FILE" TO LOAD
 3879                /       AFTER ALL USER PROGRAMS, THE CONTENTS OF THE LAST PAGE OF MEMORY IS
 3880                /       TRANSFORMED INTO A FULLY FUNCTIONAL COPY OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; ALL
 3881                /       READ/WRITE CALLS ARE FUNCTIONAL; THE SYSTEM CAN BE RESTARTED AT 07600 IN THE
 3882                /       USUAL MANNER.
 3883   
 3884                /       THE NET EFFECT OF USING THE SPECIAL RELOADING "FILE" IS THAT THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 3885                /       HANDLER IS RESIDENT IN MEMORY WITH NO ADDITIONAL LOADING RESTRICTIONS ON THE
 3886                /       USER PROGRAM SPACE.  THIS IS EQUIVALENT TO HAVING USED THE VIRTUAL LOADER TO
 3887                /       LOAD THE USER PROGRAM FILES; HOWEVER, THE SIGNIFICANT OVERHEAD OF THE VIRTUAL
 3888                /       LOADER HAS BEEN ELIMINATED.  AS SUCH, THE LOADING PROCESS IS NOTICEABLY FASTER
 3889                /       AND THE SYSTEM DEVICE CAN REMAIN WRITE-LOCKED DURING THE ENTIRE LOADING
 3890                /       PROCESS.  [NOTE: THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CAN BE CALLED FOR THE PURPOSE OF A
 3891                /       WRITE OPERATION, IN WHICH CASE A HARMLESS WRITE-PROTECT ERROR WOULD OCCUR; THE
 3892                /       USUAL ERROR RECOVERY PROCESS WOULD APPLY: WRITE ENABLE THE DRIVE AND PRESS
 3893                /       "CONTINUE" ON THE CPU FRONT PANEL.  IN CERTAIN RELEASES OF P?S/8, IF THE
 3894                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT, ALL ERRORS ARE HANDLED IN THE OVERLAY.
 3895                /       P?S/8 BIN WILL PASS THE INTENDED STARTING ADDRESS AND FIELD TO THE OVERLAY BY
 3896                /       A SPECIAL-PURPOSE INTERFACE BETWEEN THE TWO PROGRAMS DURING THE ONCE-ONLY
 3897                /       INITIALIZATION PHASE.  THE STARTING FIELD AND ADDRESS INFORMATION WILL BE THAT
 3898                /       OF AN ADDRESS WITHIN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY; THE USUAL SYSTEM DEVICE
 3899                /       HANDLER HOOKS TO THE OVERLAY WILL THEN BE APPLIED.  AFTER FULLY RESTORING THE
 3900                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ENVIRONMENT, THE USER PROGRAM WILL THEN BE STARTED AS
 3901                /       INTENDED.]
 3902   
 3903                /       THIS IS THE ACTUAL IMAGE OF BLOCK 0060 OF THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DECTAPE FOR
 3904                /       TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS TAKEN FROM A 1976 P?S/8 RELEASE FOR THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE.
 3905   
 3906                /       [NOTE: NEWER RELEASES OF P?S/8 FOR THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE ARE KNOWN TO EXIST;
 3907                /       CERTAIN ASPECTS OF THE CODING MAY BE SLIGHTLY DIFFERENT OR ENHANCED AS
 3908                /       NECESSARY.]
 3909   
 3910                /       ACTUAL CONTENTS IS HARDWARE-SPECIFIC AND ALSO SLURP LOADER RELEASE-SPECIFIC.
 3911                /       THE CODING PRESENTED HERE IS COMPATIBLE WITH THE TC01/TC08 SLURP LOADER FOR
 3912                /       THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.
 3913   
 3914                /       DUE TO THE WAY FILE HANDLES ARE PASSED TO P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS [INCLUDING
 3915                /       P?S/8 BIN], IT IS NECESSARY TO PLACE THIS CODE ON AN APPROPRIATE SYSTEM BLOCK
 3916                /       THAT IS SUFFICIENTLY SIMILAR TO THE PLACEMENT OF A FILE WITHIN THE TFS
 3917                /       DIRECTORY.  SINCE TFS FILE STARTING BLOCKS MUST BE ALIGNED ON EIGHT-BLOCK
 3918                /       BOUNDARIES, THE USE OF BLOCK 0060 MORE THAN MEETS THIS REQUIREMENT.  [NOTE:
 3919                /       ALL RELEASED P?S/8 SYSTEMS ARE ACTUALLY CONFIGURED SUCH THAT ALL TFS FILES ARE
 3920                /       ACTUALLY ALIGNED ON 16-BLOCK BOUNDARIES; THIS WAS DONE FOR A NEVER-IMPLEMENTED
 3921                /       FUTURE REQUIREMENT BEYOND THE BASIC EIGHT-BLOCK BOUNDARY; THE USE OF BLOCK
 3922                /       0060 ALSO CONFORMS TO THIS ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENT.  GIVEN THE DEVELOPMENT OF
 3923                /       THE P?S/8 SHELL OVERLAY, IT IS LIKELY THIS EXTRA BIT OF UNUSED BOUNDARY
 3924                /       ALIGNMENT WILL BE ELIMINATED IN FUTURE RELEASES.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 78

 3926                /       THE "FILE" AS DESCRIBED HERE MUST BE PLACED IN THE SENTINEL POSITION OF THE
 3927                /       PASSED LIST OF FILE HANDLES AS THE LOADING OPERATION INITIATED CAUSES
 3928                /       SELECTIVE DESTRUCTION OF THE SLURP LOADER.  ONCE THIS LOADING COMPLETES, THE
 3929                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WILL BE IN MEMORY DISPLACING THE SLURP LOADER; THE
 3930                /       ENTIRE OPERATION CAN BE PERFORMED WITH THE SYSTEM DECTAPE WRITE-LOCKED.
 3931   
 3932                /       NOTE: THE SLURP LOADER NORMALLY ALLOWS UP TO 17 [OCTAL 21] FILES TO BE PASSED
 3933                /       FOR A SINGLE SLURP LOADER EXECUTION.  SINCE SLURP FORMAT IS QUITE EFFICIENT,
 3934                /       THIS MAXIMUM NUMBER IS RARELY USED; MOST USER PROGRAMS LOAD IN THREE BINARY
 3935                /       FILES OR LESS.  FOR HUGE PROGRAMS THAT LOAD INTO MOST OF 32K [THE MAXIMUM SIZE
 3936                /       OF MEMORY] THERE IS A SMALL POSSIBILITY THAT THE LOADING OPERATION MAY
 3937                /       REQUIRE A TWO-STAGE OPERATION; FOR ANY PROJECT 24K OR SMALLER, THIS IS HIGHLY
 3938                /       UNLIKELY; HOWEVER, WHEN THE "FILE" DESCRIBED HERE IS IN EFFECT, THE MAXIMUM
 3939                /       NUMBER OF PASSED INPUT FILES IS LOWERED TO 16 FILES SINCE THE "FILE" IS
 3940                /       INTERNALLY PASSED AS THE SENTINEL FILE IN THE LIST.  CERTAIN VERSIONS OF P?S/8
 3941                /       MAY IGNORE THE INHERENT PROBLEM AND TRUNCATE THE LIST WITHOUT ERROR MESSAGES.
 3942                /       FUTURE RELEASES WILL ISSUE ERROR MESSAGES FOR THIS UNLIKELY CASE SUGGESTING
 3943                /       THE FILES BE LOADED IN TWO INDEPENDENT LOADING OPERATIONS [PREFERABLY ALL
 3944                /       EXTENDED MEMORY LOADING PERFORMED FIRST THEN ANOTHER COMMAND TO ACCOMPLISH
 3945                /       FIELD 0 AND OTHER LOADING].  [NOTE: TO AVOID THE ENTIRE PROBLEM, THE VIRTUAL
 3946                /       LOADER MAY BE USED INSTEAD OF THE SLURP LOADER AND THE "FILE" AS DISCUSSED
 3947                /       HERE; HOWEVER, THE LOADING PROCESS WILL TAKE LONGER AND THE SYSTEM DEVICE MUST
 3948                /       BE WRITE-ENABLED.]
 3949   
 3950                /       METHOD OF IMPLEMENTATION FOR THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE SYSTEM.
 3951   
 3952                /       THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE SYSTEM HANDLER IS FULLY OPERATIONAL IF ALL ADDRESSES
 3953                /       FROM "SBOOT" [07600] THROUGH "SADDR" [07746] ARE LOADED CORRECTLY IN MEMORY.
 3954                /       THE REST OF THE PAGE FROM 07747-07777 NEED NOT BE RELOADED FOR THE SYSTEM
 3955                /       DEVICE HANDLER TO FUNCTION CORRECTLY INCLUDING THE SYSTEM RESTART AT 07600.
 3956   
 3957                /       AS SUCH, THE "FILE" AS DEFINED HERE LOADS MOST OF ITS OPERATIONAL CODE INTO
 3958                /       THE PASSED FILE LIST AREA, WHICH AT THAT POINT IS OTHERWISE MEANINGLESS
 3959                /       [CONTAINING THE PASSED FILE HANDLES FOR THE NOW-LOADED INPUT FILES, INCLUDING
 3960                /       THE SENTINEL VALUE OF BLOCK 0060 ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE BOOTUP UNIT REPRESENTING
 3961                /       THE RELOADER "FILE"].
 3962   
 3963                /       THE FINAL LOADING OPERATION IS TO PATCH THE SLURP LOADER CODE THAT ATTEMPTS TO
 3964                /       LOCATE ADDITIONAL PASSED FILES [ALL FILES HAVE BEEN PROCESSED] TO INSTEAD
 3965                /       START THE OPERATIONAL CODE DEFINED BELOW AS "SLIGO" [07757].  EXECUTION OF
 3966                /       THIS CODE RELOADS THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER PORTION NEEDED FOR COMPLETE
 3967                /       FUNCTIONALITY AS DEFINED ABOVE.  AFTER THE RELOAD IS COMPLETE, THE PROGRAM
 3968                /       STARTS AS DEFINED IN THE SLURP LOADER AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.
 3969                /       [NOTE: THE SLURP LOADER STARTUP CODE AND DATA ARE DEFINED BEYOND THE RELOAD
 3970                /       POINT; AS SUCH, THE STARTUP FUNCTION OF THE SLURP LOADER IS STILL INTACT.]
 3971   
 3972                /       ONE OF THE OPERATIONS ASSOCIATED WITH THE RELOAD IS TO STOP THE DECTAPE DRIVE
 3973                /       AFTER THE RELOAD IS COMPLETE.  THE CONSTANT "S200" [07602] IS USED FOR THIS
 3974                /       PURPOSE AS IT IS AVAILABLE AS A RESULT OF THE RELOAD OPERATION.
 3975   
 3976                /       ONCE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS FULLY LOADED [OTHER THAN POSSIBLE
 3977                /       INITIZATION BY THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY], THE PROGRAM WILL BE STARTED AS
 3978                /       SPECIFIED.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 79

 3980                /       DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS CONSIDERATIONS.
 3981   
 3982                /       SINCE THE SLURP LOADER IS NO LONGER IN MEMORY, THE SAFE HALT ADDRESS USED AS
 3983                /       THE DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS WHILE THE SLURP LOADER WAS IN MEMORY IS NO
 3984                /       LONGER APPLICABLE; HOWEVER, THE RELOAD ROUTINES INCLUDE A REPLACEMENT DEFAULT
 3985                /       STARTING ADDRESS "DEFADR" [07776] WHICH IS FOLLOWED BY A "JMP DEFADR" [5376]
 3986                /       INSTRUCTION TO PROVIDE A SAFE HALT ADDRESS EQUIVALENT TO WHAT WAS AVAILABLE
 3987                /       WITHIN THE SLURP LOADER.  [NOTE: P?S/8 BIN IMPLEMENTS THE "FILE" SYSTEM DEVICE
 3988                /       HANDLER RELOAD BY THE COMMAND-LINE OPTION "/I".  AS SUCH, INTERNAL ONCE-ONLY
 3989                /       INITIALIZATION CODE WITHIN P?S/8 BIN WILL ADJUST THE DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS
 3990                /       AS NECESSARY; THERE ARE EQUIVALENT ISSUES WHEN THE VIRTUAL LOADER IS USED AND
 3991                /       A DEFAULT SAFE HALT ADDRESS IS REQUIRED.  IN CERTAIN IMPLEMENTATIONS, THE
 3992                /       RELOADED SAFE HALT ADDRESS AND THE VIRTUAL LOADER SAFE HALT ADDRESS ARE
 3993                /       IDENTICAL; HOWEVER, THERE ARE NO REQUIREMENTS FOR THIS AS SUCH.  THIS ISSUE
 3994                /       IS CONSIDERED AN INTERNAL DESIGN RECOMMENDATION ONLY.  APPROPRIATE
 3995                /       DOCUMENTATION WILL PROVIDE ALL THREE DEFAULT SAFE HALT ADDRESSES FOR ANY GIVEN
 3996                /       HARDWARE CONFIGURATION REGARDLESS OF COINCIDENTAL SIMILARITY.]
 3997   
 3998                /       SLURP LOADER AND RELOADER "FILE" SUPPORT ISSUES.
 3999   
 4000                /       ONLY CERTAIN HARDWARE DEVICES SUPPORT THE SLURP LOADER.  ON SYSTEMS THAT MUST
 4001                /       INVOKE THE VIRTUAL LOADER, THE "/I" SWITCH IS IGNORED [WHICH OBVIATES THE
 4002                /       ENTIRE PROBLEM REGARDING TRUNCATING THE PASSED FILE LIST AS DISCUSSED ABOVE].
 4003                /       [NOTE: FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 MAY ISSUE WARNING MESSAGES IF THE "/I" SWITCH
 4004                /       IS NOT BEING HONORED; HOWEVER, SINCE BATCH FILES MAY BE WRITTEN TO PERFORM
 4005                /       OPTIMAL LOADING ON ANY P?S/8 CONFIGURATION, THIS WOULD NOT BE CONSIDERED AN
 4006                /       ERROR CAUSING P?S/8 BIN TO ABORT THE LOADING OPERATION.]
 4007   
 4008                /       FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN MAY SUPPORT RELOADING THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 4009                /       FROM NON-ZERO LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS.  AS SUCH, THE "FILE" AS DESCRIBED HERE
 4010                /       MAY BE SOMEWHAT MORE COMPLEX.  [IF NECESSARY, ADDITIONAL LOCATIONS WITHIN THE
 4011                /       SLURP LOADER MAY BE TRANSIENTLY AVAILABLE FOR SOME OF THE PRELIMINARY
 4012                /       RELOADING FUNCTIONS; AS SUCH, THE CODE DESCRIBED HERE MAY BE BROKEN UP INTO
 4013                /       SEVERAL SECTIONS TO ALLOW A FEW EXTRA WORDS OF CODE TO HANDLE THE ADDITIONAL
 4014                /       LOGICAL UNIT REQUIREMENT.]
 4015   
 4016                /       THE RELOADER "FILE" IS IN SLURP BINARY FORMAT AS CREATED BY THE OUTPUT OF THE
 4017                /       P?S/8 PAL ASSEMBLER AND OTHER UTILITIES.  THE SOURCE CODE USES CERTAIN
 4018                /       TECHNIQUES TO ALLOW THE IMAGE OF THE "FILE" TO BE WRITTEN OUT AS IF THIS WAS
 4019                /       THE CASE.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 80

 4021                /       THEORETICAL CONSIDERATIONS REGARDING USING AN ACTUAL BINARY FILE INSTEAD OF
 4022                /       THE "FILE" SUPPLIED BY P?S/8 BIN.
 4023   
 4024                /       LIMITATIONS OF A USER-SUPPLIED FILE TO ACCOMPLISH THE IDENTICAL RESULTS AS
 4025                /       THE INTERNAL "FILE" ARE:
 4026   
 4027                /       1) THE USER MIGHT PLACE THE RELOAD FILE IN THE WRONG POSITION IN THE PASSED
 4028                /          FILE LIST; THE NET EFFECT WOULD PREVENT CERTAIN PASSED BINARY FILES FROM
 4029                /          LOADING; P?S/8 BIN FORCES THE "FILE" TO THE SENTINEL POSITION IN THE PASSED
 4030                /          FILE LIST AVOIDING THIS ISSUE ENTIRELY.
 4031   
 4032                /       2) THE USE OF THE RELOAD FEATURE IS GENERIC TO ALL P?S/8 SYSTEMS THAT SUPPORT
 4033                /          THE SLURP LOADER AND THE RELOAD "FILE".  AS SUCH, THE COMMAND LINE OPTION
 4034                /          IS THE ONLY USER REQUIREMENT TO ACCOMPLISH THIS FUNCTION.  FOR USERS OF
 4035                /          MULTIPLE P?S/8 SYSTEMS, RELOAD "FILES" WOULD BE NEEDED, EACH FOR EVERY
 4036                /          HARDWARE CONFIGURATION ACCESSED WHICH CAN LEAD TO NEEDLESS CONFUSION.
 4037   
 4038                /       3) THE ENTIRETY OF THE "FILE" MUST BE CONTAINED IN A SINGLE 128-WORD BLOCK.
 4039                /          THIS IS A LIMITATION OF P?S/8 AS CURRENTLY IMPLEMENTED; IF LONGER RELOAD
 4040                /          "FILES" ARE NEEDED, THE SYSTEM WOULD HAVE TO BE SLIGHTLY REDESIGNED TO
 4041                /          ALLOW SOMEWHAT LONGER RELOAD "FILE" SPACE.
 4042   
 4043                /          TO DATE, ALL RELOAD "FILES" HAVE BEEN DEFINED IN 128 WORDS OF SLURP FORMAT
 4044                /          BINARY OR LESS.
 4045   
 4046                /          IN THE CASE OF THE RX8E DISKETTE CONTROLLER AND SIMILAR DEVICES, THE
 4047                /          CONTENTS OF THE FILE MAY BE OF MIXED FORMAT: SLURP FORMAT UP TO SOME
 4048                /          DEFINED POINT FOLLOWED BY CORE-IMAGE FOR THE ADDITIONAL WORDS.  FOR DEVICES
 4049                /          SUCH AS THE RX8E, IT MAY BE MORE EFFICIENT TO LOAD IN ROUTINES TO READ IN
 4050                /          12-BIT WORDS; THE CODE IS REQUIRED TO RELOAD THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; AS
 4051                /          SUCH, THE SAME ROUTINE CAN BE USED TO READ THE REST OF THE RELOAD "FILE"
 4052                /          AS WELL.
 4053   
 4054                /          FOR CERTAIN SYSTEM HARDWARE CONFIGURATIONS, EXTENDED MEMORY IS REQUIRED FOR
 4055                /          THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER EXTENSION.  AS NECESSARY, PORTIONS OF THE SLURP
 4056                /          LOADER MAY BE RESIDENT IN THE HANDLER EXTENSION AREA.  [NOTE: MOST EXISTING
 4057                /          SYSTEMS REQUIRING EXTENDED MEMORY WERE WRITTEN BEFORE THE AVAILABLE MEMORY
 4058                /          WAS GENERALIZED TO 6000-7777 IN THE HIGHEST FIELD OF MEMORY.  FOR NEW WORK,
 4059                /          THIS REPRESENTS THE UPPER LIMIT OF AVAILABLE MEMORY FOR THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 4060                /          HANDLER WHICH IS ESSENTIAL TO RUN P?S/8 ON PROBLEMATIC DEVICES; HOWEVER,
 4061                /          FOR MOST OF THE EARLIER HANDLER CONFIGURATIONS, THERE IS OFTEN SEVERAL FREE
 4062                /          PAGES OF CODE SPACE AVAILABLE TO ENHANCE THE SLURP LOADER OR OTHER SYSTEM
 4063                /          COMPONENT.  AS SUCH, PORTIONS OF WHAT MIGHT OTHERWISE BE IN THE RELOADER
 4064                /          "FILE" COULD ACTUALLY BE RESIDENT IN THE DEVICE HANDLER EXTENSION AREA IN
 4065                /          EXTENDED MEMORY.  IF THIS IS THE CASE FOR ANY PARTICULAR HARDWARE
 4066                /          CONFIGURATION, IT WILL TEND TO SIMPLIFY IMPLEMENTATION OF THE ENTIRE "FILE"
 4067                /          DEFINITION WITHIN AT MOST 128 WORDS OF SLURP FORMAT BINARY.  [NOTE: THE
 4068                /          ACTUAL EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD USED WOULD GENERALLY HAVE BEEN DETERMINED
 4069                /          WITHIN THE SYSTEM RESTART CODE AT 07600 AS LOADED WITH THE SLURP LOADER.
 4070                /          SUFFICIENT SPACE WOULD GENERALLY BE AVAILABLE TO PROVIDE ANY NECESSARY
 4071                /          LINKAGE ROUTINES REQUIRED FOR THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOAD FUNCTION.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 81

 4073                /       THE P?S/8 "GET" COMMAND CONSIDERATIONS.
 4074   
 4075                /       THE GET COMMAND LOADS SLURP BINARY FILES INTO MEMORY AND WRITES OUT AN IMAGE
 4076                /       OF 00000-07777 ON THE SYSTEM SCRATCH AREA [BLOCKS 0020-0057] THEN REBOOTS THE
 4077                /       SYSTEM.  THIS ALLOWS PROGRAM DEBUGGING WITH P?S/8 ODT AND OTHER FUNCTIONS.
 4078   
 4079                /       THE WRITE FUNCTION IS ACTUALLY RESIDENT ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IMAGE AS
 4080                /       THE DEFAULT CONTENTS OF THE SYSTEM LOADER [07632-07637].  AS SUCH, IF THE
 4081                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS RELOADED, SETTING THE DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS TO
 4082                /       07632 WILL ACCOMPLISH THE MEMORY SAVE AND REBOOT.  [NOTE: WHEN THE VIRTUAL
 4083                /       LOADER IS IN EFFECT, THE SYSTEM LOADER WILL BE UPDATED TO INCLUDE THE SAME
 4084                /       CALL AS IF FRESHLY RELOADED.  ONCE THE PROGRAM IS STARTED AT 07632, THE NET
 4085                /       RESULT IS THE SAVING OF MEMORY AND SYSTEM RESTART AS DESCRIBED ABOVE
 4086                /       REGARDLESS OF WHICH LOADER WAS USED.]
 4087   
 4088                /       NOTE: THE GET COMMAND IS ACTUALLY AN ALIAS ENTRY FOR THE P?S/8 BIN PROGRAM.
 4089                /       THE ONLY OPERATIONAL DIFFERENCE IS FORCING THE STARTING FIELD TO 0 AND THE
 4090                /       STARTING ADDRESS TO 7632.  THE "/V" SWITCH IS ALLOWED TO FORCE THE USE OF THE
 4091                /       VIRTUAL LOADER; THE DEFAULT IS TO USE THE SLURP LOADER WITH THE "/I" SWITCH
 4092                /       FORCED ON TO RELOAD THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  ALL DISCUSSIONS REGARDING THE
 4093                /       ISSUES OF THE "/I" SWITCH AND PASSED FILE LIST LENGTH ARE IDENTICAL TO THAT
 4094                /       DESCRIBED ABOVE.  FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN AND GET MAY HAVE MORE STRINGENT
 4095                /       CONSISTENCY CHECKS TO PREVENT THE USE OF CONFLICTING OPTIONS [SUCH AS
 4096                /       SPECIFYING "/1" THROUGH "/7" FROM THE GET COMMAND.  WARNING MESSAGES MAY BE
 4097                /       ISSUED IN FUTURE RELEASES IF THE "/I" SWITCH IS EXPRESSLY USED ON SYSTEMS
 4098                /       WHERE THE VIRTUAL LOADER MUST BE USED; THE OPTIONAL USE OF "/V" IN SUCH
 4099                /       SITUATIONS WOULD BE REDUNDANT BUT OTHERWISE IGNORED; THIS ALLOWS USER COMMANDS
 4100                /       TO FORCE THE USE OF THE VIRTUAL LOADER ON ALL SYSTEMS.
 4101   
 4102                /       THE INTERNAL LOGIC OF BIN AND GET IS SUCH THAT THE REQUIREMENT OF USING THE
 4103                /       VIRTUAL LOADER IS KNOWN BY INTERNAL INSPECTION OF THE AREA RESERVED FOR AN
 4104                /       IMAGE OF THE SLURP LOADER.  FUTURE VERSIONS OF P?S/8 MAY SUPPORT A
 4105                /       CONFIGURATION PROGRAM THAT WILL DEPEND ON THIS GENERIC IMPLEMENTATION TO
 4106                /       ALLOW SYSTEM PREPARATION ON A DEVICE OTHER THAN THE INTENDED CONFIGURATION.
 4107                /       THE RESULTING SYSTEM CAN THEN BE IMAGED TO BE ULTIMATELY MADE AVAILABLE ON THE
 4108                /       INTENDED HARDWARE.
 4109   
 4110                /       NOTE: IF THE VIRTUAL LOADER IS NOT IN EFFECT, OR THE "/I" OPTION SWITCH IS
 4111                /       NOT USED TO RELOAD THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, THE USE OF =7632 IS NOT A VIABLE
 4112                /       OPTION.  STARTING THE SLURP LOADER AT INTERNAL LOCATIONS CAN CAUSE SYSTEM
 4113                /       CORRUPTION.  ALL BINARY LOADER OPERATIONS MUST BE CAREFULLY STARTED CONSISTENT
 4114                /       WITH PROGRAM REQUIREMENTS.  IN GENERALY, ALL STARTING INFORMATION IS THE
 4115                /       USER'S RESPONSIBILITY.  [NOTE: THIS IS ONE OF THE MOST IMPORTANT FEATURES OF
 4116                /       THE SLURP LOADER; PROGRAM BEHAVIOR CAN BE UNPREDICTABLE, ESPECIALLY IN THE
 4117                /       EARLY STAGES OF DEVELOPMENT; IF THE SYSTEM DEVICE IS WRITE-LOCKED, ONLY THE
 4118                /       IN-MEMORY COPY OF ANY SYSTEM COMPONENT CAN BE CORRUPTED.]
 4119   
 4120                /       FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 BIN MAY IMPOSE RESTRICTIONS ON COMMAND-SUPPLIED
 4121                /       STARTING ADDRESS AND FIELD VALUES; THIS WOULD PREVENT IMPROPER PROGRAM START
 4122                /       WITHIN ANY SYSTEM RESERVED ADDRESS SPACE.  PERMITTED ADDRESSES WOULD INCLUDE
 4123                /       ANY ADDRESS FROM 00000-07577, 07600 [WHICH HARMLESSLY RESTARTS THE SYSTEM
 4124                /       AFTER ALL LOADING] AND 07632 IF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS AVAILABLE AS THE
 4125                /       PROGRAM STARTS.  ANY EXTENDED MEMORY ADDRESS WOULD BE PERMITTED UNLESS THE
 4126                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER REQUIRES EXTENDED MEMORY EXTENSION OR THE LOGICAL
 4127                /       CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT.


