 4389                /       IMPLEMENTATION NOTES:
 4390   
 4391                /       1) THE USE OF THE CONSTANT 0700 IS ARBITRARY AS BIT[5] [WHICH SETS CONTINUOUS
 4392                /          MODE] IS IGNORED IN TC01/TC08 DECTAPE MOVE COMMANDS.  AS SUCH, 0600 WILL
 4393                /          ALSO WORK AND WOULD PERHAPS BE THE MORE LIKELY CHOICE; HOWEVER, OTHER
 4394                /          CONSIDERATIONS MAY HAVE INFLUENCED THE DESIGN.  THE SPECIFIC USE OF CERTAIN
 4395                /          CONSTANTS AND TEMPORARIES IN THIS CODE COULD BE A CONSEQUENCE OF OTHER
 4396                /          ROUTINES IN THE KEYBOARD MONITOR CODE NOT SHOWN HERE.  [THE MAJORITY OF THE
 4397                /          CODE IS KNOWN TO BE OPTIMIZED FOR MINIMAL CODE SPACE; CONSIDERATIONS DOWN
 4398                /          TO THE BIT LEVEL HAVE BEEN MADE FOR MOST ROUTINES.]
 4399   
 4400                /       2) THE SPECIFIC LENGTH OF THE RESERVED SPACE FOR THE REWIND/UNLOAD ROUTINES
 4401                /          HAS LIKELY CHANGED SLIGHTLY DEPENDING UPON THE RELEASE.  THE SOURCE CODE
 4402                /          AUTOMATICALLY ADAPTS AS NECESSARY AS LONG AS THERE IS SUFFICIENT SPACE FOR
 4403                /          THE REST OF THE PROGRAM.  IT IS PRESUMED THAT "ZBLOCK 0" IS SUPPORTED AS
 4404                /          GENERATING NOTHING; IF THIS IS NOT THE CASE, THE SOURCE CODE NEEDS TO ADD A
 4405                /          CONDITIONAL LAYER TO PREVENT ANY UNEXPECTED PROBLEMS.  THE ZBLOCK DIRECTIVE
 4406                /          IS KNOWN TO BE DEFECTIVE IN THE STANDARD RELEASE OF PAL10 FOR THE TOPS-10
 4407                /          OPERATING SYSTEM; P?S/8 PAL AND OS/8 PAL8 CORRECTLY HANDLE THIS SITUATION.
 4408   
 4409                /       3) IN MOST RELEASES OF P?S/8, SYSTEM GENERATION LOADS THE KEYBOARD MONITOR IN
 4410                /          ITS INTENDED FIELD AND ADDRESS [00000-02777]; AS SUCH, THE CODE IS NOT
 4411                /          ACTUALLY RELOCATED PER SE.  FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 SOURCE MODULES COULD
 4412                /          REQUIRE RELOCATION OR OTHER MODIFICATIONS TO IMPLEMENT ADDITIONAL SYSTEM
 4413                /          GENERATION METHODS; AS SUCH, THE CODE INCLUDES PROPER RELOCATION STATEMENTS
 4414                /          SHOULD THE VALUES BE CHANGED.  [NOTE: THE CURRENT SYSTEM GENERATION METHOD
 4415                /          USES RELOCATED ADDRESSES FOR ALL OTHER SYSTEM COMPONENTS WITH THE EXCEPTION
 4416                /          OF THE IMAGE OF THE INITIAL TFS DIRECTORY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 88

 4418                /       APPENDICES.
 4419   
 4420                /       APPENDIX A.
 4421   
 4422                /       THE SHORTEST KNOWN MANUAL TC01/TC08 BOOTSTRAP.
 4423   
 4424                /       THE FOLLOWING PROCEDURE IS CLAIMED TO BE THE SHORTEST METHOD TO BOOTUP ANY
 4425                /       TCO1/TC08 DECTAPE SYSTEM [WITH THE EXCEPTION OF THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM].
 4426   
 4427                /       IT IS ADVISABLE TO WRITE-PROTECT THE TAPE DRIVE WHEN PERFORMING MANUAL
 4428                /       BOOTSTRAP PROCEDURES REGARDLESS OF OPERATING SYSTEM.  P?S/8, THE R-L MONITOR
 4429                /       SYSTEM, POLY BASIC, EDU-SYSTEM 30 AND PERHAPS SOME ADDITIONAL SYSTEMS DO NOT
 4430                /       WRITE ON THE SYSTEM DRIVE DURING SUCCESSFUL BOOTUP OPERATIONS.
 4431   
 4432                /       PRELIMINARY TAPE PLACEMENT.
 4433   
 4434                /       MOUNT THE SYSTEM DECTAPE ON DRIVE 0 WITH SOME PORTION OF THE REVERSE END ZONE
 4435                /       OVER THE TAPE HEAD. [SEE THE NOTE BELOW IF UNFAMILIAR WITH DECTAPE FORMAT
 4436                /       SPECIFICS.] THE DRIVE SHOULD BE WRITE-PROTECTED TO PREVENT UNEXPECTED WRITING
 4437                /       ISSUES; THIS OPERATION MUST BE PERFORMED PRIOR TO STEP 6) BELOW.
 4438   
 4439                /       BOOTSTRAP PROCEDURE.
 4440   
 4441                /       1) SET FRONT PANEL ADDRESS TO 07754.
 4442   
 4443                /       2) DEPOSIT 7577 TWICE. [NOTE: CONTENTS OF 07754 MUST BE AN ACCEPTABLE VALUE
 4444                /       FROM A WIDE RANGE THAT INCLUDES 7577; 07755 MUST BE SET TO 7577.]
 4445   
 4446                /       3) SET FRONT PANEL ADDRESS TO 07615.
 4447   
 4448                /       4) DEPOSIT 7604; 6766; 5217.
 4449   
 4450                /       5) SET FRONT PANEL ADDRESS TO 07615.
 4451   
 4452                /       6) SET SWITCHES TO 0220; PRESS START.
 4453   
 4454                /       THE SYSTEM WILL START UP AUTOMATICALLY; ADDITIONAL TAPE MOTION WILL OCCUR AS
 4455                /       REQUIRED, GENERALLY ACHIEVING A COMMAND PROMPT WITHIN THREE SECONDS [ASSUMING
 4456                /       P?S/8 IS BEING BOOTSTRAPPED WITH THE OPTIONAL SHELL DISABLED; BOOTUP TIMES FOR
 4457                /       OTHER SYSTEMS MAY VARY].
 4458   
 4459                /       SHOULD THE BOOTSTRAP OPERATION FAIL, THE ENTIRE PROCEDURE NEEDS TO BE REPEATED
 4460                /       AS MOST ERRORS DESTROY THE MANUALLY ENTERED VALUES.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 89

 4462                /       ******************************************************************************
 4463                /       * THE FOLLOWING STEPS ARE ONLY NEEDED IF THE FORMATTING AND POSITIONING OF   *
 4464                /       * DECTAPES IS NOT PROPERLY UNDERSTOOD.  THE TAPE MEDIA ARE INTENDED TO BE    *
 4465                /       * FORMATTED BY *BARELY* WRAPPING ONE TURN OF TAPE ON THE TAKE-UP REEL.  THE  *
 4466                /       * FORMATTING SOFTWARE WRITES OUT A LONG REVERSE END ZONE FROM THAT POSITION  *
 4467                /       * FORWARD.  WINDING ADDITIONAL TAPE ONLY SERVES TO WASTE TAPE AND CAUSE      *
 4468                /       * CONFUSION LOCATING THE REVERSE END ZONE.  IN EXTREME CASES THE FORMATTING  *
 4469                /       * PROGRAM COULD FAIL IF THE TAPE WINDS OFF OF THE SUPPLY REEL.               *
 4470                /       *                                                                            *
 4471                /       * THERE ARE ADDITIONAL P?S/8-SPECIFIC DETRIMENTAL EFFECTS CAUSED BY USING    *
 4472                /       * MISFORMATTED TAPES. [NOTE: OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS MAY HAVE ANALOGOUS      *
 4473                /       * FEATURES CAUSING EQUIVALENT PROBLEMS.]                                     *
 4474                /       *                                                                            *
 4475                /       * THE P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR AND BUILT-IN EDITOR SUPPORTS A PAIR OF DEVICE   *
 4476                /       * CONTROL OPERATIONS WHICH TAKE IMMEDIATE ACTION AS DETERMINED BY THE SYSTEM *
 4477                /       * DEVICE HANDLER.  ON TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS PRESSING CONTROL-R REWINDS THE       *
 4478                /       * SYSTEM TAPE WHILE ALSO ALLOWING ADDITIONAL COMMAND TYPING.  THIS ALLOWS    *
 4479                /       * FOR REWINDING THE TAPE AHEAD OF COMPLETING A COMMAND THAT WOULD START THE  *
 4480                /       * TAPE MOTION TYPICALLY TOWARDS THE BEGINNING OF THE TAPE.  PROPERLY USED    *
 4481                /       * THIS CAN SAVE NOTICEABLE TIME INITIATING EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS.         *
 4482                /       *                                                                            *
 4483                /       * ON TC01/TC08 SYSTEMS PRESSING CONTROL-T UNLOADS THE SYSTEM TAPE.  THE      *
 4484                /       * MACHINE HANGS WAITING FOR THE TAPE TO REWIND COMPLETELY AND FALL OFF THE   *
 4485                /       * TAKE-UP REEL.  IF THE DRIVE IS TYPE TU55, THE DRIVE BRAKES WILL LOCK THUS  *
 4486                /       * STOPPING THE TAPE MOTION.  KEYBOARD INPUT IS RE-ENABLED ONCE THE UNLOAD    *
 4487                /       * OPERATION COMPLETES.                                                       *
 4488                /       *                                                                            *
 4489                /       * IF THE DECTAPE IS MISFORMATTED THESE OPERATIONS COULD BECOME CONFUSED.     *
 4490                /       * THIS COULD LEAD TO INCORRECT TAPE POSITIONING AND/OR A STUMBLING EFFECT    *
 4491                /       * AS THE DRIVE ATTEMPTS TO NAVIGATE REGIONS OF THE TAPE NEVER INTENDED TO    *
 4492                /       * EXIST. [MANUAL INTERVENTION COULD BE REQUIRED TO REMEDY THIS.]             *
 4493                /       *                                                                            *
 4494                /       * IF THE COMPUTER OPERATOR HAS NO IDEA ABOUT THE ACTUAL TAPE FORMAT OR JUST  *
 4495                /       * WISHES TO EXERCISE EXTREME CAUTION, THE FOLLOWING STEPS CAN BE USED TO     *
 4496                /       * ENSURE A SUCCESSFUL BOOTUP [IF AT ALL POSSIBLE].  AFTER STEP 4) ABOVE      *
 4497                /       * INSERT THE FOLLOWING ADDITIONAL OPERATIONS BEFORE MOVING TO STEP 5) ABOVE. *
 4498                /       *                                                                            *
 4499                /       * I)   SET FRONT PANEL ADDRESS TO 07615.                                     *
 4500                /       *                                                                            *
 4501                /       * II)  SET SWITCHES TO 0200; PRESS START.  THE TAPE WILL WIND FORWARD THEN   *
 4502                /       * STOP EITHER WHEN THE MACHINE IS HALTED MANUALLY OR THE FORWARD END ZONE IS *
 4503                /       * REACHED.  IT IS NOT NECESSARY TO WAIT FOR THE TAPE TO STOP AT THE FORWARD  *
 4504                /       * END ZONE.  THE ONLY PURPOSE OF THIS STEP IS TO ENSURE THE TAPE IS          *
 4505                /       * POSITIONED WELL FORWARD OF THE REVERSE END ZONE.  THIS CAN ALSO BE         *
 4506                /       * ACCOMPLISHED BY WINDING THE TAPE MANUALLY ABOUT 50 FEET. [IF THE REVERSE   *
 4507                /       * END ZONE IS NOT LOCATED BEFORE THIS PORTION OF THE TAPE, THE ORIGINAL      *
 4508                /       * FORMATTING OPERATION COULD NOT HAVE SUCCEEDED.]                            *
 4509                /       *                                                                            *
 4510                /       * III) SET FRONT PANEL ADDRESS TO 07615.                                     *
 4511                /       *                                                                            *
 4512                /       * IV)  SET SWITCHES TO 0600; PRESS START.  THE TAPE WILL WIND BACKWARDS AND  *
 4513                /       * STOP WITHIN THE REVERSE END ZONE; THE TAPE IS NOW PROPERLY POSITIONED.     *
 4514                /       ******************************************************************************
 4515   
 4516                /       END OF APPENDIX A.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 90

 4518                /       APPENDIX B.
 4519   
 4520                /       DECTAPE AND LINCTAPE DETAILS.
 4521   
 4522                /       DECTAPE FORMAT IS PARTIALLY BASED ON THE OLDER LINCTAPE FORMAT WITH SEVERAL
 4523                /       DESIGN MODIFICATIONS:
 4524   
 4525                /       1) DECTAPE FORMAT INCLUDES EXTRA INTER-BLOCK ZONES BEFORE BLOCK 0000 TO ALLOW
 4526                /          STRAIGHTFORWARD SEARCH ALGORITHMS; SEARCHING IN REVERSE WILL CAUSE AN END
 4527                /          ZONE ERROR AND THEN A SUBSEQUENT TAPE TURNAROUND. THERE WILL BE SUFFICIENT
 4528                /          DECTAPE MEDIA PRESENT TO ALLOW BLOCK 0000 TO BE DETECTED WHILE COMING OUT
 4529                /          OF THE REVERSE END ZONE DESPITE THE TWO-BLOCK DELAY ASSOCIATED WITH
 4530                /          REVERSING THE TAPE DIRECTION.
 4531   
 4532                /          LINCTAPE CONTROLLERS CAN DIRECTLY LOCATE BLOCK 0000 BECAUSE PHANTOM
 4533                /          NEGATIVE BLOCKS ARE PLACED AFTER THE END ZONE; AUTOMATIC SEARCH OPERATIONS
 4534                /          REVERSE THE TAPE AS NECESSARY.  SUFFICIENT PHANTOM BLOCKS ARE PLACED TO
 4535                /          ENSURE PROPER TURNAROUND ON THE ORIGINAL LOW-PERFORMANCE LINC [AND LINC-8]
 4536                /          BELT-DRIVEN TAPE DRIVES.
 4537   
 4538                /       2) LINCTAPE CAN ONLY BE TRANSFERRED IN THE FORWARD DIRECTION; DECTAPE CAN ALSO
 4539                /          BE TRANSFERRED IN REVERSE; HOWEVER, THIS FEATURE IS NOT USED IN MOST PDP-8
 4540                /          OPERATING SYSTEMS DUE TO THE OVERHEAD ASSOCIATED WITH THAT ACCESS SCHEME;
 4541                /          MULTIPLE PASSES OF THE TAPE ARE REQUIRED TO TRAVERSE ALL BLOCKS.  [NOTE:
 4542                /          SUPPORT FOR DECTAPE IN MOST 16/18/36-BIT SYSTEMS IS ORGANIZED THIS WAY; THE
 4543                /          DEVICE IS TREATED AS A NON-SYSTEM PERIPHERAL STORAGE UNIT; PERFORMANCE IS
 4544                /          COMPROMISED TO GENERALLY AVOID ADDITIONAL SEARCHES CAUSED BY HEAVY SYSTEM
 4545                /          LOADING; TAPE COASTING IS AN EXPECTED PART OF THE OVERALL DESIGN.]  IN
 4546                /          P?S/8, THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM, OS/8 AND THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM, THIS
 4547                /          MECHANISM IS TOTALLY IGNORED OUTSIDE OF CUSTOM INTER-SYSTEM DATA CONVERSION
 4548                /          PROGRAMS.
 4549   
 4550                /       3) LINCTAPES USED ON THE CLASSIC LINC SYSTEM MUCH BE 256 WORDS PER BLOCK.
 4551                /          DESPITE THE SMALL SYSTEM MEMORY SIZE [ORIGINALLY 1K OF 12-BIT WORDS LATER
 4552                /          UPGRADED TO 2K], RELATIVELY UNWIELDY BUFFERS ARE REQUIRED TO ACCESS THE
 4553                /          LINCTAPE BLOCKS.  THIS WAS A DESIGN COMPROMISE BASED IN PART ON THE USE OF
 4554                /          THE SHORTER 150 FOOT TAPES; DECTAPES ARE NOMINALLY 260 FEET LONG; CERTAIN
 4555                /          SYSTEMS CAN TAKE ADVANTAGE OF SELECTED LONGER TAPES.
 4556   
 4557                /          LINC SOFTWARE DOES NOT USE DEVICE HANDLERS AS THE LINCTAPE IS ACCESSED BY
 4558                /          USING HARD-WIRED INSTRUCTIONS OF LINC PROCESSOR.  ONLY NINE BITS OF BLOCK
 4559                /          ADDRESSING CAN BE USED ON THE LINC.  THE PDP-12 EXPANDS ON THE ORIGINAL
 4560                /          LINC CAPABILITY ALLOWING OTHER FORMATS SUCH AS 128 12-BIT WORDS PER BLOCK;
 4561                /          THIS FORMAT IS USED BY P?S/8, POLY BASIC, OS/8 AND OTHER SYSTEMS.
 4562   
 4563                /          ON THE LINC THERE IS NO FORMAL DESCRIPTION OF AN INTERRUPT MECHANISM; ALL
 4564                /          TAPE OPERATIONS MUST COMPLETE BEFORE ANY CUSTOM INTERRUPT HARDWARE ADDED ON
 4565                /          TO THE BASIC MACHINE CAN BE RESPONDED TO.  [FEW LINC SYSTEMS EVER HAD ANY
 4566                /          INTERRUPT HARDWARE; NO STANDARDS EXIST ON EXACTLY HOW TO CREATE ANYTHING OF
 4567                /          CONSEQUENCE.]  [NOTE: THE LINC-8 IMPLEMENTATION OF LINCTAPE IS ACTUALY A
 4568                /          PDP-8 PERIPHERAL; LINC TAPE INSTRUCTIONS ARE TRAPPED AND EMULATED BY TRAP
 4569                /          SOFTWARE WHEN THE MACHINE IS OPERATED FOR THAT PURPOSE; ALTERNATE SOFTWARE
 4570                /          DOES NOT USE THIS MODE; THERE ARE VERSIONS OF P?S/8 AND OS/8 FOR THE LINC-8
 4571                /          DEPENDING ON AVAILABLE MEMORY AND/OR HARDWARE MODIFICATIONS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 91

 4573                /          ALL DECTAPES MUST BE FORMATTED AS MULTIPLES OF 18 BITS PER WORD; THE
 4574                /          PDP-8 STANDARD FORMAT IS 86 18-BIT [OR 129 12-BIT] WORDS. THE STANDARD
 4575                /          FORMAT FOR MOST OTHER DEC COMPUTERS IS 256 18-BIT WORDS [OR 384 12-BIT
 4576                /          WORDS.
 4577   
 4578                /          ALL VIABLE PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS, OTHER THAN THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM,
 4579                /          IGNORE THE 129TH WORD.  SINCE THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM REQUIRES THE 129TH
 4580                /          WORD AS AN INTEGRAL PART OF ALL DATA STRUCTURES, IT IS IMPOSSIBLE TO
 4581                /          IMPLEMENT THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM ON MOST PDP-8 PERIPHERALS; IT WAS
 4582                /          CONSIDERED OBSOLETE/WORTHLESS BEFORE THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM WAS CREATED AND
 4583                /          PLAYED NO ROLE IN THE DEVELOPMENT OF ANY OF THE OTHER PDP-8 SYSTEMS
 4584                /          MENTIONED IN THIS DOCUMENT.  WITHIN THE SOURCE CODE OF OS/8 IT IS
 4585                /          EXPLICITLY THE OBJECT OF RIDICULE.  AS THERE ARE NO EXPLETIVES IN THIS
 4586                /          DOCUMENT, THE READER IS REFERRED TO OTHER FILES FOR FURTHER INFORMATION,
 4587                /          SUCH AS THE SOURCE CODE FILE FOR THE OS/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
 4588   
 4589                /       4) LINCTAPES DO NOT SUPPORT BUFFERED TRANSFER FORMAT; THERE IS MERELY A
 4590                /          CONSIDERATION OF THE FINAL DATA WORD.  DECTAPE FORMAT INCLUDES AN
 4591                /          ADDITIONAL PRE-FINAL WORD.  SINCE DECTAPE CAN BE WRITTEN IN THE REVERSE
 4592                /          DIRECTION, THERE ARE PRE-FINAL, FINAL AND CHECKSUM WORDS PRESENT AT BOTH
 4593                /          ENDS OF EVERY DATA BLOCK.  SINCE THERE ARE LESS UNIQUE STATES A LINCTAPE
 4594                /          CAN POSSIBLY BE IN AT ANY POINT IN TIME, THE MARK WINDOW IS ONLY FOUR BITS
 4595                /          WIDE; DECTAPES USE A SIX BIT MARK WINDOW WHICH COMPORTS WELL WITH THE
 4596                /          18-BIT ORIENTATION AS DEFINED.  THE TC01/TC08 CONTROLLER IMPLEMENTS THE SIX
 4597                /          BIT MARK WINDOW FOR COMPLETE COMPATIBILITY.
 4598   
 4599                /       5) DECTAPES AND LINCTAPES SPIN IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS.  AT THE BIT LEVEL, THE
 4600                /          DATA FOR EACH FORMAT IS THE MIRROR IMAGE OF THE OTHER.  TO READ OR WRITE
 4601                /          DECTAPES ON LINCTAPE SYSTEMS REQUIRES THE ABILITY TO LOGICALLY INVERT THE
 4602                /          TIMING TRACK PULSES TO TRIGGER THE DATA BITS AT THE PROPER TIME PHASE.
 4603                /          HARDWARE TO ACCOMPLISH THIS IS A STANDARD OPTION FOR THE PDP-12 [TC12F]; ON
 4604                /          THE LINC-8, A COMPREHENSIVE SET OF SIMPLE MODIFICATIONS MUST BE MADE TO THE
 4605                /          COMPUTER BACKPLANE INVOLVING THE ADDITION OF A SMALL NUMBER OF MODULES.
 4606                /          [NOTE: CONTACT CHARLES LASNER FOR MORE INFORMATION ON ALL SUCH HARDWARE
 4607                /          MODIFICATIONS.]  TAPE CONVERSION REQUIRES SPECIAL SOFTWARE TO ACCESS THE
 4608                /          DECTAPES FOR READING OR WRITING; IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO FORMAT DECTAPES ON
 4609                /          THESE MACHINES.
 4610   
 4611                /       6) AS A RESULT OF THE SIMPLER FORMAT OF 128 WORD/BLOCK LINCTAPES [AS COMPARED
 4612                /          TO 129 WORD/BLOCK DECTAPES], THE OVERALL DATA STORAGE OF LINCTAPES IS
 4613                /          SOMEWHAT LARGER.  PREMIUM LENGTH LINCTAPES CAN OCCASIONALLY BE FOUND THAT
 4614                /          ARE CAPABLE OF 4000 128-WORD BLOCKS [0000-3777]
 4615   
 4616                /       7) THE SAME PREMIUM TAPES CAN BE FORMATTED AS 3300 129-WORD DECTAPE BLOCKS.
 4617                /          VARIOUS P?S/8 RELEASES INCLUDE A MODIFIED VERSION OF DEC'S TOG-8 TC01/TC08
 4618                /          DECTAPE FORMATTING PROGRAM THAT SUPPORT A COMMAND-LINE SWITCH TO ENABLE
 4619                /          USING THE PASSED =XXXX OPTION TO SET THE LENGTH OF THE DECTAPE.  THE USUAL
 4620                /          PROGRAM USAGE FOR A PREMIUM LENGTH TAPE WOULD BE AS FOLLOWS:
 4621   
 4622                /       .R DTFRMT /C=3300 [NOTE: THE DEFAULT IS 2702 BLOCKS.]
 4623   
 4624                /       [NOTE: IN SOME P?S/8 RELEASES THE PROGRAM IS KNOWN AS "DT4MAT".]
 4625   
 4626                /       END OF APPENDIX B.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 92

 4628                /       APPENDIX C.
 4629   
 4630                /       P?S/8 LOGICAL UNIT EXTENSIONS FOR VARIOUS SUPPORTED SYSTEM DEVICES.
 4631   
 4632                /       SINCE CERTAIN PDP-8 USERS ARE FAMILIAR WITH THE OS/8 OPERATING SYSTEM, IT IS
 4633                /       OFTEN USEFUL TO DRAW COMPARISONS BETWEEN OS/8 AND P?S/8, ESPECIALLY FOR THE
 4634                /       PURPOSE OF EXPLAINING LOGICAL UNIT EXTENSIONS.
 4635   
 4636                /       OS/8 DEVICE HANDLERS DO NOT SUPPORT THE CONCEPT OF A LOGICAL UNIT EXTENSION;
 4637                /       THE HANDLER ADDRESSES ONLY ONE UNIT OF STORAGE; IN QUITE A FEW CASES, A
 4638                /       SIMILAR BUT DIFFERENT HANDLER IS NEEDED TO ADDRESS ANOTHER UNIT OF STORAGE,
 4639                /       DESPITE THE FACT THAT BOTH LOGICAL UNITS ARE PART OF THE SAME HARDWARE DEVICE.
 4640                /       [NOTE: IN SOME INSTANCES THERE CAN BE AS MANY AS EIGHT RELATED LOGICAL UNITS
 4641                /       ON A PARTICULAR HARDWARE CONTROLLER SUCH AS THE TC12 LINCTAPE CONTROLLER OF
 4642                /       THE PDP-12, THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER OR THE LINCTAPE CONTROLLER OF THE
 4643                /       LINC-8.]
 4644   
 4645                /       THE LOGICAL UNIT CONCEPT ALLOWS THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER TO ADDRESS
 4646                /       LARGER OVERALL STORAGE SPACE THAN THE OS/8 EQUIVALENT.  THE OS/8 TC01/TC08
 4647                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CAN ONLY ADDRESS DRIVE 0; THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE
 4648                /       HANDLER ADDRESSES ALL EIGHT PHYSICAL DRIVES AS LOGICAL UNITS 0 THROUGH 7.  DUE
 4649                /       TO LOGICAL BLOCK SIZE CONSIDERATIONS, ONE OS/8 HANDLER CAN ADDRESS AS MUCH AS
 4650                /       TWICE THE STORAGE SPACE OF ONE P?S/8 LOGICAL UNIT; HOWEVER, THIS GENERALLY
 4651                /       MEANS THE ENTIRE STORAGE SUPPORTED BY THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 4652                /       AGGREGATES TO FOUR TIMES AS MUCH STORAGE.  [NOTE: THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS
 4653                /       USED FOR ALL STORAGE ACCESS IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM; NO ADDITIONAL HANDLERS
 4654                /       ARE EVER LOADED, AVOIDING THE OVERHEAD FOUND WIITHIN OS/8 WHICH MUST PERFORM
 4655                /       SEVERAL OPERATIONS MERELY TO PLACE A NON-SYSTEM HANDLER INTO MEMORY.]
 4656   
 4657                /       IN THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT, NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS WILL BE USED TO ACCESS ALL
 4658                /       STORAGE DEVICES [THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS ONLY USED INTERNALLY FOR SUCH
 4659                /       OPERATIONS AS FETCHING THE BINARY IMAGE OF THE NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS]; HOWEVER,
 4660                /       EACH NON-SYSTEM HANDLER IS CAPABLE OF ADDRESSING AS MUCH AS 16384 TIMES AS
 4661                /       MUCH STORAGE AS ONE OS/8 HANDLER.]
 4662   
 4663                /       LOGICAL UNIT CONSIDERATIONS ON LARGER DISK DEVICES.
 4664   
 4665                /       DEPENDING ON THE SIZE OF THE STORAGE DEVICE, LOGICAL UNITS MAY BE DETERMINED
 4666                /       BY A MIX OF PHYSICAL AND MAPPING/PARTITIONING CONSIDERATIONS.  THE LARGEST
 4667                /       DISKS MAY BE MAPPED INTO A SERIES OF PERHAPS EIGHT PARTITIONS, EACH A LOGICAL
 4668                /       UNIT IN THE RANGE OF 0 THROUGH 7, ALL RESIDENT ON THE SAME PHYSICAL DRIVE.
 4669   
 4670                /       EXAMPLES OF LOGICAL UNIT CONFIGURATION ON THE RK8E/RK05.
 4671   
 4672                /       THE RK8E/RK05 LOGICAL UNIT CONFIGURATION IS DEPENDENT ON BOTH DRIVE SELECT AND
 4673                /       PARTITIONING CONSIDERATIONS.  VARIOUS P?S/8 CONFIGURATIONS HAVE BEEN SUPPORTED
 4674                /       ON THE RK8E/RK05 DISK WITH DIFFERENT OBJECTIVES.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 93

 4676                /       THE EARLIEST CONFIGURATION PROVIDES SUPPORT FOR THE ENTIRETY OF UP TO TWO RK05
 4677                /       DRIVES DEFINING LOGICAL UNITS 0 THROUGH 7.  SINCE THE RK8E CAN BE BOOTSTRAPPED
 4678                /       TO ONE OF FOUR PHYSICAL DRIVES, THE MAPPING OF LOGICAL UNITS IS DETERMINED
 4679                /       DYNAMICALLY AS THE OPERATING SYSTEM LOADS.  IF THE SYSTEM BOOTS FROM RK05
 4680                /       DRIVE 0, EACH 1/4 OF THE DISK IS A LOGICAL UNIT IN THE RANGE OF 0 THROUGH 3;
 4681                /       LOGICAL UNITS 4 THROUGH 7 RESIDE ON RK05 DRIVE 1 [IF PRESENT].  THE SAME
 4682                /       CONFIGURATION APPLIES IF THE SYSTEM BOOTS FROM RK05 DRIVE 1.  IN THIS
 4683                /       INSTANCE, THE SYSTEM DEVICE CAN ACCESS THE ENTIRETY OF BOTH DRIVES AS LOGICAL
 4684                /       UNITS 0 THROUGH 7 [ASSUMING BOTH DRIVES ARE PRESENT].
 4685   
 4686                /       IF THE SYSTEM BOOTS FROM RK05 DRIVE 2 OR 3, ALL CONSIDERATIONS ARE THE SAME AS
 4687                /       ABOVE EXCEPT THAT THE SYSTEM HANDLER CAN ACCESS THE ENTIRETY OF DRIVES 2 AND 3
 4688                /       [ASSUMING BOTH DRIVES ARE AVAILABLE].
 4689   
 4690                /       IT SHOULD BE NOTED THAT EACH LOGICAL UNIT IS EXACTLY HALF THE SIZE OF THE
 4691                /       TYPICAL OS/8 SUPPORT FOR THE SAME DEVICE.  THE AGGREGATE OF ALL EIGHT LOGICAL
 4692                /       UNITS ADDRESSES FOUR TIMES AS MUCH AS THE OS/8 HANDLER.
 4693   
 4694                /       IN THIS CONFIGURATION, IT IS POSSIBLE TO WRITE AN EXTENSION TO THE STANDARD
 4695                /       RK8E BOOTSTRAP; THIS WOULD ALLOW P?S/8 TO BOOT TO ANY LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT 0
 4696                /       THROUGH 7 [BEYOND THE STANDARD BOOTSTRAP DEFINITION WHICH ONLY SUPPORTS
 4697                /       LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS 0 AND 4 SINCE THEY ARE THE ONLY LOGICAL UNITS AT THE
 4698                /       BEGINNING OF THE PHYSICAL PACK].  EACH COPY OF THE SYSTEM IS CAPABLE OF
 4699                /       DETERMINING THE RELATIVE LOGICAL UNIT [0 THROUGH 3] AS WELL AS WHETHER BOOTING
 4700                /       TO THE EVEN [0 OR 2] OR ODD 1 OR 3] PACK AS APPROPRIATE TO DYNAMICALLY CREATE
 4701                /       THE SYSTEM BOOTUP LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT.  [NOTE: CERTAIN VERSIONS OF THE P?S/8
 4702                /       TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SUPPORT AN EXTENSION TO THE TC01/TC08
 4703                /       BOOTSTRAP PROGRAM ALLOWING STARTING FROM ANY DRIVE UNIT 0 THROUGH 7.]
 4704   
 4705                /       THIS EARLIER CONFIGURATION WAS USED BEFORE THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE P?S/8
 4706                /       SHELL OVERLAY.  IN THE SHELL VARIANT, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CAN ACCESS UP
 4707                /       TO THE FIRST 4096 BLOCKS OF THE DEVICE [AND OFTEN WOULD BE CONFIGURED FOR
 4708                /       PERHAPS A SIGNIFICANTLY SMALLER FRACTION OF THE AVAILABLE STORAGE] WHILE
 4709                /       THE NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS ASSOCIATED WITH THE SHELL ADDRESS THE REST OF
 4710                /       THE DEVICE.
 4711   
 4712                /       AS SUCH, IN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT, THE RK8E SYSTEM CONFIGURATION WILL ADDRESS
 4713                /       ENTIRE DRIVES AS LOGICAL UNITS.  P?S/8 ADDRESSES STORAGE IN TERMS OF 128-WORD
 4714                /       LOGICAL BLOCKS; ON ANY RK05 PACK THERE ARE 31300 LOGICAL BLOCKS [00000-31277].
 4715                /       PRACTICAL SYSTEMS MAY RESERVE SOMEWHAT LESS THAN THE FIRST 200 BLOCKS TO THE
 4716                /       BASE OPERATING SYSTEM LEAVING THE REST OF THE DRIVE TO THE SHELL OVERLAY
 4717                /       ENVIRONMENT.  [NOTE: SYSTEMS THAT USE BOTH THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM AND THE
 4718                /       SHELL OVERLAY EXTENSIVELY WILL TEND TO BE CONFIGURED FOR ADDITIONAL STORAGE
 4719                /       AVAILABLE TO THE BASIC SYSTEM, AS MUCH AS BLOCKS 00000-07777, THE MAXIMUM
 4720                /       POSSIBLE FOR ANY SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.]
 4721   
 4722                /       ASSUMING THE BASIC SYSTEM IS CONFIGURED TO THE MINIMUM VALUE SUITABLE TO
 4723                /       MERELY SUPPORT THE SHELL OVERLAY, THE STORAGE SPACE AVAILABLE IN THE SHELL
 4724                /       ENVIRONMENT IS SLIGHTLY LESS THAN TWICE THE SPACE AVAILABLE TO ANY ONE OS/8
 4725                /       HANDLER.  [NOTE: ON A NON-BOOTING P?S/8 DEVICE MADE EXCLUSIVELY AVAILABLE TO
 4726                /       THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT, THE STORAGE SPACE IS EXACTLY TWICE THE SIZE OF ANY ONE
 4727                /       OS/8 HANDLER FOR THE RK05 AS EACH OS/8 HANDLER CAN ONLY ADDRESS HALF OF THE
 4728                /       STORAGE SPACE ON THE RK05 PACK.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 94

 4730                /       IN THIS NEWER CONFIGURATION, THE ENTIRE RK05 DRIVE 0 IS LOGICAL UNIT 0; THIS
 4731                /       ADDRESSING SCHEME IS USED TO ACCESS THE OTHER RK05 DRIVES 1 THROUGH 3 AS THE
 4732                /       SAME LOGICAL UNIT AS THE PACK NUMBER.  [NOTE: THERE IS AN OBSCURE VARIANT OF
 4733                /       THE RK8E CONTROLLER KNOWN AS THE RKS8E, WHICH SUPPORTS EIGHT RK05 DRIVES.  IN
 4734                /       THIS CONFIGURATION, ONE P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLER FOR RKS8E COULD ADDRESS ALL
 4735                /       EIGHT DRIVES.]  AS SUCH, THE SINGLE P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLER FOR RKS8E
 4736                /       ADDRESSES THE EQUIVALENT OF 16 OS/8 HANDLERS.  [NOTE: OS/8 CANNOT BE
 4737                /       CONFIGURED TO HAVE AS MANY AS 16 HANDLERS IN ANY ONE CONFIGURATION.  THE
 4738                /       UNWIELDY PROCESS OF SYSTEM RECONFIGURATION WOULD HAVE TO BE APPLIED OFTEN TO
 4739                /       WORK WITH THIS MUCH STORAGE IN OS/8 AS WELL AS HAVING TO USE TWICE AS MANY
 4740                /       DIRECTORIES.]
 4741   
 4742                /       [NOTE: THE RK05F FIXED DISK DRIVE IS ALSO SUPPORTED AS AN EVEN/ODD PAIR OF
 4743                /       DRIVES. NO SPECIAL SOFTWARE EXISTS TO TREAT THIS DEVICE AS OTHER THAN THE
 4744                /       EQUIVALENT OF TWO RK05/RK05J REMOVABLE DISKS.  HOWEVER, IF A P?S/8
 4745                /       CONFIGURATION CAN BE CREATED FOR A DRIVE PAIR SUCH AS THE RK05F, THE
 4746                /       CONFIGURATION COULD BE MODIFIED TO TREAT THE ENTIRE RK05F DRIVE AS ONE DEVICE
 4747                /       CONTAINING 62600 BLOCKS.  BY COMPARISON, OS/8 WOULD REQUIRE FOUR DIFFERENT
 4748                /       DEVICE HANDLERS, EACH ADDRESSING 1/4 OF THE RK05F PACK.]
 4749   
 4750                /       THERE ARE STILL LARGER DEVICES FOR THE PDP-8/A SUCH AS THE MDC8 SCSI
 4751                /       CONTROLLER FROM CESI.  A STANDARD CONFIGURATION USES ONE OR TWO SEAGATE
 4752                /       ST-4096 80 MB HARD DISKS EACH OF WHICH PROVIDE THE FORMATTED CAPACITY OF 64 MB
 4753                /       [WHERE A BYTE IS SIX BITS].  THE ENTIRE DRIVE CAPACITY OF 32 MWORDS [WHERE A
 4754                /       WORD IS 12-BITS] IS 32 TIMES THE MAXIMUM CAPACITY OF ANY ONE OS/8 HANDLER.
 4755   
 4756                /       P?S/8 COULD BE CONFIGURED TO ACCESS THE ENTIRE DRIVE AS ONE LOGICAL UNIT.
 4757                /       HOWEVER, IT WOULD BE PRUDENT TO CONSIDER SOMEWHAT SMALLER LOGICAL UNITS SUCH
 4758                /       AS SUCH AS 4.0 MWORDS [WHERE EACH LOGICAL UNIT ADDRESSES FOUR TIMES THE
 4759                /       MAXIMUM STORAGE ADDRESSED BY ANY ONE OS/8 HANDLER].  THIS WOULD ALLOW EACH
 4760                /       OF EIGHT LOGICAL UNITS OF ONE P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLER TO ACCESS THE ENTIRE
 4761                /       DISK.  [NOTE: THIS WOULD REQUIRE A SLIGHT REWORKING OF THE CURRENT P?S/8
 4762                /       SUPPORT FOR THE MDC8; THE CURRENT DEFINITION OF THE SYSTEM HANDLER MAPS THE
 4763                /       FIRST 32768 BLOCKS OF THE DISK AS THE STORAGE SPACE ADDRESSED BY THE SYSTEM
 4764                /       DEVICE HANDLER; THE MAPPING WOULD HAVE TO BE REWORKED TO ALLOW A PROPER OFFSET
 4765                /       BETWEEN LOGICAL UNITS.  THE MDC8 IS AN ELEGANT DEVICE, EASILY REPROGRAMMED TO
 4766                /       ACCOMMODATE THE ALTERNATE MAPPING FOR SUCH A CONFIGURATION.  SINCE THE
 4767                /       ORIGINAL CONFIGURATION ALLOWS BOTH P?S/8 AND OS/8 TO SHARE PORTIONS OF THE
 4768                /       DISK IN UNEQUAL PROPORTIONS, SOME HYBRID ARRANGEMENT CAN BE CONFIGURED TO
 4769                /       ALLOW BOTH OPERATING SYSTEMS TO ACCESS A RATIONAL PORTION OF THE AVAILABLE
 4770                /       STORAGE SPACE.
 4771   
 4772                /       SINCE THE MAXIMUM ADDRESSIBILITY OF A P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLER IS 16777216
 4773                /       LOGICAL BLOCKS, THE MDC8 ONLY ADDRESSES A SMALL FRACTION OF THEORETICALLY
 4774                /       POSSIBLE STORAGE CONFIGURATIONS.  NEWER DESIGNS COULD ADDRESS THIS MORE
 4775                /       REALISTICALLY IN THE FUTURE.
 4776   
 4777                /       END OF APPENDIX C.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 95

 4779                /       APPENDIX D.
 4780   
 4781                /       P?S/8 LOGICAL BLOCKSIZE CONSIDERATIONS.
 4782   
 4783                /       EXACTLY HOW EACH P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLER ADDRESSES THE UNDERLYING STORAGE DEVICE
 4784                /       IS A FUNCTION OF SEVERAL FACTORS DEPENDING ON HARDWARE PARTICULARS.  ON THE
 4785                /       TC01/TC08 CONTROLLER THE LOGICAL BLOCK DATA IS THE FIRST 128 WORDS OF EACH
 4786                /       PHYSICAL DECTAPE BLOCK; ALL BLOCKS ARE READ AND WRITTEN AS 128 DATA WORDS.
 4787                /       THE 129TH WORD IS IGNORED; ARBITRARY DATA IS WRITTEN INTO THE 129TH WORD AS A
 4788                /       HARDWARE REQUIREMENT OF THE DECTAPE CONTROLLER.  [NOTE: A FORMATTED DECTAPE
 4789                /       MUST CONTAIN INTEGER MULTIPLES OF 18-BIT WORDS; STANDARD PDP-8 DECTAPES ARE
 4790                /       FORMATTED AS 86 18-BIT WORDS OR 129 12-BIT WORDS.]  IN P?S/8 [AND MOST OTHER
 4791                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS FOR THE PDP-8] ALL BLOCKS ARE READ AND WRITTEN FORWARD.
 4792   
 4793                /       LOGICAL BLOCK NUMBERS PASSED TO THE P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLER TRANSLATE DIRECTLY
 4794                /       INTO PHYSICAL BLOCK NUMBERS ON THE DECTAPE.  THE OVERALL DESIGN OF P?S/8
 4795                /       DEPENDS ON THIS FOR EFFICIENT OPERATION, ESPECIALLY ON DEVICES SUCH AS
 4796                /       DECTAPES AND LINCTAPES.
 4797   
 4798                /       OS/8 IS A SYSTEM THAT USES A LOGICAL BLOCKSIZE OF 256 WORDS/LOGICAL BLOCK.
 4799                /       [NOTE: WITHIN OS/8 THERE IS THE NOTION OF A LOGICAL RECORD SINCE AT TIMES
 4800                /       MENTION IS MADE OF UNDERLYING BLOCKS, ESPECIALLY ON DECTAPE AND LINCTAPE
 4801                /       HARDWARE.  UNFORTUNATELY, SLOPPY HANDLING OF THIS ISSUE HAS LED TO MANY OS/8
 4802                /       USERS CONFUSING THE TERMS AS INTERCHANGEABLE, DESPITE THE ORIGINAL INTENT
 4803                /       CLEARLY BEING TO EQUATE THEM BY THE INHERENT 2:1 RATIO.  AT THIS POINT, IT IS
 4804                /       HARD TO EVEN NOTICE, AS VARIOUS OS/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS COUNT THE LOGICAL RECORDS
 4805                /       AND APPEND THE WORD "BLOCKS" INTO THE VARIOUS UTILITY LISTINGS.]
 4806   
 4807                /       THE THEORETICAL ADVANTAGE OF COUNTING LOGICAL RECORDS WHERE THE LOGICAL
 4808                /       BLOCKSIZE IS 256 WORDS COMES WHEN THE DEVICE IS LARGE ENOUGH THAT ADVANTAGE IS
 4809                /       TAKEN ON A DEVICE WHILE ADDRESSING MORE STORAGE SPACE.  [NOTE: THIS IS NOT THE
 4810                /       CASE ON DECTAPES AS THE CAPACITY IS FAR LESS THAN 4096 BLOCKS.]  FOR EXAMPLE,
 4811                /       USING THE P?S/8 ACCESS METHOD OF LOGICAL BLOCKS OF 128 WORDS, ONLY THE FIRST
 4812                /       4096 BLOCKS OF ONE HALF OF AN RK8E/RK05 PACK CAN BE ACESSED WHILE OS/8
 4813                /       ADDRESSES THE ENTIRE HALF OF THE PACK AS 3248 RECORDS [EQUIVALENT TO 6496
 4814                /       P?S/8 LOGICAL BLOCKS].  CERTAIN OS/8 HANDLERS CAN ADDRESS THE FULL RANGE OF
 4815                /       4096 LOGICAL RECORDS; IN THESE CASES, OS/8'S HANDLERS CAN ADDRESS EXACTLY
 4816                /       TWICE THE SPACE OF A P?S/8 HANDLER'S MAXIMUM CAPABILITY ON ANY INDIVIDUAL
 4817                /       LOGICAL UNIT.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 96

 4819                /       THIS ADVANTAGE IS COUPLED WITH SOME DISADVANTAGES:
 4820   
 4821                /       1) ALL OS/8 PROGRAMS MUST ALLOCATE MULTIPLES OF 256 WORDS FOR ANY FORM OF
 4822                /          READ/WRITE BUFFER.
 4823   
 4824                /       2) DUE TO THE ILL-DEFINED DEFINITION OF EXTENDED MEMORY, LOCATIONS 7600-7777
 4825                /          ARE UNAVAILABLE IN ANY EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD.  AS A RESULT, THE LARGEST
 4826                /          POSSIBLE BUFFER SIZE IN OS/8 IS 30 PAGES.
 4827   
 4828                /          BY COMPARISON, P?S/8 ALLOWS ANY NUMBER OF PAGES FROM ONE THROUGH 32.  EVEN
 4829                /          THOUGH OS/8 REQUIRES A MINIMUM OF 8K MEMORY [SOME SYSTEMS REQUIRE 12K
 4830                /          MINIMUM DEPENDING ON HARDWARE REQUIREMENTS], PROGRAMS CAN CLEARLY BECOME
 4831                /          COMPROMISED BY THESE AND OTHER CONSIDERATIONS THAT PUT CONSTRAINTS ON THE
 4832                /          USAGE OF MEMORY ASSOCIATED WITH OS/8.
 4833   
 4834                /       3) DESIGNING AN OS/8 HANDLER REQUIRES ADDRESSING EACH LOGICAL RECORD WITH THE
 4835                /          ABILITY TO READ ONLY THE FIRST HALF OF THE RECORD; NOT ALL DEVICES ARE
 4836                /          CAPABLE OF THIS.  [NOTE: OMNI-8 IS A PDP-8-BASED TIME-SHARING SYSTEM THAT,
 4837                /          AMONG OTHER THINGS, EMULATES OS/8 IN A VIRTUALIZED ENVIRONMENT WITH THE
 4838                /          USE OF "ERSATZ" [TRAP EMULATED] HANDLERS; HOWEVER, THE USUAL STORAGE
 4839                /          DEVICE FOR OMNI-8 IS THE CESI MDC8 SCSI CONTROLLER CONFIGURED FOR THE MOST
 4840                /          COMMON HARD DISK BLOCKSIZE; THIS REQUIRES THE MINIMUM READ TO BE THE ENTIRE
 4841                /          LOGICAL RECORD.  AS SUCH, OS/8 CANNOT RUN ON THIS HARDWARE; OMNI-8 IS A
 4842                /          STAND-ALONE BOOTABLE PROGRAM; IT DOES NOT SHARE OS/8'S REQUIREMENT OF READS
 4843                /          LIMITED TO THE FIRST HALF OF A LOGICAL RECORD.  P?S/8 CAN RUN ON THIS
 4844                /          HARDWARE AS LONG AS 8K OF MEMORY [OR MORE] IS AVAILABLE TO ALLOW AN
 4845                /          EXTENDED SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; SEVERAL P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATIONS CAN LOGICALLY
 4846                /          ADDRESS 256 WORD RECORDS TO THE NEAREST 128 WORDS, INCLUDING RETAINING THE
 4847                /          SECOND HALF OF THE PHYSICAL RECORD, A FUNCTION NOT REQUIRED WITHIN OS/8.]
 4848   
 4849                /       CLEARLY, THAT OS/8 HAS TO CONFORM TO THE NOTION OF READING AND WRITING ANY
 4850                /       NUMBER OF PDP-8 MEMORY PAGES TO THE STORAGE DEVICE IS THE ROOT CAUSE OF WHY
 4851                /       THE NOTION OF LOGICAL RECORDS TWICE THAT SIZE CAN EASILY COME INTO CONFLICT
 4852                /       WITH THE HARDWARE UNLESS ACCOMMODATIONS ARE MADE FOR OS/8.  [NOTE: IT IS
 4853                /       RUMORED THAT RICHARD LARY HAD TO "BEG" THE PDP-8 HARDWARE GROUP TO MODIFY THE
 4854                /       RK8E CONTROLLER TO ALLOW THE IMPLEMENTATION OF A "FIRST HALF" READ/WRITE BIT
 4855                /       IN THE CONTROLLER DESIGN; CLEARLY, WITHOUT THIS HARDWARE HELP-OUT, OS/8 CANNOT
 4856                /       RUN ON THIS DEVICE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 97

 4858                /       SINCE OS/8 CANNOT RUN ON 4K MACHINES, P?S/8 IS THE ONLY VIABLE SYSTEM OF ITS
 4859                /       KIND FOR THESE MACHINES; ONE OF THE REASONS P?S/8 IS SUCCESSFUL IS THE USE OF
 4860                /       128 WORD BLOCKSIZE.
 4861   
 4862                /       WHEREVER POSSIBLE, P?S/8 SYSTEM COMPONENTS ATTEMPT TO DYNAMICALLY ALLOCATE
 4863                /       ANY AVAILABLE EXTENDED MEMORY.  [NOTE: P?S/8 CONFIGURATIONS HAVE MORE COMPLEX
 4864                /       CONVENTIONS REGARDING EXTENDED MEMORY; IN CERTAIN CIRCUMSTANCES, MORE MEMORY
 4865                /       MAY BE AVAILABLE, PERHAPS AS A SUBSTANTIAL FRACTION OF AN ADDITIONAL MEMORY
 4866                /       FIELD.  ALL AVAILABLE MEMORY IS USED CONSTRUCTIVELY THROUGHOUT THE SYSTEM.]
 4867   
 4868                /       IN MANY EXTENDED MEMORY SCENARIOS, THE AVAILABLE EXTENDED MEMORY IS USED TO
 4869                /       ALLOCATE LARGER MEMORY BUFFERS FOR READ/WRITE PURPOSES ON ONE OR MORE DEVICES
 4870                /       [OR LOGICAL UNITS].  SINCE THE BLOCKSIZE IS 128 WORDS/BLOCK, ANY WHOLE NUMBER
 4871                /       OF MEMORY PAGES CAN BE USED FOR THIS PURPOSE [IN THE RANGE OF 1 THROUGH 32
 4872                /       INCLUSIVE].
 4873   
 4874                /       WHILE THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM IS LIMITED TO AT MOST LOGICAL BLOCKS 0000-7777 ON
 4875                /       ANY LOGICAL UNIT THE EXTENDED DEFINITION OF P?S/8 USING THE SHELL OVERLAY
 4876                /       ALLOWS ACCESS TO AS MUCH AS 4096 TIMES THAT STORAGE; DOUBLE-PRECISION BLOCK
 4877                /       ARGUMENTS ARE PASSED TO THE VARIOUS NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS.  [NOTE: ALL P?S/8
 4878                /       SYSTEMS SUPPORT SOME NUMBER OF LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS, UP TO EIGHT LOGICAL
 4879                /       DEVICE UNITS PER HANDLER.  FOR MOST HARDWARE CONFIGURATIONS, THIS MEANS THE
 4880                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ACCESSES FOUR TIMES THE TOTAL STORAGE OF THE OS/8 SYSTEM
 4881                /       DEVICE HANDLER IN AGGREGATE.]
 4882   
 4883                /       IN PRACTICAL CONFIGURATIONS, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ADDRESSES THE FIRST
 4884                /       4096 LOGICAL BLOCKS ON THE DEVICE WHILE THE ASSOCIATED NON-SYSTEM HANDLER CAN
 4885                /       ADDRESS AS MUCH AS 4096 TIMES THAT SPACE.  [NOTE: THIS IS PER LOGICAL DEVICE
 4886                /       UNIT; MOST DEVICES ARE FAR SMALLER THAN THIS MAXIMUM.  SINCE THERE COULD BE AS
 4887                /       MANY AS EIGHT LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS, THE AMOUNT OF SPACE A SUITABLY CONFIGURED
 4888                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM CAN ADDRESS IS AS MUCH AS 16384 TIMES THE STORAGE SPACE OF AN
 4889                /       OS/8 HANDLER.]
 4890   
 4891                /       REGARDLESS OF HANDLER CONFIGURATION, ALL ACCESS TO ALL DEVICES IS MADE USING
 4892                /       LOGICAL BLOCKS OF 128 WORDS/BLOCK; ANY CALL TO A P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLER CAN
 4893                /       TRANSFER ONE THROUGH 32 BLOCKS PER CALL AS REQUIRED.
 4894   
 4895                /       END OF APPENDIX D.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 98

 4897                /       APPENDIX E.
 4898   
 4899                /       ADDITIONAL DECTAPE-RELATED PERFORMANCE CONSIDERATIONS.
 4900   
 4901                /       CONTIGUOUS BLOCK ARRANGEMENT CONSIDERATIONS.
 4902   
 4903                /       A SIGNIFICANT PERFORMANCE ADVANTAGE IS OBTAINED WHEN READING OR WRITING AS
 4904                /       MANY CONTIGUOUS BLOCKS AS POSSIBLE IN A SINGLE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 4905                /       SUBROUTINE CALL.  THIS WAS FIRST DEVELOPED IN THE EARLIER LIMITED-PURPOSE
 4906                /       SYSTEM KNOWN AS THE DECTAPE LIBRARY SYSTEM [WHICH CAN ONLY SAVE CORE IMAGES
 4907                /       AND LATER LOAD THEM INDIVIDUALLY INTO MEMORY].
 4908   
 4909                /       THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM USED THE SAME BASIC ACCESS TO DECTAPE PRESERVING THE
 4910                /       DYNAMICS OF TAPE TRANSFER TO AVOID EXCESS SEARCHES AND THE ATTENDANT OVERHEAD.
 4911                /       THIS DESIGN COMPLETELY CARRIES OVER INTO P?S/8; THE ONLY DIFFERENCES ARE:
 4912   
 4913                /       1) MUCH TIGHTER CODE AS A RESULT OF LITERALLY YEARS OF PROGRAM DEVELOPMENT
 4914                /          AIMED AT ACHIEVING THE SAME OUTCOME IN LESS TOTAL CODE SPACE.
 4915   
 4916                /       2) THE RELEASED CODE SPACE WAS USED TO IMPLEMENT ADDITIONAL FEATURES SUCH AS
 4917                /          LOGICAL UNIT SUPPORT AND ADDITIONAL INFORMATION PASSED TO SYSTEM PROGRAMS
 4918                /          RUN FROM THE SYSTEM LOADER; THE SYSTEM LOADER IS A DEDICATED CALL AND
 4919                /          STARTUP ROUTINE LOCATED WITHIN THE RESERVED MEMORY AREA [07632-07637] THAT
 4920                /          COEXISTS WITH THE SYSTEM HANDLER AND SYSTEM RESTART AT 07600.  THIS GREATLY
 4921                /          IMPROVES PERFORMANCE OVER THE R-L METHOD OF AN INTERMEDIARY DEDICATED
 4922                /          SYSTEM LOADING PROGRAM AND SYSTEM RESTART ROUTINE.
 4923   
 4924                /       3) ADDITIONAL INFORMATION AVAILABLE TO ALL PROGRAMS SUCH AS OPTION SWITCHES,
 4925                /          SYSTEM DATE AND MEMORY MANAGEMENT INFORMATION, SUPPORT FOR OUTPUT FILES
 4926                /          AND AN OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD.
 4927   
 4928                /       4) THE SUBROUTINE CALL WAS GREATLY MODIFIED TO BETTER HANDLE THE ADDITIONAL
 4929                /          FUNCTIONALITY.  [NOTE: THE R-L MONITOR DOES NOT SUPPORT MULTIPLE LOGICAL
 4930                /          DRIVE UNITS OR EXTENDED MEMORY CALLS OR EXTENDED MEMORY TRANSFERS; IT
 4931                /          REQUIRES TWO ENTRY POINTS, ONE FOR READING, THE OTHER FOR WRITING.]
 4932   
 4933                /       UNLIKE THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM ON DECTAPE, ALL BLOCKS ARE ACCESSED IN THE SAME
 4934                /       OPERATION FOLLOWING A SUCCESSFUL INITIAL FORWARD SEARCH TO THE FIRST PHYSICAL
 4935                /       BLOCK OF A GROUP; ANY ADDITIONAL BLOCKS TRANSFER IMMEDIATELY WITHOUT FURTHER
 4936                /       SEARCH OPERATIONS. [NOTE: THE DEVICE HANDLER CALL IS LIMITED TO A 32-BLOCK
 4937                /       MAXIMUM TRANSFER NOT ONLY BY SOFTWARE DESIGN BUT ALSO BY HARDWARE CONSTRAINTS
 4938                /       OF THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CONTROLLER AND THE PDP-8 3-CYCLE DATA BREAK [DMA]
 4939                /       MECHANISM.]  [NOTE: IN THE DISK MONITOR ENVIRONMENT, ARRANGING BLOCKS IN A
 4940                /       CONTIGUOUS MANNER IS ACTUALLY A DRAWBACK; EACH CALL IS LIMITED TO A SINGLE
 4941                /       BLOCK TRANSFER.  IF THE NEXT BLOCK NEEDED IS ADJACENT, IT IS NECESSARY TO
 4942                /       FIRST PERFORM A REVERSE SEARCH UNTIL THE DESIRED BLOCK IS HAS BEEN PASSED,
 4943                /       THEN TURN AROUND AGAIN TO TRANSFER THE BLOCK FORWARD.  MANY OF THE DISK
 4944                /       MONITOR STRUCTURES ARE ARRANGED IN THIS PESSIMISTIC WAY; ADDITIONALLY, THE
 4945                /       DEVICE HANDLER DOESN'T SUPPORT THE CONCEPT OF ATTEMPTING TO INITIALLY SEARCH
 4946                /       FORWARD EVEN WHEN ACTUALLY BENEFICIAL.  THE NET EFFECT IS THE FAMILIAR
 4947                /       "SHOESHINE" PATTERN OF TAPE MOTION THAT SEVERELY DEGRADES PERFORMANCE OF THIS
 4948                /       SYSTEM.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 99

 4950                /       SIMILAR IDEAS WERE APPLIED TO OS/8 [WHICH SUPPORTS EXTENDED MEMORY IN A
 4951                /       MANNER SIMILAR TO P?S/8]; HOWEVER, USING OS/8 DEVICE HANDLERS TO TRANSFER AN
 4952                /       ENTIRE MEMORY FIELD IS FROWNED UPON; WHAT MAY WORK ON A SPECIFIC SYSTEM
 4953                /       CONFIGURATION COULD CRASH ON A DIFFERENT CONFIGURATION.  THUS, THE GENERAL
 4954                /       RULE IS TO NOT USE THIS FEATURE; ALL GENERIC OS/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS CANNOT TAKE
 4955                /       ADVANTAGE OF THIS FORM OF SUBROUTINE CALL.  [NOTE: THIS CANNOT EVEN BE
 4956                /       ATTEMPTED ON MACHINES SMALLER THAN 12K MEMORY; NO MEMORY SIZE IS CONSIDERED
 4957                /       COMPLETELY SAFE TO ALLOW THE OPERATION OTHER THAN IN SPECIFIC CIRCUMSTANCES;
 4958                /       OS/8 PROGRAMS EXIST THAT WILL CRASH IF THIS IS ATTEMPTED.]
 4959   
 4960                /       [NOTE: THIS ISSUE IS A SORE POINT OF OS/8 AND REPRESENTS POOR SYSTEM DESIGN.
 4961                /       THE AVAILABILITY OF LOCATIONS X7600-X7777 WHERE X IS ANY PARTICULAR EXTENDED
 4962                /       MEMORY FIELD WAS NEVER PLANNED OUT; THIS MERELY EVOLVED ON A PRAGMATIC
 4963                /       [READ KLUDGE HERE] BASIS.]
 4964   
 4965                /       VIRTUALLY ALL PDP-8 SYSTEMS RESTRICT ACCESS TO FIELD 0 7600-7777; THIS IS A
 4966                /       CLEAR REQUIREMENT BORN OF THE ORIGINAL PAPER-TAPE LOADER SPECIFICATIONS AND
 4967                /       THE HARDWARE RESTRICTIONS OF THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK DEVICES SUCH AS THE DF32,
 4968                /       RF08, TC58 MAGNETIC TAPE CONTROLLER AS WELL AS THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE HARDWARE.
 4969                /       UNDER P?S/8, ALL EXTENDED MEMORY FIELDS ARE COMPLETELY AVAILABLE TO ALL
 4970                /       PROGRAMS.  [NOTE: THERE ARE NOTABLE EXCEPTIONS WHICH ARE INDICATED BY SYSTEM
 4971                /       STATUS BITS WITHIN THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  AS REQUIRED, VARIOUS
 4972                /       PORTIONS OF THE HIGHEST IMPLEMENTED MEMORY FIELD MAY BE RESERVED IF REQUIRED
 4973                /       BY THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  ADDITIONAL PORTIONS OF THE HIGHEST MEMORY
 4974                /       FIELD MAY BE IN USE BY PORTIONS OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY, AN IMPORTANT
 4975                /       FEATURE OF P?S/8 THAT HAS NO CONCEPTUAL EQUIVALENT IN OTHER PDP-8 OPERATING
 4976                /       SYSTEMS AND/OR PORTIONS OF THE OPTIONAL P?S/8 SHELL.  IF NONE OF THESE APPLY,
 4977                /       ALL EXTENDED MEMORY IS AVAILABLE TO ALL PROGRAMS.  CONFORMING PROGRAMS CAN
 4978                /       EASILY ADAPT TO THESE RESTRICTIONS BY INSPECTING THE SYSTEM STATUS WORD.]
 4979   
 4980                /       [NOTE: THE OVERALL MEMORY ALLOCATION SCHEME IS FOR LESS INTRUSIVE INTO
 4981                /       PROGRAM DESIGN THAN OS/8.  ASSUMING ENOUGH MEMORY IS AVAILABLE, CERTAIN
 4982                /       FEATURES OF THE PDP-8 INSTRUCTION SET CAN BE USED TO CREATE POWERFUL BUFFERING
 4983                /       AND RELATED STRUCTURES; CODING TECHNIQUES THAT USE AUTO-INDEX REGISTERS TO
 4984                /       FULL ADVANTAGE CAN BE USED WITHOUT RESORTING TO USING DEFENSIVE CODING
 4985                /       MEASURES TO RESERVE PORTIONS OF MEMORY FIELDS, SUCH AS IS REQUIRED UNDER OS/8.
 4986                /       THIS IS THE MAIN REASON WHY SUCH APPLICATIONS ARE NEVER WRITTEN FOR OS/8; THE
 4987                /       USE OF THESE TECHNIQUES IS ENCOURAGED IN P?S/8; SEVERAL SETS OF USER-WRITTEN
 4988                /       REAL-TIME DATA COLLECTION PROGRAMS HAVE BEEN WRITTEN FOR USE ONLY WITH P?S/8
 4989                /       THAT CANNOT BE RUN FROM OS/8 BY ANY POSSIBLE MEANS.]
 4990   
 4991                /       DECTAPE HANDLING ISSUES BEYOND THE SCOPE OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
 4992   
 4993                /       IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO WRITE A GENERAL SUBROUTINE FOR TC01/TC08 DECTAPE THAT
 4994                /       CAN TRANSFER ADJACENT BLOCKS ACROSS CALLS UNLESS THERE IS CONCEPTUAL SUPPORT
 4995                /       FOR MAINTAINING TAPE MOTION AFTER THE EARLIER TRANSFER.  THERE ARE REAL-TIME
 4996                /       CONSTRAINTS ON MAINTAINING TAPE MOTION AFTER THE COMPLETION OF A CALL; TIMING
 4997                /       ISSUES COULD PREVENT THE NEXT CALL FROM BEING SUCCESSFUL WITHOUT EXCESSIVE
 4998                /       TAPE TURNAROUND.  AN INTERRUPT-DRIVEN REAL-TIME SYSTEM COULD HAVE AN AUTOMATIC
 4999                /       SHUTDOWN TIMER TASK TO STOP TAPE MOTION IF THE WINDOW OF TIME IS EXCEEDED.
 5000                /       OTHERWISE, THE DESIGN SHOULD REDUCE TO THAT IMPLEMENTED IN P?S/8 WITHOUT THE
 5001                /       REQUIREMENT OF INTERRUPTS [PERHAPS AS A TASK PERFORMED ONLY IN THE
 5002                /       BACKGROUND].  ELABORATE SCHEMES FOR HANDLING DECTAPE GENERALLY WILL ADD MANY
 5003                /       LAYERS OF COMPLICATION WITH DUBIOUS PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENT.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 100

 5005                /       HISTORICALLY, NO ONE HAS BOTHERED TO IMPLEMENT SUCH DECTAPE HANDLING AT THE
 5006                /       OPERATING SYSTEM LEVEL BECAUSE IT IS TOO CRITICAL AND ERROR-PRONE.  [NOTE:
 5007                /       CHARLES LASNER HAS EXTENSIVE EXPERIENCE WRITING REAL-TIME DECTAPE PROGRAMMING
 5008                /       AT THE CUSTOM APPLICATION LEVEL.]  CERTAIN 18/36-BIT SYSTEMS USE A DECTAPE
 5009                /       LAYOUT THAT DOES NOT DEMAND ADJACENT BLOCK TRANSFER; THE BLOCKS THAT PASS OVER
 5010                /       THE HEADS DURING THE TIME THE DECTAPE IS SLOWING TO A COMPLETE STOP ARE NOT
 5011                /       INTENDED TO BE ACCESSED AT THAT POINT.  THE OVERALL LOGICAL DECTAPE STRUCTURE
 5012                /       IS INTERLEAVED TO AVOID PROBLEMS ASSOCIATED WITH COASTING.  HOWEVER, SUCH
 5013                /       SCHEMES REQUIRE THE ABILITY TO SPECIFY THE DIRECTION OF TRANSFER ON THE
 5014                /       DECTAPE TO BE OF MUCH USE.  AS SUCH, CERTAIN BLOCKS MUST BE WRITTEN IN THE
 5015                /       REVERSE DIRECTION.  ADDITIONALLY, THE BEST POSSIBLE HANDLING REQUIRES THREE
 5016                /       COMPLETE PASSES ACROSS THE DECTAPE TO ACCESS ALL BLOCKS IN A MAPPED ORDER.
 5017   
 5018                /       THE DEC PDP-8 STANDARD DECTAPE COPY PROGRAM USES THIS METHOD TO COPY DECTAPES
 5019                /       SUCCESSFULLY WITHOUT THE USE OF INTERRUPTS AND REQUIRES TWO DECTAPE DRIVES.
 5020                /       [NOTE: IF THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM ADAPTED PART OF THE TECHNIQUE USED IN THE
 5021                /       STANDARD DECTAPE COPY PROGRAM, AND ALSO COULD IMPLEMENT SMART INITIAL SEARCH
 5022                /       DIRECTION SUPPORT, IT COULD PERFORM SIGNIFICANTLY BETTER; HOWEVER, THIS WOULD
 5023                /       REQUIRE A TOTAL REDESIGN OF WHAT STORAGE BLOCKS ARE USED FOR WHICH PARTICULAR
 5024                /       SYSTEM RESOURCES.  THE NET EFFECT WOULD STILL BE A MEDIOCRE SYSTEM, BUT WOULD
 5025                /       BE MATERIALLY BETTER THAN THE ACTUAL RELEASED VERSION.  IT WOULD APPEAR THE
 5026                /       DECTAPE VERSION OF THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM IS AN EXPEDIENT DESIGN CREATED
 5027                /       AFTER THE DF32/RF08 VERSIONS WERE CREATED; ON THOSE CONFIGURATIONS, MOST OF
 5028                /       THESE CONSIDERATIONS ARE MOOT.  WHEN YOU OPTIMIZE A SYSTEM DESIGN FOR A
 5029                /       HEAD PER TRACK DISK STORAGE UNIT, RUNNING IT ON OTHER HARDWARE CAN EXHIBIT
 5030                /       DRAMATICALLY POORER PERFORMANCE.]
 5031   
 5032                /       IN THEORY, AN INTERRUPT-DRIVEN DECTAPE SUBSYSTEM CAN BE DEVELOPED WITH
 5033                /       PRESUMPTIVE BUFFERING TECHNIQUES.  ADJACENT BLOCKS CAN BE READ IN REAL TIME
 5034                /       WHILE MAINTAINING DECTAPE MOTION UP TO THE LIMITS OF DEFINED BUFFERS.  WHEN
 5035                /       THE PROJECTED BLOCKS BECOME OBSOLETE [SUCH AS WHEN AN UNRELATED BLOCK READ IS
 5036                /       REQUESTED], THE BUFFERED BLOCKS' CONTENTS ARE ABANDONED; A NEW SET OF
 5037                /       ANTICIPATORY READS MUST BE INITIATED.  THE OVERHEAD TO CREATE SUCH A SYSTEM
 5038                /       WOULD TIE UP FAR TOO MANY MEMORY BUFFERS TO BE PRACTICAL FOR ANY REASONABLE
 5039                /       FORM OF OPERATING SYSTEM, BUT PERHAPS SERVES SOME THEORETICAL DEDICATED
 5040                /       APPLICATION PROGRAM. [NOTE: OBSOLETE VERSIONS OF THE COS-300 SYSTEM HAVE
 5041                /       LIMITED SUPPORT FOR TC01/TC08 DECTAPE USING SOME OF THESE TECHNIQUES;
 5042                /       REQUIREMENTS INCLUDE THE PRESENCE OF 32K OF MEMORY AND LIMITING EXECUTION TO
 5043                /       OTHERWISE NOT ALLOWING ACCESS TO UPPER EXTENDED MEMORY FOR ANY OTHER PURPOSE;
 5044                /       THIS WAS EVENTUALLY SCRAPPED IN FAVOR OF MORE GENERAL FEATURES WITHIN THIS 
 5045                /       SPECIAL-PURPOSE OPERATING SYSTEM.]
 5046   
 5047                /       SYSTEMS SUCH AS THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM, OS/8 AND P?S/8 DO NOT USE AN ELABORATE
 5048                /       DECTAPE LAYOUT SCHEME, AS THE TRANSFERS ARE HARDWARE-LIMITED TO AT MOST 32
 5049                /       BLOCKS AT A TIME.  AS SUCH, IT IS A FAR BETTER COMPROMISE TO MERELY TRANSFER
 5050                /       ALL DATA FORWARD WITH LINEAR ACCESS TO THE DECTAPE BLOCKS.  EVEN WITH
 5051                /       OCCASIONAL DECTAPE SEARCHES STARTED IN REVERSE, THE TOTAL TIME TO TRANSFER AN
 5052                /       ENTIRE DECTAPE IS ACTUALLY LESS USING AN ADJACENT BLOCK SCHEME SUCH AS THE
 5053                /       METHOD DESCRIBED ABOVE. REGARDING THE DEC STANDARD DECTAPE COPY PROGRAM.
 5054                /       [NOTE: THE DECTAPE COPY PROGRAM TRANSFERS BOTH 18-BIT AND 12-BIT ORIENTED
 5055                /       DECTAPES AND RUNS ENTIRELY IN 4K.  LARGER TRANSFERS TO REALIZE THE IMPROVEMENT
 5056                /       DESCRIBED HERE REQUIRE EXTENDED MEMORY TO ALLOW SUFFICIENTLY LARGE BUFFERS TO
 5057                /       MINIMIZE OVERALL TRANSFER TIME.]
 5058   
 5059                /       END OF APPENDIX E.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 101

 5061                /       APPENDIX F.
 5062   
 5063                /       P?S/8 ERROR HANDLING SPECIFICS.
 5064   
 5065                /       WHILE THIS IS A GENERIC ISSUE FOR ALL P?S/8 SUPPORTED DEVICES, THE TC01/TC08
 5066                /       DECTAPE CONTROLLER WILL BE USED AS AN EXAMPLE.
 5067   
 5068                /       IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM [THAT CAN RUN IN 4K OF MEMORY], DETECTED ERRORS ARE
 5069                /       EITHER HANDLED AUTOMATICALLY OR CAUSE AN INTERNAL HALT WITHIN THE HANDLER.
 5070                /       END ZONE ERRORS ARE GENERALLY HANDLED BY REVERSING THE DIRECTION OF TAPE
 5071                /       MOTION; THIS ONLY HAPPENS OCCASIONALLY WHEN THE DECTAPE IS LIKELY INITIALLY
 5072                /       MOUNTED BY THE USER.  ON THE TC01/TC08 CONTROLLER, TRANSFERS FROM BLOCKS LOWER
 5073                /       THAN BLOCK 0002 CAN ONLY BE ACCOMPLISHED BY DRIVING THE TAPE INTO THE REVERSE
 5074                /       END ZONE, THEN TURNING THE TAPE AROUND TO LOCATE THE DESIRED BLOCK WHILE THE
 5075                /       TAPE IS MOVING FORWARD.
 5076   
 5077                /       ALL OTHER ERRORS WILL CAUSE THE INTERNAL HALT.  IN THE CASE OF THE TC01/TC08
 5078                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, THE CONTENTS OF THE ACCUMULATOR WILL CORRESPOND TO THE
 5079                /       DECTAPE CONTROLLER STATUS REGISTER "B" CONTENTS.  PRESSING "CONTINUE" ON THE
 5080                /       CPU FRONT PANEL WILL RETRY THE LATEST OPERATION.  ASSUMING THE CONDITIONS
 5081                /       CAUSING THE PROBLEM WERE CORRECTED BY THE OPERATOR, THE DEVICE HANDLER CALL
 5082                /       WILL THEN GENERALLY SUCCEED.  CONTINUED OCCURRENCE OF ERRORS INDICATES ONE OF
 5083                /       SEVERAL POSSIBLE HARDWARE ERRORS INVOLVING EITHER THE DECTAPE MEDIA OR A
 5084                /       DEGRADED CONDITION OF THE DRIVE TAPE GUIDES.  THIS SITUATION MUST BE REMEDIED
 5085                /       QUICKLY AS THE UNDERLYING CONDITION CAN EASILY BECOME CHRONIC POSSIBLY LEADING
 5086                /       TO DATA LOSS AND PERHAPS DESTRUCTION OF THE DECTAPE MEDIA.  [NOTE:  IN THE
 5087                /       EARLY STAGES OF MOST OF THESE PROBLEMS, THERE ARE PERIODIC OCCURRENCES OF
 5088                /       EITHER MARK OR TIMING ERRORS; RETRY OPERATIONS EVENTUALLY SUCCEED AFTER ENOUGH
 5089                /       RETRIES TO MOVE THE TAPE TO THE TARGET BLOCK.  AS THE HARDWARE FAILURES GET
 5090                /       PROGRESSIVELY WORSE, THE RATE OF ERRORS CAN INCREASE DRASTICALLY.  BY APPYING
 5091                /       MAINTENANCE PROCEDURES BEFORE THIS HAPPENS, THE PROBLEM CAN BE CORRECTED MORE
 5092                /       EASILY.]
 5093   
 5094                /       WARNING ABOUT USING OS/8 ON DAMAGED DECTAPE HARDWARE.
 5095   
 5096                /       OS/8 DECTAPE HANDLERS USE A DEFECTIVE ALGORITHM TO HANDLE DECTAPES THAT IS
 5097                /       QUITE DANGEROUS TO USE ON MARGINAL DRIVES.  DURING THE SEARCH PHASE, ALL
 5098                /       ERRORS [OTHER THAN END ERROR WHICH TURNS THE TAPE AROUND] ARE IGNORED; THERE
 5099                /       IS ONLY A SMALL OPPORTUNITY FOR TIMING AND MARK ERRORS TO BE DETECTED, ONLY
 5100                /       DURING THE TIME A DATA TRANSFER IS IN PROGRESS.  AS SUCH, IT IS EASY FOR A
 5101                /       MARGINAL SITUATION TO BECOME A CHRONIC ONE.  TYPICALLY, IF OS/8 IS USED
 5102                /       EXCLUSIVELY, THE SITUATION IS ONLY NOTICED WHEN IT IS LIKELY TOO LATE TO
 5103                /       RECOVER DATA AND PERHAPS IMMINENT HARDWARE FAILURE IS POSSIBLE.  USING P?S/8
 5104                /       WILL REVEAL THE PROBLEM AT AN EARLIER STAGE OF FAILURE POTENTIALLY SAVING
 5105                /       BOTH THE DATA AND PERHAPS THE DECTAPE MEDIA [AND DRIVE GUIDE HARDWARE] ITSELF.
 5106   
 5107                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY CONSIDERATIONS.
 5108   
 5109                /       WHEN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS ENABLED, ALL ERRORS ARE INTERCEPTED AND
 5110                /       HANDLED WITHIN THE OVERLAY.  INFORMATIVE ERROR MESSAGES ARE ISSUED ON THE
 5111                /       CONSOLE DEVICE ALLOWING THE USER TO DECIDE WHAT TO DO NEXT FROM A LIST OF
 5112                /       OPERATIONAL CHOICES [RETRY, ABORT AND EXIT TO 07600, ETC.].  THE SYSTEM WILL
 5113                /       NOT HALT UNDER ANY CIRCUMSTANCES.  [NOTE: THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERALY IS ONLY
 5114                /       AVAILABLE IN CERTAIN RELEASES OF P?S/8; EXTENDED MEMORY IS ALWAYS REQUIRED.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 102

 5116                /       [NOTE: THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY ALSO INTERACTS WITH CERTAIN PORTIONS OF THE
 5117                /       HANDLER TO IMPROVE PERFORMANCE; THIS MECHANISM IS DEVICE-DEPENDENT AND MAY
 5118                /       ONLY BE PRESENT FOR SPECIFIC BOOTABLE DEVICES.  THE TC01/TC08 HANDLER CODE
 5119                /       ELSEWHERE WITHIN THIS DOCUMENT CONTAINS SPECIFIC REFERENCES TO THIS REGARDING
 5120                /       IMPROVEMENT TO THE OVERALL SEARCH PERFORMANCE OF ALL DECTAPE DRIVES.  FUTURE
 5121                /       RELEASES OF THIS DOCUMENT WILL INCLUDE AN EXPLICIT SECTION COVERING THE
 5122                /       INTERCEPTING CODE DESIGNATED FOR COMMUNICATIONS BETWEEN THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 5123                /       HANDLER AND THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY; AS OF THIS WRITING, THE CODE
 5124                /       DESCRIBED IN THIS DOCUMENT PREDATES VERSIONS OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY
 5125                /       SUPPORTING THIS FEATURE.]
 5126   
 5127                /       OS/8 AND P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ERROR CONSIDERATIONS.
 5128   
 5129                /       ALL OS/8 PROGRAMS CALLING THE OS/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ARE REQUIRED TO
 5130                /       PROVIDE A ROUTINE TO HANDLE ERROR RETURNS REGARDLESS OF THE NATURE OF THE
 5131                /       CALLER; THIS INCLUDES CALLS MADE TO THE HANDLER FROM WITHIN OS/8 ITSELF.
 5132                /       WHILE PROGRAMS CALLING THE HANDLER MAY HAVE VARYING DEGREES OF ERROR RECOVERY,
 5133                /       IT IS ALWAYS NECESSARY TO HAVE A VIABLE STRATEGY; HOWEVER, THE QUALITY OF
 5134                /       IMPLEMENTATION VARIES GREATLY; TYPICAL OS/8 PROGRAMS PROVIDE GENERIC MESSAGES
 5135                /       FOR HANDLER AND OTHER PROBLEMS THAT GENERALLY INVOLVE FAILURE OF THE LATEST
 5136                /       OPERATION WITHOUT RECOURSE; THE OVERALL OPERATION MUST BE RESTARTED.
 5137   
 5138                /       THERE IS A SERIOUS DESIGN FLAW WITHIN OS/8 WHEREIN A CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 5139                /       HANDLER IS MADE TO LOAD THE LAST SEGMENT OF MEMORY WHEN STARTING ANY PROGRAM
 5140                /       UP.  SINCE THERE IS NO CODE SPACE AVAILABLE, THE ERROR RETURN CONSISTS MERELY
 5141                /       OF A "HLT" INSTRUCTION; AS SUCH, THE SITUATION IS UNRECOVERABLE.  WORSE STILL,
 5142                /       PRESSING "CONTINUE" ON THE CPU FRONT PANEL COULD POTENTIALLY HARM THE SYSTEM
 5143                /       AS THE INTENDED PROGRAM SEGMENT IS NOT LOADED; THE UNINTENDED PRIOR CONTENTS
 5144                /       OF MEMORY WILL BE EXECUTED WITH UNKNOWN CONSEQUENCES.  [NOTE: IN RECENT MEMORY
 5145                /       ALONE, SYSTEM DESTRUCTION HAS BEEN REPORTED WHEN THIS SCENARIO WAS ATTEMPTED
 5146                /       IN AT LEAST TWO SPECIFIC INSTANCES.  FOR MORE INFORMATION ON THIS PROBLEM, SEE
 5147                /       THE SECTION OF THIS DOCUMENT REGARDING IMAGE PROGRAM LOADING AND THE P?S/8
 5148                /       SYSTEM LOADER.]
 5149   
 5150                /       UNLIKE OS/8, IN THE EVENT OF AN ERROR, P?S/8 PROVIDES AN EXPECTATION OF
 5151                /       SUCCESSFUL RECOVERY FROM THE PROBLEM.  CALLING PROGRAMS DO NOT HAVE TO PROVIDE
 5152                /       ERROR RECOVERY ROUTINES; ALL ERRORS ARE HANDLED INTERNALLY BASED ON THE
 5153                /       PARTICULARS OF THE ERROR CONDITION; THE CALLER PLAYS NO DIRECT ROLE IN THE
 5154                /       RECOVERY OPERATION.
 5155   
 5156                /       IN CERTAIN INSTANCES, THIS ERROR RECOVERY METHOD HAS ALLOWED MARGINAL DATA
 5157                /       TO BE SUCCESSFULLY READ BEFORE THE MEDIA BECAME TOTALLY UNREADABLE.  IN ONE
 5158                /       PARTICULAR INSTANCE, P?S/8,  BY PROVIDING PROPER ERROR RECOVERY, SOLVED A DATA
 5159                /       RECOVERY ISSUE WHILE OS/8 ONLY AGGRAVATED THE SITUATION.  [NOTE: WHILE THE
 5160                /       ORGANIZATION OF P?S/8 AND OS/8 IS QUITE DIFFERENT ON THE RK8E/RK05, THE
 5161                /       SUPERIOR P?S/8 ERROR RECOVERY ALLOWED USER DATA TO BE RECOVERED WHERE OS/8
 5162                /       COULD NOT EVEN AFTER EXTENSIVE RETRY ATTEMPTS.  COMPATIBLE P?S/8 GENERIC
 5163                /       BLOCK-ORIENTED UTILITIES WERE USED TO COPY ALL AFFECTED DISK SECTORS TO
 5164                /       ALTERNATE MEDIA RESULTING IN A COMPLETE DATA RECOVERY.]
 5165   
 5166                /       END OF APPENDIX F.


