 5168                /       APPENDIX G.
 5169   
 5170                /       P?S/8 CODE OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES.
 5171   
 5172                /       THE ORIGINAL GODS OF PDP-8 SOFTWARE, THE WILLIAM CLAUDE FIELDS MEMORIAL
 5173                /       PROGRAMMING GROUP [WCFMPG] ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR MANY ASPECTS OF PDP-8
 5174                /       PROGRAMMING CONCEPTS [AS WELL AS OTHER INDUSTRY-WIDE LEGENDS].  ONE OF THEIR
 5175                /       IMPORTANT LEGACIES CONCERNS THE "MATHEMATICS" BEHIND WRITING TIGHT PDP-8 CODE.
 5176                /       [NOTE: THE P?S/8 TC01/TC08 DECTAPE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS THE QUINTESSENTIAL
 5177                /       EXAMPLE ADVANCING THEIR PRINCIPLES; THIS CODE HAS BEEN WORKED ON FOR DECADES
 5178                /       AS FIRST THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND RELATED CODE, THEN AS THE
 5179                /       P?S/8 DESCENDANT.  IN THE PROCESS, MANY OPTIMIZATIONS HAVE BEEN PERFORMED ON
 5180                /       THIS PORTION OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM.  FEATURES HAVE BEEN ADDED SOLELY BASED
 5181                /       ON THE ABILITY TO FIND SUFFICIENT CODE SPACE IN THE HANDLER TO IMPLEMENT THE
 5182                /       RESIDENT PORTIONS.  SOMETIMES THIS COMES DOWN TO CREATING FREE BITS IN A
 5183                /       SINGLE WORD.  [NOTE: IT IS BEYOND THE SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT TO EXPLAIN THE
 5184                /       CONSEQUENCES OF THE OPTIMIZATION ALREADY INHERENT IN THE CODE AS PRESENTED
 5185                /       HERE.  FURTHER, THIS IS NOT LIKELY THE FINAL VERSION OF THE CODE AS AT LEAST
 5186                /       ANOTHER DECADE OF SERIOUS DEVELOPMENT EFFORT WAS EXPENDED AFTER THIS VERSION
 5187                /       OF THE CODE WAS WRITTEN.  FUTURE VERSIONS OF THIS FILE WILL UPDATE THE CODE
 5188                /       TO SHOW A PORTION OF THE PROGRESSION TOWARDS THE ELUSIVE "PERFECTION" NEEDED
 5189                /       TO EXPAND P?S/8 WITH ADDITIONAL FEATURES.  AS OF THIS WRITING, SEVERAL ARE IN
 5190                /       THE PLANNING STAGES PRESUMING SUFFICIENT SPACE WILL BECOME AVAILABLE].
 5191   
 5192                /       SOME OF THE SPECIFIC CONCEPTS DISCUSSED HERE CAN BE PROPERLY EXPLAINED BY
 5193                /       APPLYING THE WORK OF JACK BURNESS AND LENNY ELEKMAN.  ELEKMAN'S THEOREM STATES
 5194                /       THAT, IN THEORY, ANY PROGRAM CAN ALWAYS BE FURTHER OPTIMIZED; THE DIFFICULTY
 5195                /       OF IMPLEMENTATION IS PROPORTIONAL TO THE AMOUNT OF EFFORT ALREADY EXPENDED TO
 5196                /       GET TO THE CURRENT PROGRAM REVISION.  BURNESS' COROLLARY STATES THAT THE TIME
 5197                /       TO INTRODUCE FURTHER CODE OPTIMIZATION IS PROPORTIONAL TO THE AMOUNT OF TIME
 5198                /       AND EFFORT ALREADY EXPENDED TO GET TO THE CURRENT OPTIMIZATION STATE.  IN THE
 5199                /       CASE OF THIS DEVICE HANDLER, THESE PRINCIPLES APPEAR TO BE QUITE ACCURATE IN
 5200                /       PREDICTING THE COURSE OF PROGRAM DEVELOPMENT; SOME OF THE OPTIMIZATIONS
 5201                /       DESCRIBED BELOW DEVELOPED OVER A PERIOD OF MANY YEARS.
 5202   
 5203                /       THE PDP-8 IS A NEARLY UNIQUE MACHINE IN THE SENSE THAT CODE CAN BE WRITTEN TO
 5204                /       OCCUPY LESS SPACE BY APPLYING SOME KNOWN TECHNIQUES COUPLED WITH WHAT CAN ONLY
 5205                /       BE REFERRED TO AS MACHINE-SPECIFIC BRAINSTORMING.  WHAT IS TRULY REMARKABLE IS
 5206                /       AS VARIOUS TALENTED INDIVIDUALS LOOKED AT THE CODE CONSIDERED BY OTHERS TO BE
 5207                /       "IMPOSSIBLE TO MAKE SMALLER", SOME OF THESE PEOPLE CAME UP WITH ADDITIONAL
 5208                /       TECHNIQUES TO ACTUALLY ACHIEVE AN OVERALL CODE SPACE SIZE IMPROVEMENT.
 5209   
 5210                /       THE OPTIMIZATION PROCESS IS OFTEN REGENERATIVE; THE LATEST IMPROVEMENT SPAWNS
 5211                /       A SERIES OF REACTIONS THAT IN TURN MIGHT FURTHER OPTIMIZE OTHER CODE SECTIONS.
 5212                /       WHAT WAS ONCE CONSIDERED "IMPOSSIBLE" TURNS INTO THE THEORETICALLY POSSIBLE.
 5213                /       AT LEAST 12 INDIVIDUALS HAVE STUDIED THIS CODE TO SOME EXTENT.  AT SEEMINGLY
 5214                /       RANDOM INTERVALS, IMPROVEMENTS IN THE CODE REDUCED THE OVERALL CODE SPACE TO
 5215                /       ROUGHLY THE SIZE AS PRESENTED IN THIS DOCUMENT.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 104

 5217                /       AT THE SAME TIME AS THE OPTIMIZATION OF CODE SPACE WAS OCCURRING, OTHERS WERE
 5218                /       SUGGESTING NEW FEATURES THAT IN TURN MADE THE PROGRAM LARGER, ALWAYS RAISING
 5219                /       THE BAR FOR ANY FORM OF OPTIMIZATION.  THE SET OF FEATURES AVAILABLE AS OF
 5220                /       THIS WRITING IS CLEARLY NEARING STABILITY; SOME MIGHT CLAIM WE CAN GO NO
 5221                /       FURTHER IN TERMS OF PASSING ADDITIONAL PROGRAM PARAMETERS.  HOWEVER, ALL OF
 5222                /       THE INDIVIDUALS DESIGNING THIS CODE WERE WRONG ABOUT THE NOTION OF FINALITY AT
 5223                /       SOME POINT.  MOST OF THEM WERE INCORRECT -- AT LEAST SIX TIMES.  WHAT THE
 5224                /       FUTURE HOLDS FOR THIS CODE IN TERMS OF OPTIMIZATION, NO ONE REALLY KNOWS.
 5225   
 5226                /       SPECIFIC OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES.
 5227   
 5228                /       1) USE OF INSTRUCTION PLACEMENT TO GENERATE USEFUL CONSTANTS.  BY CAREFULLY
 5229                /          DETERMINING EXACT REQUIREMENTS, IT IS POSSIBLE TO CREATE CONSTANTS OUT OF
 5230                /          INSTRUCTIONS.  ALTHOUGH PERHAPS NOT QUITE "CLEAN" IN SOME INSTANCES, AN
 5231                /          ADEQUATE SOLUTION CAN OFTEN BE CONSTRUCTED.
 5232   
 5233                /          AS NECESSARY, CERTAIN BIT COMBINATIONS MAY BE DISCOVERED THAT FULFILL ALL
 5234                /          REQUIREMENTS.  THIS COULD INCLUDE DON'T-CARE BITS AS WELL AS OTHER BITS
 5235                /          THAT MUST BE SET WHILE OTHERS MUST BE CLEARED.  OFTEN IT IS USEFUL TO
 5236                /          DEFINE THE REQUIREMENTS OF EACH BIT TO PERFORM A FIRST-LEVEL ANALYSIS OF
 5237                /          THE FEASIBILITY OF THIS TECHNIQUE.
 5238   
 5239                /       2) USE OF EXTRANEOUS BITS IN OPERATE-CLASS INSTRUCTIONS TO GENERATE USEFUL
 5240                /          CONSTANTS.  ALL SKIP-CLASS OPERATE INSTRUCTIONS HAVE COMMON SPECIFIC
 5241                /          FEATURES SUCH AS CLEAR THE ACCUMULATOR AFTER THE TEST.  BY SETTING CERTAIN
 5242                /          BITS, THERE MAY BE SOME INSTRUCTION FLOW ISSUES REGARDING THE INTENDED
 5243                /          PROGRAM LOGIC THAT MUST BE PROPERLY HANDLED.  PROGRAM ANALYSIS MAY BE
 5244                /          REQUIRED TO DETERMINE THE CONSEQUENCES OF UNINTENDED SKIP CONDITIONS.
 5245   
 5246                /          AN AVAILABLE CONSTANT THAT IS THE SAME CODE AS AN INNOCUOUS INSTRUCTION MAY
 5247                /          EXIST [AN OPERATE INSTRUCTION THAT DOESN'T EFFECT THE LOGIC WERE IT TO BE
 5248                /          EXECUTED] THAT CAN BE PLACED AFTER THE PROBLEMATIC SKIP-CLASS OPERATE
 5249                /          INSTRUCTION AS PROTECTION IN THE CASE OF AN UNINTENDED SKIP RESULT.  A
 5250                /          SIMILAR SOLUTION MIGHT BE OBTAINED USING AN AVAILABLE CONSTANT THAT FORMS
 5251                /          AN "AND XXXX" INSTRUCTION WHICH WOULD BE HARMLESS IF EXECUTED WHILE ALSO
 5252                /          PROTECTING AGAINST AN UNINTENDED SKIP OPERATION.
 5253   
 5254                /          OTHER NEEDED CONSTANTS PLACED IN THE INSTRUCTION STREAM MIGHT ALWAYS SKIP
 5255                /          [PERHAPS PERFORMING A NECESSARY FUNCTION SUCH AS CLEARING THE ACCUMULATOR].
 5256                /          AS SUCH, ANY AVAILABLE USAGE COULD BE ASSIGNED TO THE FOLLOWING LOCATION,
 5257                /          CONSTANT OR TEMPORARY.  OTHER VARIATIONS OF THIS TECHNIQUE MIGHT APPLY.
 5258   
 5259                /       3) MISCELLANEOUS TECHNIQUES MIGHT APPLY.  A CONSTANT MAY BE NEEDED AS A
 5260                /          SUBROUTINE ARGUMENT WITH MUCH LATITUDE AS TO ITS EXACT VALUE.  THIS MIGHT
 5261                /          REQUIRE BELONGING TO A NARROW RANGE OF CONTIGUOUS ADDRESSES OR A MEMBER OF
 5262                /          A SMALL CLASS OF ALLOWED SIMILAR VALUES, ETC.  THE VALUE CHOSEN TO FORM THE
 5263                /          NEEDED CONSTANT MAY ALSO BE SUBJECT TO ANALYSIS FOR DON'T CARE BITS AS
 5264                /          DESCRIBED IN 1) ABOVE.
 5265   
 5266                /          SOME SPECIFIC TECHNIQUES CANNOT BE CATEGORIZED AND MAY ONLY APPLY TO THE
 5267                /          EXACT CIRCUMSTANCES THEY WERE USED IN.  THE OVERALL GOAL IS TO ACHIEVE CODE
 5268                /          THAT FUNCTIONS.  IT MAY NOT BE POSSIBLE TO CREATE SIMPLE LANGUAGE TO
 5269                /          EXPLAIN THE RESULTS OF BRAINSTORMING.  ONLY BY STUDYING THE CODE
 5270                /          SUCCESSFULLY EXPLOITING THE TECHNIQUE MAY SOME UNDERSTANDING BE OBTAINED.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 105

 5272                /       OTHER TECHNIQUES NOT ENCOURAGED IN P?S/8 PROGRAMMING.
 5273   
 5274                /       CERTAIN OPPORTUNISTIC PROGRAMMERS HAVE BEEN KNOWN TO "BEND" THE SPECIFIC
 5275                /       REQUIREMENTS OF A PROGRAM DUE TO CIRCUMSTANCES OF TIGHT CODING.  NEEDED VALUES
 5276                /       MAY NOT EASILY BE SQUEEZED INTO AVAILABLE CODE SPACE.  AS SUCH, INAPPROPRIATE
 5277                /       EXPEDIENCY MAY CAUSE BIZARRE AND ARBITRARY IMPLEMENTATION OF A PROGRAM ELEMENT
 5278                /       OR FUNCTION; DOCUMENTATION OF EXACTLY HOW THE PROGRAM WORKS MAY APPEAR
 5279                /       BAFFLING TO THE END USER OR MAY REASONABLY BE DEEMED QUITE ARBITRARY.  OTHER
 5280                /       EFFECTS ON THE PROGRAM DESIGN MAY NOT BE DETECTABLE BY THE END USER WITHOUT
 5281                /       APPLYING UNREASONABLE EFFORT OF PROGRAMMING ANALYSIS; PERHAPS THERE IS NO
 5282                /       SPECIFIC YARDSTICK TO JUDGE THE DEGRADATION IN PERFORMANCE BY.  INSPECTION OF
 5283                /       THE SOURCE CODE MAY BE THE ONLY WAY TO DETERMINE THAT A BETTER JOB COULD HAVE
 5284                /       BEEN DONE TO PRODUCE A BETTER OUTCOME.
 5285   
 5286                /       BY AND LARGE THIS IS AN INDICATION OF EXPEDIENT PROGRAMMING AND THE HIGH
 5287                /       LIKELIHOOD OF NOT APPLYING THE TECHNIQUES DISCUSSED ABOVE.  TO THE FULLEST
 5288                /       EXTENT POSSIBLE TECHNIQUES OF THIS SORT ARE NOT USED THROUGHOUT P?S/8.  PROPER
 5289                /       APPLICATION OF THE PROVEN METHODS DISCUSSED ABOVE GENERALLY WILL ALLOW
 5290                /       MAINTAINING DESIGN GOALS WITHOUT COMPROMISE.  AS NECESSARY, IMPLEMENTATION
 5291                /       SCHEDULES SHOULD BE FLEXIBLE ENOUGH TO ALLOW APPLICATION OF ACCEPTABLE
 5292                /       OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES.  THIS WILL AVOID EMBARRASSMENTS SUCH AS DESCRIBED
 5293                /       HERE.  [NOTE: CERTAIN INDIVIDUALS ARE KNOWN FOR REPEATED USE OF THIS SORT OF
 5294                /       PROBLEMATIC TECHNIQUE; WE WILL NOT MENTION THEM BY NAME.]
 5295   
 5296                /       FURTHER DEVELOPMENT HISTORY OF THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
 5297   
 5298                /       SOME USEFUL FEATURES WERE ADDED AT A POINT IN TIME WHERE IT SEEMED IMPOSSIBLE
 5299                /       TO DO SO.  BY INTENSIVELY STUDYING THE PROGRAM, MORE CODE SPACE WAS
 5300                /       OCCASIONALLY FOUND, OFTEN BY EXPLOITING SOME ADDITIONAL QUIRK OF THE
 5301                /       INSTRUCTION SET.  IN SOME CASES THE IMPROVEMENT WAS FORMULATED BY TWO
 5302                /       INDIVIDUALS BOUNCING IDEAS OFF OF EACH OTHER.  THE SINGLE LARGEST EXAMPLE OF
 5303                /       AN ADDED FEATURE IS THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE COMPLETELY FUNCTIONAL
 5304                /       REPLACEMENT SYSTEM LOADER INTERNAL CALL MECHANISM.  [NOTE: THE SYSTEM LOADER
 5305                /       IS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.]
 5306   
 5307                /       A CONCEPT VIRTUALLY UNIQUE TO DATA BREAK DEVICES WAS DEVELOPED SPECIFICALLY
 5308                /       FOR THE TC01/TC08 HANDLER.  PROGRAM EXIT TO 07600 CAN BE INITIATED BY WHAT
 5309                /       APPEARS TO BE A SUBROUTINE CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WITH THREE
 5310                /       CUSTOMARY IN-LINE ARGUMENTS.  AS DESCRIBED WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 5311                /       CODING [ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT], THE SYSTEM DOES IN FACT BOOT UP WHEN THE
 5312                /       ENTIRE PROCESS IS FINISHED.  THIS UNORTHODOX TECHNIQUE SAVES SEVERAL WORDS OF
 5313                /       CODE SPACE OVER THE FORMER CODE.  [NOTE: TWO OF THE THREE ARGUMENT WORDS ALSO
 5314                /       SERVE AS CONSTANTS NEEDED ELSEWHERE.]  CERTAIN ASPECTS OF THIS TECHNIQUE MAY
 5315                /       BE IMPLEMENTED IN SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS FOR OTHER HARDWARE CONFIGURATIONS.
 5316   
 5317                /       AS OF SOME POINT IN TIME [NOT WELL REMEMBERED], IT WAS KNOWN THAT "ALL"
 5318                /       REQUIRED FEATURES WERE IMPLEMENTED WITH EXACTLY ONE 12-BIT WORD TO SPARE, AND
 5319                /       AT THE SAME TIME NO KNOWN FEATURE PLANNED TO TAKE ADVANTAGE OF THE SPACE;
 5320                /       HOWEVER, AS NEW FEATURES WERE ADDED, THE NOTION OF "EXTRA" AVAILABLE WORDS
 5321                /       DISAPPEARED.  EVEN WITH SO MUCH EFFORT ALREADY APPLIED, IT IS NOT CLEAR IF WE
 5322                /       WILL EVER EXCEED THE PRACTICAL UPPER LIMIT OF BURNESS' COROLLARY.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 106

 5324                /       DEVICE HANDLER ENTRY POINT DESIGN CONSIDERATION.
 5325   
 5326                /       NOTE: THIS POINT IS ACTUALLY EXPLOITED IN THE REST OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND
 5327                /       CONFORMING PROGRAMS.
 5328   
 5329                /       THE SYSTEM HANDLER IS ENTERED BY A CALL TO LOCATION 07640 WITH APPROPRIATE
 5330                /       IN-LINE ARGUMENTS.  THE REASONS FOR CHOOSING THIS SPECIFIC ADDRESS ARE AS
 5331                /       FOLLOWS:
 5332   
 5333                /       IT IS QUITE COMMON FOR MOST PDP-8 CODE TO USE AN "SZA CLA" INSTRUCTION EITHER
 5334                /       "NATURALLY" OR BY EASILY IMPLEMENTED CONTRIVANCE USING ONE OF THE TECHNIQUES
 5335                /       SUGGESTED ABOVE [OR PERHAPS SOME OTHER TRICK NOT DOCUMENTED HERE].  AS SUCH,
 5336                /       IT IS GENERALLY FAIRLY EASY TO DESIGN CALLS TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER THAT
 5337                /       USE "SZA CLA" AS USED IN AN INSTRUCTION ON THE SAME PAGE AS THE CALL, THUS
 5338                /       ELIMINATING THE NEED FOR ANY FORM OF CURRENT PAGE [OR PAGE ZERO] LITERAL
 5339                /       CONSTANT VALUE TO ACCOMPLISH IT.
 5340   
 5341                /       CODE THAT USES TRICKS SUCH AS THIS IS REQUIRED TO HAVE FAILING CONDITIONAL
 5342                /       ASSEMBLY EXPRESSIONS TO ENFORCE THE PARTICULARS THAT MATTER FOR THE TRICK TO
 5343                /       WORK.  WITHOUT THIS LEVEL OF SAFEGUARD, SOURCE CODE CAN EASILY BECOME BROKEN
 5344                /       BY PROGRAMMERS UNAWARE OF THE TECHNIQUES IN EFFECT.
 5345   
 5346                /       THIS HANDLER IS NO EXCEPTION [SEE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ENTRY POINT CODE
 5347                /       FOR MORE INFORMATION].  NOTE: THE MAIN P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR AND INTEGRATED
 5348                /       EDITOR COMPONENT OF P?S/8 IS 2048 WORDS OF CODE SPACE LONG NOT COUNTING
 5349                /       OVERLAYS, BUT INCLUDING THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND A COMPLETE IMAGE OF THE
 5350                /       TINY FILE SYSTEM [TFS] DIRECTORY.
 5351   
 5352                /       IN LIEU OF THE ORDINARILY DESERVED PAGE ZERO LITERAL CONSTANT 7640, ALL CODE
 5353                /       REQUIRING THE ADDRESS POINTER INSTEAD USES LOCAL "SZA CLA" INSTRUCTIONS ON THE
 5354                /       SAME PAGE AS THE SUBROUTINE CALL POINTER.  BY USING THIS AND OTHER RELATED
 5355                /       TECHNIQUES, THE PAGE ZERO OF THAT MODULE FUNCTIONS AS IF THERE WERE ABOUT 150
 5356                /       MEMORY LOCATIONS INSTEAD OF THE NOMINAL 128.  AT THE SOURCE CODE LEVEL, ALL
 5357                /       CASES ARE PROTECTED BY APPROPRIATE FAILING CONDITIONAL ASSEMBLY EXPRESSIONS
 5358                /       THAT MAINTAIN THE VIABILITY OF THE SOURCE CODE.
 5359   
 5360                /       IN GENERAL, PROTECTION AGAINST SOURCE CODE DAMAGE IS ACHIEVED WITH EXPRESSIONS
 5361                /       SUCH AS THIS:
 5362   
 5363                /       IFNZRO  .-7640  <ERROR  .>
 5364   
 5365                /       NOTE: THE ERROR DIRECTIVE IS A FEATURE OF P?S/8 PAL AND FOLLOW-ON ASSEMBLERS.
 5366                /       SHOULD THE CODE BECOMES UNACCEPTABLY MODIFIED, THE CONDITIONAL DOES NOT FAIL
 5367                /       AND INSTEAD THE ERROR DIRECTIVE IS ASSEMBLED.  DURING THE ASSEMBLY PROCESS,
 5368                /       ERROR MESSAGES ARE ISSUED DURING ALL ASSEMBLER PASSES.  AN ERROR IS LOGGED IN
 5369                /       THE OVERALL ASSEMBLY ERROR COUNT TO PREVENT SUBSEQUENT BINARY LOADING DURING
 5370                /       CHAINED ASSEMBLIES.  WHEN USING OS/8 PAL8, THIS MERELY GENERATES AN ORDINARY
 5371                /       UNDEFINED SYMBOL [US] ERROR; EITHER WAY IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO GET AN
 5372                /       ERROR-FREE ASSEMBLY WHEN THE CODE IS MANGLED BY A NITWIT.  [OF COURSE THERE IS
 5373                /       NO PROTECTION FROM A NITWIT WHO DISABLES THE PROTECTION MECHANISM!]
 5374   
 5375                /       END OF APPENDIX G.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 107

 5377                /       APPENDIX H.
 5378   
 5379                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM GENERATION CONSIDERATIONS.
 5380   
 5381                /       STUDYING THE DOCUMENTATION OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SHOULD LEAD TO AN
 5382                /       UNDERSTANDING OF HOW IT FUNCTIONS ONCE IT IS LOADED INTO THE INTENDED MEMORY
 5383                /       LOCATIONS; HOWEVER, THE PROCESS OF CREATING THE ACTUAL IMAGE ON BLOCK 0000 OF
 5384                /       A BOOTABLE SYSTEM REQUIRES RELOCATING THE BINARY CODE TO AN ALTERNATE LOCATION
 5385                /       FOR THE PURPOSE OF WRITING THE IMAGE.  IF THE CODE WERE SIMPLY WRITTEN FROM A
 5386                /       WORKING VERSION OF THE DEVICE HANDLER RUNNING IN THE INTENDED MEMORY SPACE IT
 5387                /       IS DESIGNED FOR, A DEFECTIVE [MODIFIED] IMAGE WOULD RESULT.  THUS, A STATIC
 5388                /       IMAGE COPY OF THE DEVICE HANDLER IS REQUIRED TO PRODUCE THE INTENDED RESULT.
 5389   
 5390                /       A PRIOR REQUIREMENT IS THAT ANOTHER OPERATING SYSTEM HAS TO LOAD THE BINARY
 5391                /       FILE[S] INTO MEMORY.  AS SUCH, ALL LOADING OF OPERATING SYSTEM COMPONENTS
 5392                /       MUST BE ACCOMPLISHED FROM AVAILABLE MEMORY OUTSIDE OF THE RESERVED AREA OF THE
 5393                /       CURRENT OPERATING SYSTEM ACCOMPLISHING THE LOADING.  AS SUCH, ONLY MEMORY
 5394                /       FULLY CONSIDERED AS USER MEMORY IS USED TO LOAD ALL OF THE COMPONENTS OF P?S/8
 5395                /       INCLUDING A SYSTEM GENERATION ROUTINE DESIGNED TO WRITE OUT COMPONENTS LOADED
 5396                /       INTO MEMORY AT ONCE. [NOTE: CREATING AN OPERATING SYSTEM IS ACCOMPLISHED IN
 5397                /       SEVERAL STAGES TO MINIMIZE THE MEMORY REQUIREMENTS OF SYSTEM GENERATION.]
 5398   
 5399                /       ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM COMPONENTS ARE THUS LOADED ONLY INTO LOCATIONS X0000-X7577
 5400                /       WHERE X IS ANY FIELD USED.  THIS WILL SATISFY THE REQUIREMENTS OF CREATING
 5401                /       P?S/8 UNDER ANOTHER RUNNING COPY OF P?S/8, OS/8 OR THE PAPER-TAPE BINARY
 5402                /       LOADER [BIN].  [FOR A DISCUSSION OF POTENTIAL ALTERNATE METHODS, SEE BELOW
 5403                /       WITH REGARD TO PROPOSED SYSTEM BUILDING UTILITIES.]
 5404   
 5405                /       THE CURRENT METHOD OF P?S/8 SYSTEM GENERATION REQUIRES 16K OF MEMORY.  OTHER
 5406                /       THAN LOCATIONS 7600-7777 IN FIELDS 0-3, ALL OF THIS MEMORY IS LOADED WITH
 5407                /       VARIOUS SYSTEM COMPONENTS, GENERALLY RELOCATED TO AVAILABLE BUFFERS FOR THE
 5408                /       PURPOSE OF WRITING OUT IMAGES OF THE VARIOUS COMPONENTS.  IN A FEW CASES
 5409                /       [SUCH AS THE KEYBOARD MONITOR AND INTEGRATED TEXT EDITOR] THE LOADING ADDRESS
 5410                /       CORRESPONDS TO THE OPERATING ADDRESS USED WHEN THE SYSTEM IS BOOTED UP.
 5411                /       [NOTE: 16K OF MEMORY IS GENERALLY REQUIRED TO ASSEMBLE SEVERAL OF THE LARGER
 5412                /       SYSTEM COMPONENTS; AS SUCH, THE CURRENT METHOD WAS CHOSEN FOR SYSTEM CREATION
 5413                /       ON A SUFFICIENTLY LARGE SYSTEM CAPABLE OF ASSEMBLING THE SOURCE FILES INTO
 5414                /       BINARY FILES SUITABLE FOR LOADING AND CREATING A COMPLETE SYSTEM.]
 5415   
 5416                /       MOST COMPONENTS ARE LOADED INTO ARBITRARY BUFFER ADDRESSES SUITABLE FOR
 5417                /       WRITING OUT CONTIGUOUS IMAGES OF EACH MODULE.  THIS ALLOWS EACH TO BE WRITTEN
 5418                /       BY A SINGLE CALL TO A DEVICE HANDLER FOR EACH INSTANCE.  EACH MODULE IS
 5419                /       LOADED INTO THE DESIGNATED BUFFER; HOWEVER, THE CODE IS ASSEMBLED FOR USE IN
 5420                /       THE OPERATIONAL ADDRESS IT IS INTENDED FOR.  [NOTE: IN A FEW INSTANCES, THERE
 5421                /       IS MORE THAN ONE UNIQUE ADDRESS ASSOCIATED WITH SPECIFIC USAGE.  THIS OCCURS
 5422                /       WHEN THE BLOCK WHERE THE CODE RESIDES IS READ DURING THE BOOTUP PROCESS INTO
 5423                /       AN ALTERNATE BUFFER ADDRESS TEMPORARILY BEFORE FINALLY BEING MOVED TO THE
 5424                /       INTENDED EXECUTION ADDRESS.  WHERE NECESSARY, THIS MULTIPLE RELOCATION IS
 5425                /       INDICATED IN THE SOURCE CODE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 108

 5427                /       IN ALL CASES, THE LOADING ADDRESS AND FIELD MUST BE CLEARLY STATED, FOLLOWED
 5428                /       BY RELOCATION STATEMENTS TO ENSURE THE PROGRAM SEGMENT ASSEMBLES AS NEEDED
 5429                /       ONCE THE CODE IS LOADED WHERE INTENDED.  EVERY SECTION OF CODE WILL THUS
 5430                /       CONTAIN SEVERAL STATEMENTS REGARDING BOTH LOADING ORIGIN AND EXECUTION ORIGIN
 5431                /       AS REQUIRED.  [NOTE: THE USE OF LITERALS IS STRICTLY AVOIDED WITHIN P?S/8
 5432                /       DUE TO THE INHERENT COMPLICATIONS INVOLVING LITERAL DUMP OPERATIONS.]
 5433   
 5434                /       A SYSTEM GENERATION PROGRAM IS REQUIRED TO CARRY OUT ALL OF THE VARIOUS WRITE
 5435                /       OPERATIONS NEEDED AT ANY STAGE OF SYSTEM CREATION.  THIS IS ESSENTIALLY A
 5436                /       SERIES OF CALLS TO A DEVICE HANDLER TO WRITE OUT APPROPRIATE BLOCKS ON THE
 5437                /       TARGET DEVICE.  WRITING THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND THESE FUNDAMENTAL
 5438                /       SYSTEM SEGMENTS CREATES A BOOTABLE P?S/8 OPERATING SYSTEM CAPABLE OF LOADING
 5439                /       PROGRAMS AND EXECUTION OF A FEW ADDITIONAL UTILITIES. [NOTE: THIS PROCESS
 5440                /       MERELY CREATES A STARTER SYSTEM; MANY STANDARD UTILITIES ARE NOT PRESENT.
 5441                /       HOWEVER, THIS MINIMAL SYSTEM IS CAPABLE OF RUNNING ADDITIONAL PROGRAMS TO
 5442                /       CONTINUE THE PROCESS OF BUILDING UP A MORE COMPLETE SYSTEM.]
 5443   
 5444                /       AS A PRACTICAL MATTER, THE PROCESS OF CREATING A COMPLETE OPERATING SYSTEM IS
 5445                /       CARRIED OUT IN STAGES TO ACCOMMODATE A PARTICULAR SYSTEM GENERATION SCHEME.
 5446                /       CERTAIN RELEVANT ISSUES WILL BE DISCUSSED BELOW.
 5447   
 5448                /       HISTORIC P?S/8 SYSTEM GENERATION METHOD.
 5449   
 5450                /       GENERATION OF P?S/8 HAS BEEN PERFORMED ON 16K OR LARGER MACHINES.  THE ENTIRE
 5451                /       USER SPACE IS UTILIZED TO LOAD IN ALL COMPONENTS OF A DESIGNATED STARTER
 5452                /       SYSTEM.  THIS CONSISTS OF MORE THAN THE MINIMUM COMPLEMENT OF COMPONENTS
 5453                /       REQUIRED TO BOOTSTRAP THE SYSTEM; SOME OF THE ADDITIONAL COMPONENTS ARE NEEDED
 5454                /       IN SUBSEQUENT STEPS TO CREATE THE ADDITIONAL COMPONENTS FROM THE BOOTED UP
 5455                /       STARTER SYSTEM. [NOTE: CREATING CERTAIN COMPONENTS REQUIRES 16K TO ASSEMBLE
 5456                /       THE SOURCE CODE; THERE IS NO INTENTION TO ATTEMPT TO REDUCE SYSTEM DEVELOPMENT
 5457                /       MEMORY REQUIREMENTS WHEN USING THIS METHOD.]
 5458   
 5459                /       EACH OF THE ADDITIONAL SYSTEM COMPONENTS HAS ITS OWN MEMORY REQUIREMENT; AS OF
 5460                /       THIS WRITING, ALL ADDITIONAL SYSTEM PROGRAMS REQUIRE LESS THAN 16K TO BE ADDED
 5461                /       TO AN EXISTENT STARTER SYSTEM; MOST REQUIRE 4K MEMORY TO LOAD THE COMPONENT
 5462                /       [ALTHOUGH AS MUCH AS 16K MAY BE REQUIRED TO ASSEMBLE THE SOURCE CODE INTO
 5463                /       LOADABLE BINARY FILES].  SEVERAL SPECIFIC COMPONENTS REQUIRE 8K OR 12K FOR
 5464                /       SYSTEM GENERATION PURPOSES.  [NOTE: ALL OF THE SYSTEM PROGRAMS CAN MINIMALLY
 5465                /       RUN ON A 4K SYSTEM ONCE CREATED; CERTAIN RESTRICTIONS MIGHT APPLY ON USAGE ON
 5466                /       CERTAIN MEMORY CONFIGURATIONS.  MANY PROGRAMS ATTEMPT TO ADAPT TO AVAILABLE
 5467                /       MEMORY WITH VARYING DEGREES OF PERFORMANCE.  IN GENERAL, MOST PROGRAMS ARE
 5468                /       AT LEAST NOMINALLY USEFUL ON A 4K SYSTEM; IN SPECIFIC CASES, PERFORMANCE MAY
 5469                /       DRAMATICALLY INCREASE WITH MORE MEMORY AVAILABLE.  CERTAIN PROGRAMS ARE
 5470                /       IMPLEMENTED WITH INTERNAL OVERLAY STRUCTURES; CREATING THESE PROGRAMS REQUIRES
 5471                /       ALL SUB-COMPONENTS BE LOADED INTO MEMORY AT THE SAME TIME.]
 5472   
 5473                /       ALTERNATE SYSTEM GENERATION METHODS WITH LESSER MEMORY REQUIREMENTS.
 5474   
 5475                /       AS OF THIS WRITING, NO OTHER SYSTEM GENERATION METHOD HAS BEEN IMPLEMENTED;
 5476                /       HOWEVER, MANY OF THE ISSUES HAVE BEEN CAREFULLY STUDIED.  THE FOLLOWING
 5477                /       SECTIONS OUTLINE ALTERNATE MECHANISMS, INCLUDING A METHOD THAT WILL SUCCEED
 5478                /       ON 4K MEMORY SYSTEMS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 109

 5480                /       THE SECONDARY SYSTEM GENERATION METHOD AND TENTATIVE MODIFICATION TO RUN ON A
 5481                /       4K MEMORY SYSTEM.
 5482   
 5483                /       NOTE: CERTAIN P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS REQUIRE A MEMORY EXTENSION DEVOTED
 5484                /       TO ADDITIONAL PORTIONS OF THE DEVICE HANDLER.  THE MEMORY EXTENSION MUST BE
 5485                /       LOADED INTO EXTENDED MEMORY; LOCATIONS X6000-X7777 OF ANY EXTENDED MEMORY
 5486                /       FIELD MUST BE AVAILABLE TO RUN THESE CONFIGURATIONS.  WHEN THESE SYSTEMS FIRST
 5487                /       BOOT UP, THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD IS DETERMINED; THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 5488                /       EXTENSION IS THEN LOADED INTO 6000-7777 OF THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD AVAILABLE.
 5489                /       ANY CONSIDERATIONS REGARDING SYSTEM GENERATION CANNOT CONSIDER THIS RESERVED
 5490                /       MEMORY; AS SUCH, FOR SYSTEM GENERATION PURPOSES, THE MACHINE WILL BE
 5491                /       CONSIDERED AS HAVING ONE LESS MEMORY FIELD AVAILABLE.
 5492   
 5493                /       AS OF THIS WRITING, ONCE THE STARTER SYSTEM IS CREATED [ON A SYSTEM WITH 16K
 5494                /       OR MORE], THE SECONDARY SYSTEM GENERATION IS ACHIEVED USING AN INTERCHANGE
 5495                /       MEDIA CREATED UNDER OS/8.  [NOTE: THE RELEVANT INTERCHANGE MEDIA FOR THE
 5496                /       TC01/TC08 DECTAPE SYSTEM IS AN OS/8 NON-SYSTEM DECTAPE WHICH STARTS WITH AN
 5497                /       EMPTY OS/8 DIRECTORY; OTHER INTERCHANGE MEDIA ARE SUPPORTED FOR OTHER P?S/8
 5498                /       CONFIGURATIONS.]
 5499   
 5500                /       THE STARTER SYSTEM INCLUDES THE P?S/8 "DUMP" PROGRAM, A UTILITY THAT IS A
 5501                /       DESCENDANT OF A FORMER PROGRAM WRITTEN FOR THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM.  THE MAIN
 5502                /       FUNCTIONALITY IS THE ABILITY TO TRANSFER SEVERAL STORAGE BLOCKS AT ANY ONE
 5503                /       TIME FROM ONE DEVICE UNIT TO ANOTHER; INDIVIDUAL BLOCKS CAN BE UPDATED BY
 5504                /       APPROPRIATE PATCH-ORIENTED COMMANDS.
 5505   
 5506                /       THE DUMP PROGRAM IS DESIGNED TO RUN FROM P?S/8 ONLY; IT NOT ONLY DEPENDS ON
 5507                /       THE AVAILABILITY OF THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, BUT ALSO CAN TAKE INPUT
 5508                /       FROM TFS TEXT FILES CONTAINING DUMP COMMANDS.
 5509   
 5510                /       ONCE THE STARTER SYSTEM IS CREATED, THE INTERCHANGE MEDIA IS MOUNTED ON AN
 5511                /       ACCESSIBLE LOGICAL UNIT [SUCH AS DECTAPE UNIT 1].  DUMP IS RUN FROM THE BOOTED
 5512                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM ON DRIVE 0; COMMAND FILES ARE PASSED THAT ARE DESIGNED TO MOVE
 5513                /       CORE IMAGE SUBSECTIONS FROM KNOWN AREAS ON THE OS/8 INTERCHANGE MEDIA TO
 5514                /       DESIGNATED BLOCKS ON THE STARTER SYSTEM.  ONCE THIS PROCESS IS COMPLETED, A
 5515                /       COMPLETE [OR NEARLY COMPLETE] P?S/8 SYSTEM HAS BEEN CREATED.
 5516   
 5517                /       AS WRITTEN, THIS PROCESS REQUIRES 8K OF AVAILABLE MEMORY; DUMP CAN TRANSFER
 5518                /       AN ENTIRE MEMORY FIELD [32 BLOCKS] AT ANY ONE TIME.  MANY OF THE SPECIFIC
 5519                /       COMMANDS WITHIN THE DUMP COMMAND FILE REQUIRE 31 PAGES BE TRANSFERRED IN A
 5520                /       SINGLE OPERATIONS.  WHEN DUMP IS RUN ON A 4K SYSTEM, THE MAXIMUM AMOUNT THAT
 5521                /       CAN BE TRANSFERRED IS LOWERED TO ABOUT 20 PAGES DUE TO THE PRESENCE IN FIELD 0
 5522                /       OF THE ENTIRE PROGRAM AND THE READ/WRITE BUFFERS.
 5523   
 5524                /       BY SLIGHTLY REWRITING THE DUMP COMMAND FILE, THE DEPENDENCY ON REQUIRING 8K OF
 5525                /       MEMORY CAN BE LIFTED; SEVERAL COMMANDS CURRENTLY PERFORMED IN A SINGLE
 5526                /       OPERATION WILL BE REQUIRED TO BE ACCOMPLISHED IN TWO STEPS.
 5527   
 5528                /       THUS, WHILE THE CREATION OF THE STARTER SYSTEM REQUIRES 16K, THE SECONDARY
 5529                /       SYSTEM GENERATION PROCESS CAN BE ACCOMPLISHED IN 4K [PRESENTLY 8K].


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 110

 5531                /       CREATING THE STARTER SYSTEM ON 4K SYSTEMS.
 5532   
 5533                /       IF THE 16K MEMORY LOAD USED TO CREATE THE STARTER SYSTEM IS SAVED TO ANOTHER
 5534                /       OS/8 INTERCHANGE MEDIA [OR PERHAPS A DIFFERENT SECTION OF THE SAME MEDIA USED
 5535                /       FOR THE SECONDARY SYSTEM CREATION], THE ENTIRE PROCESS OF CREATING A NEW P?S/8
 5536                /       SYSTEM CAN BE ACCOMPLISHED ON A 4K SYSTEM.  [NOTE: A FUNCTIONING P?S/8 SYSTEM
 5537                /       MUST ALREADY BE AVAILABLE.]  [NOTE: DUE TO THE OVERALL SIZE OF ALL BINARY
 5538                /       FILES, CERTAIN OS/8 DEVICES MAY NOT BE ABLE TO STORE ALL FILES REQUIRED TO
 5539                /       SUPPORT THIS METHOD.  ON RX01 SYSTEMS, THIS MAY REQUIRE AS MANY AS THREE
 5540                /       DISKETTES; THE DUMP COMMAND FILES CAN BE MODIFIED AS REQUIRED.]
 5541   
 5542                /       TRUE 4K-ORIENTED SYSTEM BUILDER ISSUES.
 5543   
 5544                /       A STAND-ALONE SYSTEM BUILDER UTILITY CAN BE DEFINED TO ALLOW CREATING P?S/8
 5545                /       FROM INDIVIDUAL BINARY COMPONENTS.  THERE ARE SEVERAL ISSUES SPECIFIC TO
 5546                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAMS:
 5547   
 5548                /       1) DEFINING THE DEVICE HANDLER USED TO CARRY OUT THE VARIOUS WRITE OPERATIONS.
 5549   
 5550                /          A STAND-ALONE SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM REQUIRES THE AVAILABILITY OF A DEVICE
 5551                /          HANDLER APPROPRIATE TO THE TARGET HARDWARE FOR THE BUILD OPERATION.  IN
 5552                /          CERTAIN INSTANCES, THIS WOULD NOT BE THE SAME HARDWARE AS THE SYSTEM IS
 5553                /          BEING CREATED FOR.  FOR EXAMPLE, A TD8E-ORIENTED P?S/8 SYSTEM COULD BE
 5554                /          CREATED ON A TC01/TC08 SYSTEM; THE RESULTING DECTAPE WOULD ONLY BE BOOTABLE
 5555                /          ON THE CORRECT TD8E CONFIGURATION.  [NOTE: THERE ARE TWO DISTINCT TD8E
 5556                /          CONFIGURATIONS: A) REQUIRES 8K OR MORE MEMORY, B) REQUIRES THE MR8E-C ROM
 5557                /          BE INSTALLED.]
 5558   
 5559                /          WITHIN THE 16K BINARY SYSTEM GENERATION METHOD, A PORTION OF MEMORY IS
 5560                /          RESERVED FOR A P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE CREATED SPECIALLY FOR THIS PURPOSE; IT
 5561                /          MAY BE ORIENTED TOWARDS DIFFERENT HARDWARE FROM THE TARGET SYSTEM BEING
 5562                /          GENERATED.  ADDITIONALLY, SINCE THIS CODE IS TAKEN FROM A DIFFERENT P?S/8
 5563                /          SYSTEM GENERATION CONFIGURATION, THE IMAGE OF THIS HANDLER MAY NOT RUN IF
 5564                /          MERELY MOVED TO 07600-07777 [AND THIS DOES NOT ADDRESS THE NOTION THAT THE
 5565                /          HANDLER MAY ALSO HAVE AN EXTENDED DEVICE HANDLER SECTION].  EVEN IF THE
 5566                /          HANDLER IS FOR THE IDENTICAL HARDWARE AS THE TARGET SYSTEM, THE IMAGE OF
 5567                /          THE HANDLER OFTEN IS NOT USABLE MERELY BY MOVING THE CODE.  AS SUCH, THERE
 5568                /          IS ALSO AN ASSOCIATED FIXUP ROUTINE THAT MAKES THE HANDLER OPERATIONAL
 5569                /          AFTER THE HANDLER IS MOVED.
 5570   
 5571                /          THIS ISSUE IS BEST SOLVED BY USING A P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLER.  WHILE THE
 5572                /          LARGEST THEORETICAL NON-SYSTEM HANDLER CAN OCCUPY AN ENTIRE MEMORY FIELD
 5573                /          [WHICH WOULD RAISE THE MEMORY REQUIREMENT FOR A SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM TO
 5574                /          8K], MOST CAN BE ACCOMMODATED IN EIGHT PAGES OF MEMORY [OR LESS].  A
 5575                /          BUILDER PROGRAM MUST BE ABLE TO LOAD SUCH A HANDLER BEFORE ATTEMPTING TO
 5576                /          WRITE OUT THE SYSTEM COMPONENTS.  APPROPRIATE PARAMETERS EXPLAINING THE
 5577                /          PARTICULARS OF ANY NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CAN BE PROVIDED BY USING A
 5578                /          SERIES OF SPECIAL ORIGIN SETTINGS WITHIN THE BINARY FILE OF THE DEVICE
 5579                /          HANDLER TO GUIDE THE SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM IN LOADING THE HANDLER WITHIN
 5580                /          THE UTILITY.  [THIS WOULD ALSO INCLUDE THE FEW DEVICE HANDLERS THAT WOULD
 5581                /          REQUIRE 8K MEMORY.]  [NOTE: THE TOPIC OF EMBEDDED SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN
 5582                /          SETTINGS IS DISCUSSED BELOW.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 111

 5584                /       2) DEFINING THE MECHANISM FOR LOADING BINARY FILES OF THE SYSTEM COMPONENTS.
 5585   
 5586                /          THE ONLY UNIVERSAL MECHANISM AVAILABLE TO A STAND-ALONE BUILDER PROGRAM IS
 5587                /          THE USE OF BINARY PAPER-TAPES IN BIN FORMAT.  HOWEVER, IF THE BUILDER
 5588                /          PROGRAM IS LOADED FROM A P?S/8 SYSTEM, TFS BINARY FILES CAN BE PASSED TO
 5589                /          THE BUILDER PROGRAM.  HAVING THIS ABILITY CAN TRANSFORM A POTENTIALLY
 5590                /          TEDIOUS SYSTEM GENERATION INTO A SERIES OF SMALLER OPERATIONS WHERE ALL OF
 5591                /          THE SECONDARY OPERATIONS CAN BE PERFORMED WITHOUT THE USE OF PAPER-TAPES.
 5592   
 5593                /       3) ABSOLUTE SYSTEM BLOCK NUMBERS VERSUS RELATIVE BLOCK NUMBERS.
 5594   
 5595                /          CERTAIN SYSTEM COMPONENTS MUST BE LOADED TO DEFINED ABSOLUTE BLOCK NUMBERS.
 5596                /          THIS INCLUDES TRULY ABSOLUTE DEFINED BLOCKS AS WELL AS THE ARBITRARY BLOCKS
 5597                /          DESIGNATED FOR IMPORTANT SYSTEM PROGRAMS CONSIDERED PART OF THE STARTER
 5598                /          SYSTEM.  [NOTE: THE ONLY ABSOLUTELY REQUIRED P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAM IS THE
 5599                /          "BIN" UTILITY PROGRAM; WITHOUT THE AVAILABILITY OF P?S/8 BIN, NO BINARY
 5600                /          FILES CAN BE LOADED.  P?S/8 DUMP WILL LIKELY ALSO BE DESIGNATED AS AN
 5601                /          ABSOLUTE REQUIREMENT DUE TO ITS ABILITY TO RECTIFY SMALL DISCREPANCIES AS
 5602                /          THEY ARISE.]
 5603   
 5604                /          FURTHER SYSTEM BUILDING OPERATIONS PERTAIN TO ADDITIONAL SYSTEM UTILITY
 5605                /          PROGRAMS SUCH AS P?S/8 PAL, FOCAL, THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY AND OTHER
 5606                /          OPTIONAL COMPONENTS.  SINCE ALL OF THESE PROGRAMS CAN BE ADDED ON AT ANY
 5607                /          TIME AFTER A STARTER SYSTEM IS CREATED, THE DESIGNATED METHOD WOULD BE TO
 5608                /          USE THE SYSTEM BUILDER UTILITY WITH PASSED BINARY FILES CONTAINING RELATIVE
 5609                /          BLOCK NUMBERS TO DEFINE THE INTERNAL STRUCTURE OF THE PROGRAM [SUCH AS
 5610                /          INTERNAL OVERLAY STRUCTURES].
 5611   
 5612                /       4) INITIAL STARTER DIRECTORY CREATION.
 5613   
 5614                /          AS THE SYSTEM IS BEING BUILT UP, THE LOWEST AVAILABLE BLOCK FOR THE TFS
 5615                /          DIRECTORY INHERENTLY INCREASES.  AS SYSTEM PROGRAMS ARE ADDED, THE TFS
 5616                /          DIRECTORY MUST BE REWRITTEN WITH CONSISTENT INTERNAL PARAMETERS.  AS LONG
 5617                /          AS THE SYSTEM BUILDER UTILITY IS USED TO ADD ADDITIONAL PROGRAMS, THE
 5618                /          TFS DIRECTORY EXTENTS CAN BE UPDATED PROPERLY.  [NOTE: IF FILES ARE ADDED
 5619                /          TO THE TFS DIRECTORY, THEY MUST BE REMOVED BEFORE RUNNING THE SYSTEM
 5620                /          BUILDER UTILITY.  EXTERNAL MEANS MUST BE AVAILABLE TO DELETE ANY EXISTENT
 5621                /          FILES.]
 5622   
 5623                /          THE TFS DIRECTORY RESIDES IN BLOCKS 0015-0016 OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE; THE
 5624                /          CURRENT 16K METHOD OF SYSTEM GENERATION WRITES OUT A STARTER COPY OF THE
 5625                /          TFS DIRECTORY CONSISTENT WITH THE SYSTEM DEVICE TARGETED.
 5626   
 5627                /          HOWEVER, A SYSTEM BUILDER MUST CHECK FOR VALIDITY OF THE TFS DIRECTORY; IF
 5628                /          THE STRUCTURE IS INVALID, IT CAN BE CREATED ACCORDINGLY.  HOWEVER, IF THE
 5629                /          TFS DIRECTORY IS ALREADY VALID, IT MUST BE CONFIRMED AS EMPTY TO ALLOW THE
 5630                /          SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM TO PROCEED; ALTERNATIVELY, THE USER CAN BE ASKED TO
 5631                /          HAVE ALL FILES DESTROYED FROM THE BUILDER PROGRAM.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 112

 5633                /          NOTE: IT IS BEYOND THE SCOPE OF A SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM TO REMOVE EXISTING
 5634                /          SYSTEM PROGRAMS.  EITHER AN ALTERNATE PROGRAM CAPABLE OF THIS FUNCTION MUST
 5635                /          BE WRITTEN, OR THE PROGRAM CAN BE PROVIDED AS A P?S/8 SYSTEM UTILITY
 5636                /          PROGRAM [AS OPPOSED TO THE STAND-ALONE DEFINITION AS USED HERE].  ONCE THE
 5637                /          PROGRAM IS PART OF AN EXISTING P?S/8 SYSTEM, ADDITIONAL FUNCTIONS CAN BE
 5638                /          ADDED.  [NOTE: P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS CAN DEFINE OVERLAY STRUCTURES; THE
 5639                /          ABILITY TO REMOVE EXISTING PROGRAMS INCLUDING POTENTIALLY SQUEEZING OUT THE
 5640                /          EMPTY BLOCKS FORMERLY OCCUPIED, COUPLED WITH THE POTENTIAL OF HAVING TO
 5641                /          RECOVER THE SPACE FOR THE TFS DIRECTORY AND PERHAPS MOVING OF TFS FILES IS
 5642                /          A FAR LARGER PROGRAM THAN A STAND-ALONE BUILDER PROGRAM.  HOWEVER, THE
 5643                /          ENVIRONMENT OF A P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAM INCLUDES ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 5644                /          HANDLER AS WELL AS PASSED TEXT AND BINARY FILES.  AS SUCH, THERE ARE
 5645                /          ACTUALLY TWO VARIANT FORMS OF SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAMS.]
 5646   
 5647                /          [NOTE: P?S/8 SYSTEMS ALSO INCLUDE PARTIAL SUPPORT FOR AN EXTENDED DIRECTORY
 5648                /          STRUCTURE BEYOND THE TFS STRUCTURE.  AS LONG AS THE OVERALL LENGTH OF THE
 5649                /          DEVICE IS OBTAINABLE, THIS STRUCTURE CAN BE CREATED TO USE ALL AVAILABLE
 5650                /          SPACE AFTER THE TFS DIRECTORY [IF ANY].  THIS FUNCTION WOULD ALSO BE PART
 5651                /          OF A UTILITY CREATED AS A P?S/8 SYSTEM UTILITY.]
 5652   
 5653                /       USING SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN SETTINGS.
 5654   
 5655                /       CERTAIN READERS OF THIS DOCUMENT MAY BE FAMILIAR WITH THE OS/8 BUILD.SV
 5656                /       PROGRAM, WHICH IS PARTIALLY ANALOGOUS TO THE P?S/8 SYSTEM BUILDER PROCESS.
 5657   
 5658                /       A USEFUL TECHNIQUE FOR PASSING INFORMATION WITHIN A BINARY FILE INVOLVES THE
 5659                /       USE OF DELIBERATE ORIGIN SETTINGS [OR GROUPS OF ORIGIN SETTINGS] FOR THE
 5660                /       PURPOSE OF PASSING ADDITIONAL INFORMATION ABOUT THE FILE APART FROM THE BINARY
 5661                /       FILE CONTENTS BEING LOADED.
 5662   
 5663                /       THIS TECHNIQUES IS ACTUALLY AN OUTGROWTH OF AN EARLIER METHOD FIRST CREATED
 5664                /       FOR THE TYPESET-8 GROUP BY THE IMPLEMENTORS OF THE PAL10 CROSS-ASSEMBLER FOR
 5665                /       PDP-8 PROGRAMMING THAT RUNS UNDER THE TOPS-10 OPERATING SYSTEM ON THE PDP-10.
 5666   
 5667                /       TYPESET-8 USES LOGICAL BLOCKS IDENTICALLY TO P?S/8 [AND TYPESET-8 ONLY RUNS ON
 5668                /       THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE].  EACH BLOCK IS 128 12-BIT WORDS.  A MEANS WAS ADDED TO
 5669                /       PAL10 TO ALLOW DEFINING THE ABSOLUTE BLOCK NUMBERS FOR DESIGNATED COMPONENTS
 5670                /       OF TYPESET-8.  [NOTE: TYPICAL TYPESET-8 DEVELOPMENT WAS TO ASSEMBLE SOURCE
 5671                /       CODE USING PAL10, THEN PUNCHING OUT LENGTHY BINARY PAPER-TAPES TO BE WALKED TO
 5672                /       REMOTE PDP-8 SYSTEMS WITH ONLY 4K OF MEMORY; THE ENTIRE TYPESET-8 OPERATION
 5673                /       WAS DONE WITH NO KNOWLEDGE OF P?S/8.  CLEARLY, P?S/8 COULD HAVE BEEN USEFUL TO
 5674                /       THIS PROJECT ON SOME BASIS.]
 5675   
 5676                /       THE DESIGNATED BLOCKS WHERE TO WRITE OUT PDP-8 PAGES OF LOADABLE BINARY DATA
 5677                /       IS DEFINED USING THE "DTORG" DIRECTIVE AVAILABLE IN PAL10 [AND LATER IN PAL8].
 5678   
 5679                /       HOWEVER, THE IMPLEMENTATION IS CONCEPTUALLY FLAWED: BINARY FILES CREATED BY
 5680                /       PAL10 OR PAL8 CAN BE LOADED BY ALL CONVENTIONAL MEANS AS LONG AS THE DTORG
 5681                /       DIRECTIVE IS NOT USED.  DTORG STATEMENTS CREATE DELIBERATE CHECKSUM ERRORS
 5682                /       BECAUSE THE TYPESET-8 IMPLEMENTORS WERE TOO SHORT-SIGHTED TO REALIZE THIS IS
 5683                /       COMPLETELY UNNECESSARY.  [NOTE: LENGTHY BINARY PAPER-TAPES WERE CREATED THAT
 5684                /       WERE INCOMPATIBLE WITH THE BIN LOADER; ANY ATTEMPT TO LOAD THE TAPES WILL END
 5685                /       WITH A CHECKSUM ERROR; INSTEAD, A SPECIAL TYPESET-8 PROGRAM WAS USED THAT HAD
 5686                /       NO CHECKSUMMING CAPABILITY; THIS IS INHERENTLY UNRELIABLE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 113

 5688                /       BIN PAPER-TAPE FORMAT STRICTLY DEFINES ALL POSSIBILITIES.  MANY PAPER-TAPE
 5689                /       FRAMES CONTAIN SIX BITS OF A 12-BIT ORIGIN OR DATA WORD.  THE HIGH-ORDER BITS
 5690                /       OF THESE WORDS ARE USED TO DISTINGUISH BETWEEN THE TWO VARIANTS, EACH
 5691                /       EXPRESSES AS A PAIR OF ADJACENT FRAMES, INCLUDING THE BIT THAT DISTINGUISHES
 5692                /       BETWEEN LOADING ORIGIN AND DATA WORD; ALL BITS OF ALL DATA FRAMES EXCEPT FOR
 5693                /       FIELD SETTINGS CONTRIBUTE TO THE OVERALL CHECMSUM.
 5694   
 5695                /       SINCE THERE WERE NO MORE AVAILABLE COMBINATIONS [IF YOU SIMPLISTICALLY MAP
 5696                /       OUT THE DATA STRUCTURES OF BIN FORMAT], THEY DECIDED TO CREATE AN INCOMPATIBLE
 5697                /       EXTENSION WHERE THE BIT ASSOCIATED WITH THE ORIGIN SETTING DATA IS ALSO
 5698                /       PRESENT IN THE SECOND FRAME OF THE PAIR DEFINING A 12-BIT VALUE.  WHEN BOTH
 5699                /       BITS ARE SET, THIS IS A DTORG DATA PAIR.  [THE DATA IS USED WITHIN THE
 5700                /       TYPESET-8 GENERATION PROGRAM TO SPECIFY THE ABSOLUTE DECTAPE BLOCK TO WRITE
 5701                /       OUT THE [UP TO] 128 WORDS OF DATA THAT FOLLOW.]  AS SUCH, BINARY PAPER-TAPES
 5702                /       CONTAINING DTORG FRAMES ARE INCOMPATIBLE WITH THE BIN LOADER.
 5703   
 5704                /       WHEN THIS FEATURE WAS ADDED TO PAL8, THE SAME EXACT FLAWED METHOD WAS USED;
 5705                /       THE RESULTANT BINARY FILES OR PAPER-TAPES ARE INCOMPATIBLE WITH OS/8 ABSLDR.SV
 5706                /       AND AS SUCH ARE USELESS FOR ALL OTHER PURPOSES.
 5707   
 5708                /       BY THINKING THE PROBLEM THROUGH, THIS DILEMMA CAN EASILY BE AVOIDED.  P?S/8
 5709                /       PAL CURRENTLY IMPLEMENTS DTORG AS A NON-FUNCTIONAL DIRECTIVE TO MAINTAIN
 5710                /       SOURCE CODE COMPATIBILITY; ANY TYPESET-8 SOURCE FILE CAN BE ASSEMBLED
 5711                /       CORRECTLY WITH P?S/8 PAL [WHILE THE CREATED BINARY ISN'T USEFUL TO TYPESET-8
 5712                /       AT THE PRESENT TIME].
 5713   
 5714                /       BY REDEFINING THE BINARY OUTPT OF A DTORG STATEMENT AS A SERIES OF
 5715                /       SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN SETTINGS, TOTAL COMPATIBILITY CAN BE ACHIEVED.  [NOTE:
 5716                /       TYPESET-8 COMPATIBILITY CAN BE ACHIEVED WITH A CONVERSION UTILITY; WHEN THE
 5717                /       SEQUENCE OF SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN SETTINGS IS DISCOVERED WITHIN THE BINARY
 5718                /       FILE, SUBSTITUTE THE DTORG FRAME PAIR AS TYPESET-8 REQUIRES ON THE BINARY
 5719                /       PAPER-TAPE BEING CREATED FOR THE BENEFIT OF TYPESET-8 COMPATIBILITY.  IN OS/8
 5720                /       THIS COULD HAVE BEEN EASILY ACCOMPLISHED WITH A SWITCH ADDED FOR THE EXPRESS
 5721                /       PURPOSE OF TYPESET-8 BINARY PAPER-TAPE PREPARATION; THE ENTIRE INCOMPATIBILITY
 5722                /       PROGRAM WAS COMPLETELY AVOIDABLE.]
 5723   
 5724                /       OS/8 BUILD DOES NOT USE DTORG STATEMENTS; HOWEVER, IT DOES MAKE GOOD USE OF
 5725                /       SPECIFIC SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN SETTINGS TO PASS PARAMETERS ABOUT DEVICE
 5726                /       HANDLERS WITHIN THE BINARY FILE OF THE HANDLER.
 5727   
 5728                /       A P?S/8 SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM CAN ALSO USE THIS TECHNIQUE; HOWEVER, SINCE THE
 5729                /       DTORG DIRECTIVE IS ALREADY DEFINED, FUTURE VERSIONS OF P?S/8 PAL WILL BE
 5730                /       ENHANCED TO OUTPUT A DESIGNATED SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN SETTING SEQUENCE FOR
 5731                /       EVERY USAGE OF THE DTORG DIRECTIVE ESSENTIALLY AS IT WAS INTENDED, EXCEPT FOR
 5732                /       THE PURPOSE OF P?S/8 SYSTEM GENERATION [AND NOT FOR TYPESET-8 PURPOSES].
 5733   
 5734                /       NOTE: PAL8 MAY CONTINUE TO PLAY A ROLE IN THE PROCESS OF P?S/8 SYSTEM
 5735                /       GENERATION.  AS SUCH, A BINARY CONVERSION UTILITY MAY BE WRITTEN [AS A PRELUDE
 5736                /       TO "PIPOS8" A TENTATIVE PROGRAM PLANNED FOR THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT] TO
 5737                /       ALLOW PAL8 OUTPUT TO BE CONVERTED TO TFS BINARY FILES.  THIS WOULD INCLUDE
 5738                /       CONVERSION OF DTORG STATEMENTS RECAST AS SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN SETTINGS.
 5739                /       SUCH A PROGRAM CAN CALCULATE CHECKSUM OFFSETS CAUSED BY DTORG STATEMENTS TO
 5740                /       PRODUCE A PROPER CHECKSUM DESPITE THE FACT THAT NO OS/8 UTILITY EXISTS TO
 5741                /       VALIDATE THESE FILES.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 114

 5743                /       NOTE: SINCE A COMPLETE P?S/8 SYSTEM BUILDER PROGRAM REQUIRES BOTH ABSOLUTE AND
 5744                /       RELATIVE BLOCK NUMBERS FOR THE VARIOUS SYSTEM COMPONENTS, SOME MEANS MUST BE
 5745                /       PROVIDED TO SWITCH OPERATING MODES WITHIN THE BUILDER PROGRAM.  A SUGGESTED
 5746                /       METHOD TO IMPLEMENT THIS WOULD BE THE USE OF SPECIFIC SYNTAX SUCH AS DTORG
 5747                /       7777 AND DTORG 7776.  BY NATURE, NO VALID SYSTEM COMPONENT WOULD NORMALLY
 5748                /       SPECIFY SUCH A LARGE VALUE; THUS, THESE VALUES CAN BE USED TO SWITCH SYSTEM
 5749                /       BUILDER DTORG BLOCK INTERPRETATION MODES.  ADDITIONAL SIMILAR VALUES CAN BE
 5750                /       DEFINED AS NECESSARY.
 5751   
 5752                /       [NOTE: FOR FURTHER INFORMATION ON THE GENERAL TOPIC OF SPECIAL-PURPOSE ORIGIN
 5753                /       GROUPS, THE SHORTCOMINGS OF TYPESET-8 USING THE DTORG DIRECTIVE AND ITS
 5754                /       NEGATIVE IMPACT ON OS/8 PERFORMANCE, REFER TO A RELATED DOCUMENT ADDRESSING
 5755                /       THESE SPECIFIC ISSUES.  EXAMPLES ARE GIVEN TO SOLVE ANALOGOUS PROBLEMS SUCH AS
 5756                /       AN ALTERNATIVE METHOD FOR THE EXPRESS BENEFIT OF TYPESET-8 SUPPORT AND A BRIEF
 5757                /       DISCUSSION OF OS/8 BUILD WHICH DEALS WITH VAGUELY ANALOGOUS ISSUES.  THE
 5758                /       DOCUMENT IS WRITTEN FROM A UNIQUE VANTAGE POINT: NEEDLESS OVERHEAD WAS ADDED
 5759                /       TO OS/8 MERELY TO PLACATE THE TYPESET-8 GROUP WITH NO COMPROMISE ALLOWED.
 5760                /       THEY INSISTED ON NOT CHANGING ANYTHING WHATSOEVER DESPITE THE FACT THAT SOME
 5761                /       OF THE IDEAS EXPRESSED WOULD HAVE AIDED THE DEVELOPMENT OF TYPESET-8 AS WELL
 5762                /       AS ELIMINATING SOME OVERHEAD WITHIN OS/8.  ADDITONAL TOPICS INCLUDE AN
 5763                /       UNRELATED ACCOMMODATION TO THE TYPESET-8 GROUP WITH REGARD TO SERIOUS OVERHEAD
 5764                /       WITHIN THE OS/8 PAPER-TAPE PUNCH HANDLER THAT AFFECTS ALL USERS AT ALL TIMES;
 5765                /       THE PURPOSE OF THE MODIFICATIONS WILL SURPRISE MANY READERS AS THE "PROBLEM"
 5766                /       SOLVED FOR THE TYPESET-8 GROUP IS PARTICULARLY FANTASTIC AS WELL AS SOMEWHAT
 5767                /       COMICAL.]
 5768   
 5769                /       ADDITIONAL SYSTEM GENERATION CONSIDERATIONS.
 5770   
 5771                /       A COMPLETE SYSTEM GENERATION METHOD MUST INCLUDE MEANS OF CREATING OR UPDATING
 5772                /       THE "SLURP" LOADER AND THE RELATED SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOADER CODE "FILE".
 5773                /       ADDITIONAL SECTIONS MUST INCLUDE DEVICE-SPECIFIC REWIND/UNLOAD ROUTINES
 5774                /       FOR THE KEYBOARD MONITOR AND SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER INTERACTION MODULES THAT
 5775                /       ARE REQUIRED TO INTERFACE WITH THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY.
 5776   
 5777                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS MAY INCLUDE EXTENDED MEMORY SECTIONS AND RELATED
 5778                /       "HELPER" BLOCK IMAGES IF APPLICABLE.
 5779   
 5780                /       A COMPREHENSIVE UTILITY SHOULD BE CAPABLE OF CREATING A SYSTEM FROM SCRATCH
 5781                /       [WITH INITIALLY ONLY THE UTILITY IN MEMORY ON A 4K MACHINE] OR USABLE FROM A
 5782                /       RUNNING SYSTEM TO UPDATE AN IMAGE OF A SYSTEM [POSSIBLY ORIENTED TOWARDS
 5783                /       ALTERNATE HARDWARE].  [NOTE: APPROPRIATE NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS MAY BE REQUIRED
 5784                /       BOTH WITHIN THE GENERATION PROGRAM AND ON THE P?S/8 SYSTEM THE UTILITY IS
 5785                /       BEING RUN FROM TO HANDLE ALL CASES.]
 5786   
 5787                /       END OF APPENDIX H.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 115

 5789                /       APPENDIX I.
 5790   
 5791                /       THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DATE WORD HAS ALWAYS BEEN DEFINED AS "SDATE" [MEMORY LOCATION
 5792                /       07610]; HOWEVER, THE EXACT IMPLEMENTATION HAS CHANGED SEVERAL TIMES.
 5793   
 5794                /       WHEN THE CONCEPT OF A SYSTEM DATE WORD WAS FIRST DISCUSSED, THE ONLY EXAMPLE
 5795                /       OF ANOTHER PDP-8 SYSTEM WITH A DATE WORD WAS PS/8 [LATER RENAMED TO OS/8].
 5796                /       THAT SYSTEM DEFINED THE SYSTEM DATE AS ANY VALID DATE IN THE RANGE OF 1970
 5797                /       THROUGH 1977.  SINCE THE PDP-8 HAD ALREADY PROVEN TO BE A POPULAR SYSTEM
 5798                /       STARTING IN 1965, IT WAS QUITE OBVIOUS THE DATE HAD TO BE DEFINED OVER A
 5799                /       LONGER TIME FRAME.  WHILE 1970 IS AN OBVIOUS STARTING DATE [ALSO USED BY THE
 5800                /       MAJORITY OF UNIX SYSTEMS], IT IS ACTUALLY NOT A GOOD CHOICE FROM THE VANTAGE
 5801                /       POINT OF 12-BIT COMPUTING.
 5802   
 5803                /       1960 IS GENERALLY CONSIDERED THE DAWN OF 12-BIT COMPUTING WITH THE RELEASE OF
 5804                /       THE CDC 160 AS DESIGNED BY SEYMOUR CRAY.  THE DESIGN OF THE CDC 160 IS IN
 5805                /       CERTAIN WAYS A CLEAR PRECURSOR TO THE PDP-8 ARCHITECTURE.
 5806   
 5807                /       BY 1962, THE MIT LINC COMPUTER [LATER INCORPORATED INTO THE LINC-8 AND PDP-12
 5808                /       DESIGN] ADDED SOME ADDITIONAL REFINEMENTS THAT INFLUENCED THE PDP-8; BY 1963
 5809                /       THE FIRST [COMMERCIALLY UNSUCCESSFUL] DEC 12-BIT MACHINE, THE PDP-5, WAS
 5810                /       RELEASED.
 5811   
 5812                /       PDP-6/PDP-10 SOFTWARE USES A DATE RANGE STARTING IN 1964.  BY 1975 THE
 5813                /       END-POINT OF THE ORIGINAL SUPPORTED RANGE WAS REACHED; FORTUNATELY, AN
 5814                /       EXTENSION WAS CREATED [BY SCRAMBLING FOR UNUSED BITS WITHIN THE DIRECTORY
 5815                /       STRUCTURE] SOME TIME BEFORE THE ACTUAL DATE RANGE EXPIRATION.
 5816   
 5817                /       IT WAS CLEAR THAT OS/8'S SHORT DATE RANGE OF EIGHT YEARS WAS ENTIRELY
 5818                /       INADEQUATE; THE EARLIEST IMPLEMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 DATE STROVE FOR BETTER
 5819                /       UTILIZATION OF A 12-BIT VALUE.
 5820   
 5821                /       OS/8'S INHERENT LIMITATION IS THE USE OF ONLY THREE BITS TO STORE THE YEAR
 5822                /       OFFSET [FROM 1970].  THE REST OF THE OS/8 DATE WORD IS USED TO STORE THE
 5823                /       MONTH OFFSET FROM THE BEGINNING OF THE YEAR AND THE DAY OFFSET FROM THE
 5824                /       BEGINNING OF THE MONTH.  TO CONVERT THE STORED VALUE INTO PRINTABLE FORM IS A
 5825                /       FAIRLY STRAIGHTFORWARD PROCESS.
 5826   
 5827                /       NOTE: OS/8 EXHIBITS A DESIGN BLUNDER: THE BIT-WISE ORDERING OF THESE ARBITRARY
 5828                /       BIT GROUPINGS WAS NOT CHOSEN WELL.  BY PROPER USE OF BIT-WEIGHTING, ANY DATE
 5829                /       VALUE CAN BE DIRECTLY COMPARED TO ANOTHER DATE VALUE BY SIMPLE ARITHMETIC
 5830                /       COMPARISON; THIS CAN BE USEFUL IN UTILITIES THAT SPECIFY A DESIRED DATE RANGE
 5831                /       FOR SELECTION/FILTERING.  HOWEVER, SINCE OS/8'S CHOICE OF BITS DEFIES
 5832                /       ARITHMETIC ORDER, ANY SUCH CALCULATION MUST FIRST CONVERT THE VALUES INTO A
 5833                /       VALUE WHERE THE RESULT IS WEIGHTED CORRECTLY; THIS IS NEEDLESS OVERHEAD THAT
 5834                /       COULD HAVE BEEN AVOIDED [AND IS NOT PRESENT IN THE LATEST P?S/8 DATE
 5835                /       IMPLEMENTATION WHICH IS OTHERWISE SOMEWHAT ANALOGOUS TO THE OS/8 METHOD].
 5836   
 5837                /       THE DESIGNERS OF P?S/8 CHOSE AN ALTERNATIVE THAT IS SLIGHTLY MORE COMPLICATED
 5838                /       TO PROCESS WITH THE ADVANTAGE OF HAVING A DATE RANGE OF JUST UNDER 12 YEARS.
 5839                /       THE BASE YEAR CHOSEN AT THE TIME WAS 1976, TO COINCIDE WITH THE FIRST
 5840                /       IMPLEMENTATION OF THE DATE FUNCTION WITHIN P?S/8.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 116

 5842                /       THE METHODOLOGY USED IS AS FOLLOWS:
 5843   
 5844                /       ASSUMING ALL 12 MONTHS HAVE 31 DAYS, THERE WILL BE 372 DAYS IN A YEAR.  THE
 5845                /       VALUE FOR THE DATE PARTICULARS IS CALCULATED THUS:
 5846   
 5847                /       YEAR OFFSET = (SDATE CONTENTS)/372.
 5848   
 5849                /       THE RELATIVE DAY OF THE YEAR IS THE REMAINDER FROM THE ABOVE DIVISION.
 5850   
 5851                /       MONTH OFFSET = (REMAINDER)/31
 5852   
 5853                /       THE DAY OFFSET IS IN TURN THE REMAINDER OF THIS ADDITIONAL DIVISION.
 5854   
 5855                /       THE YEAR OFFSET IS CALCULATED ORIGIN 0 TO MAXIMIZE THE DURATION OF THE DATE
 5856                /       RANGE [APPROXIMATELY 11-3/4 YEARS].  THE BASE DATE YEAR OF 1976 IS ADDED ON TO
 5857                /       CREATE THE ABSOLUTE YEAR.  [NOTE: MODERN ERA YEARS ARE EASILY CALCULATED AS AN
 5858                /       UNSIGNED 12-BIT VALUE.]  THE MONTH OFFSET IS ALSO CALCULATED ORIGIN 0 TO BE
 5859                /       USED AS AN OFFSET INTO A LOOKUP TABLE OF APPROPRIATE MONTH STRINGS.  THE DAY
 5860                /       OFFSET IS DELIBERATELY CALCULATED ORIGIN 1 TO ALLOW THE 12-BIT VALUE OF 0000
 5861                /       TO INDICATE THE SYSTEM DATE HAS NOT BEEN SET.
 5862   
 5863                /       THIS INITIAL RELEASE OF THE DATE FACILITY ALLOWED P?S/8 TO MAINTAIN A PROPER
 5864                /       DATE THROUGH THE END OF 1986.  [NOTE: THE PARTIAL 12TH YEAR WAS SUPPRESSED
 5865                /       WITHIN THE P?S/8 "DATE" PROGRAM TO AVOID CONFUSION.]
 5866   
 5867                /       SHORTLY AFTER THE INITIAL RELEASE OF OS/8 [WHEN THE NAME WAS CHANGED FROM
 5868                /       PS/8 AT THE REQUEST OF A DEC SALESMAN], VARIOUS OS/8 USERS EXPRESSED DEEP
 5869                /       CONCERN ABOUT OS/8'S DATE EXPIRATION.  [MANY WERE WELL AWARE OF THE EFFORTS ON
 5870                /       THE PDP-10 SYSTEMS THAT PREVENTED THE DATE RANGE EXPIRATION IN 1975.]  DEC
 5871                /       RELEASED AN "IMPROVED" OS/8 CLAIMING TO SUPPORT A DATE RANGE EXTENSION THROUGH
 5872                /       THE YEAR 2001; HOWEVER, THE ACTUAL IMPLEMENTATION IS QUITE DEFICIENT:
 5873   
 5874                /       1) OS/8 RECKONS FILE DATES IN ONLY ONE OF TWO POSSIBLE WAYS: A) THE DATE IS IN
 5875                /          THE RANGE OF 1970-1977 EXPRESSED AS THE OFFSET PAST THE START OF 1970; THIS
 5876                /          WAS EFFECTIVELY NOT CHANGED, B) THE DATE IS NOT STORED IN THE DIRECTORY.
 5877   
 5878                /          A SURPRISING NUMBER OF OS/8 USERS DELIBERATELY CONFIGURE DIRECTORIES
 5879                /          WITHOUT DATE SUPPORT.  THE UNDERLYING REASON IS THAT OS/8 DIRECTORIES ARE
 5880                /          QUITE INADEQUATE FOR MOST USERS; DUE TO THE OVERHEAD OF THE HALF-BAKED
 5881                /          IMPLEMENTATION OF DIRECTORY SUPPORT, OS/8'S INTERNAL STRUCTURE WAS NEVER
 5882                /          FINISHED.  A LINKED-LIST STRUCTURE IS USED TO ACCESS A LINEAR LIST OF
 5883                /          RECORDS THAT, AS A PRACTICAL MATTER, CANNOT BE EITHER EXPANDED, CONTRACTED
 5884                /          OR REORDERED.  IN HINDSIGHT, THIS ONLY MAKES THE ALREADY OVERLOADED
 5885                /          DIRECTORY STRUCTURE MORE PRONE TO OVERFLOWING THE DIRECTORY CAPABILITY WELL
 5886                /          BEFORE THE UNDERLYING STORAGE RUNS OUT.  BY ELIMINATING DATE WORD STORAGE,
 5887                /          AN APPRECIABLE QUANTITY OF ADDITIONAL FILES CAN BE STORED ON THE DEVICE.
 5888   
 5889                /          THE IMPLEMENTATION OF FILE DATES IN AN OS/8 DIRECTORY IS CONTROLLED BY AN
 5890                /          INTERNAL FACTOR KNOWN AS THE COUNT OF "ADDITIONAL INFORMATION WORDS".  THIS
 5891                /          WAS ONLY DEFINED FOR THE FIRST WORD AS THE FILE DATE VALUE FOR EACH FILE
 5892                /          ENTRY.  TO INCREASE THE DATE RANGE WOULD REQUIRE AN ADDITIONAL INFORMATION
 5893                /          WORD BEYOND THE FIRST ONE; HOWEVER, THIS WOULD ONLY FURTHER ALIENATE USERS
 5894                /          ALREADY REMOVING THE BASIC DATE INFORMATION TO INCREASE THE FILE CAPACITY
 5895                /          OF THE DIRECTORY.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 117

 5897                /       2) OS/8 IMPLEMENTATION OF THE SYSTEM DATE IN MEMORY WAS EXPANDED BY TWO BITS.
 5898                /          THIS THEORETICALLY MEANS THAT THE CURRENT YEAR OFFSET IS EXPANDED BY
 5899                /          ADDING ON 0-3 GROUPS OF EIGHT YEARS EXTENDING THE DATE RANGE TO 1970
 5900                /          THROUGH 2001.  [NOTE: THE YEAR 2001 IS ALREADY WELL IN THE PAST AS OF THE
 5901                /          WRITING OF THIS DOCUMENT; IT IS HIGHLY LIKELY THE RANGE CANNOT BE EXTENDED
 5902                /          ANY FURTHER.]  SINCE THE ACTUAL DATE IMPLEMENTATION DOES NOT STORE THE TWO
 5903                /          BITS FROM SYSTEM MEMORY, A FICTION WAS CREATED TO DESCRIBE THE FILE DATE:
 5904   
 5905                /          ALL FILE DATES ARE PRESUMED TO HAVE BEEN CREATED EITHER THIS YEAR OR THE
 5906                /          PREVIOUS SEVEN YEARS.  AS TIME PROGRESSES, THE ANOMALY OF THIS INCREASES.
 5907                /          THIS IS ESPECIALLY TROUBLESOME FOR FILES ACTUALLY CREATED EIGHT YEARS
 5908                /          EARLIER; THEY APPEAR TO BE IN THE FUTURE DAYS OF THE CURRENT YEAR.
 5909   
 5910                /       DURING THE EARLY YEARS OF THIS SHAM MODIFICATION, FEW USERS ACTUALLY HAD FILES
 5911                /       CREATED EIGHT YEARS EARLIER, AS WIDESPREAD ADOPTION OF OS/8 DIDN'T OCCUR UNTIL
 5912                /       ROUGHLY 1973-1974; AS SUCH, OS/8'S TRULY DEFICIENT IMPLEMENTATION DIDN'T
 5913                /       IMMEDIATELY AFFECT MANY USERS.  MANY WERE FOOLED BECAUSE IT WOULD NOT BE UNTIL
 5914                /       1980 BEFORE THE TRUE NATURE OF WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED WOULD BE MEANINGFUL TO
 5915                /       THEM PERSONALLY.  THE DEVELOPERS OF P?S/8 REALIZED THE FOLLY OF THIS SCHEME
 5916                /       IMMEDIATELY; ACTUAL SOURCE FILES WERE DEMONSTRATED THAT WERE CREATED IN 1970
 5917                /       REVEALING THE ANOMALY OF THE DIRECTORY LISTINGS CLAIMING THE FILES WERE
 5918                /       CREATED EIGHT YEARS LATER.  [NOTE: SOME OF THE ACTUAL FILES WERE CREATED PRIOR
 5919                /       TO 1970; THE CONTRIVED DATE OF 01-JAN-1970 WAS USED AS THE CLOSEST
 5920                /       APPROXIMATION OS/8 COULD ALLOW TO THE PROPER FILE DATES.]
 5921   
 5922                /       WHEN OS/8 WAS USED DURING THE WANING YEARS OF THE FICTIONAL IMPLEMENTATION,
 5923                /       MANY USERS RESORTED TO MANUAL EDITING OF DIRECTORY LISTINGS TO CREATE AN
 5924                /       ARTIFICIAL REALITY.  [THE LISTINGS WERE ACCURATE, BUT NO OS/8 UTILITY COULD
 5925                /       HAVE AUTOMATICALLY CREATED THE DIRECTORY PRINTOUT FILE.]  CERTAIN USERS SET
 5926                /       THE YEAR TO 1986 BECAUSE THE DAY OF THE WEEK CORRESPONDS TO THE SAME VALUES IN
 5927                /       2014.  [NOTE: CERTAIN OS/8 PROGRAMS SUFFER FROM THE INFAMOUS "Y2K" PROBLEM.
 5928                /       JUNK PRINTOUT OCCURS IF THE DATE IS SET PAST 1999.]
 5929   
 5930                /       AT THIS POINT, IT BECAME CLEAR SOMETHING HAD TO BE DONE TO EXTEND THE P?S/8
 5931                /       DATE RANGE SIGNIFICANTLY.  [THERE WAS A CLEAR NEED TO IMPLEMENT SOMETHING THAT
 5932                /       ACTUALLY COVERED THE CURRENT DATE WITHOUT ANOMALY; A QUICK FIX WAS SOUGHT TO
 5933                /       EXTEND THE DATE RANGE DRAMATICALLY.
 5934   
 5935                /       REVISING THE THEN-CURRENT METHOD, AN EXTENSION WAS CREATED ON PAPER; THIS
 5936                /       WOULD GENERALLY FOLLOW OS/8'S LEAD IN TERMS OF ADDING EXTENSION BITS TO
 5937                /       EXPRESS A FAR GREATER RANGE THAN 11 YEARS.  A DESIGN WAS ATTEMPTED TO EXTEND
 5938                /       THE RANGE TO A TOTAL OF 512 YEARS.  BECAUSE OF THE SIGNIFICENCE OF THE YEAR
 5939                /       1960 TO 12-BIT COMPUTER HISTORY, THIS WAS CHOSEN AS THE NEW BASE YEAR.
 5940   
 5941                /       FOR EACH INCREMENT BY ONE OF THE BASE GROUP FIELD OF SIX BITS, THE BASE YEAR
 5942                /       INCREASES BY EIGHT YEARS.  AS SUCH, 1976 STILL PLAYED A ROLE AS A BASE YEAR,
 5943                /       BUT THE ABSOLUTE ORIGIN BECAME 1960; THE SIX-BIT DATE GROUP WOULD BE SET TO
 5944                /       000010 TO ALLOW VALID DATES IN THE RANGE OF 1976-1983.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 118

 5946                /       UNFORTUNATELY, AT THE TIME OF THIS DESIGN MODIFICATION, THERE WERE NO
 5947                /       ADDITIONAL RESERVED BITS AVAILABLE WITHIN THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; A
 5948                /       MODIFIED SCHEME WAS IMPLEMENTED:
 5949   
 5950                /       1) ALL PROGRAMS CONCERNED WITH THE PRESENT DATE RESERVED A PATCHABLE WORD
 5951                /          WITHIN THE PROGRAM IMAGE TO ALLOW CUSTOM PATCHING OF THE CURRENT DATE GROUP
 5952                /          VALUE.  WHILE THERE WAS NO SPECIFIC DESIGNATED LOCATION, THE LOCATION
 5953                /          WITHIN EACH IMAGE WAS CHOSEN TO BE THE INITIAL CONTENTS OF A PAGE ZERO
 5954                /          LOCATION IF POSSIBLE; IN CERTAIN INSTANCES, EQUIVALENT LOCATIONS WERE
 5955                /          DEFINED WHEN THE ONLY PURPOSE OF THE DATE CALCULATION WAS IN CONJUNCTION
 5956                /          WITH OTHER ONCE-ONLY INITIALIZATION CODE.  A STATIC DATE STRING OF
 5957                /          CHARACTERS TO PRINT OUT THE CURRENT DATE ELSEWHERE IN THE PROGRAM WAS THE
 5958                /          ONLY DESIRED RESULT; AFTER THE CORRESPONDING STRING WAS STORED ELSEWHERE,
 5959                /          ALL OF THE ONCE-ONLY INITIALIZATION CODE WAS NO LONGER NEEDED; THE CODE
 5960                /          SPACE WAS OFTEN RECLAIMED AS AN INPUT BUFFER FOR THE PURPOSE OF SUBSEQUENT
 5961                /          FILE READING OR WRITING.
 5962   
 5963                /          A VIABLE P?S/8 SYSTEM WAS ACHIEVED BY PROPERLY PATCHING ALL AFFECTED
 5964                /          LOCATIONS WITHIN EACH IMAGE; THIS VALUE WOULD HAVE TO BE UPDATED NOMINALLY
 5965                /          EVERY EIGHT YEARS.
 5966   
 5967                /       2) RECOGNIZING THAT THE SYSTEM DATE WORD HAS AN INTRINSIC RANGE OF NEARLY
 5968                /          TWELVE YEARS, IT BECAME CLEAR THAT NEARLY EVERY THEORETICALLY POSSIBLE
 5969                /          CAN BE EXPRESSED IN TWO DIFFERENT WAYS: A) AN OFFSET OF LESS THAN EIGHT
 5970                /          YEARS FROM ONE DATE BASE GROUP VALUE, B) AN OFFSET OF GREATER THAN EIGHT
 5971                /          YEARS FROM THE PREVIOUS DATE BASE GROUP VALUE.
 5972   
 5973                /          AS SUCH, THERE IS EFFECTIVELY A "GRACE PERIOD" ON PERFORMING THIS DATE
 5974                /          GROUP MAINTENANCE OF NEARLY FOUR YEARS AFTER THE CHANGE OUGHT TO BE MADE.
 5975                /          IT WAS ACCEPTABLE TO DEFER THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE UPDATES.  [NOTE: DUE
 5976                /          TO THE RELATIVE FREQUENCY OF P?S/8 RELEASES TO A CONTROLLED DISTRIBUTION,
 5977                /          OVER TIME MOST USERS RECEIVED A SYSTEM ALREADY SET TO MAXIMIZE THE VIABLE
 5978                /          RANGE OF THE CURRENT DATE BASE GROUP; EXPECTATIONS WERE THAT AN EVENTUAL
 5979                /          RELEASE MADE AFTER THE NEXT EIGHT YEAR GROUP VALUE UPDATED WOULD BE RESET
 5980                /          UPON RELEASE; AT THE SOURCE CODE LEVEL, IT WAS ACCOMPLISHED BY MERELY
 5981                /          CHANGING ONE LINE WITHIN EACH AFFECTED SOURCE FILE; THE ACTUAL TEXT CHANGE
 5982                /          WAS ALWAYS THE SAME FOR ALL AFFECTED FILES; AS NEW PROGRAMS WERE RELEASED
 5983                /          WITH SIMILAR DATE REQUIREMENTS, THE SAME SOURCE CODE WAS COPIED INTO THE
 5984                /          SOURCE FILES OF THE NEW PROGRAMS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 119

 5986                /       EVENTUALLY THE NEED TO ACCURATELY STORE FILE DATES WAS CONSIDERED.  [NOTE: TFS
 5987                /       FILES DO NOT SUPPORT FILE DATES.]
 5988   
 5989                /       WHILE SEVERAL EXTENDED DIRECTORY ALTERNATIVES WERE BEING DISCUSSED [WHERE SOME
 5990                /       DESIGNS MIGHT STORE THE CURRENT SYSTEM DATE IN COMPLETE FORM], THE
 5991                /       IMPLEMENTATION OF ADDITIONAL FEATURES WITHIN THE EXTENDED DIRECTORY WAS
 5992                /       TABLED.  [NOTE: AS A PRACTICAL MATTER, THE EXTENDED DIRECTORY IS ONLY USEFUL
 5993                /       IN APPLICATIONS RELATED TO TEMPORARILY CREATING INDEFINITE LENGTH SOURCE
 5994                /       FILES, GENERALLY ACCOMPLISHED USING THE EXTENDED DIRECTORY FEATURES OF THE
 5995                /       P?S/8 "CONVRT" PROGRAM, A BI-DIRECTIONAL TEXT FILE CONVERSION UTILITY BETWEEN
 5996                /       OS/8 TEXT FORMAT AND AN EXTENDED VARIANT OF THE TEXT FORMAT CONTAINED WITHIN
 5997                /       TFS FILES WITHOUT LINE NUMBERS.  THIS FEATURE WAS AN OUTGROWTH OF AN EARLIER
 5998                /       [NOW OBSOLETE] WORK KNOWN AS "BIGCON" WHICH PERFORMED THE SAME FUNCTION, BUT
 5999                /       IS DANGEROUSLY PASSED THE BLOCK NUMBER OF A TFS FILE.  ALL STORAGE ON THE
 6000                /       DEVICE FROM THAT POINT ONWARD IS SUBJECT TO REPLACEMENT BY THE CONVERTED TEXT;
 6001                /       THE CONSOLE SWITCH REGISTER IS USED TO SET THE UPPER BLOCK LIMIT OF THE
 6002                /       CONVERSION.  MULTIPLE FILES ARE SUPPORTED ON DIFFERENT LOGICAL DEVICE UNITS
 6003                /       ADDRESSED BY THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  SINCE AT LEAST SIX LOGICAL UNITS ARE
 6004                /       GENERALLY AVAILABLE ON A SYSTEM OF REASONABLE SIZE, THE EFFECTIVE SIZE OF THE
 6005                /       CONVERSION EXCEEDS THE CAPABILITY OF MOST OS/8 SYSTEMS IN TERMS OF THE ABILITY
 6006                /       TO EXPRESS A SINGLE OS/8 TEXT FILE.  WHILE EIGHT LOGICAL UNITS ARE GENERALLY
 6007                /       AVAILABLE, ONE MUST BE RESERVED FOR THE OS/8 MEDIA; ANOTHER IS GENERALLY
 6008                /       RESERVED FOR OTHER FUNCTIONS SUCH AS SCRATCH STORAGE WHEN ASSEMBLING THE
 6009                /       PROGRAM WITH P?S/8 PAL.]
 6010   
 6011                /       EVENTUALLY, THE CONCEPT OF THE P?S/8 SHELL WAS CONSIDERED; THIS WOULD BE A
 6012                /       COMPLETE OVERLAY TO THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM THAT WOULD SUPPORT A MUCH LARGER
 6013                /       FILE STRUCTURE ACCESSED THROUGH NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS WITH GREATLY IMPROVED
 6014                /       ABILITY TO ACCESS HUGE AMOUNTS OF STORAGE [IF AVAILABLE] WITH A DIRECTORY
 6015                /       STRUCTURE GEARED TOWARDS HANDLING A LARGE NUMBER OF FILES AS NECESSARY.
 6016   
 6017                /       WHILE ADDITIONAL WORK WAS DONE ON REFINING THE SYSTEM HANDLER, IT WAS REALIZED
 6018                /       THE SPACE FOR THE ADDITIONAL SIX BITS OF THE DATE BASE GROUP VALUE COULD BE
 6019                /       PLACED INTO MEMORY, REQUIRING ONLY A SYSTEM WIDE MINOR MODIFICATION OF
 6020                /       ONCE-ONLY INITIALIZATION CODE IN EVERY PROGRAM THAT PROCESSES THE POSSIBILITY
 6021                /       OF PASSED OUTPUT FILES.
 6022   
 6023                /       THE EARLIER DEFINITION OF THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD "SOUTCT" [07607] WAS THE
 6024                /       COUNT OF HOW MANY [IF ANY] OF THE PASSED FILE HANDLES STARTING AT "SFILES"
 6025                /       [07757] [IF ANY] WERE OUTPUT FILES.  [ANY ADDITIONAL ENTRIES IN THE LIST ARE
 6026                /       INPUT FILES.]
 6027   
 6028                /       SINCE THERE ARE AT MOST 17 FILES IN THE LIST, THIS COUNT CANNOT EXCEED FIVE
 6029                /       SIGNIFICANT BITS.  AS SUCH, ALL REFERENCES TO THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD MADE
 6030                /       IN ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS THAT HANDLE THIS WORD SHOULD ADD ONE INSTRUCTION:
 6031   
 6032                /               TAD I   (SOUTCT)                /GET THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD
 6033                /               AND     (37)                    /STRIP OUT OTHER BITS
 6034   
 6035                /       WHEN ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS HAVE BEEN CONSISTENTLY UPDATED TO CONFORM TO
 6036                /       THIS EASILY-IMPLEMENTED CHANGE, THE REST OF THE BITS IN "SOUTCT" CAN BE
 6037                /       REPURPOSED.  BITS [1-6] ARE NOW DEFINED AS THE IN-MEMORY COPY OF THE CURRENT
 6038                /       DATE BASE GROUP VALUE; WHEN ADDED TO THE LOW-ORDER SYSTEM DATE WORD "SDATE"
 6039                /       [07610], THE RESULT IS A UNIQUE SYSTEM DATE EXPRESSED IN 18 BITS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 120

 6041                /       SINCE THERE IS NO PARTICULAR NEED TO EXTEND THE LOW-ORDER DATE WORD'S RANGE
 6042                /       BEYOND EIGHT YEARS [AND HAVING TO DEAL WITH THE NOW UNDESIRABLE ANOMALY], THE
 6043                /       DATE HAS NOW BEEN REDEFINED THUS:
 6044   
 6045                /       WORD    BITS    MEANING
 6046   
 6047                /       SOUTCT  1-6     HIGH-ORDER SIX BITS OF THE FULL DATE.
 6048   
 6049                /       SDATE   0-2     RELATIVE YEAR OFFSET IN THE RANGE 0-7.
 6050   
 6051                /       SDATE   3-6     RELATIVE MONTH OFFSET IN THE RANGE OF 0-11.
 6052   
 6053                /       SDATE   7-11    CURRENT DAY OF THE MONTH IN THE RANGE OF 1-31.
 6054   
 6055                /       AS WAS FIRST USED WITH THE PREVIOUS DATE FORMAT, THE RELATIVE DAY OF THE
 6056                /       MONTH IS CALCULATED ORIGIN 1; WHEN THE DAY BITS ARE SET TO 00000, THE SYSTEM
 6057                /       IS CONSIDERED UNDATED.  [NOTE: ALL OTHER BITS SHOULD BE SET TO ZERO WHEN THE
 6058                /       SYSTEM IS UNDATED; VALIDATION ROUTINES CAN DECLARE THE COMBINATION OF ALL DAY
 6059                /       BITS SET TO ZERO WHILE ANY OTHER BITS ARE NON-ZERO AS AN ILLEGAL COMBINATION.]
 6060   
 6061                /       DATE RANGE SELECTION/FILTERING CAN STILL BE ACCOMPLISHED BY ARITHMETIC MEANS;
 6062                /       HOWEVER, DOUBLE-PRECISION ROUTINES MUST BE USED TO MAINTAIN 18-BIT ACCURACY.
 6063   
 6064                /       THE DATE RANGE STARTING YEAR WAS EVENTUALLY RECONSIDERED.  ALL NEW WORK WILL
 6065                /       ALLOW SYSTEM DATES [AND FILE DATES WITHIN THE P?S/8 SHELL DIRECTORIES] FROM
 6066                /       1900 THROUGH 2411.  WITH VERY FEW EXCEPTIONS, ANY FILE PERTINENT TO ANY
 6067                /       COMPUTING SUBJECT SHOULD BE CORRECTLY EXPRESSED IN THE DATE RANGE STARTING
 6068                /       WITH THE DAWN OF THE TWENTIETH CENTURY.  [NOTE: MANY IBM SYSTEMS USE THE
 6069                /       IDENTICAL STARTING DATE.  MANY ACTUAL PDP-8 AND PDP-10 FILES CANNOT BE
 6070                /       EXPRESSED PROPERLY WITHIN SO-CALLED "MODERN" OPERATING SYSTEMS WITH STARTING
 6071                /       DATES OF 1970 OR EVEN 1980.]
 6072   
 6073                /       END OF APPENDIX I.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 121

 6075                /       APPENDIX J.
 6076   
 6077                /       P?S/8 CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD ISSUES.
 6078   
 6079                /       THE P?S/8 CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD MANAGES MEMORY AVAILABLE TO ALL P?S/8
 6080                /       UTILITIES AND COMPLIANT USER PROGRAMS.  ADDITIONALLY, THE STATUS OF THE
 6081                /       CONSOLE OVERLAY AND SOFTWARE ABORT BITS ARE STORED HERE.
 6082   
 6083                /       BITS    MEANING
 6084   
 6085                /       0-2     THE PHYSICAL HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD AVAILABLE.  THIS IS BASED ON DYNAMIC
 6086                /               DISCOVERY AT BOOTUP TIME.  VALUES RANGE FROM 000 FOR A 4K MACHINE TO
 6087                /               111 FOR A 32K MACHINE.
 6088   
 6089                /       3-5     THE LOGICAL HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD POSSIBLE.  ON MOST CONFIGURATIONS
 6090                /               THIS VALUE IS IDENTICAL TO THE PHYSICAL HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD CONTAINED
 6091                /               IN BITS[0-2] AS DESCRIBED ABOVE; ON CERTAIN SYSTEMS THIS VALUE IS ONE
 6092                /               MEMORY FIELD LOWER DUE TO ONE OR MORE CONFIGURATION ISSUES.
 6093   
 6094                /       6-8     THE LOGICAL HIGHEST FIELD CURRENTLY ALLOWED. POSSIBLE SETTINGS RANGE
 6095                /               FROM 000 THROUGH THE LOGICAL HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD CONTAINED IN
 6096                /               BITS[3-5] AS DESCRIBED ABOVE.
 6097   
 6098                /         9     LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY STATUS.  IF THIS BIT IS SET, ALL
 6099                /               OVERLAY-COMPLIANT PROGRAMS DIRECT INPUT AND OUTPUT FOR THE CONSOLE
 6100                /               TERMINAL AND LINEPRINTER TO DEDICATED CONSOLE OVERLAY ROUTINES LOCATED
 6101                /               IN THE PHYSICAL HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD CONTAINED IN BITS[0-2] AS
 6102                /               DESCRIBED ABOVE.
 6103   
 6104                /        10     LOGICAL CONTROL-B ABORT STATUS.
 6105   
 6106                /        11     LOGICAL CONTROL-C ABORT STATUS.
 6107   
 6108                /       THE PHYSICAL HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD IS DEFINED AS THE HIGHEST DETECTABLE MEMORY
 6109                /       FIELD [UP TO FIELD 7] ASSUMING THE STANDARD ARCHITECTURE [KNOWN AS THE KM8A AS
 6110                /       IMPLEMENTED ON THE PDP-8/A].  [NOTE: ALL PRIOR MODELS ARE COMPATIBLE WITH THIS
 6111                /       EXTENDED MEMORY SPECIFICATION TO THE EXTENT EXTENDED MEMORY IS AVAILABLE;
 6112                /       CERTAIN MACHINES CANNOT SUPPORT THE FULL MEMORY EXTENSION TO 32K.]  ON THE
 6113                /       PDP-8/A, MEMORY CAN BE EXTENDED BEYOND 32K IN SEVERAL WAYS:
 6114   
 6115                /       1) THE DEC KT8A CAN EXTEND MEMORY TO EITHER 64K OR 128K.  THE 64K EXTENSION IS
 6116                /          ACCESSED USING EXTENDED IOT INSTRUCTIONS BASED ON THE STANDARD INSTRUCTIONS
 6117                /          TO ACCESS THE FIRST 32K.  EXPANSION IS ALSO POSSIBLE TO 128K BY ALSO USING
 6118                /          ALL IOT CODES FROM DEVICE 30 THROUGH DEVICE 37; THIS CAUSES LOSS OF
 6119                /          COMPATIBILITY WITH MANY EXISTANT PERIPHERAL DEVICES.
 6120   
 6121                /          DMA CAPABILITY OF ALL DEVICES IS EXTENDED BY A SERIES OF 13 TWO-BIT
 6122                /          REGISTERS; THERE ARE 13 DMA PRIORITIES ON THE OMNIBUS.  SINCE THERE IS NO
 6123                /          WAY TO DYNAMICALLY DETERMINE DMA PRIORITIES, EVERY SYSTEM ATTEMPTING TO
 6124                /          PERFORM EXTENDED DMA TRANSFERS MUST BE CUSTOM-CONFIGURED AT THE DEVICE
 6125                /          LEVEL.  [NOTE: THE OMNIBUS DOES NOT DEMAND SETTING A DMA PRIORITY; AS SUCH,
 6126                /          THERE IS PRIORITY 0 THOROUGH 11 AND NONE, WHERE 0 IS THE HIGHEST PRIORITY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 122

 6128                /          P?S/8 CAN SUPPORT THE OPERATION OF THE KT8A, BUT AS IMPLEMENTED, P?S/8
 6129                /          CANNOT LOAD ANY PROGRAMS OR DATA BEYOND THE FIRST 32K; IT IS THE PROGRAM'S
 6130                /          RESPONSIBILITY FOR ALL ADDITIONAL MEMORY BANK ACCESS.
 6131   
 6132                /       2) THE CESI MEC8 SUPPORTS EXTENDED MEMORY UP TO EITHER 128K OR 512K.  AT THE
 6133                /          BUSS LEVEL, THE 128K CONFIGURATION IS HARDWARE COMPATIBLE WITH THE KT8A.
 6134                /          PROGRAMMING EXTENDED MEMORY IS NOT COMPATIBLE WITH THE KM8A.  THE MEC8 CAN
 6135                /          DISABLE EXTENDED SUPPORT; IN THIS MODE, THE MACHINE APPEARS TO HAVE 32K
 6136                /          ONLY; KM8A COMPATIBILITY IS MAINTAINED IN THIS MODE.  ALL PREVAILING PDP-8
 6137                /          OPERATING SYSTEMS MUST USE THIS MODE TO OPERATE.
 6138   
 6139                /          THE MEC8 NATIVE MODE IS EASILY EXTENDED TO 512K MAXIMUM.  HARDWARE JUMPERS
 6140                /          MUST BE MODIFIED TO CHANGE THE WAY THE MEMORY EXTENSION IS ADDRESSED; THE
 6141                /          INSTRUCTIONS ARE EASILY EXTENDED FOR THE FULL 512K MEMORY SUPPORT; HOWEVER,
 6142                /          NONE OF THE OPERATIONS ARE KM8A-COMPATIBLE.  [NOTE: A MINOR ADVANTAGE OF
 6143                /          THE MEC8 OVER THE 128K VARIANT OF THE KT8A IS DEVICE CODES 30 THROUGH 37
 6144                /          ARE NOT RESERVED BY THE MEC8.  HOWEVER, THE MEC8 DOES NOT MANAGE THE
 6145                /          MEMORY BANK EXTENSION FOR DMA PERIPHERALS.  THE ONLY PERIPHERAL THAT CAN
 6146                /          PERFORM EXTENDED TRANSFERS IN THIS ENVIRONMENT IS THE MDC8 SCSI CONTROLLER.
 6147                /          IN THIS MODE, TRANSFERS OF UP TO 32K ARE POSSIBLE FOR ANY MEMORY BANK.  THE
 6148                /          MDC8 CAN ONLY BE SET TO PRIORITY 0 OR 1.  ANY OTHER PERIPHERALS MUST BE SET
 6149                /          TO A LOWER PRIORITY SETTING [2 THROUGH 11 OR NONE].  HOWEVER, ALL OTHER
 6150                /          PERIPHERAL TRANSFERS ARE LIMITED TO THE FIRST 32K OF MEMORY.
 6151   
 6152                /          THE ONLY SOFTWARE THAT RUNS IN THIS MODE IS OMNI-8, WHICH WAS SPECIFICALLY
 6153                /          TAILORED FOR THIS ENVIRONMENT.  32K DMA TRANSFERS ARE ADVANTAGEOUS TO ROLL
 6154                /          IN AND OUT OF MEMORY ENTIRE 32K TASK IMAGES.  [NOTE: OMNI-8 SIMULATES THE
 6155                /          KM8A ENVIRONMENT IN EACH OF AS MANY AS 128 TASKS.  AS MANY AS 15 CAN BE
 6156                /          RESIDENT IN MEMORY AT ANY ONE TIME.]
 6157   
 6158                /          OMNI-8 SUPPORT OF THE MDC8 REQUIRES 256 WORDS/BLOCK, A STANDARD VARIANT
 6159                /          BLOCKSIZE FOR THIS DEVICE.  HOWEVER, OS/8 CANNOT RUN ON THIS CONFIGURATION.
 6160                /          P?S/8 CAN SUPPORT A VERSION FOR THIS SYSTEM REQUIRING EXTENDED MEMORY
 6161                /          [HARDLY A PROBLEM SINCE ALL CONFIGURATIONS WILL HAVE 32K].  HOWEVER, THE
 6162                /          MEC8 MUST BE SET BACK TO KM8A-COMPATIBILITY MODE BY THROWING A SWITCH ON
 6163                /          THE MODULE [PHYSICALLY SIMILAR TO THE FORMAT SWITCH ON THE TD8E].
 6164   
 6165                /          OMNI-8 DEVELOPMENT FROM OS/8 REQUIRES ANOTHER DEVICE SUCH AS THE RK8E/RK05
 6166                /          WHEN THE MEC8 IS SET TO KM8A COMPATIBILITY MODE; THE MDC8 IS INCAPABLE OF
 6167                /          OS/8 SUPPORT EVEN WITH NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS.  GENERATION OF OMNI-8 IS
 6168                /          ACHIEVED BY USING OS/8 TO LOAD UP TO 32K OF OMNI-8 SYSTEM GENERATION CODE,
 6169                /          PAUSING THE MACHINE AND CHANGING THE HARDWARE OVER TO THE FULL MEC8 MEMORY
 6170                /          MODE.  THE OMNI-8 GENERATION PACKAGE THEN WRITES ITSELF OUT TO THE MDC8;
 6171                /          OMNI-8 IS A BOOTABLE APPLICATION SYSTEM WITH ITS OWN INTERNAL RULES.
 6172                /          WITHIN OMNI-8, THE ENTIRE OS/8 ENVIRONMENT IS EMULATED; A SIMILAR VERSION
 6173                /          OF P?S/8 [OR COS-300/310] CAN RUN USING THE SAME EMULATED HARDWARE
 6174                /          ENVIRONMENT AS OS/8.  HOWEVER, A P?S/8 VARIANT FOR THE 256 WORD BLOCKS CAN
 6175                /          ALSO BE BOOTED TO THE SAME HARDWARE [IN KM8A COMPATIBILITY MODE].  THUS,
 6176                /          P?S/8 IS ARGUABLY A BETTER DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT FOR OMNI-8 PROGRAM
 6177                /          DEVELOPMENT.  [ADDITIONAL HARDWARE WOULD NOT BE NECESSARY; CONSIDERING THE
 6178                /          SCSI DISKS USED ON THE MDC8 ARE OVER 16 TIMES AS LARGE AS THE RK05, THIS
 6179                /          WOULD ELIMINATE A LOT OF OVERHEAD.]


