 6322                /       IF THE SIZE OF PHYSICAL MEMORY IS CHANGED FOR ANY REASON [OR A REMOVABLE
 6323                /       SYSTEM DEVICE IS BOOTED ON A DIFFERENT COMPUTER WITH A DIFFERENT MEMORY
 6324                /       CONFIGURATION], CERTAIN OF THE MEMORY SIZE VALUES MAY BE CONFUSED; CERTAIN
 6325                /       IMPLEMENTATIONS OF THE CORE COMMAND WRITE OUT THE MAXIMUM LOGICAL CORE SIZE AS
 6326                /       WELL AS THE CURRENT LOGICAL CORE SIZE.  THIS SITUATION CAN ALWAYS BE REMEDIED
 6327                /       BY USING THE "CORE ALL" COMMAND TO RESET ALL VALUES TO THE MAXIMUM CURRENTLY
 6328                /       AVAILABLE; AFTERWARDS, THE CURRENT LOGICAL CORE SIZE CAN OPTIONALLY BE LOWERED
 6329                /       AS NECESSARY.
 6330   
 6331                /       IF BIT[9] OF THE CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD IS SET, THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY
 6332                /       IS IN EFFECT.  ALL CONFORMING PROGRAMS WILL SUBSTITUTE LOGICAL CALLS TO THE
 6333                /       OVERLAY INSTEAD OF THE NORMAL PHYSICAL SUBROUTINE CALLS MADE WITH RESPECT TO
 6334                /       ALL INPUT AND OUTPUT TO THE DEVICE 03/04 CONSOLE TERMINAL AND THE DEVICE 66
 6335                /       SYSTEM LINEPRINTER.  CALLS ARE GENERALLY MADE IN A MANNER AS DESCRIBED BELOW:
 6336   
 6337                /               CDF     MYFLD           /INDICATE CALLING FIELD WHICH WILL BE RETAINED
 6338   
 6339                /               CIF     OVLYFLD         /THIS IS "CIF" TO THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD
 6340                /               TAD     CHAR            /LATEST CHARACTER IF OUTPUT; CLEAR AC IF INPUT
 6341                /               JMS     20              /CALL THE ROUTINE
 6342                /               JMP     .-3             /IMMEDIATE RETURN; RESOURCE NOT AVAILABLE
 6343                /               NOP                     /SKIP RETURN, THE RESOURCE WAS PROCESSED; THE
 6344                /                                       /AC IS UNCHANGED IF OUTPUT; THE LATEST
 6345                /                                       /CHARACTER IS IN THE AC IF INPUT
 6346   
 6347                /       ROUTINES EXIST TO SEPARATELY HANDLE CONSOLE INPUT AND CONSOLE OUTPUT.  THE
 6348                /       LOGICAL LINEPRINTER CALL RESEMBLES THE CONSOLE OUTPUT ROUTINE.  FOR MOST
 6349                /       LINEPRINTER OUTPUT, THERE IS AN INTERNAL BUFFER WITHIN THE CONSOLE OVERLAY
 6350                /       THAT CAN DRAMATICALLY INCREASE PERFORMANCE WITHOUT USING INTERRUPTS DUE TO
 6351                /       INTELLIGENT BUFFERING AND DEVICE FLAG HANDLING.  THE CALL ALWAYS MAKES THE
 6352                /       USUAL RETURN, BUT NOT NECESSARILY WHEN THE LATEST CHARACTER HAS BEEN PRINTED;
 6353                /       THE RETURN MIGHT INDICATE THE CHARACTER HAS BEEN PLACED INTO THE INTERNAL
 6354                /       BUFFER; ADDITIONALLY, ANY CHARACTERS WAITING IN THE BUFFER WILL BE PROCESSED
 6355                /       AS LONG AS THERE IS LITTLE OR NO WAITING TIME; AS SOON AS ANY BUFFERED
 6356                /       CHARACTER [OR THE LATEST CHARACTER IF THE BUFFER IS EMPTY] IS TAKING EXCESSIVE
 6357                /       TIME TO CLEAR THE FLAG, NO FURTHER ATTEMPTS WILL BE MADE TO OUTPUT CHARACTERS
 6358                /       AT THIS TIME.  BY NATURE, LINEPRINTERS ARE CAPABLE OF PROCESSING MANY
 6359                /       CHARACTERS IN 15 MICROSECONDS OR LESS EACH UNTIL INTERNALLY, THE PRINTER IS
 6360                /       SUFFICIENTLY BACKED UP; AT THAT POINT, IT IS PREDICTABLE THE PRINTER FLAG WILL
 6361                /       NOT RAISE AGAIN FOR MANY MILLISECONDS [OR PERHAPS LONGER].
 6362   
 6363                /       TO GUARANTEE THE ENTIRE PRINT BUFFER IS PRINTED BEFORE ANY ORDERLY PROGRAM
 6364                /       EXIT, RECEIPT OF A <FF> CHARACTER CAUSES THE CALL TO WAIT UNTIL THE ENTIRE
 6365                /       BUFFER EMPTIES [INCLUDING THE <FF> CHARACTER].  [NOTE: LINEPRINTER FORMATTING
 6366                /       RULES ARE SUCH THAT ALL PRINTING ENDS WITH A <FF> CHARACTER FOR ALL PRINTED
 6367                /       PAGES; EVEN IF SLOPPILY ALLOWED TO OVERFLOW ONTO ADDITIONAL PAGES, THE PROGRAM
 6368                /       IS OBLIGATED TO OUTPUT A <FF> CHARACTER TO FINALIZE THE LAST PRINTED PAGE
 6369                /       JUST PRIOR TO EXIT.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 127

 6371                /       A FOURTH CALL IS ALSO IMPLEMENTED TO SUPPORT A POTENTIAL LINEPRINTER REVERSE
 6372                /       CHANNEL INPUT.  ON CERTAIN PRINTERS, A BI-DIRECTIONAL SERIAL INTERFACE IS IN
 6373                /       EFFECT.  IN THESE CONFIGURATIONS, THE LINEPRINTER OUTPUT CALL HANDLES THIS
 6374                /       INTERNALLY; PROGRAMS THAT OPTIONALLY PARTICIPATE IN THE REVERSE CHANNEL INPUT
 6375                /       PROTOCOL MAY SPEED LINEPRINTER THROUGHPUT; HOWEVER, FULL COMPATIBILITY IS
 6376                /       AVAILABLE MERELY BY CALLING THE LOGICAL OUTPUT ROUTINE.  [NOTE: SERIAL
 6377                /       PRINTERS HAVE INTERNAL HARDWARE BUFFERS; THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY
 6378                /       CONFIGURATION FOR SERIAL PRINTERS DOES NOT PROVIDE ADDITIONAL SOFTWARE
 6379                /       BUFFERS.]
 6380   
 6381                /       AN OVERALL LOGICAL INTERRUPT ROUTINE IS ALSO IMPLEMENTED; IT IS CALLED BY
 6382                /       EXECUTION OF A "JMS 0" INSTRUCTION REMINISCENT OF THE ACTION OF THE INTERRUPT
 6383                /       HARDWARE.  INTERRUPT-DRIVEN PROGRAMS SHOULD IMPLEMENT ALL FIVE SUBROUTINE
 6384                /       CALLS TO PROPERLY HANDLE LOGICAL INTERRUPTS; ALL CALLS SHOULD BE MADE AHEAD OF
 6385                /       ANY PHYSICAL DEVICES.  IF IMPLEMENTED CORRECTLY, HARDWARE FLAG-HANDLING
 6386                /       INTERRUPT SERVICE ROUTINES WILL NOT INTERFERE WITH THE LOGICAL ROUTINES FOR
 6387                /       THE SAME DEVICES.  THIS ALLOWS FULL CONSOLE SUPPORT ON OTHER DEVICES WHILE
 6388                /       STILL ALLOWING FOR INPUT AND OUTPUT ON THE NORMAL DEVICES EVEN WHILE USING
 6389                /       INTERRUPTS.  [NOTE: SOME LOGICAL CONSOLE CONFIGURATIONS ADDRESS ALTERNATE
 6390                /       CONSOLE TERMINALS ON A DIFFERENT INTERFACE SUCH AS DEVICE 10/11 FOR THE PT08
 6391                /       OR DEVICE 40/41 FOR A SUITABLY CONFIGURED KL8-TYPE SERIAL INTERFACE.  IT IS
 6392                /       THE PROGRAM'S RESPONSIBILITY TO DEAL WITH THE NORMAL DEVICES WHEN THE INPUT
 6393                /       AND OUTPUT IS LOGICALLY REDIRECTED; ONE RECOMMENDED METHOD IS TO LIMIT CONSOLE
 6394                /       SUPPORT TO CHECKING ONLY FOR ABORT CHARACTERS SUCH AS CONTROL-C.  THIS WOULD
 6395                /       ALLOW PROGRAM EXIT INITIATED FROM EITHER THE PHYSICAL OR LOGICAL CONSOLE
 6396                /       DEVICE WHEN BOTH SETS OF HARDWARE ARE IN USE.]  [NOTE: RECENT RELEASES OF
 6397                /       P?S/8 FOCAL IMPLEMENT CONSOLE HANDLING FULLY CONFORMING TO THIS DESIGN.]
 6398   
 6399                /       LOGICAL ABORT ISSUES.
 6400   
 6401                /       ALL DECMATE SYSTEMS ARE INCAPABLE OF PROPER CONSOLE INPUT DUE TO HARDWARE
 6402                /       INCOMPATIBILITY WITH THE PDP-8 CONSOLE DEVICE.  [NOTE: THE STANDARD CONSOLE
 6403                /       HARDWARE IS COMPATIBLE IN ALL MODELS FROM THE PDP-5 THROUGH THE VT78.  VARIOUS
 6404                /       NON-DEC HARDWARE IS ALSO FULLY COMPATIBLE.]
 6405   
 6406                /       WHILE CERTAIN OF THE DEVICE 03/04 INSTRUCTIONS ARE REMINISCENT OF THE ORIGINAL
 6407                /       IMPLEMENTATION, ANY PROGRAM ATTEMPTING TO USE ANYTHING BUT THE MOST SIMPLISTIC
 6408                /       DESIGN WILL SIMPLY NOT FUNCTION CORRECTLY ON DECMATES.
 6409   
 6410                /       IN PARTICULAR, THE ABILITY TO PASS PROGRAM ABORT STATUS VIA THE KEYBOARD INPUT
 6411                /       FLAG DOES NOT EXIST ON DECMATES; THE "KRS" [6034] INSTRUCTION WAS REDESIGNED
 6412                /       IN A MANNER RENDERING IT USELESS FOR THE ORIGINAL PURPOSE.  THE "KSF" [6031]
 6413                /       INSTRUCTION WAS MODIFIED TO NOT ONLY SKIP IF THE INPUT FLAG IS SET, BUT TO
 6414                /       ALSO CLEAR THE FLAG; THERE IS SIMPLY NO WAY TO PASS ABORT STATUS BACK TO THE
 6415                /       SYSTEM KERNEL UPON PROGRAM EXIT AS HAS BEEN CLASSICALLY DEFINED IN ALL PDP-8
 6416                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS [INCLUDING P?S/8].


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 128

 6418                /       TO SOLVE THIS PROBLEM, RECENT RELEASES OF P?S/8 SUPPORT LOGICAL ABORT BITS
 6419                /       FOR BOTH SUPPORTED ABORT CHARACTERS.
 6420   
 6421                /       BIT[11] OF THE CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD IS SET IF A PROGRAM EXITS DUE TO
 6422                /       DETECTION OF CONTROL-C.  THIS IS THE UNIVERSAL EXIT METHOD USED IN MOST PDP-8
 6423                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS.  [NOTE: IF A P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAM DETECTS IT IS RUNNING ON
 6424                /       A COMPATIBLE MACHINE, IT IS NOT NECESSARY TO HANDLE THE ABORT LOGICALLY; THE
 6425                /       NORMAL CONSOLE HARDWARE IS ALSO TESTED; HOWEVER, THE HARDWARE ABORT CANNOT BE
 6426                /       DETECTED ON DECMATES.]
 6427   
 6428                /       BIT[10] OF THE CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD IS SET IF A PROGRAM EXITS DUE TO
 6429                /       DETECTION OF CONTROL-B.  THIS IS A P?S/8-SPECIFIC EXTENSION FOR ABORT PURPOSES
 6430                /       IMPORTANT TO P?S/8 BATCH OPERATIONS.  WHEN BATCH IS RUNNING, CONTROL-C WILL
 6431                /       ABORT THE CURRENT PROGRAM; HOWEVER, THE BATCH WILL ATTEMPT TO CONTINUE TO RUN
 6432                /       TO PROCESS OTHER PROGRAMS.  CONTROL-B WILL ABORT ALL BATCH OPERATIONS; WHEN
 6433                /       RUN WITHOUT BATCH, BOTH CHARACTERS ABORT THE CURRENT PROGRAM.
 6434   
 6435                /       ALL CONFORMING PROGRAMS CHECK TO SEE IF THE CPU IS THE HARRIS 6120; IF THIS IS
 6436                /       THE CASE, THE PHYSICAL ABORT-CHECKING ROUTINES ARE MODIFIED INTO HANDLING
 6437                /       LOGICAL ABORTS; THE APPROPRIATE ABORT BIT IS SET WHEN THE CORRESPONDING
 6438                /       CHARACTER IS DETECTED.
 6439   
 6440                /       AS SUCH, P?S/8 IS THE ONLY PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEM THAT CAN ALSO RUN ON THE
 6441                /       ENTIRE DECMATE SERIES WITHOUT BASTARDIZING THE ENTIRE SYSTEM.  [NOTE: OS/8
 6442                /       CANNOT RUN ON A DECMATE; THE VARIANT SYSTEM GENERALLY REFERRED TO AS "OS/278
 6443                /       V2" IS A SERIOUSLY FLAWED SYSTEM.  THERE ARE MANY QUIRKS STEMMING FROM THE
 6444                /       HARDWARE-RELATED PROBLEM AS WELL AS FLAWS IN IMPLEMENTATION NOT TRACEABLE TO
 6445                /       HARDWARE INCOMPATIBILITY.  IT IS KNOWN THAT THE QUALITY OF PROGRAMMING
 6446                /       EXPERTISE IN THE 12-BIT COMPUTER GROUP WITHIN DEC WENT DOWNHILL WHEN THE
 6447                /       DECMATES WERE INTRODUCED; ONE SPECIFIC POINT IS WORTH NOTING:  WITHIN CERTAIN
 6448                /       OS/278 SOURCE FILES IS A SMALL CHART CLAIMING TO BE THE WAY TO PROGRAM THE
 6449                /       CONSOLE INTERFACE COMPARING PDP-8 TO DECMATE TO POINT OUT THE DIFFERENCES.
 6450                /       UNFORTUNATELY, WHILE MANY PROGRAMS SLAVISHLY FOLLOWED THIS CHART, IT HAS
 6451                /       ERRONEOUS INFORMATION DESCRIBING SOMETHING QUITE DIFFERENT FROM ACTUAL DECMATE
 6452                /       CONSOLE HARDWARE.  THIS IS THE APPARENT ROOT CAUSE FOR VARIOUS BUGS IN
 6453                /       PROGRAMS SUCH AS OS/278 "ODT" NOT ACTUALLY RELATED TO CONSOLE HARDWARE
 6454                /       INCOMPATIBILITY PER SE; NONE OF THESE BUGS OUGHT TO EXIST; ONLY PROBLEMS
 6455                /       RELATED TO PROGRAM EXIT SHOULD BE PRESENT ALBEIT UNFIXABLE; OS/8 CANNOT
 6456                /       SUPPORT THE RIGOROUS CONSOLE ROUTINES UNIVERSALLY USED WITHIN ALL CONFORMING
 6457                /       P?S/8 PROGRAMS; NOT ONLY IS THE DECMATE INCOMPATIBILITY PROGRAM SOLVED, ALL
 6458                /       CONFORMING P?S/8 PROGRAMS CORRECTLY HANDLE ALL CONTROL-S/CONTROL-Q FLOW
 6459                /       CONTROL ISSUES AS WELL, A KNOWN SORE POINT THROUGHOUT OS/8.  PARADOXICALLY,
 6460                /       SOME OF THE MODIFICATIONS MADE TO THE DECMATES WERE INITIATED TO PARTIALLY
 6461                /       CORRECT THIS WEAKNESS WHILE ACTUALLY MAKING THE OVERALL SITUATION
 6462                /       UNSUPPORTABLE.  IT HAS BEEN REPORTED THAT DEC MANAGEMENT APPROVED THESE
 6463                /       ARCHITECTURAL CHANGES WITHOUT ASKING COMPETENT PERSONELL WHO WERE OUTRAGED BY
 6464                /       THE MODIFICATIONS SUGGESTED AND THEN IMPLEMENTED BY HARRIS SEMICONDUCTOR;
 6465                /       APPARENTLY ALL RESPONSIBLE PARTIES SOON RESIGNED AND SOUGHT EMPLOYMENT
 6466                /       ELSWHERE THUS EVADING RESPONSIBILITY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 129

 6468                /       ADDITIONAL INFORMATION ON SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER EXTENSIONS.
 6469   
 6470                /       P?S/8 DEVELOPMENT METHODOLOGY STRIVES TO OBTAIN MAXIMUM PERFORMANCE AND
 6471                /       EFFICIENCY FROM EVERY SUPPORTED HARDWARE CONFIGURATION.  AS SUCH, EVERY EFFORT
 6472                /       IS MADE TO SUPPORT THE SYSTEM STORAGE DEVICE IN 4K IF POSSIBLE; HOWEVER,
 6473                /       SEVERAL SUPPORTED DEVICES CANNOT ACHIEVE THIS GOAL IN PRACTICE.  IF THE DEVICE
 6474                /       IS NOT SUPPORTABLE IN A 4K MEMORY CONFIGURATION, ADDRESSES 6000-7777  OF THE
 6475                /       PHYSICAL HIGHEST FIELD OF EXTENDED MEMORY IS RESERVED FOR EXTENDED DEVICE
 6476                /       SUPPORT.
 6477   
 6478                /       TYPICAL SUPPORT CODE CONSISTS OF ADDITIONAL PROGRAMMING THAT EXTENDS THE
 6479                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CODING DIRECTLY.  CERTAIN DEVICES REQUIRE AN INTERNAL
 6480                /       MEMORY BUFFER TO DEAL WITH THE ISSUES OF LOGICAL SUPPORT OF DEVICES WITH
 6481                /       PHYSICAL RECORDS LARGER THAN THE LOGICAL BLOCK SIZE [SUCH AS THE RK8E/RK05] OR
 6482                /       DISJOINT STORAGE OF LOGICAL BLOCKS [SUCH AS VARIOUS DISKETTE FORMATS ON THE
 6483                /       VARIOUS RX-SERIES DISKETTE CONTROLLERS WHEN OPERATING IN BYTE MODE].  WHERE
 6484                /       APPLICABLE, ADDITIONAL EXTENDED SUPPORT CODE MAY BE REQUIRED TO IMPLEMENT THE
 6485                /       SLURP LOADER OR THE RELATED DEVICE HANDLER RELOAD ROUTINES, OR THE SYSTEM
 6486                /       BOOTSTRAP AT 07600.  [NOTE: IN CERTAIN SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS, THE LOGICAL
 6487                /       CONSOLE OVERLAY INTERFACES WITH THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER TO ENHANCE
 6488                /       PERFORMANCE OR ERROR RECOVERY; IT IS CONCEIVABLE SOME OF THE AFFECTED ROUTINES
 6489                /       COULD BE RESIDENT WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER EXTENSION.  CERTAIN
 6490                /       IMPLEMENTATIONS OF THE SLURP LOADER OR SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOAD ROUTINES
 6491                /       MAY REQUIRE INTERACTION WITH THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY; IN MANY CASES, IT
 6492                /       MAY BE NECESSARY TO LOCATE THIS CODE WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 6493                /       EXTENSION RESERVED AREA.]
 6494   
 6495                /       BY PROVIDING AN ADDITIONAL EIGHT PAGES OF MEMORY FOR SYSTEM DEVICE SUPPORT,
 6496                /       P?S/8 GENERALLY AVOIDS FRUSTRATING DESIGN DILEMMAS INVOLVING UNREASONABLE
 6497                /       COMPROMISES OF FUNCTIONALITY, PERFORMANCE OR ERROR RECOVERY.  [NOTE: IN
 6498                /       VIRTUALLY EVERY CASE WHERE OS/8 REQUIRES 12K TO OPERATE, THE TWO-PAGE SYSTEM
 6499                /       DEVICE HANDLERS ARE GREATLY COMPROMISED IN SEVERAL OF THESE AREAS.  IN
 6500                /       GENERAL, JUST UNDER TWO PAGES OF MEMORY CANNOT PROVIDED ADEQUATE SUPPORT TO
 6501                /       DEAL WITH THE REAL-WORLD ISSUES OF VARIOUS COMMON STORAGE DEVICES; SPECIFICS
 6502                /       REGARDING CERTAIN MARGINAL OS/8 DEVICE HANDLERS AND ATTENDANT LIMITATIONS ARE
 6503                /       THE SUBJECT OF A NUMBER OF ARTICLES OUTSIDE OF THIS DOCUMENT.]
 6504   
 6505                /       BY NATURE, EXTENDED SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS REQUIRE 8K OF MEMORY OR MORE.  IF
 6506                /       NONE OF THE OTHER OPTIONAL SYSTEM COMPONENTS ARE PRESENT, A P?S/8 SYSTEM
 6507                /       PROGRAM CAN ALLOCATE AS MUCH AS 3K OF THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD AVAILABLE.
 6508                /       [NOTE: SHELL-BASED PROGRAMS MUST ALSO CHECK FOR THE PRESENCE OF THE SHELL FILE
 6509                /       AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA POTENTIALLY LOADED INTO 5400-5777 OF THE HIGHEST
 6510                /       MEMORY FIELD.  AS SUCH, A SHELL-BASED PROGRAM MIGHT ONLY BE ABLE TO ALLOCATE
 6511                /       2.75K ADDITINAL MEMORY IN THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD DEPENDING ON THE SHELL
 6512                /       CONFIGURATION.]
 6513   
 6514                /       NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER DOES NOT USE ANY FORM OF EXTENDED
 6515                /       MEMORY SUPPORT.  IT IS CONSIDERED THE P?S/8 "STANDARD BEARER" CONFIGURATION
 6516                /       BECAUSE THE R-L MONITOR [WHICH P?S/8 IS PARTIALLY BASED ON] ONLY SUPPORTS
 6517                /       THE TC01/TC08 AND A SINGLE TU55/TU56 DRIVE.  [NOTE: ENHANCEMENTS TO ANY P?S/8
 6518                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER MAY BE MADE BY THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY REGARDING
 6519                /       EITHER SPECIFIC PERFORMANCE ISSUES OR ERROR RECOVERY INTERCEPTION.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 130

 6521                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY ISSUES.
 6522   
 6523                /       P?S/8 SUPPORTS AN OPTIONAL SYSTEM PROGRAM EXTENSION KNOWN AS THE LOGICAL
 6524                /       CONSOLE OVERLAY.  WHEN ENABLED, ALL COMPLIANT P?S/8 SYSTEM UTILITIES WILL
 6525                /       SELF-MODIFY TO REDIRECT CONSOLE INPUT AND OUTPUT TO LOGICAL SUBROUTINE CALLS
 6526                /       WITHIN THE OVERLAY.  ALL SUBROUTINE CALLS HAVE CONDITIONAL SKIP RETURN
 6527                /       MECHANISMS TO INDICATE THE SUCCESS OF THE CALL; MOST PROGRAMS WILL RETRY THE
 6528                /       CALL INDEFINITELY UNTIL THE COMMAND SUCCEEDS, LARGELY MIMICKING THE USUAL
 6529                /       METHOD OF PROGRAMMING THE STANDARD CONSOLE HARDWARE.
 6530                 
 6531                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE REDIRECTED LINEPRINTER SUPPORT.
 6532   
 6533                /       NORMALLY LINEPRINTER OUTPUT IS DIRECTED TO A DEVICE 66 LINEPRINTER; THERE IS
 6534                /       NO SUPPORT FOR A REVERSE CHANNEL ISSUING CONTROL-S AND CONTROL-Q FLOW-CONTROL
 6535                /       CHARACTERS [AS IS REQUIRED FOR PROPER SERIAL PRINTER SUPPORT] OR OTHER
 6536                /       HARDWARE VARIANTS.  WHEN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS ENABLED, ALL COMPLIANT
 6537                /       SYSTEM UTILITIES SELF-MODIFY TO REDIRECT LINEPRINTER OUTPUT TO ADDITIONAL
 6538                /       SUBROUTINE CALLS WITHIN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY.  THE UNDERLYING PHYSICAL
 6539                /       PRINTING DEVICE MAY BE TOTALLY DIFFERENT FROM THE STANDARD DEVICE 66 PRINTER.
 6540                /       [THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS EASILY MODIFIED TO SUPPORT ALTERNATE HARDWARE
 6541                /       CONFIGURATIONS USING BINARY UPDATE FILES.]
 6542   
 6543                /       LINEPRINTER FLOW CONTROL SUPPORT.
 6544   
 6545                /       A LOGICAL REVERSE CHANNEL SUBROUTINE CALL IS PROVIDED FOR USE BY
 6546                /       FULLY-COMPLIANT PROGRAMS TO MANAGE FLOW-CONTROL ON SERIAL PRINTERS.  HOWEVER,
 6547                /       USE OF THIS ROUTINE IS NOT ABSOLUTELY REQUIRED AS THE OVERLAY ITSELF WILL
 6548                /       BUFFER AND SUPPORT THE REVERSE CHANNEL PROTOCOL WITHIN THE LOGICAL LINE
 6549                /       PRINTER OUTPUT ROUTINES.  PROGRAMS THAT ACTIVELY SUPPORT THE REVERSE CHANNEL
 6550                /       CAN PERHAPS SLIGHTLY IMPROVE LINEPRINTER THROUGHPUT; HOWEVER, THE PRIMARY
 6551                /       PURPOSE FOR THE SUPPORT IS TO ALLOW LOGICAL INTERRUPT HANDLING OF ALL RELATED
 6552                /       RESOURCES.
 6553   
 6554                /       LOGICAL INTERRUPT SUPPORT.
 6555   
 6556                /       WHEN AN INTERRUPT-DRIVEN P?S/8-COMPLIANT PROGRAM IS EXECUTED WITH THE LOGICAL
 6557                /       CONSOLE OVERLAY PRESENT, THE PROGRAM WILL CALL A LOGICAL INTERRUPT HANDLER
 6558                /       AHEAD OF ALL CONSOLE [AND LINEPRINTER] INPUT AND OUTPUT ROUTINES AS PART OF
 6559                /       A GENERAL INTERRUPT SERVICE ROUTINE.  PROPERLY IMPLEMENTED, THIS PRODUCES
 6560                /       COMPLETE FUNCTIONALITY FOR INTERRUPT-DRIVEN PROGRAMS SUCH AS THE P?S/8
 6561                /       ENHANCED IMPLEMENTATION OF FOCAL, 1969.
 6562   
 6563                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ENHANCEMENTS.
 6564   
 6565                /       CUSTOM SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ENHANCEMENTS CAN BE PROVIDED BY THE LOGICAL
 6566                /       CONSOLE OVERLAY AS DEFINED WITHIN A SMALL AUXILIARY INFORMATION BLOCK
 6567                /       ASSOCIATED WITH THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  ANY SUCH ENHANCEMENTS ARE SPECIFIC
 6568                /       TO THE DEVICE HANDLER ITSELF.  COMMONLY IMPLEMENTED COMPONENTS PROVIDE
 6569                /       PERFORMANCE ENHANCEMENTS SUCH AS UNIT-SPECIFIC SEARCH TABLES OR THE ABILITY
 6570                /       TO PERFORM FORWARD SEARCHES ON PDP-12 LINCTAPE SYSTEMS. [NOTE: PDP-12
 6571                /       LINCTAPE HARDWARE ALWAYS FORCES INITIAL REVERSE SEARCH UNLESS SUBSTANTIAL
 6572                /       CODING IN MAINTENANCE MODE IS IMPLEMENTED TO OVERRIDE THE HARDWARE DEFAULTS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 131

 6574                /       INTERACTIVE ERROR RECOVERY SUPPORT.
 6575   
 6576                /       WHEN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER DOES
 6577                /       NOT HALT ON ERRORS.  INSTEAD, ALL ERROR PARTICULARS ARE REDIRECTED TO THE
 6578                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY WHICH INTERACTS WITH THE USER TO DETERMINE THE PROPER
 6579                /       DISPOSITION OF DEVICE ERRORS [SUCH AS RETRY, ABORT, ETC.].  AS NECESSARY,
 6580                /       ERROR MESSAGE STRINGS ARE PROVIDED TO EMBELLISH THE SUPPORT IF THE USER
 6581                /       DESIRES VERBOSE ERROR MESSAGES DURING THE RECOVERY OPERATION.
 6582   
 6583                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY MEMORY LAYOUT.
 6584   
 6585                /       WHEN ENABLED, THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY OCCUPIES AS MUCH AS 3K OF MEMORY
 6586                /       OF THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL MEMORY FIELD. [THIS IS ONE OF THE REASONS THE
 6587                /       LARGEST MEMORY FIELD ALLOWED MAY BE ONE LOWER THAN THE LARGEST PHYSICAL
 6588                /       MEMORY FIELD.]
 6589   
 6590                /       THE EARLIEST IMPLEMENTATIONS OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY WERE ORIGINALLY
 6591                /       SPECIFICALLY DESIGNED FOR THE VT8E DATA BREAK DISPLAY DEVICE IN TEXT MODE.
 6592                /       EVENTUALLY THE PROGRAM WAS GENERALIZED TO ALLOW FOR A VARIETY OF ALTERNATE
 6593                /       DEVICES IMPLEMENTED BY OVERLAYING THE STANDARD VERSION WITH CUSTOM BINARY
 6594                /       FILES FOR A VARIETY OF ALTERNATE DEVICES.  EACH CONFIGURATION CHANGE IS
 6595                /       PERMANENT.  HOWEVER, CUSTOM BINARY FILES ARE AVAILABLE FOR ALL SUPPORTED
 6596                /       CONFIGURATIONS INCLUDING THE ORIGINAL VT8E CONFIGURATION AND SEVERAL
 6597                /       VARIATIONS.  THE NEW DEFAULT CONFIGURATION SUPPORTS THE DEVICE 03/04 CONSOLE
 6598                /       TERMINAL.  ALL OTHER LOGICAL OVERLAY ENHANCEMENTS ARE GENERIC TO THE SYSTEM
 6599                /       DEVICE SUPPORTED, NOT THE PARTICULAR CONSOLE DEVICE SUPPORTED.  A VARIETY OF
 6600                /       ALTERNATE STANDARD CONSOLE DEVICES IS SUPPORTED [COMPATIBLE WITH THE STANDARD
 6601                /       CONSOLE BUT IMPLEMENTED ON ALTERNATE DEVICE CODE PAIRS SUCH AS IS FOUND ON
 6602                /       PT08 OR KL8 TYPE SERIAL INTERFACES].
 6603   
 6604                /       THE STANDARD LINEPRINTER SUPPORT IS FOR THE USUAL DEVICE 66 LINEPRINTER.  AS
 6605                /       NECESSARY, ALTERNATE HARDWARE MAY BE SUPPORTED IN A CUSTOMIZED CONFIGURATION.
 6606                /       FOR ANY SUPPORTED CONFIGURATION, ENHANCED BUFFERING IS RECOMMENDED; DEPENDING
 6607                /       ON HARDWARE PARTICULARS, THIS CAN HAVE A PROFOUND EFFECT ON LINEPRINTER
 6608                /       THROUGHPUT.
 6609   
 6610                /       DUE TO THE NATURE OF THE PARALLEL INTERFACE, MOST DEVICE 66 LINEPRINTERS HAVE
 6611                /       REAL-TIME QUIRKS THAT MUST BE DEALT WITH OR ELSE THROUGHPUT MAY SUFFER.
 6612   
 6613                /       IN THE EARLIEST IMPLEMENTATION OF DEVICE 66 LINEPRINTERS, IT WAS PREDICTABLE
 6614                /       THE INTERFACE WOULD HANG FOR LONGER PERIODS OF TIME WHEN PRINTING FORM-FEED,
 6615                /       CARRIAGE RETURN OR LINE FEED CHARACTERS; ALL OTHER CHARACTERS WERE QUICKLY
 6616                /       PLACED INTO AN INTERNAL BUFFER.  [NOTE: SOME PRINTERS HAVE MORE COMPLEX TIMING
 6617                /       ISSUES SUCH AS DRUM PRINTERS WHERE CHARACTER PRINTING TIME IS IN PART
 6618                /       DETERMINED BY ROTATIONAL DELAY; HOWEVER, THE GENERAL RULE THAT NON-PRINTING
 6619                /       PAPER MOTION TAKE MORE TIME FOR THE DONE FLAG TO RAISE THAN FOR PRINTABLE
 6620                /       CHARACTERS STILL APPLIES.]
 6621   
 6622                /       UNFORTUNATELY, NEWER DESIGNS ADD SOME INTERNAL BUFFERING BETWEEN THE PRINT
 6623                /       MECHANISM AND THE INTERFACE; THIS ESPECIALLY APPLIES TO SERIAL PRINTERS WHICH
 6624                /       ARE KNOWN TO BE HEAVILY BUFFERED TO ACHIEVE MAXIMUM THROUGHPUT ASSUMING THE
 6625                /       HOST SUPPORT CODE PAYS CLOSE ATTENTION TO THE REVERSE INPUT CHANNEL.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 132

 6627                /       MOST PDP-8 SOFTWARE DOES NOT NORMALLY ADDRESS THESE ISSUES; ACHIEVED
 6628                /       THROUGHPUT MAY BE FAR LESS THAN DESIGN SPECIFICATIONS INDICATE.  THE BUFFERED
 6629                /       LINEPRINTER SUPPORT OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY OVERCOMES THIS COMPLETELY:
 6630   
 6631                /       EVERY TIME THE LINEPRINTER OUTPUT ROUTINES ARE ENTERED, CHARACTERS ARE
 6632                /       POTENTIALLY BUFFERED TO AVAILABLE BUFFER SPACE WITHIN THE OVERLAY.  AS THE
 6633                /       PRINTER DONE FLAG RAISES, THE NEXT CHARACTER FROM THE BUFFER IS SENT TO THE
 6634                /       PRINTER; FOR A VERY SHORT TIME INTERVAL, THE PRINTER DONE FLAG IS TESTED.
 6635   
 6636                /       IF THE PRINTER DONE FLAG RAISES QUICKLY AND THERE ARE MORE CHARACTERS IN THE
 6637                /       BUFFER TO PRINT, THE PROCESS LOOPS UNTIL EITHER ALL CHARACTERS ARE SENT
 6638                /       [INCLUDING THE ONE ASSOCIATED WITH THE LATEST CALL, IN WHICH CASE THE PRINTER
 6639                /       FULLY CONTAINS ALL PRINT OUTPUT THROUGH THAT MOMENT IN TIME], OR THE DONE FLAG
 6640                /       FAILS TO RAISE QUICKLY.  [NOTE: FOR MOST ACTUAL DEVICE 66 PRINTERS, THE TIME
 6641                /       INTERVAL IS AROUND 25 MICROSECONDS; SHOULD THE FLAG NOT RAISE BY THAT TIME,
 6642                /       IT WILL NOT RAISE FOR MANY MILLISECONDS OR PERHAPS LONGER.]
 6643   
 6644                /       EACH LOGICAL LINEPRINTER OUTPUT OPERATION KEEPS THE PRINTER BUFFER IN PLAY
 6645                /       TO THE FULLEST EXTENT POSSIBLE.  SHOULD A CALL BE MADE AND THERE IS NO ROOM
 6646                /       AVAILABLE FOR THE LATEST CHARACTER, THE OVERLAY WILL INDICATE [VIA A NON-SKIP
 6647                /       RETURN TO THE CALLER] THE LATEST CHARACTER CANNOT BE PROCESSED.  MOST CALLERS
 6648                /       WILL LOOP BACK AND REPEAT THE CALL UNTIL A SKIP RETURN OCCURS AS NOTHING ELSE
 6649                /       CAN BE DONE TO ALLOW OVERLAPPING PROCESSING WITHIN THE CALLING PROGRAM WHEN
 6650                /       CALLING THE OVERLAY IN THIS STATE.  WHEN AT LEAST ONE CHARACTER HAS BEEN
 6651                /       PROCESSED, THE LATEST CHARACTER CAN BE BUFFERED [AND THE LOGICAL CALL TAKES
 6652                /       THE SKIP RETURN INDICATING SUCCESSFUL TRANSMISSION OF THE LATEST CHARACTER].
 6653   
 6654                /       ONCE ONE CHARACTER HAS BEEN PROCESSED, PERHAPS WELL OVER 100 ADDITIONAL
 6655                /       CHARACTERS MAY ALSO BE PROCESSED AS LONG AS SUBSEQUENT DONE FLAG EVENTS RAISE
 6656                /       THE FLAG QUICKLY. [GENERALLY, THE ONLY ACTUAL REAL-TIME EXCEPTION CASE WOULD
 6657                /       BE JUST AFTER A CARRIAGE RETURN WAS PROCESSED ASSUMING THE NEXT CHARACTER SENT
 6658                /       TO THE PRINTER IS A LINE FEED.]  BY NOT BEING CONCERNED WITH THE ACTUAL
 6659                /       CHARACTERS, MERELY CHECKING THE DONE FLAG TIMING WILL BUFFER THE PRINTED
 6660                /       OUTPUT AT NEARLY THE SPEED OF A FULLY INTERRUPT-DRIVEN PROGRAM EVEN WITH
 6661                /       INTERRUPTS DISABLED.  [NOTE: OS/8 DOES NOT IMPLEMENT LINEPRINTER BUFFERING
 6662                /       WHATSOEVER; AS SUCH, THE MAXIMUM LINEPRINTER SPEED IS LIMITED TO JUST UNDER
 6663                /       300 LINES PER MINUTE REGARDLESS OF ANY ADDITIONAL PRINTER PERFORMANCE
 6664                /       CAPABILITY.  P?S/8 WITH THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY ENABLED HAS SUCCESSFULLY
 6665                /       DRIVEN 1200 LINE PER MINUTE PRINTERS TO VIRTUALLY FULL SPEED VIA THE METHOD
 6666                /       DESCRIBED HERE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 133

 6668                /       HISTORIC OVERLAY PRINTER BUFFERING IMPLEMENTATION.
 6669   
 6670                /       TYPICALLY, LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY CONFIGURATIONS OCCUPY A SIGNIFICANT PORTION
 6671                /       OF THE AVAILABLE MEMORY SPACE [X0000-X5777 WHERE X IS THE LARGEST PHYSICAL
 6672                /       FIELD AVAILABLE], BUT HARDLY THE ENTIRE MEMORY AREA.  AS SUCH, THE SOURCE CODE
 6673                /       DEFINED ALL ADDITIONAL SPACE PAST THE CODE [THROUGH X5777] AS THE GENERAL
 6674                /       LINEPRINTER OUTPUT BUFFER. [NOTE: AT THE TIME OF IMPLEMENTATION, THIS WAS THE
 6675                /       UPPER LIMIT OF MEMORY RESERVED FOR THE OVERLAY; THERE WAS NO REASON TO LEAVE
 6676                /       THE MEMORY UNUSED.]
 6677   
 6678                /       HOWEVER, IT IS GENERALLY TRUE THAT THE LARGEST BUFFER NEEDED TO ACCOMPLISH
 6679                /       VIRTUALLY ALL PRINT BUFFERING THROUGHPUT ADVANTAGE IS JUST LARGER THAN AT MOST
 6680                /       TWO COMPLETE PRINTING LINES; ASSUMING 132 COLUMNS/PAGE, THIS MEANS THERE IS NO
 6681                /       PARTICULAR ADVANTAGE TO RESERVING MORE THAN 268 CHARACTERS OR SO [THIS
 6682                /       INCLUDES CARRIAGE RETURN AND LINE FEED CHARACTERS FOR EACH LINE].  ANY
 6683                /       ADDITIONAL BUFFER MEMORY USED CREATES NO PARTICULAR THROUGHPUT ADVANTAGE [OR
 6684                /       DISADVANTAGE].  [NOTE: THE VT8E CONFIGURATION IS THE MOST COMPLEX OVERLAY
 6685                /       IMPLEMENTED TO DATE.  THE VT8E SUPPORT CODE IS NOT PARTICULARLY LONG,
 6686                /       CONSISTING OF ONLY A BASIC TERMINAL EMULATOR WITH MINOR FRILLS.  HOWEVER, AN
 6687                /       INTERNAL BUFFER IS USED FOR THE 1280 TEXT POSITIONS ON THE VT8E SCREEN.
 6688                /       THERE IS ALSO A MORE THAN ADEQUATE LINEPRINTER BUFFER PRESENT IN THE VT8E
 6689                /       CONFIGURATION; THE DEVICE 66 LINEPRINTER IS A STANDARD FEATURE OF THE VT8E.]
 6690   
 6691                /       IF FOR ANY REASON BIT[9] IS CLEARED ON THE WORD ON BLOCK 0000 CORRESPONDING TO
 6692                /       THE CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD [RELATIVE WORD 011], THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY
 6693                /       WILL BE ABANDONED IN FAVOR OF THE PHYSICAL DEVICE 03/04 CONSOLE DEVICE.  THE
 6694                /       "CONSOL" COMMAND MUST BE RUN WITH THE "/E" SWITCH ["CONSOLE /E"] TO ENABLE THE
 6695                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE FUNCTION.  IF NECESSARY, THE "CORE ALL" COMMAND CAN BE GIVEN
 6696                /       TO RESET BITS[0-8] OF THE CORE SIZE AND STATUS WORD TO STANDARD MAXIMUM VALUES
 6697                /       FOR THE CURRENT SYSTEM CONFIGURATION.  [NOTE: FUTURE VERSIONS OF THE
 6698                /       "CORE ALL" COMMAND WILL ALSO ENSURE THE PERMANENT COPY OF THE CORRESPONDING
 6699                /       WORD ON BLOCK 0000 IS WRITTEN WITH THE LOGICAL ABORT BITS [BITS[10-11]] CLEAR
 6700                /       TO PREVENT INDEFINITE LOGICAL ABORT EVENT LOOPS WITHOUT USER INPUT.  THIS
 6701                /       RARE EVENT CAN BE CLEARED USING THE P?S/8 "DUMP" FACILITY FOR THE PRESENT.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 134

 6703                /       P?S/8 SHELL IMPLEMENTATION.
 6704   
 6705                /       THE P?S/8 SHELL IS CURRENTLY UNDER DEVELOPMENT. [NOTE: MOST OF THE PARAMETERS
 6706                /       OF THE SHELL OVERLAY HAVE BEEN DEFINED.  THE ISSUES ASSOCIATED WITH POTENTIAL
 6707                /       INTERACTION WITH THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY WILL BE ADDRESSED HERE.]  OTHER
 6708                /       THAN THE ISSUES RAISED HERE, IT IS NOT EXPECTED THE SHELL WILL HAVE ANY
 6709                /       ADDITIONAL IMPACT ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER OR OTHER SYSTEM COMPONENTS.
 6710   
 6711                /       WHEN THE P?S/8 SHELL IS ENABLED, IT IS ENCOURAGED THE OVERALL SIZE OF THE
 6712                /       LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY BE SLIGHTLY REDUCED [FROM X0000-X5777 TO X0000-X5377
 6713                /       WHERE X IS THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL FIELD OF MEMORY AVAILABLE].  THIS IS
 6714                /       REQUIRED TO ALLOW THE 256 WORD SHELL FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA TO BE
 6715                /       RESIDENT IN EXTENDED MEMORY FOR MAXIMUM PERFORMANCE.
 6716   
 6717                /       THE SHELL CAN OPERATE WITH THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA NON-RESIDENT AND
 6718                /       REDIRECT ALL PROGRAMS TO THE CONTENTS OF TWO LOW-ORDER ADJACENT SYSTEM BLOCKS
 6719                /       FOR ALL RELEVANT PURPOSES.  THIS IS GENERALLY NOT RECOMMENDED; IF AT ALL
 6720                /       POSSIBLE, THE MEMORY RESIDENT VARIANT SHOULD BE USED TO AVOID NOTICEABLE
 6721                /       DEGRADATION OF SYSTEM PERFORMANCE [ESPECIALLY ON DEVICES SUCH AS DECTAPES AND
 6722                /       LINCTAPES].
 6723   
 6724                /       AS SUCH, ALL NEW WORK ON THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY WILL CONFORM TO THIS NEW
 6725                /       MEMORY LAYOUT.  IT IS NOT CONTEMPLATED THAT ANY EXISTENT CONFIGURATION WILL BE
 6726                /       AFFECTED BEYOND THE MINOR BUFFER LIMIT DEFINITION CHANGES.  FOR THE REMAINDER
 6727                /       OF THIS DOCUMENT, ASSUME THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY LOADS INTO X0000-X5377.
 6728   
 6729                /       P?S/8 SHELL RESIDENT FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA.
 6730   
 6731                /       WHEN THE P?S/8 SHELL IS IN EFFECT, THERE IS A NEED FOR A 256 WORD FILE AND
 6732                /       COMMUNICATIONS AREA TO COORDINATE ALL SHELL-RELATED FUNCTIONS.  IF AT ALL
 6733                /       POSSIBLE, THIS SHOULD BE LOADED INTO X5400-X5777 [X IS THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL
 6734                /       MEMORY FIELD AS DESCRIBED ABOVE].
 6735   
 6736                /       AS STATED ELSEWHERE, THE HIGH-ORDER BIT OF THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD "SOUTCT"
 6737                /       [07607] INDICATES IF THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA IS RESIDENT OR
 6738                /       VIRTUALIZED TO THE CONTENTS OF TWO ADJACENT LOW SYSTEM BLOCKS.  [THIS PRESUMES
 6739                /       THE SHELL IS IN EFFECT.]  [NOTE: THE EXACT DEFINITION OF THE TWO SYSTEM BLOCKS
 6740                /       HAS NOT BEEN COMPLETELY DETERMINED.  AT THE TIME OF THIS WRITING, POSSIBLE
 6741                /       IMPLEMENTATION SHOULD BE IN THE RANGE OF BLOCK 0061 THROUGH BLOCK 0130.
 6742                /       ALTERNATIVELY, THE BLOCKS COULD BE LOCATED WITHIN THE SYSTEM PROGRAM [NAMED
 6743                /       "SHELL"] USED TO ENABLE OR DISABLE THE SHELL AS REQUIRED.  THIS COULD HELP
 6744                /       FACILITATE LOCATING THESE BLOCKS ON AN ALTERNATE SYSTEM DEVICE LOGICAL UNIT TO
 6745                /       IMPROVE THROUGHPUT WHEN THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA IS VIRTUALIZED.  THE
 6746                /       CHALLENGE WILL BE TO DETERMINE WHAT CAPABILITIES CAN BE RECONFIGURED FROM
 6747                /       WITHIN THE SHELL WHILE IT IS ENABLED; IN GENERAL THE SHELL HAS NO ACCESS TO
 6748                /       THE BASIC SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORIES TO LOCATE THIS INFORMATION UNLESS CERTAIN
 6749                /       SYSTEM CODING IS ESSENTIALLY CREATED REDUNDANTLY [WHICH IS A RELATIVELY EASY
 6750                /       TASK].  HOWEVER, THE SHELL IS CAPABLE OF DETERMINING ITS OWN LOADING BLOCK AND
 6751                /       LOGICAL UNIT; OFFSETS CAN BE USED TO DERIVE THE NEEDED INFORMATION WITHOUT
 6752                /       DIRECT LOOKUP CAPABILITY.  THE LOGICAL UNIT FOR VIRTUALIZATION CAN BE WRITTEN
 6753                /       INTO THE SHELL CODE FROM EITHER SYSTEM OPERATING MODE.  IF PERMITTED, THE
 6754                /       SHELL CONFIGURATION COULD ALSO MOVE THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA TO
 6755                /       EXTENDED MEMORY FURTHER REDUCING SYSTEM OVERHEAD.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 135

 6757                /       THE P?S/8 SHELL IS INTENDED FOR USE WITH THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA
 6758                /       RESIDENT IN MEMORY.  VIRTUALIZATION IS CONSIDERED A PERFORMANCE TRADEOFF WHICH
 6759                /       SHOULD ONLY BE USED IF PHYSICAL MEMORY IS DEEMED INADEQUATE FOR THE PURPOSE AT
 6760                /       HAND.  SHELL CONTROL UTILITIES CAN RECONFIGURE THE SHELL TO OPERATE IN EITHER
 6761                /       MODE WITHOUT A MAJOR CONFIGURATIONAL CHANGE.  THIS WILL FACILITATE OPERATING
 6762                /       IN EITHER MODE AS APPROPRIATE.  [NOTE: IT IS CONTEMPLATED THAT USERS OF THE
 6763                /       SHELL ON SYSTEMS WITH LESS THAN 12K PHYSICAL MEMORY WILL EXPERIENCE PALPABLE
 6764                /       LOSS OF EFFECTIVE FUNCTIONALITY; THIS ASSUMES THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 6765                /       REQUIRES EXTENDED MEMORY AND/OR ENABLING THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY.]
 6766   
 6767                /       CERTAIN FORMS OF PROGRAM DEBUGGING MAY EITHER BE COMPROMISED OR PERHAPS
 6768                /       TOTALLY UNAVAILABLE DEPENDING ON PHYSICAL MEMORY SIZE.  [NOTE: ALL EFFORTS
 6769                /       WILL BE MADE TO MINIMIZE THE EFFECTS OF INADEQUATE MEMORY WHERE POSSIBLE;
 6770                /       HOWEVER, THE USE OF THE SHELL IS NOT RECOMMENDED ON MINIMAL SYSTEMS.  CURRENT
 6771                /       SHELL DESIGN PLANS PREDICT THAT GENERAL PROGRAM DEBUGGING WILL NOT BE POSSIBLE
 6772                /       ON 4K SYSTEMS WHICH FURTHER REQUIRE THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA TO BE
 6773                /       VIRTUALIZED.  AS A COMPROMISE, A 4K-ONLY DEBUGGING MODE MAY BE OFFERED THAT
 6774                /       CANNOT PROCESS EXTENDED MEMORY INFORMATION.  IT IS NOT CLEAR IF THERE IS
 6775                /       SUFFICIENT CODE SPACE ON A 4K SYSTEM; THIS SUBJECT WILL BE RESEARCHED AT SOME
 6776                /       POINT FOR POTENTIAL IMPLEMENTATION LATER.]
 6777   
 6778                /       THERE IS NO RESIDENT STATUS INDICATOR FOR THE SHELL ITSELF; THE KEYBOARD
 6779                /       MONITOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR HANDING OFF CONTROL TO THE SHELL WHEN IT IS ENABLED
 6780                /       FROM THE BASIC SYSTEM.  AS SUCH, SHELL-BASED PROGRAMS ARE IMPLICITLY AWARE OF
 6781                /       THEIR OPERATING ENVIRONMENT [WHICH SHARES MOST OF THE SAME SYSTEM RESIDENT
 6782                /       AREA PARAMETERS WITH THE BASIC SYSTEM].
 6783   
 6784                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS FOR THE BASIC SYSTEM DO NOT RUN WITHIN THE SHELL
 6785                /       ENVIRONMENT.  THUS, FOR ALL BASIC SYSTEM OPERATIONS, THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE
 6786                /       SHELL CAN BE IGNORED.  IF DESIRED, A SYSTEM PROGRAM CAN ALLOCATE THE FIRST 3K
 6787                /       OF THE HIGHEST FIELD OF MEMORY IF THE SYSTEM HANDLER EXTENSION PREVENTS ACCESS
 6788                /       TO THE LAST 1K OF THE HIGHEST FIELD [ASSUMING THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS
 6789                /       DISABLED].
 6790   
 6791                /       THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE SHELL PLAYS NO ROLE IN ANY BASIC SYSTEM PROGRAM
 6792                /       MEMORY ALLOCATION SCHEME.  HOWEVER, IF THE SHELL IS ENABLED AND THE FILE AND
 6793                /       COMMUNICATIONS AREA IS MEMORY-RESIDENT, SHELL-BASED PROGRAMS MUST RESPECT THIS
 6794                /       AREA; IF THE SHELL IS ENABLED AND THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS NOT ENABLED,
 6795                /       ONLY 2.75K OF MEMORY IN THE HIGHEST FIELD CAN BE ALLOCATED.  [SINCE ALL SHELL
 6796                /       PROGRAMS ARE NEW WORK, IT IS RECOMMENDED THAT MOST PROGRAMS NOT CHECK FOR THE
 6797                /       FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA TO BE VIRTUALIZED FOR THE SOLE PURPOSE OF
 6798                /       OBTAINING AN ADDITIONAL .25K OF MEMORY UNLESS THIS IS IMPORTANT TO THE
 6799                /       SPECIFICS OF THE UTILITY.] [NOTE: ASSUMING THE SHELL IS ENABLED, SHOULD THE
 6800                /       FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA BE MEMORY RESIDENT, THE LARGEST AVAILABLE LOGICAL
 6801                /       MEMORY SIZE WILL BE REDUCED BY ONE FIELD REGARDLESS OF OTHER POTENTIALLY
 6802                /       MEMORY-RESIDENT COMPONENTS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 136

 6804                /       OTHER THAN THE RELEVANT SOURCE CODE CHANGES FOR THE NEW BUFFER ALLOCATION
 6805                /       PARAMETERS, NO PROBLEMS ARE FORESEEN WITHIN ANY LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY
 6806                /       IMPLEMENTATION; THE VT8E VERSION IS KNOWN TO FUNCTION WITH THE REDUCED MEMORY
 6807                /       ALLOCATION.  TO DATE, ALL OTHER LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAYS REQUIRE LESS OVERALL
 6808                /       MEMORY TO IMPLEMENT ALL INTERNAL FUNCTIONS.  THE LOSS OF .25K OF SPACE WILL
 6809                /       NOT AFFECT EXISTING LINEPRINTER BUFFERING ROUTINES FOR DEVICES SUCH AS THE
 6810                /       PARALLEL PRINTER INTERFACE.  [NOTE: SERIAL PRINTERS GENERALLY HAVE EVEN LARGER
 6811                /       BUFFERS WITHIN THE PRINTER.  THE TYPICAL REVERSE CHANNEL IMPLEMENTATION SENDS
 6812                /       CONTROL-S CHARACTERS WHEN THE BUFFER IS 3/4 FULL, THEN SENDS A CONTROL-Q
 6813                /       CHARACTER WHEN THE BUFFER IS 3/4 EMPTY.  AS SUCH, PRINTER DATA BUFFERING
 6814                /       WITHIN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY CAN BE MINIMAL FOR SERIAL PRINTER SUPPORT.]
 6815   
 6816                /       END OF APPENDIX J.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 137

 6818                /       APPENDIX K.
 6819   
 6820                /       IMAGE BINARY LOADING CONSIDERATIONS IN P?S/8.
 6821   
 6822                /       THERE ARE TWO CATEGORIES OF BINARY LOADING IN MOST PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS:
 6823   
 6824                /       1) LOADING BINARY USER FILES INTO MEMORY.
 6825   
 6826                /       2) LOADING BINARY IMAGES INTO MEMORY.
 6827   
 6828                /       BINARY FILE LOADING IS A DYNAMIC PROCESS GENERALLY ASSOCIATED WITH USER
 6829                /       PROGRAMS [OR PERHAPS CONVERTED PAPER-TAPE IMAGES]; LOADING IS NOT REGIMENTED
 6830                /       INTO ANY PARTICULAR UNITS OF MEMORY OTHER THAN OVERALL LOADING LIMITS.  SOME
 6831                /       ASPECTS OF BINARY FILE LOADING ARE DISCUSSED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.
 6832   
 6833                /       LOADING OF BINARY IMAGES INVOLVES DIRECT LOADING OF AN IMAGE OF SOME PORTION
 6834                /       OF MEMORY; THE SMALLEST UNIT OF LOADING IS THE 128 WORD BLOCK CORRESPONDING
 6835                /       TO A PDP-8 PAGE.  [NOTE: BINARY IMAGES ARE GENERALLY ALIGNED ON PAGE
 6836                /       BOUNDARIES; HOWEVER, IT IS POSSIBLE TO LOAD AN IMAGE WHILE CROSSING A PAGE
 6837                /       BOUNDARY AS LONG AS NO SYSTEM RESERVED AREA IS VIOLATED.  GENERALLY, MOST
 6838                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS DO NOT SUPPORT MECHANISMS TO CREATE NON-ALIGNED IMAGES;
 6839                /       WITHOUT AN ALIGNMENT MECHANISM, THE LARGEST IMAGES COULD NOT BE LOADED DUE TO
 6840                /       CONFLICT WITH THE SYSTEM RESERVED AREA.  AS SUCH, MOST PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS
 6841                /       WILL RESTRICT IMAGES TO LOADING ON COMPLETE PDP-8 PAGE BOUNDARIES.]
 6842   
 6843                /       P?S/8 SUPPORTS THE DIRECT LOADING OF ALL OF USER MEMORY AS DEFINED BELOW.
 6844                /       [NOTE: THE DEFINITION OF USER MEMORY SPACE APPLIES TO BOTH METHODS OF BINARY
 6845                /       LOADING; ONLY BINARY IMAGES ARE LAID OUT IN A REGIMENTED FORMAT.]
 6846   
 6847                /       USER MEMORY ADDRESS SPACE CONSIDERATIONS.
 6848   
 6849                /       OTHER THAN SOME NITPICKS REGARDING POOR PROGRAMMING TECHNIQUES GENERALLY
 6850                /       FROWNED UPON [YET KNOWN TO OCCASIONALLY BE PRESENT IN SOME "ROGUE" DEC
 6851                /       DIAGNOSTIC AND OTHER PROGRAMS], THE GENERAL CONVENTION, UNIVERSALLY ACCEPTED
 6852                /       IN ALL PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS, IS THAT ALL LOCATIONS IN THE FIRST 31 PAGES OF
 6853                /       MEMORY [00000-07577] IS FULLY AVAILABLE TO ANY PROGRAM FOR ANY PURPOSE.
 6854                /       [NOTE: ALL GENERAL-PURPOSE PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS RUN WITHOUT INTERRUPTS.
 6855                /       APPLICATION PROGRAMS CAN MAKE FULL USE OF THE INTERRUPT SYSTEM AS LONG AS
 6856                /       APPROPRIATE INTERRUPT HANDLING IS PROVIDED WITHIN THE PROGRAM; ALL INTERRUPTS
 6857                /       ARE HANDLED WITHIN THE USER MEMORY SPACE.  INTERRUPTS SHOULD BE DISABLED WHEN
 6858                /       EXITING TO THE OPERATING SYSTEM TO AVOID CONFLICTS AS THE OPERATING SYSTEM
 6859                /       RESIDENT ROUTINES LOAD OVER THE INTERRUPT HANDLER LOCATIONS IN PAGE 0.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 138

 6861                /       THIS CONVENTION AROSE OUT OF A VARIETY OF HARDWARE AND SOFTWARE CONCERNS:
 6862   
 6863                /       1) TO ALLOW THE USER MEMORY SPACE TO BE AS LARGE AS POSSIBLE.
 6864   
 6865                /       2) TO HAVE A UNIVERSAL EXIT TO A STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM; THIS IS
 6866                /          DEFINED AS 07600, THE LOWEST LOCATION ON THE LAST PAGE OF MEMORY.
 6867   
 6868                /       3) THE NEED TO HAVE THE THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK LOCATIONS RESERVED TO AVOID
 6869                /          CONFLICTS WITH USER-WRITTEN CODE; ONLY SPECIAL PROGRAMS SPECIFICALLY
 6870                /          WRITTEN FOR THE SPECIFIED DEVICES [TC01/TC08 DECTAPE, DF32/RF08,
 6871                /          TC58-SERIES MAGNETIC TAPE CONTROLLERS] CAN SAFELY ACCESS THESE LOCATIONS.
 6872   
 6873                /       4) THE NEED TO SUPPORT PAPER-TAPE LOADERS THAT ARE TOTALLY RESIDENT IN THE
 6874                /          RESERVED AREA.  [NOTE: A STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM OCCUPIES THIS AREA
 6875                /          IF THE PAPER-TAPE LOADERS ARE NOT IN USE.]
 6876   
 6877                /       5) THE NEED TO HAVE A SYSTEM RESIDENT KERNEL OF A STORAGE-BASED OPERATING
 6878                /          SYSTEM, ESPECIALLY IF THE THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK LOCATIONS ARE AN INTEGRAL
 6879                /          PART OF THE KERNEL DEVICE [SUCH AS IS THE CASE WITH THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE
 6880                /          CONTROLLER].
 6881   
 6882                /       NON-CONFORMING ["ROGUE"] PROGRAM CONSIDERATIONS.
 6883   
 6884                /       CERTAIN PROGRAMS EXIST THAT ARE KNOWN TO VIOLATE THESE UNIVERSAL CONVENTIONS
 6885                /       FOR NO APPARENT REASON OTHER THAN SHEER IGNORANCE ON THE PART OF CERTAIN
 6886                /       PROGRAMMERS AND THEIR MANAGERS.  THE UNDERLYING MECHANISM AT FAULT IS THE
 6887                /       UNREASONABLE DEPENDENCY ON SEEMINGLY AVAILABLE MEMORY LOCATIONS PAST 07600.
 6888   
 6889                /       IN EVERY CASE, THE PROGRAMMER ASSUMED THAT THE INCIDENTAL MEMORY SPACE BETWEEN
 6890                /       07600 AND SOME SLIGHTLY HIGHER ADDRESS WAS SOMEHOW AVAILABLE MERELY BECAUSE
 6891                /       THE PARTICULAR VERSION OF THE BINARY PAPER TAPE LOADER THEY WERE FAMILIAR WITH
 6892                /       COULD ALLOW THIS.
 6893   
 6894                /       UNFORTUNATELY, THE EXACT MEMORY MAP OF THE DEC BIN LOADER VARIES WITH SPECIFIC
 6895                /       RELEASE; AS SUCH, THIS ILL-ADVISED PROGRAMMING TECHNIQUE COULD EASILY FAIL
 6896                /       MERELY BY BEING LOADED BY A DIFFERENT VERSION OF THE BIN LOADER PROGRAM.
 6897                /       NOTE: THERE HAVE BEEN AT LEAST THREE REVISIONS OF THE BINARY PAPERTAPE
 6898                /       LOADING PROGRAM, EACH OCCUPYING A SLIGHTLY DIFFERENT PORTION OF THE MEMORY
 6899                /       SPACE BETWEEN 07600-07755.  [07756-07776 IS RESERVED FOR THE RIM LOADER; 07777
 6900                /       IS RESERVED AS THE OFFICIAL START OF THE BIN LOADER; THE ACTUAL START IS
 6901                /       ACHIEVED INDIRECTLY BY USING A "JMP BEGIN" INSTRUCTION AT 07777.]
 6902   
 6903                /       AS SUCH, NOT ONLY ARE THESE OFFENDING PROGRAMS INCAPABLE OF BEING LOADED FROM
 6904                /       ANY STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM, THEY MAY BE EXCESSIVELY DEPENDENT ON A
 6905                /       PARTICULAR RELEASE OF THE BIN LOADER NOT NECESSARILY AVAILABLE.  [NOTE: OF THE
 6906                /       THREE MOST COMMON RELEASES, ONE IS INCOMPATIBLE WITH THE TC01/TC08 CONTROLLER;
 6907                /       ANOTHER IS KNOWN TO HAVE BUGS IN IT RELATED TO IMPROPERLY HANDLING FIELD
 6908                /       SETTINGS; P?S/8 IMPLEMENTS A FOURTH VERSION THAT PARTIALLY CORRECTS SOME OF
 6909                /       THE PROBLEMS ASSOCIATED WITH ALL OF THESE DEC RELEASES INCLUDING CERTAIN
 6910                /       INCOMPATIBILITY PROBLEMS WITH DF32/RF08 PROGRAMS WHICH CAN CORRUPT THE BIN
 6911                /       LOADER PROGRAM ONCE EXECUTED.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 139

 6913                /       WITH REGARD TO THE DEC-RELEASED ROGUE DIAGNOSTIC PROGRAMS:
 6914   
 6915                /       THAT THESE PROGRAMS EVEN EXIST IS UNFORTUNATELY A LEGACY OF POOR MANAGEMENT OF
 6916                /       ONE OR MORE PDP-8 SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT GROUPS.  WHILE IT IS PERFECTLY
 6917                /       REASONABLE TO EXPECT OFFICIAL DIAGNOSTIC PROGRAMS TO CONFORM TO PREVAILING
 6918                /       CONVENTIONS, UNFORTUNATELY THIS LEVEL OF SCRUTINY WAS NOT ALWAYS APPLIED.
 6919                /       IRONICALLY, MOST OF THESE PROGRAMS WERE RELEASED YEARS AFTER THE SIMPLE SET
 6920                /       OF PROGRAMMING RULES WERE FIRST DOCUMENTED.  [NOTE: THIS IS CONSISTENT WITH
 6921                /       THE WELL-KNOWN GRADUAL BREAKDOWN OF DEC INTERNAL COMPANY STANDARDS.]
 6922   
 6923                /       THESE PROGRAMS ARE NOT LOADABLE BY ANY OF THE PREVAILING PDP-8 STORAGE-BASED
 6924                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS.  P?S/8 HAS A TENUOUS FACILITY [READ "KLUDGE" HERE] WHEREBY
 6925                /       THESE PROGRAMS CAN BE LOADED, ALBEIT WITH DIFFICULTY.  THE EXACT METHOD USED
 6926                /       MUST BE TAILORED NOT ONLY TO THE PROGRAM'S SPECIFIC LOADING RANGES, BUT ALSO
 6927                /       TO P?S/8 RELEASE SPECIFICS; LOADING ANY OF THESE PROGRAMS WILL CORRUPT THE
 6928                /       MEMORY-RESIDENT OPERATING SYSTEM; MANUAL REBOOT [OR EQUIVALENT] WILL BE
 6929                /       REQUIRED AFTER LOADING.  [NOTE: FOR MORE INFORMATION, SEE DOCUMENTATION OF THE
 6930                /       P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM REGARDING VIRTUAL LOADING OF IMPROPERLY WRITTEN PROGRAMS
 6931                /       SUCH AS DEC DIAGNOSTICS.]
 6932   
 6933                /       P?S/8 IMAGE BINARY PROGRAM LOADING CONSIDERATIONS.
 6934   
 6935                /       SINCE P?S/8 SYSTEMS EXIST WITH OR WITHOUT THE OPTIONAL SHELL ENVIRONMENT,
 6936                /       THERE ARE TWO DIFFERENT APPROACHES TO IMAGE BINARY PROGRAM LOADING:
 6937   
 6938                /       1) EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS IN THE BASIC P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT.
 6939   
 6940                /       2) EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS IN THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT.
 6941   
 6942                /       THE GENERAL MECHANISM USED TO LOAD IMAGE BINARY PROGRAMS IN THESE TWO VERY
 6943                /       DIFFERENT ENVIRONMENTS SHARES A COMMON UNDERLYING MECHANISM GENERALLY KNOWN AS
 6944                /       THE "SYSTEM LOADER".  [NOTE: THIS IS THE HISTORICAL NAME DERIVED FROM A
 6945                /       VAGUELY ANALOGOUS COMPONENT OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM; HOWEVER, THE ACTUAL
 6946                /       IMPLEMENTATION IS TOTALLY DIFFERENT FROM THIS FORERUNNER OPERATING SYSTEM FROM
 6947                /       WHICH P?S/8 IS PARTIALLY DERIVED; DETAILS OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM COMPONENT
 6948                /       ARE DESCRIBED BELOW ALONG WITH EQUIVALENT MECHANISMS IN SEVERAL OTHER
 6949                /       OPERATING SYSTEMS.]
 6950   
 6951                /       LOADING IMAGE BINARY PROGRAMS IN THE BASIC P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT.
 6952   
 6953                /       IN THE BASIC P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT THE SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY CONTAINS ALL
 6954                /       PARAMETERS TO LOAD ANY AVAILABLE SYSTEM PROGRAM.  EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS
 6955                /       CAN BE INVOKED BY THE USER TO LOAD ANY SYSTEM PROGRAM CONTAINED WITHIN THIS
 6956                /       DIRECTORY; A VARIETY OF OPTIONAL PASSED PARAMETERS, OPTION SWITCHES, INPUT
 6957                /       AND/OR OUTPUT FILES, ETC. CAN BE PASSED TO THE PROGRAM BY APPROPRIATE
 6958                /       COMMAND-LINE PARAMETERS.  [NOTE: THIS METHOD IS A DIRECT DESCENDANT OF THE
 6959                /       ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM; SYNTACTICAL CHANGES, IMPROVEMENTS AND ADDITIONAL
 6960                /       FEATURES HAVE BEEN ADDED AS P?S/8 HAS BEEN DEVELOPED; HOWEVER, THE CONCEPT OF
 6961                /       A SEPARATE DIRECTORY EXCLUSIVELY FOR SYSTEM PROGRAM IMAGES HAS BEEN RETAINED.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 140

 6963                /       THE SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY ALLOWS FOR A FLEXIBLE OVERLAY STRUCTURE; IT IS
 6964                /       THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE PROGRAMMER TO ORGANIZE OVERLAYS AS REQUIRED.  SINCE
 6965                /       THE SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY ONLY DEFINES LOADING INTO THE USER MEMORY AREA
 6966                /       [00000-07577], IT IS UP TO THE PROGRAM ITSELF TO LOAD ANY ADDITIONAL SEGMENTS
 6967                /       [OR DATA] FROM OVERLAYS THAT IMMEDIATELY FOLLOW THE PRIMARY SEGMENT.  NOTE: AS
 6968                /       AN IMPLEMENTATION RESTRICTION, SYSTEM PROGRAMS ARE REQUIRED TO START WITHIN
 6969                /       FIELD 0 [GENERALLY 00000-07577].  [NOTE: STARTING AT 07600 IS ALLOWED; AFTER
 6970                /       LOADING, THE PROGRAM HARMLESSLY EXITS.  THIS IS THE RECOMMENDED METHOD FOR
 6971                /       TEMPORARILY DISABLING A SYSTEM PROGRAM WHILE UNDER GENERAL DEVELOPMENT THAT IS
 6972                /       PRESENTLY DORMANT.]  [NOTE: SINCE THE SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY CONTAINS
 6973                /       LOADING INFORMATION ABOUT MEMBER PROGRAMS, THIS IS NOT THE SAME AS LOADING
 6974                /       FILES FROM THE TFS OR ANY EXTENDED VARIANT DIRECTORIES.  TO DATE,
 6975                /       ADDING/DELETING SYSTEM PROGRAMS HAS ONLY BEEN ACCOMPLISHED BY EXPERTS FAMILIAR
 6976                /       WITH THIS DATA STRUCTURE.  IT IS POSSIBLE TO WRITE A GENERAL UTILITY OR GROUP
 6977                /       OF UTILITIES TO SIMPLIFY THE PROCESS; IT WILL BE NECESSARY TO MANAGE THE
 6978                /       UNDERLYING STORAGE ALLOCATED FOR ALL SYSTEM PROGRAMS TO ENSURE SUFFICIENT
 6979                /       SPACE EXISTS FOR A TENTATIVE SYSTEM PROGRAM.  [NOTE: WHEN THE SYSTEM IS FIRST
 6980                /       CONFIGURED, ADDITIONAL SPACE IS OFTEN PROVIDED FOR ADDING A SMALL NUMBER OF
 6981                /       ADDITIONAL UTILITIES.]  AS A RESULT, THE STARTING BLOCK OF TFS DIRECTORY
 6982                /       FILE STORAGE MUST BE ADJUSTED ACCORDINGLY; ANY STRUCTURES FURTHER UP THE
 6983                /       DEVICE MAY NEED ADDITIONAL ADJUSTMENT FOR CONSISTENCY.  ONCE THIS IS
 6984                /       ACCOMPLISHED, A SYSTEM PROGRAM BUILDER UTILITY CAN BE DEFINED; THIS WILL
 6985                /       LIKELY BE IN CONJUNCTION WITH SIGNIFICANT CHANGES MADE TO THE OVERALL P?S/8
 6986                /       SYSTEM GENERATION METHODOLOGY.  ONCE COMPLETED, THIS UTILITY CAN BE USED TO
 6987                /       CREATE OR MODIFY THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM FAR MORE FLEXIBLY AND BE PERFORMED BY
 6988                /       LESS QUALIFIED PERSONNEL.  HISTORICALLY, ALL RELATED FUNCTIONS HAVE BEEN
 6989                /       CARRIED OUT USING THE P?S/8 "DUMP" PROGRAM EITHER MANUALLY OR BY USING DUMP
 6990                /       SCRIPT FILES.  FOR MORE INFORMATION, SEE DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8
 6991                /       DUMP UTILITY.]
 6992   
 6993                /       LOADING IMAGE BINARY PROGRAMS IN THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT.
 6994   
 6995                /       WHEN THE P?S/8 SHELL IS IN EFFECT, IMAGE BINARY PROGRAMS ARE LOADED BY VARIOUS
 6996                /       NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS.  DEPENDING UPON SPECIFICS, A FINAL SEGMENT MAY BE
 6997                /       LOADED BY THE SYSTEM LOADER INTO FIELD 0.  [NOTE: AS STATED ABOVE, THE SHELL
 6998                /       CAN SUPPORT A LOADING PROCESS USING AVAILABLE EXTENDED MEMORY; MOVING DATA
 6999                /       THROUGH MEMORY CAN BE ACCOMPLISHED WITHOUT WRITING ON THE SYSTEM SCRATCH
 7000                /       AREA.]
 7001   
 7002                /       THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT USES AN ALL-PURPOSE GENERAL DIRECTORY.  EACH IMAGE
 7003                /       PROGRAM INCLUDES AN INTERNAL LOAD MAP FULLY DESCRIBING UP TO 32K OF MEMORY
 7004                /       USAGE.  AS SUCH, IMAGE PROGRAMS OCCUPY MORE STORAGE SPACE THAN IN THE STANDARD
 7005                /       P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT [WHICH DESCRIBES ONE CONTIGUOUS MEMORY SEGMENT IN FIELD 0].
 7006                /       SINCE EACH IMAGE BINARY FILE INCLUDES ITS OWN LOADING PARAMETERS AND
 7007                /       ADDITIONAL INFORMATION REGARDING INTERACTION WITH THE SHELL COMMAND
 7008                /       INTERPRETER, THE AGGREGATE SIZE OF A SHELL-BASED IMAGE PROGRAM WILL EXCEED THE
 7009                /       LOADABLE SIZE BY [A PROJECTED] TWO BLOCKS. [NOTE: THIS MAY EXPAND AS
 7010                /       ADDITIONAL FEATURES ARE IMPLEMENTED SUCH AS AN EXTENDED INTERNAL OVERLAY
 7011                /       STRUCTURE.]
 7012   
 7013                /       ASSUMING A FIELD 0 SEGMENT IS INDICATED [NOT ABSOLUTELY REQUIRED BUT TYPICALLY
 7014                /       PRESENT], THE SYSTEM LOADER IS USED TO PLACE THIS FINAL SEGMENT INTO MEMORY
 7015                /       [EXCEPT IN CERTAIN CONFIGURATIONS AS INDICATED ABOVE].  UNLIKE THE STANDARD
 7016                /       P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT, SHELL EXECUTABLE PROGRAMS CAN START ANYWHERE IN THE 32K
 7017                /       MEMORY SPACE WITHOUT RESTRICTION.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 141

 7019                /       REGARDLESS OF WHICH ENVIRONMENT IS IN EFFECT, THE SYSTEM LOADER IS GENERALLY
 7020                /       USED TO FINALIZE PROGRAM LOADING AND INITIATE PROGRAM EXECUTION WITH ALL
 7021                /       USER-SPECIFIED OPTIONS AVAILABLE TO THE PROGRAM AS REQUIRED.  [SEE VARIOUS
 7022                /       PORTIONS OF THIS DOCUMENT FOR A DESCRIPTION OF THE OPTIONAL PASSED PARAMETERS
 7023                /       AND FILE HANDLE MECHANISMS IMPLEMENTED.]
 7024   
 7025                /       COMPARING THE P?S/8 SYSTEM LOADER FUNCTION TO OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS.
 7026   
 7027                /       DESPITE THE OBVIOUS BASIC FUNCTIONALITY OF LOADING IMAGE BINARY PROGRAMS AS
 7028                /       DESCRIBED, OTHER SMALL-SCALE PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS FAIL TO PROPERLY PROVIDE
 7029                /       THIS FACILITY [OR EQUIVALENT] AND/OR THE IMPLEMENTATION IS SEVERELY FLAWED.
 7030                /       [NOTE: MANY READERS WILL SCOFF AT THIS STATEMENT BEFORE READING WHAT FOLLOWS.]
 7031                /       THE RELEVANT ISSUES OF ALL OTHER PREVAILING SYSTEMS ARE DISCUSSED BELOW:
 7032   
 7033                /       1) THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM DOES NOT DEFINE ANY OPERATING SYSTEM SERVICES TO
 7034                /          EXECUTABLE PROGRAMS.  ALL INTERFACING WITH THE USER OR THE REST OF THE
 7035                /          OPERATING ENVIRONMENT MUST BE LOADED BY THE PROGRAM.  OTHER THAN THE
 7036                /          AVAILABILITY OF THE CRUDE COMPONENTS TO INTERFACE FILES WITH SEVERAL SYSTEM
 7037                /          PROGRAMS [THAT ABSOLUTELY REQUIRE DETAILED INFORMATION BE KNOWN REGARDING
 7038                /          WHERE THESE COMPONENTS EXIST ON CERTAIN SYSTEM BLOCKS], THERE IS NO
 7039                /          INFORMATION PROVIDED WITHOUT EXPENDING SIGNIFICANT PROGRAMMING EFFORT TO
 7040                /          OBTAIN THE RELEVANT INFORMATION.
 7041   
 7042                /          THE BEST THAT CAN BE SAID ABOUT THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM KERNEL IS THAT BY
 7043                /          ESSENTIALLY IMPLEMENTING NOTHING INTRINSIC IN THE SYSTEM KERNEL OTHER THAN
 7044                /          THE ABILITY TO EXIT TO 07600 AND THE BASIC SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER READ AND
 7045                /          WRITE FUNCTIONS [ALBEIT EXHIBITING POOR PERFORMANCE, ESPECIALLY ON DECTAPE]
 7046                /          IS THAT IT FAILS TO DO ANYTHING PARTICULARLY FAULTY.  [CONTRAST THIS WITH
 7047                /          THE OS/8 EQUIVALENT AS DESCRIBED BELOW.]
 7048   
 7049                /          THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM HAS THE DUBIOUS DISTINCTION OF BEING IMPOSSIBLE TO
 7050                /          IMPLEMENT ON OTHER THAN A VERY SMALL FEW PDP-8 STORAGE DEVICES BECAUSE ALL
 7051                /          DATA IS ORGANIZED IN 129 WORD LOGICAL BLOCKS.  VIRTUALLY ALL PDP-8 STORAGE
 7052                /          PERIPHERALS DEVELOPED AFTER THE TC01/TC08 AND DF32/RF08 ARE INCAPABLE OF
 7053                /          THIS AT THE HARDWARE LEVEL.
 7054   
 7055                /       2) OS/8 NEARLY ACCOMPLISHES THE IMAGE BINARY LOADING REQUIREMENT BUT ACTUALLY
 7056                /          FAILS TO COMPLETELY IMPLEMENT A VIABLE SOLUTION DESPITE REQUIRING AT LEAST
 7057                /          8K OF MEMORY.  A FATAL FLAW IS ALWAYS A DEVICE ERROR AWAY FROM SURFACING.
 7058   
 7059                /          THE ROOT CAUSE OF THE PROBLEM IS OS/8'S CRUDE IMPLEMENTATION OF AN ERROR
 7060                /          RETURN AS PART OF THE CALLING REQUIREMENT OF EVERY INSTANCE OF A CALL TO
 7061                /          ALL HANDLERS, EVEN INTERNAL USAGE BY THE OPERATING SYSTEM ITSELF.  THIS IS
 7062                /          CONCEPTUALLY IMPOSSIBLE TO IMPLEMENT AS THERE IS NO DEFINED CODE FOR
 7063                /          HANDLING THE ERROR RETURN FOR THE PORTION OF OS/8 IMAGE LOADING ANALOGOUS
 7064                /          TO THE P?S/8 SYSTEM LOADER.  SHOULD AN ERROR OCCUR WHEN LOADING IN THE LAST
 7065                /          SEGMENT OF ANY EXECUTABLE PROGRAM, OS/8 WILL HALT WITH A FATAL ERROR
 7066                /          SOMEWHERE IN THE LAST PAGE OF FIELD 0.  [NOTE:  IT IS GENERALLY A TRIBUTE
 7067                /          TO THE RELIABILITY OF MOST PDP-8 PERIPHERAL HARDWARE TO DATE THAT THE
 7068                /          EXISTENCE OF THIS FATAL DESIGN BLUNDER IS NOT WIDELY KNOWN.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 142

 7070                /          THE CONSEQUENCES OF THIS PROBLEM CAN BE QUITE SERIOUS.  SHOULD THE USER
 7071                /          PRESS "CONTINUE" ON THE CPU FRONT PANEL PRESUMING THIS ERROR IS SOMEHOW
 7072                /          INNOCUOUS, EXECUTION OF THE PRIOR CONTENTS OF MEMORY WILL OCCUR INSTEAD OF
 7073                /          THE INTENDED FUNCTION.  THE ONLY NOTICE GIVEN OF THIS UNEXPECTED PROBLEM IS
 7074                /          THE CPU HALTING WITH NO POSSIBLE RECOURSE.  [NOTE: ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM LOADER
 7075                /          CALLS ARE HANDLED AS ANY OTHER CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  EVEN
 7076                /          THE BASIC 4K SYSTEM SUPPORTS RECOVERABLE HALTS.  PRESSING "CONTINUE" ALWAYS
 7077                /          RETRIES THE LATEST OPERATION.  IF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT
 7078                /          ALL SUCH PROBLEMS ARE HANDLED ELEGANTLY BY THE OVERLAY; IN THIS
 7079                /          CONFIGURATION THERE ARE NO DEFINED SYSTEM HALTS.]
 7080   
 7081                /       3) THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM USES AN INDEPENDENT SYSTEM LOADER THAT DISPLACES
 7082                /          THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ENTIRELY.  THE ONLY FUNCTIONALITY IS TO LOAD THE
 7083                /          BINARY IMAGE PROGRAM AND START AT THE DESIGNATED STARTING ADDRESS.  THE
 7084                /          R-L SYSTEM CAN PASS A SUBSET OF THE INFORMATION THAT P?S/8 DOES.  HOWEVER,
 7085                /          LOADED PROGRAMS HAVE NO ABILITY TO ACCESS ANY PART OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM
 7086                /          WITHOUT PROVIDING THEIR OWN DEVICE-DEPENDENT STORAGE DEVICE SUBROUTINES.
 7087   
 7088                /          IF AN ERROR SHOULD OCCUR ON THE SYSTEM DECTAPE, THE SYSTEM LOADER WILL
 7089                /          HALT THE MACHINE REGARDLESS OF ERROR [INCLUDING END ERROR].  THE OPERATION
 7090                /          CAN BE RETRIED [IF POSSIBLE] OR EXIT CAN BE MADE TO 07600.  THUS, AS
 7091                /          NECESSARY, PRESSING "CONTINUE" IS RECOMMENDED TO RECOVER FROM AN ERROR.
 7092   
 7093                /          THE R-L MONITOR FAILS TO PROVIDE EVEN THE MOST FUNDAMENTAL RESIDENT SERVICE
 7094                /          OR THE ABILITY TO READ ANY PORTION OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM STRUCTURES.  AS
 7095                /          SUCH, IT ALSO FAILS TO PROVIDE THE NECESSARY SYSTEM SERVICES AS ARE
 7096                /          AVAILABLE IN EITHER OF THE P?S/8 OPERATING ENVIRONMENTS.
 7097   
 7098                /       4) CPS-4K IS AN OPERATING SYSTEM FOR THE 4K UNMODIFIED LINC-8 OR OTHER MACHINE
 7099                /          EQUIPPED WITH THE EQUIVALENT HARDWARE TAKEN FROM THE LINC CPU REGISTERS
 7100                /          ASSOCIATED WITH CONTROLLING THE LINCTAPE HARDWARE.  [NOTE: A POSITIVE BUSS
 7101                /          PERIPHERAL THAT IS ADEQUATELY COMPATIBLE WITH THE LINC CPU REGISTERS WAS
 7102                /          SOLD AS A THIRD-PARTY STORAGE DEVICE FOR THE PDP-8/L AND SIMILAR SYSTEMS
 7103                /          WITH 4K AND A POSITIVE BUSS OR LEVEL CONVERTER.  DUE TO THE LACK OF
 7104                /          ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS ASSOCIATED WITH CERTAIN LINC-8 MODIFICATIONS, P?S/8
 7105                /          CAN ONLY RUN ON THIS DEVICE IF 8K OR MORE MEMORY IS AVAILABLE.]  WHILE THE
 7106                /          CPS-4K OPERATING SYSTEM CAN ACCESS ANY PARTICULAR COMPONENT OF THE DUAL
 7107                /          LINCTAPE DRIVE, EXECUTABLE PROGRAMS CAN ONLY BE PARTIALLY LOADED BY A
 7108                /          SYSTEM LOADER SIMILAR TO THE R-L MONITOR ENVIRONMENT [ALBEIT SOMEWHAT MORE
 7109                /          RESTRICTIVE].
 7110   
 7111                /          UNDER CPS-4K, THERE IS A DISTINCTION MADE BETWEEN PROGRAMS THAT LOAD
 7112                /          EXCLUSIVELY INTO 00000-07377 AND THOSE THAT LOAD INTO ANY PORTION OF
 7113                /          07400-07577.  THE CONTENTS OF 07600-07777 ARE CHANGED TO AN IMAGE LOADING
 7114                /          PROGRAM THUS NOT PROVIDING FUNDAMENTAL RESIDENT SERVICES MUCH LIKE THE R-L
 7115                /          MONITOR SYSTEM.  ANALOGOUS TO THE R-L ENVIRONMENT, ALL IMAGE BINARY
 7116                /          PROGRAMS MUST PROVIDE THEIR OWN COPY OF LINCTAPE READ/WRITE ROUTINES.
 7117                /          [NOTE: THE NOTION OF DEVICE DEPENDENCY IS MOOT IN CPS-4K AS IT ONLY RUNS ON
 7118                /          LINC-8 LINCTAPE HARDWARE.  EXPERIMENTAL VERSIONS OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM
 7119                /          HAVE BEEN CREATED FOR OTHER DEVICES SUCH AS THE DF32 MINIDISK.  AS IN THE
 7120                /          CASE OF THE STANDARD R-L MONITOR SYSTEM, EACH SYSTEM PROGRAM MUST PROVIDE A
 7121                /          COPY OF AN APPROPRIATE READ/WRITE SUBROUTINE FOR THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  AS
 7122                /          SUCH, CPS-4K AND THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM ARE EQUALLY INCAPABLE OF ANY FORM
 7123                /          OF DEVICE INDEPENDENCE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 143

 7125                /          BEYOND THIS THERE IS AN ADDITIONAL CONSIDERATION:  THE PROGRAM LOADER
 7126                /          PLACED IN 07600-07777 FOR THE PURPOSES OF LOADING IN IMAGES THAT DO NOT
 7127                /          INVOLVE LOADING 07400-07577 IS MOSTLY A SYSTEM BOOTSTRAP; IT IS ENTIRELY
 7128                /          DEPENDENT UPON A COMPANION ROUTINE LOADED INTO 07400-07577 TO CARRY OUT
 7129                /          MOST OF THE ACTUAL LOADING.
 7130   
 7131                /          WHEN THE IMAGE PROGRAM INVOLVES LOADING INTO 07400-07577, THE LAST PAGE OF
 7132                /          LOADING IS ACCOMPLISHED BY A SPECIAL DEDICATED LOADING ROUTINE LOCATED
 7133                /          WITHIN 07600-07777 THAT LACKS THE ABILITY TO CHECKSUM THE CONTENTS OF THE
 7134                /          LOADING BLOCK; THUS EACH AND EVERY ATTEMPT TO RUN PROGRAMS THAT INCLUDE
 7135                /          07400-07577 AS A LOADABLE SECTION IS SUBJECT TO THE POSSIBILITY OF
 7136                /          UNDETECTED LOAD FAILURE AND THE POSSIBILITY OF EXECUTION OF UNINTENDED
 7137                /          CONTENTS OF MEMORY [SOMEWHAT ANALOGOUS TO OS/8'S INTERNAL LIMITATION WHEN A
 7138                /          DEVICE FAILURE OCCURS DURING THE LOADING PROCESS].
 7139   
 7140                /       AT THIS POINT IT SHOULD BE APPARENT THAT CREATING A TRULY DEVICE-INDEPENDENT
 7141                /       SYSTEM PROGRAM LOADER WITHOUT LIMITATIONS IS NOT EASILY ACHIEVED.  THE
 7142                /       ADVANCED TECHNIQUES USED IN THE P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ALLOW
 7143                /       FOR A PROPER KERNEL COMPONENT THAT COEXISTS WITH THE MAIN DEVICE HANDLER AND
 7144                /       ASSOCIATED FEATURES.  THAT P?S/8 ACCOMPLISHES THIS WHILE ALSO IMPLEMENTING A
 7145                /       SUBSTANTIAL SUPERSET OF THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR PASSED PROGRAM PARAMETERS
 7146                /       MAKES THIS ALL THE MORE REMARKABLE.  [NOTE: THIS DOCUMENT ONLY DISCUSSES THE
 7147                /       TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  THE SYSTEM LOADER IS DEVICE-INDEPENDENT AND
 7148                /       PRESENT IN ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS FOR ALL SUPPORTED DEVICES.  ALL
 7149                /       MAJOR COMPONENTS OF P?S/8 DEPEND ON THE PRESENCE OF THE SYSTEM LOADER AS
 7150                /       DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.]
 7151   
 7152                /       HISTORY OF THE SYSTEM PROGRAM LOADING FUNCTION.
 7153   
 7154                /       WHILE THE ENTIRE DEVELOPMENT STORY OF THE SYSTEM LOADER CANNOT EASILY BE
 7155                /       RECOUNTED IN DETAIL, CERTAIN ASPECTS OF THIS EVOLUTIONARY PROCESS CAN BE
 7156                /       HIGHLIGHTED, STARTING FROM THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM'S CRUDE EQUIVALENT.
 7157   
 7158                /       THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS INCAPABLE OF SUPPORTING AN EMBEDDED
 7159                /       CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  ALTERNATE MEANS WERE PRAGMATICALLY
 7160                /       CREATED SINCE A WORKING SYSTEM WAS MORE IMPORTANT AT THE TIME; MOST OF THE
 7161                /       ORIGINAL EFFORT DEALT WITH THE LARGER WORK OF A COMPLEX OF MANY COMPONENTS.
 7162                /       LIKE MANY OTHER PROJECTS, THERE ARE INITIAL TRADEOFFS AT LEAST WHEN SOME
 7163                /       SEMBLANCE OF OVERALL FUNCTIONALITY HAS BEEN ACHIEVED. [TAKEN AS A WHOLE, THE
 7164                /       GRADUAL EMERGENCE OF P?S/8 REPRESENTS THE CONTINUING WORK STARTED BY THE
 7165                /       PIONEERING EFFORTS OF THE EARLY IMPLEMENTORS STILL AS AN EVOLVING PROCESS EVEN
 7166                /       NOW.  BY ELIMINATING THE NOTION OF IMPOSED DEADLINES, INCREASED FUNCTIONALITY
 7167                /       CAN ALWAYS BE ADDED.]
 7168   
 7169                /       WHEN THE R-L MONITOR WAS CREATED USING THE MOST PRIMITIVE POSSIBLE ENVIRONMENT
 7170                /       [CONSISTING OF MANUAL ASSEMBLY OF THE CODE AND MANUAL ENTRY OF THE BINARY
 7171                /       VIA THE PDP-8 FRONT PANEL DIRECTLY], THE IMPETUS CLEARLY WAS TO CREATE SOME
 7172                /       USEFUL FUNCTIONALITY.  [UNFORTUNATELY, THE ORIGINAL IMPLEMENTORS WENT ON TO
 7173                /       PURSUE OTHER ACTIVITIES, PRIMARILY AS EMPLOYEES OF DEC WHERE MANAGERS DECIDED
 7174                /       WHAT WAS THE BEST USE OF THEIR PRODUCTIVE TIME, ARGUABLY A QUITE UNFORTUNATE
 7175                /       SET OF EVENTS; P?S/8 WAS CREATED BY OTHERS WHO "CUT THEIR PDP-8 TEETH" ON THE
 7176                /       R-L MONITOR SYSTEM AND THUS HAD MUCH MORE OF A DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT TO WORK
 7177                /       FROM.  HAVING MERELY THE ABILITY TO MAKE SMALL SOURCE CODE CHANGES THAT CAN
 7178                /       HAVE LARGE IMPACT ON THE BINARY OUTCOME PROFOUNDLY CHANGES WHAT IS POSSIBLE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 144

 7180                /       THE MANY LIMITATIONS OF THE R-L MONITOR IMAGE PROGRAM LOADING PROCESS CAN BE
 7181                /       "EXCUSED" GIVEN THE LACK OF SUFFICIENT SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER PROGRAM
 7182                /       DEVELOPMENT AT THE TIME.  PRODUCING A MORE POWERFUL REPLACEMENT DEVICE HANDLER
 7183                /       WAS ULTIMATELY THE RESULT OF TREMENDOUS EFFORT ON THE PART OF MANY INDIVIDUALS
 7184                /       PRIMARILY CONCERNED WITH REDUCING TOTAL CODE SPACE WHILE ALSO ADDING
 7185                /       IMPORTANT FEATURES; THIS PROCESS LITERALLY TOOK YEARS TO ACHIEVE A VIABLE
 7186                /       RESULT.  ARGUABLY THE HANDLER AS DESCRIBED IN THIS DOCUMENT [ALONG WITH THE
 7187                /       ASSOCIATED INTERNAL FEATURES] IS THE MOST WORKED OVER PIECE OF PDP-8 CODE IN
 7188                /       HISTORY.  AS OF THE TIME OF THIS WRITING, EVEN THE CODE DESCRIBED IN THIS
 7189                /       DOCUMENT IS NOT AT THE MOST ADVANCED STATE KNOWN. [SEE ELSEWHERE FOR A
 7190                /       DISCUSSION OF ADDITIONAL CHANGES AND CRITICISMS OF THE CODE DESCRIBED IN THIS
 7191                /       DOCUMENT.  SOME OF THE ADDITIONAL CHANGES OCCURRED A FULL DECADE AFTER THIS
 7192                /       CODE WAS WRITTEN.  IT IS HOPED NEWER VERSIONS WILL BE MADE AVAILABLE;
 7193                /       ADDITIONAL SPECIFIC CHANGES ARE PLANNED FOR THE FUTURE.]
 7194   
 7195                /       THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM LOADER AND RELATED ISSUES.
 7196   
 7197                /       THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR SYSTEM [WITHOUT ANY OF THE LATTER DAY IMPROVEMENTS
 7198                /       LEADING TO P?S/8] REQUIRES AN ASSORTMENT OF UGLY KLUDGES TO PRODUCE A VIABLE
 7199                /       SYSTEM.  EVERY PROGRAM REQUIRING ACCESS TO ANY ASPECT OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 7200                /       [SUCH AS THE CONTENTS OF FILES] MUST INCLUDE AN IMBEDDED DEVICE-DEPENDENT
 7201                /       READ [AND PERHAPS WRITE] SUBROUTINE.  HAVING AN INTERNAL DEVICE HANDLER IN
 7202                /       EVERY SYSTEM UTILITY WASTES OVERALL STORAGE SPACE AND INCREASES PROGRAM SIZE.
 7203                /       [NOTE: ORIGINALLY ONLY THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT 0 WAS SUPPORTED.  ON
 7204                /       AN EXPERIMENTAL BASIS, ALTERNATE PROGRAMMING WAS CREATED TO ADDRESS OTHER
 7205                /       DEVICES SUCH AS THE DF32.  DUE TO ADDITIONAL LIMITATIONS OF THE OPERATING
 7206                /       SYSTEM, ONLY DEVICES THAT CAN SUPPORT [RICHARD LARY'S] "SLURP" BINARY FILE
 7207                /       LOADER CAN BE CONSIDERED.  EVERY RELEVANT PROGRAM REQUIRES SIGNIFICANT
 7208                /       PATCHING TO SUPPORT THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  BY NATURE, MUCH OF THIS WORK IS HIGHLY
 7209                /       REDUNDANT AT THE SOURCE CODE LEVEL BUT QUITE DIFFERENT AT THE BINARY LEVEL;
 7210                /       THE R-L MONITOR NEVER HAD COMPLETE SOURCE CODE AVAILABLE COMPLICATING MATTERS
 7211                /       STILL FURTHER.]
 7212   
 7213                /       SUPPORTING R-L MONITOR TEXTUAL INPUT FILES REQUIRES ALL OF THE FOLLOWING:
 7214   
 7215                /       1) AN INPUT UNPACKING ROUTINE THAT OCCUPIES ABOUT 0.5 PAGES OF CODE SPACE.
 7216                /          [NOTE: THIS FIGURE MUST BE ADJUSTED DEPENDING ON ADDITIONAL PROGRAM
 7217                /          REQUIREMENTS.  CERTAIN PROGRAMS MERELY PROCESS THE CONTENTS OF THE FILES
 7218                /          WHILE OTHERS REQUIRE KEYBOARD SUPPORT AFTER THE AGGREGATE FILE CONTENTS IS
 7219                /          COMPLETELY READ.  CERTAIN PROGRAMS MAY REQUIRE IMBEDDED COMMANDS TO
 7220                /          TRANSLATE CHARACTER SEQUENCES INCOMPATIBLE WITH THE TEXT FILE CHARACTER
 7221                /          SET.  THE MORE COMPLEX THE REQUIREMENTS, THE MORE CODE SPACE REQUIRED.]
 7222   
 7223                /       2) A ONE PAGE INPUT BUFFER TO READ THE RAW FILE CONTENTS.  [NOTE: CERTAIN
 7224                /          PROGRAMS HAVE LARGER READ BUFFERS TO INCREASE THROUGHPUT.  HOWEVER, ANY
 7225                /          PROGRAM DESIGNED TO REQUIRE MOST OF 4K MEMORY IS ALREADY QUITE TAXED BY THE
 7226                /          MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS.  A PROGRAM THAT MUST ALSO WRITE OUT DATA WOULD
 7227                /          REQUIRE ADDITIONAL RESOURCES SUCH AS AN OUTPUT BUFFER OF ONE OR MORE PAGES
 7228                /          AND AN OUTPUT DATA PACKING ROUTINE.]
 7229   
 7230                /       3) AN ADDITIONAL PAGE FOR THE [TC01/TC08 DECTAPE] READ/WRITE ROUTINES. [NOTE:
 7231                /          CHANGING THE SYSTEM DEVICE REQUIRES THIS CODE BE REPLACED WITH CODE FOR THE
 7232                /          ALTERNATE DEVICE.  MOREOVER, IT WOULD NOT BE FEASIBLE TO RESERVE MORE CODE
 7233                /          SPACE TO SUPPORT MORE DIFFICULT DEVICES AS SPACE IS AT A PREMIUM IN
 7234                /          VIRTUALLY ALL CASES.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 145

 7236                /       ALL OF THESE REQUIREMENTS TAKEN TOGETHER APPROACHES THREE PAGES OF CODE SPACE,
 7237                /       NEARLY TEN PERCENT OF THE TOTAL USER SPACE OF 00000-07577.
 7238   
 7239                /       AS SUCH, DESIGNING SYSTEM PROGRAMS TO FUNCTION UNDER THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM
 7240                /       IS QUITE AN ARDUOUS TASK.  FOR MANY PROGRAMS, THE REQUIREMENTS OF INTERFACE
 7241                /       EXCEED THE FREE SPACE AVAILABLE IN 00000-07577.  EARLIER PROGRAMS GENERALLY
 7242                /       WERE DESIGNED TO BE LOADED UNDER THE PAPER-TAPE ENVIRONMENT WHERE ALL INPUT
 7243                /       AND OUTPUT IS DIRECTED TO EITHER THE SYSTEM CONSOLE OR PERHAPS THE PAPER-TAPE
 7244                /       READER/PUNCH; COMPARATIVELY LITTLE CODE SPACE IS NEEDED TO SUPPORT THESE
 7245                /       DEVICES.  WITHOUT EITHER COMPROMISE OR RADICAL REDESIGN, IT MAY PROVE
 7246                /       IMPOSSIBLE TO INTERFACE CERTAIN PROGRAMS TO THIS OPERATING SYSTEM.
 7247   
 7248                /       THE BASIC R-L MONITOR IMAGE PROGRAM LOADING PROCESS.
 7249   
 7250                /       JUST TO LOAD A CONFORMING PROGRAM INTO MEMORY, THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM USES A
 7251                /       SEPARATE MODULE KNOWN AS THE "SYSTEM LOADER".  WHILE THE MAIN OPERATING SYSTEM
 7252                /       CONTAINS READ/WRITE ROUTINES LOCATED IN 07600-07777, THERE ISN'T SUFFICIENT
 7253                /       CODE SPACE TO ACCOMPLISH ALL OF THE FOLLOWING REQUIREMENTS:
 7254   
 7255                /       1) MAINTAIN THE VIABILITY OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER FOR READ/WRITE
 7256                /          OPERATIONS ONCE PROGRAM EXECUTION IS INITIATED.
 7257   
 7258                /       2) PROVIDE THE MEANS TO LOAD IN THE DESIGNATED PROGRAM.  THE PROGRAM COULD BE
 7259                /          AS LARGE AS A SINGLE SEGMENT MEANT TO LOAD INTO 00000-07577. [ACCOMPLISHING
 7260                /          THIS REQUIRES CODE RESIDENT IN 07600-07777.]
 7261   
 7262                /       3) START THE PROGRAM UP FROM AN ARBITRARY STARTING ADDRESS OBTAINED FROM THE
 7263                /          SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY.
 7264   
 7265                /       4) PASS FILE HANDLES ASSOCIATED WITH FILE NAMES OPTIONALLY INCLUDED IN THE
 7266                /          "RUN" COMMAND USED TO INITIATE THE PROGRAM.  THE FILES WILL BE USED IN A
 7267                /          MANNER DETERMINED SOLELY BY THE PROGRAM [OR PERHAPS IGNORED].  [NOTE: P?S/8
 7268                /          IMPLEMENTS SEVERAL EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS; THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM ONLY
 7269                /          HAS A PRIMITIVE "RU" COMMAND WHERE ONLY THE FIRST TWO LETTERS OF THE
 7270                /          PROGRAM ARE SIGNIFICANT.]
 7271   
 7272                /       5) PASS USER-PROVIDED PARAMETER INFORMATION.  [NOTE: IN THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM
 7273                /          THIS CONSISTS OF MERELY A SINGLE 12-BIT VALUE OBTAINED FROM AN "=XXXX"
 7274                /          OPTION INCLUDED IN THE RUN COMMAND-LINE.  PROGRAMS GENERALLY HAVE TO DEFINE
 7275                /          BITWISE APPORTIONMENT OF THIS PARAMETER TO CHANGE THE DYNAMICS OF PROGRAM
 7276                /          EXECUTION.]
 7277   
 7278                /       6) MAINTAIN A PROPER EXIT AND OPERATING SYSTEM RESTART AT 07600.
 7279   
 7280                /       AS SUCH, THE FIRST REQUIREMENT WAS COMPLETELY DROPPED.  ALL PROGRAMS THAT
 7281                /       REQUIRE ACCESS TO ANY ASPECT OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HAVE TO INCLUDE ROUTINES TO
 7282                /       READ OR WRITE TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE AS NECESSARY; THE OPERATING SYSTEM CANNOT
 7283                /       PROVIDE EVEN BASIC ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  [NOTE: R-L MONITOR BATCH IS
 7284                /       ABLE TO WORK WITH THE SYSTEM LOADER ON A SPECIALIZED BASIS; THIS IS NOT
 7285                /       RECOMMENDED FOR GENERAL-PURPOSE USE.  FUNCTIONALITY IS LIMITED TO SINGLE PAGE
 7286                /       READ-ONLY OPERATIONS TO A DESIGNATED BUFFER WITHIN THE KEYBOARD MONITOR.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 146

 7288                /       THE R-L KEYBOARD MONITOR LOADS A ONE-PAGE SKELETAL SYSTEM LOADER PROGRAM INTO
 7289                /       MEMORY AND EXPANDS ALL SPECIFICS AS REQUIRED BY THE SYSTEM PROGRAM SELECTED;
 7290                /       LOADING ADDRESS, NUMBER OF BLOCKS TO LOAD AND STARTING ADDRESS ARE STORED INTO
 7291                /       THE CODE AT DESIGNATED LOCATIONS.  A TC01/08 COMPATIBLE DECTAPE BOOTSTRAP IS
 7292                /       PRESENT AT RELATIVE 0000 OF THE PAGE DESIGNED TO FUNCTION ONLY WHEN MOVED TO
 7293                /       07600-0777.  THE "=XXXX" VALUE IS STORED INTO RELATIVE 0156 OF THE PAGE.  WHEN
 7294                /       THE PAGE IS LATER MOVED TO 07600-07777, THIS VALUE CAN BE READ BY THE LOADED
 7295                /       SYSTEM PROGRAM BY ACCESSING 07756.  [NOTE: THIS CONVENTION IS MAINTAINED IN
 7296                /       P?S/8 FOR BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY.]
 7297   
 7298                /       THE FILE HANDLE PARAMETERS REPRESENTING THE PASSED FILES INCLUDED IN THE RUN
 7299                /       COMMAND ARE STORED INTO RELATIVE 0157 AND FOLLOWING UP TO RELATIVE 0177.  IF
 7300                /       THE LIST IS SHORTER THAN THE MAXIMUM ALLOWED [17 INPUT FILES], A SENTINEL 0000
 7301                /       VALUE IS PLACED IN THE NEXT WORD AS A LIST TERMINATOR.
 7302   
 7303                /       FULL LENGTH LISTS DO NOT REQUIRE A TERMINATOR DUE TO THE DESIGNATED WAY TO
 7304                /       ACCESS THE FILES AS ILLUSTRATED IN THE EXAMPLE BELOW.
 7305   
 7306                /       CODE FRAGMENT TO ACCESS PASSED R-L FILES.
 7307   
 7308                /       TAD     (SFILES-1)              /SETUP THE
 7309                /       DCA     POINTER                 /LIST POINTER
 7310   
 7311                /       AT THIS POINT THE "POINTER" ADDRESS NEEDS TO BE INCREMENTED TO POINT TO THE
 7312                /       LATEST FILE HANDLE.
 7313   
 7314                /UPLUP, ISZ     POINTER                 /BUMP TO NEXT FILE HANDLE
 7315                /       TAD I   POINTER                 /GET THE NEXT FILE BLOCK
 7316   
 7317                /       THE AC EITHER CONTAINS THE STARTING BLOCK OF A FILE OR 0000.  THERE ARE TWO
 7318                /       WAYS FOR THE 0000 CASE TO OCCUR:
 7319   
 7320                /       1) THE LIST SENTINEL [0000] WAS LOADED SIGNIFYING THE END OF THE LIST.
 7321   
 7322                /       2) THE "ISZ POINTER" INSTRUCTION SKIPPED SINCE THE LAST ELEMENT IN A
 7323                /          FULL-LENGTH LIST WAS PROCESSED PREVIOUSLY.  THE CONTENTS OF "POINTER"
 7324                /          CHANGED FROM 7777 TO 0000.
 7325   
 7326                /       SNA                             /SKIP IF ANOTHER PASSED FILE TO BE PROCESSED
 7327                /       JMP     ENDOFALLFILES           /JUMP IF NO ADDITIONAL FILES TO PROCESS
 7328                /       DCA     BLOCK                   /STORE IN-LINE WHERE NEEDED
 7329   
 7330                /       THE LATEST FILE IS PROCESSED HERE; EVENTUALLY LOOPING BACK TO THE START OF THE
 7331                /       UNPACKING LOOP.
 7332   
 7333                /       JMP     UPLUP                   /GO PROCESS ANOTHER PASSED FILE [IF ANY MORE]
 7334   
 7335                /       THE FINAL ACTION TAKEN BY THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM IS TO MOVE THE ENTIRE PAGE
 7336                /       NOW FULLY PRELOADED WITH ALL RELEVANT PARAMETERS TO 07600-07777; AS A RESULT
 7337                /       THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS DESTROYED.  THE SYSTEM LOADER IS STARTED AT ITS
 7338                /       DESIGNATED STARTING ADDRESS TO CARRY OUT THE REST OF THE ACTUAL PROGRAM LOAD.
 7339                /       FINALLY, THE SYSTEM PROGRAM IS STARTED VIA A "JMP I" INSTRUCTION REFERENCING
 7340                /       THE LOCATION WHERE THE PROGRAM STARTING ADDRESS WAS STORED EARLIER.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 147

 7342                /       AT THIS POINT, THE DESIGNATED PROGRAM IS IN MEMORY; NOTHING REMAINS OF THE R-L
 7343                /       MONITOR SYSTEM OTHER THAN THE MEANS TO REBOOT THE SYSTEM AT 07600 AND
 7344                /       ARTIFACTS OF THE SYSTEM LOADER CODE.
 7345   
 7346                /       OTHER THAN PRESERVING THE ORIGINAL SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, ALL OTHER ASPECTS OF
 7347                /       LOADING A SYSTEM PROGRAM HAVE BEEN ACHIEVED, ALBEIT BY HIGH-OVERHEAD
 7348                /       TECHNIQUES INVOLVING READING IN THE SYSTEM LOADER AS WELL AS THE SYSTEM
 7349                /       DIRECTORY.  AS STATED ABOVE, IT IS THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE SYSTEM PROGRAM TO
 7350                /       PERFORM ANY FILE ACCESS OR OTHER OPERATIONS BY PROVIDING EMBEDDED READ/WRITE
 7351                /       ROUTINES.  THE ONLY FACILITIES PROVIDED ARE:
 7352   
 7353                /       1) THE ABILITY TO RESTART AT 07600. [NOTE: THE AC MUST BE CLEAR.]
 7354   
 7355                /       2) THE LIST OF PASSED FILE HANDLES IS PRESENT AT 07757 AND FOLLOWING.
 7356   
 7357                /       3) THE "=XXXX" VALUE IS PRESENT IN 07756.
 7358   
 7359                /       REPLACEMENT OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM LOADER WITH THE P?S/8 SYSTEM LOADER.
 7360   
 7361                /       REMOVAL OF THE OVERHEAD OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM LOADING RESTRICTIONS AND
 7362                /       KLUDGES WAS EVENTUALLY ACHIEVED WHILE RETAINING BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY.  A
 7363                /       RESIDENT DEVICE-INDEPENDENT SYSTEM LOADER ELIMINATES ALL ASSOCIATED OVERHEAD
 7364                /       WHILE STILL SUPPORTING THE ENTIRE USER MEMORY SPACE [00000-07577] FOR PROGRAM
 7365                /       LOADING.  ALL EXISTING R-L MONITOR PASSED PARAMETERS ARE RETAINED WHILE NEW
 7366                /       FEATURES ARE ADDED TO ENHANCE THE PROGRAM OPERATING ENVIRONMENT.
 7367   
 7368                /       REPLACEMENT OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM HANDLER WITH THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE
 7369                /       HANDLER.
 7370   
 7371                /       A SEVERE WEAKNESS OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM IS THE LIMITED CAPABILITY OF THE
 7372                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER [WHICH IS NOT PRESENT WHEN A PROGRAM IS RUNNING].  [WHEN
 7373                /       AVAILABLE,] THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS CALLED BY THE FOLLOWING METHOD:
 7374   
 7375                /       JMS I   (HANDLER)               /CALL THE READ [OR WRITE] HANDLER.
 7376                /       BUFFER                          /[READ OR WRITE] BUFFER ADDRESS.
 7377                /       -BLKCNT                         /TWOS' COMPLEMENT OF NUMBER OF BLOCKS.
 7378                /       BLOCK                           /STARTING BLOCK NUMBER
 7379   
 7380                /       THERE IS NO SUPPORT FOR A LOGICAL UNIT NUMBER AS THE SYSTEM IS LIMITED TO
 7381                /       SUPPORTING DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT 0 ONLY.  NO SUPPORT EXISTS FOR EXTENDED MEMORY.
 7382                /       ALL OF FIELD 0 CAN BE WRITTEN; HOWEVER, READING OVER 07600-07777 DESTROYS THE
 7383                /       DEVICE HANDLER. [NOTE: IF EXTENDED MEMORY OPERATIONS WERE SUPPORTED, FULL
 7384                /       FIELD READS COULD BE IMPLEMENTED.  THIS COULD FACILITATE EFFICIENT COPY
 7385                /       UTILITIES ASSUMING EXTENDED MEMORY IS AVAILABLE.]
 7386   
 7387                /       ADDITIONALLY, TWO CALLING ADDRESSES ARE REQUIRED; ONE IS FOR READING WHILE THE
 7388                /       OTHER IS FOR WRITING.  NEITHER ADDRESS IS PARTICULARLY OPTIMAL IN ANY SENSE.
 7389                /       ALL CALLING PROGRAMS REQUIRE ADDRESS CONSTANTS TO ACCESS EITHER ROUTINE.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 148

 7391                /       THE P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLER IS FAR MORE CAPABLE:
 7392   
 7393                /       JMS I   (7640)                  /CALL THE HANDLER. [NOTE: "SZA CLA" IS 7640].
 7394                /       BUFFER                          /BUFFER ADDRESS
 7395                /       FUNCTION WORD                   /COMPOSITE FUNCTION WORD [SEE BELOW FOR DETAILS]
 7396                /       BLOCK                           /STARTING BLOCK NUMBER
 7397   
 7398                /       EXTENDED MEMORY CALLS ARE SUPPORTED BY THE CALLING PROGRAM SETTING ITS OWN
 7399                /       DATA FIELD BEFORE CALLING THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IN FIELD 0.
 7400   
 7401                /       THE FUNCTION WORD BREAKS DOWN AS FOLLOWS:
 7402   
 7403                /       BIT[0] 1=WRITING, 0=READING.
 7404   
 7405                /       BITS[1-5] NUMBER OF PAGES IN THE ENTIRE TRANSFER 01-37; 0 MEANS 40.
 7406   
 7407                /       BITS[6-8] BUFFER FIELD FOR THE TRANSFER.
 7408   
 7409                /       BITS[9-11] LOGICAL UNIT FOR THE TRANSFER.
 7410   
 7411                /       LOGICAL UNITS ARE MEANINGFUL FOR ALL PROGRAMS AND FILES.  FILE HANDLES ARE
 7412                /       MADE MORE POWERFUL WITHOUT CHANGING OVERALL STORAGE SIZE.  THIS IS ACHIEVED BY
 7413                /       IMPOSING A BOUNDARY RESTRICTION ON THE STARTING BLOCK OF ALL FILES:
 7414   
 7415                /       FILE SYSTEM CHANGES.
 7416   
 7417                /       ONCE LOGICAL UNIT SUPPORT WAS ADDED, IT BECAME NECESSARY TO PASS LOGICAL UNIT
 7418                /       AS WELL AS BLOCK INFORMATION WITHIN ALL FILE HANDLES.  TO ACCOMMODATE THIS
 7419                /       WITHOUT COMPROMISING THE PASSED FILE LIST, THE FILE SYSTEM IMPOSES A BOUNDARY
 7420                /       RESTRICTION ON FILE PLACEMENT WITHIN EACH DEVICE.  [NOTE: ALL R-L MONITOR AND
 7421                /       EARLY P?S/8 RELEASES ALLOW FREE PLACEMENT OF THE FILE SYSTEM; NEWER RELEASES
 7422                /       OF P?S/8 CAN DIRECTLY COPY INDIVIDUAL FILES TO A DEVICE CONFORMING TO THE NEW
 7423                /       RESTRICTION VIA KEYBOARD MONITOR COMMANDS.  AS SUCH, ALL USER FILES CAN ALWAYS
 7424                /       BE COPIED TO A NEWER RELEASE DESPITE CHANGING SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS.  EXISTING
 7425                /       TEXT FILES CREATED ON THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM WERE FIRST BE PASSED THROUGH AN
 7426                /       IMPROMPTU IN-PLACE FILE CONVERSION PROGRAM THAT DESTROYS THE VIABILITY OF THE
 7427                /       R-L SYSTEM IN PREPARATION FOR THE REST OF THE COPYING PROCESS.  BINARY FILES
 7428                /       ARE DIRECTLY COMPATIBLE; P?S/8 SUPPORTS A PROPER SUPERSET OF THE EARLIER R-L
 7429                /       MONITOR BINARY FILE FORMAT WHICH INCLUDES EXTENDED MEMORY SUPPORT.]  [NOTE: IF
 7430                /       THERE IS SUFFICIENT INTEREST, A NON-DESTRUCTIVE CONVERSION PROGRAM CAN BE
 7431                /       WRITTEN THAT PRESERVES THE ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR TEXT FILES; TWO DECTAPE DRIVES
 7432                /       WILL BE REQUIRED ADDRESSABLE BY EXISTING P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLERS SUCH AS THE
 7433                /       CURRENT TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE OR ONE OF THE TD8E-BASED P?S/8 SYSTEMS.]
 7434   
 7435                /       THE NEWEST SYSTEMS REQUIRE ALL FILES BE ALIGNED ON BOUNDARIES OF EIGHT BLOCKS.
 7436                /       [NOTE: SINCE ALL TFS FILES ARE SIXTEEN BLOCKS LONG, THIS RESTRICTION IS EASILY
 7437                /       COMPLIED WITH.  TO DATE, ALL ACTUAL FILE SYSTEMS HAVE BEEN CREATED WITH A
 7438                /       SIXTEEN BLOCK BOUNDARY IN ANTICIPATION OF A FUTURE UNSPECIFIED REQUIREMENT.]
 7439   
 7440                /       THE FILE HANDLES NOW CONSIST OF THE LOGICAL UNIT BITS ADDED TO THE FILE
 7441                /       STARTING BLOCK; THIS IS A FUNCTIONAL SOLUTION AS THE LOW-ORDER THREE BITS OF
 7442                /       THE STARTING BLOCK ARE ALWAYS ZERO [DUE TO THE BOUNDARY ALIGNMENT RESTRICTION
 7443                /       IMPOSED].  THE FILE HANDLES MUST BE PROCESSED AS FOLLOWS:


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 149

 7445                /       ACCESSING THE P?S/8 FILE HANDLES LIST.
 7446   
 7447                /       PROCESSING INDIVIDUAL FILE HANDLES IS ACCOMPLISHED USING THE SAME OVERALL
 7448                /       METHOD AS IN THE R-L MONITOR EXAMPLE ABOVE WITH THE FOLLOWING MODIFICATIONS:
 7449   
 7450                /       TAD I   POINTER                 /GET THE LATEST FILE HANDLE
 7451                /       AND     (7770)                  /JUST BLOCK BITS
 7452                /       DCA     BLOCK                   /STORE WHERE NEEDED [AS IN THE R-L ORIGINAL]
 7453                /       TAD I   POINTER                 /GET THE FILE HANDLE AGAIN
 7454                /       AND     (7)                     /ISOLATE UNIT BITS
 7455   
 7456                /       GENERALLY THE LOGICAL UNIT IS COMBINED WITH ADDITIONAL FUNCTION WORD VALUES
 7457                /       AND STORED AS A COMPOSITE IN-LINE ARGUMENT TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
 7458   
 7459                /       TAD     (1^100)                 /READ ONE PAGE FROM CURRENT LOGICAL UNIT
 7460                /       DCA     FUNCTION                /STORE IN-LINE
 7461   
 7462                /       FOR CALLING PROGRAMS WITH LITTLE CODE SPACE TO SPARE, IT IS OFTEN HELPFUL THE
 7463                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ADDRESS IS THE SAME BINARY CODE AS THE "SZA CLA"
 7464                /       INSTRUCTION; VIRTUALLY ALL PDP-8 PROGRAMMING WILL TEND TO CONTAIN THIS
 7465                /       INSTRUCTION ON THE SAME PAGE AS A CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER. [NOTE: IF
 7466                /       THIS IS NOT "NATURALLY" THE CASE, IT IS GENERALLY EASILY CONTRIVED MERELY BY
 7467                /       SLIGHTLY REORGANIZING EXISTING PROGRAMMING AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS
 7468                /       DOCUMENT.]
 7469   
 7470                /       P?S/8 SYSTEM LOADER USAGE.
 7471   
 7472                /       P?S/8 PROVIDES A FAR RICHER SET OF OPERATING PARAMETERS PASSED TO THE LOADED
 7473                /       IMAGE PROGRAM THAN THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM IT REPLACES.  THIS INCLUDES THE
 7474                /       CURRENT SYSTEM DATE, ACCESS TO SPECIFIED OUTPUT FILES AND SINGLE-CHARACTER
 7475                /       OPTION SWITCHES [/A THROUGH /9] AMONG OTHER FEATURES.  [NOTE: THE FULL LIST OF
 7476                /       PASSED PARAMETERS IS RELEASE-DEPENDENT AND SUBJECT TO CHANGE.]  THIS WAS MADE
 7477                /       POSSIBLE BECAUSE OF THE EXCEPTIONAL EFFORT EXPENDED TO OPTIMIZE THE TC01/TC08
 7478                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER TO OCCUPY LESS CODE SPACE.  AS THE INITIALLY SUPPORTED
 7479                /       DEVICE, THIS HAS BECOME THE STANDARD TO WHICH ALL OTHER DEVICE HANDLERS MUST
 7480                /       BE HELD; ALL SUPPORTED DEVICES MUST INCLUDE ALL DESIGNATED OPTIONS FOR TOTAL
 7481                /       COMPATIBILITY.  WHERE POSSIBLE, DEVICE SUPPORT IS IMPLEMENTED IN 4K FOR THE
 7482                /       MINIMUM VIABLE SYSTEM; ALL SYSTEMS SUPPORT THE SAME PASSED PARAMETERS
 7483                /       REGARDLESS OF ACTUAL MEMORY SIZE.
 7484   
 7485                /       WITHIN THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SPACE IS A RESERVED AREA FOR THE
 7486                /       SYSTEM LOADER, AN IMBEDDED DEDICATED CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER
 7487                /       FOLLOWED BY A DEDICATED "JMP I" STARTING INSTRUCTION AND DEDICATED STARTING
 7488                /       ADDRESS POINTER.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 150

 7490                /       THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND SYSTEM LOADER ARE ORDINARILY NEVER
 7491                /       DISPLACED FROM 07600-07777.  [NOTE: THERE IS ONE EXCEPTION: THE P?S/8 "BIN"
 7492                /       PROGRAM CAN PROVIDE AN OPTIONAL DEVICE-SPECIFIC BINARY FILE "SLURP" LOADER OR
 7493                /       AN EQUIVALENT TO DEC'S PAPER-TAPE BIN AND RIM LOADERS; EITHER OPTION ALSO
 7494                /       INCLUDES A DEVICE-SPECIFIC BOOTSTRAP AT 07600 TO RESTART P?S/8.  IN EITHER OF
 7495                /       THESE OPERATING MODES, PROGRAMS ARE LOADED WITH NO EXPECTATION OF OPERATING
 7496                /       SYSTEM SERVICES WITH THE EXCEPTION OF THE ABILITY TO REBOOT THE OPERATING
 7497                /       SYSTEM BY EXITING TO 07600.  AS AN IMPROVEMENT TO THE ANALOGOUS SITUATION IN
 7498                /       THE R-L MONITOR, THE AC NEED NOT BE CLEAR.  THE DATA FIELD MUST BE SET TO
 7499                /       ZERO AND INTERRUPTS MUST BE TURNED OFF.  REFER TO ADDITIONAL DOCUMENTATION OF
 7500                /       THE BIN SYSTEM PROGRAM FOR MORE INFORMATION.]  [NOTE: AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE
 7501                /       IN THIS DOCUMENT, THE SLURP LOADER SUPPORTS A MEANS OF OVERLAY TO RESTORE THE
 7502                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AFTER LOADING ALL USER BINARY FILES; THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 7503                /       CAN BE WRITE-PROTECTED AS THE ENTIRE RELOAD PROCESS TAKES PLACE IN MEMORY.]
 7504   
 7505                /       AS NECESSARY, VARIOUS PORTIONS OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM INSERT VALUES INTO THE
 7506                /       SYSTEM LOADER TO EFFECT DIFFERENT IMAGE-ORIENTED SERVICES.  MOST IMPLEMENTED
 7507                /       FUNCTIONS FALL INTO ONE OF THE FOLLOWING CATEGORIES:
 7508   
 7509                /       1) IMAGE PROGRAM EXECUTION.  VARIOUS EXECUTION-CLASS COMMANDS [REPLACING THE
 7510                /          ORIGINAL R-L MONITOR RU[N] COMMAND AND PARTICULAR TO VARIOUS OPERATING
 7511                /          MODES OF P?S/8 AND THE P?S/8 SHELL] WILL STORE LOADING AND STARTING
 7512                /          PARAMETERS [LOADING ADDRESS, FUNCTION WORD, STARTING BLOCK AND STARTING
 7513                /          ADDRESS] TO INITIATE PROGRAM EXECUTION THAT GENERALLY OVERLAYS THE SYSTEM
 7514                /          SEGMENT THAT INITIATED THE COMMAND.  UNLIKE THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM, THIS
 7515                /          IS ALL THAT IS REQUIRED TO INITIATE IMAGE PROGRAM LOADING.  THE SYSTEM
 7516                /          DEVICE HANDLER IS PRESENT AT ALL TIMES DURING PROGRAM EXECUTION.  ALL
 7517                /          PROGRAM REQUIREMENTS [OTHER THAN SHELL-BASED EXTENSIONS IF APPLICABLE] ARE
 7518                /          MET BY THE RESIDENT SYSTEM WITHOUT REQUIRING DEVICE-DEPENDENT CODE.  ANY
 7519                /          FUTURE IMPLEMENTATION OF SYSTEM FEATURES WILL MAINTAIN COMPATIBILITY WITH
 7520                /          THIS DESIGN FURTHER DISTINGUISHING P?S/8 FROM THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM.
 7521   
 7522                /       2) PROGRAM OVERLAY SEGMENTATION.  PROGRAMS THAT REQUIRE OVERLAYS CAN CALCULATE
 7523                /          ABSOLUTE BLOCKS FOR ADDITIONAL PROGRAM [OR DATA] SEGMENTS BY INSPECTING THE
 7524                /          SYSTEM LOADER VALUES AND ADDING APPROPRIATE OFFSETS TO THE ORIGINAL LOADING
 7525                /          PARAMETERS. [NOTE: THE ANALOGOUS FUNCTION IS AVAILABLE IN THE R-L MONITOR
 7526                /          SYSTEM TO THE EXTENT THAT WHEN A SYSTEM PROGRAM IS LOADED, THE SYSTEM
 7527                /          LOADER WAS PASSED THE STARTING BLOCK.  ACTUAL ACCESS TO CALCULATED BLOCKS
 7528                /          CAN ONLY BE ACCOMPLISHED USING PROGRAM-SUPPLIED DEVICE-DEPENDENT
 7529                /          SUBROUTINES.  THIS TECHNIQUE WAS USED TO IMPLEMENT THE R-L MONITOR VERSION
 7530                /          OF FOCAL, 1969.]  WHEN NECESSARY, SECONDARY PROGRAM SECTIONS CAN BE LINKED
 7531                /          TO WITHOUT REQUIRING RESERVATION OF USER MEMORY.  [THIS TECHNIQUE IS USED
 7532                /          IN THE P?S/8 PAL ASSEMBLER TO IMPLEMENT END-OF-ASSEMBLY OVERLAYS SUCH AS
 7533                /          THE SYMBOL TABLE SORT/PRINT ROUTINE AND THE OPTIONAL DYNAMIC LINK TO THE
 7534                /          BITMAP PROGRAM AND/OR BINARY FILE LOADER IF THERE ARE NO ASSEMBLY ERRORS.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 151

 7536                /       3) SAVING A COMPLETE MEMORY IMAGE.  THE PRELOADED CONTENTS OF THE SYSTEM
 7537                /          LOADER IS A CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER TO SAVE ALL OF FIELD 0 TO THE
 7538                /          DEFINED SAVE AREA: BLOCKS 0020-0057. [NOTE: THESE BLOCKS CORRESPOND TO THE
 7539                /          "%" AND "$" FILES IN THE BASIC SYSTEM; ALL IMAGE-ORIENTED UTILITIES OBEY
 7540                /          THIS CONVENTION INCLUDING P?S/8 "ODT", "BSAVE", ETC.  THE SHELL OVERLAY
 7541                /          USES THE IDENTICAL CONVENTION ALLOWING THE COMMAND PROCESSOR TO LOAD INTO
 7542                /          FIELD 0 WITH THE CONTENTS OF MEMORY AVAILABLE TO RELEVANT COMMANDS SUCH
 7543                /          AS THE SYSTEM DEBUGGING FACILITY.]  UNLIKE OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS, THIS
 7544                /          FACILITY IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY INVOKED; NECESSARY OPERATIONS ONLY OCCUR WHEN
 7545                /          USER COMMANDS INITIATE SAVING MEMORY.  [NOTE: THE ACTUAL REBOOT PROCESS IS
 7546                /          READ-ONLY.]
 7547   
 7548                /          APPROPRIATE SYSTEM PROGRAMS [SUCH AS "GET"] CAN UTILIZE THIS FACILITY;
 7549                /          TYPICAL OPERATING SYSTEM USAGE CONSISTS OF RELOADING THE APPROPRIATE
 7550                /          COMMAND PROCESSOR AFTER THE PROGRAM EXITS.  BY AVOIDING UNNECESSARY WRITE
 7551                /          OPERATIONS, TYPICAL SYSTEM RELOADS ARE FASTER WITH GREATER SAFETY.  [NOTE:
 7552                /          IF NOT WRITING OUT AN IMAGE OF MEMORY, THE TYPICAL RESTART OPERATION IS A
 7553                /          SINGLE OPTIMIZED READ OPERATION.  DEVICES THAT SUPPORT WRITE-PROTECT SUCH
 7554                /          AS THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE CAN BEST BE USED THIS WAY TO PREVENT SYSTEM
 7555                /          DESTRUCTION CAUSED BY A PROGRAM LOGIC ERROR.]  [NOTE: THE BIN PROGRAM MAY
 7556                /          INCLUDE AN OPTIONAL BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER AS DISCUSSED ABOVE AND
 7557                /          ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT.  AN ADDITIONAL OPTION CAN RELOAD THE SYSTEM
 7558                /          DEVICE HANDLER AFTER LOADING ALL PASSED FILES.  AS SUCH, SPECIFYING 07632
 7559                /          AS THE STARTING ADDRESS WILL SAVE ALL OF FIELD 0 THEN RELOAD THE OPERATING
 7560                /          SYSTEM/KEYBOARD MONITOR.  THIS IS DISCUSSED IN GREATER DETAIL ELSEWHERE IN
 7561                /          THIS DOCUMENT.  A DEVICE-INDEPENDENT VIRTUAL LOADER CAN ALSO BE USED TO
 7562                /          LOAD BINARY FILES ON ALL SUPPORTED SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS; THE DEFAULT
 7563                /          CONTENTS OF THE SYSTEM LOADER ARE RESTORED TO ALLOW THE IDENTICAL
 7564                /          FUNCTIONALITY FOR EITHER BINARY LOADER.  THE BIN LOADING PROGRAM SETS ALL
 7565                /          PARAMETERS REGARDLESS OF OPERATING MODE.  THE DEFAULT WRITE OPERATION
 7566                /          DESCRIBED ABOVE WILL BE RESTORED AS NECESSARY.  FOR MORE DETAILS ON THE
 7567                /          VIRTUAL LOADING PROCESS, SEE DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 BIN PROGRAM.]
 7568   
 7569                /       END OF APPENDIX K.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 152

 7571                /       APPENDIX L.
 7572   
 7573                /       A KEYBOARD MONITOR TIP TO ASSEMBLE PROGRAMS CONTAINED IN A GROUP OF TFS FILES
 7574                /       FASTER TAKING ADVANTAGE OF INTELLIGENT INITIAL SEARCH SUPPORT ON TAPE SYSTEMS.
 7575   
 7576                /       P?S/8 HAS SEVERAL STRUCTURES ORGANIZED TO MINIMIZE SEARCH TIME WHERE POSSIBLE,
 7577                /       INCLUDING THE USE OF SEPARATE DIRECTORIES FOR SYSTEM PROGRAMS AND USER FILES.
 7578                /       FORWARD INITIAL SEARCH OPPORTUNITIES WILL OCCUR TO AN APPRECIABLE PERCENT OF
 7579                /       THE TIME ASSUMING TYPICAL SYSTEM USAGE.
 7580   
 7581                /       A PRACTICAL EXAMPLE OF EXPLOITING FORWARD INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION IN P?S/8
 7582                /       INVOLVES THE USE OF SEVERAL ADJACENT TFS FILES.  OVERALL THROUGHPUT CAN BE
 7583                /       IMPROVED BY INTERLEAVING THE FILES ON THE TAPE. [P?S/8 USERS HAVE COMPLETE
 7584                /       CONTROL OVER THE EXACT PLACEMENT OF FILES USED TOGETHER AS A CONCATENATED
 7585                /       DATA STREAM.] EACH TFS FILE IS 16 BLOCKS LONG [WHICH IS MORE THAN THE
 7586                /       WORST-CASE OVERSHOOT FACTOR].
 7587   
 7588                /       BY ARRANGING THE FILES IN AN INTERLEAVED FASHION, THE OVERALL ACCESS TIME CAN
 7589                /       BE LESSENED SINCE EACH PASS THROUGH THE FILES CONSISTS OF ONLY TWO SECTIONS
 7590                /       EACH SEARCHED FORWARD WITHIN; THE DATA-WISE ADJACENT FILES ARE SEPARATED BY
 7591                /       THE FILES ASSOCIATED WITH THE OTHER PASS; AS SUCH, ALL OTHER SEARCHES ARE
 7592                /       INITIATED FORWARD. [NOTE: THIS EXAMPLE ASSUMES AT LEAST 8K MEMORY IS
 7593                /       AVAILABLE; IN 4K SYSTEMS, EACH FILE MIGHT REQUIRE SEVERAL READ OPERATIONS EACH
 7594                /       REQUIRING REVERSE INITIAL SEARCH FOR ANY SECONDARY READ CALLS; WHEN THE P?S/8
 7595                /       PAL ASSEMBLER DISCOVERS MORE AVAILABLE MEMORY, THE INPUT BUFFER IS INCREASED
 7596                /       SUBSTANTIALLY.  IN GENERAL, THE TEXT PORTION OF A TYPICAL TFS FILE WILL BE
 7597                /       READ IN COMPLETELY USING A SINGLE FORWARD SEARCH AS DESCRIBED IN THIS EXAMPLE.
 7598                /       IT IS NOT NECESSARY TO READ THE ENTIRE TFS FILE AS THE REAR-MOST DATA CONSISTS
 7599                /       ONLY OF FREE SPACE FOLLOWED BY LINE NUMBER AND TEXT POINTER INFORMATION.  SEE
 7600                /       DETAILS ON TFS INTERNAL FILE FORMAT ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT FOR MORE
 7601                /       INFORMATION.] FOR EXAMPLE, CONSIDER A PROGRAM THAT SPANS EIGHT TFS FILES:
 7602   
 7603                /       FILE1   FILE2   FILE3   FILE4   FILE5   FILE6   FILE7   FILE8
 7604   
 7605                /       A TYPICAL ASSEMBLY COMMAND MIGHT BE:
 7606   
 7607                /       PAL FILE1,FILE2,FILE3,FILE4,FILE5,FILE6,FILE7,FILE8 /B
 7608   
 7609                /       IN THIS SCENARIO, EVERY FILE MUST BE SEARCHED INITIALLY IN REVERSE BECAUSE THE
 7610                /       NEXT BLOCK WHEN TRAVERSING FILES IS ADJACENT TO THE BLOCKS JUST READ.
 7611   
 7612                /       BY REARRANGING THE FILES THUS:
 7613   
 7614                /       FILE1   FILE5   FILE2   FILE6   FILE3   FILE7   FILE4   FILE8
 7615   
 7616                /       THE SAME COMMAND WILL HAVE DRAMATICALLY BETTER THROUGHPUT AS REVERSE INITIAL
 7617                /       SEARCH DIRECTION WILL ONLY APPLY WHEN ACCESSING FILES "FILE1" AND "FILE5".
 7618                /       ALL OF THE TRANSFERS ACCESSING FILES WITH LOGICALLY ADJACENT CONTENTS WILL BE
 7619                /       SUCCESSFULLY SEARCHED WHILE MOVING FORWARD.  [NOTE: THE TIME SPENT WINDING
 7620                /       FROM THE END OF FILE "FILE4" BACK TO JUST BELOW THE FIRST BLOCK OF FILE
 7621                /       "FILE5" IS FAR LESS THAN THE OVERHEAD OF THE REVERSE INITIAL SEARCH THAT IS
 7622                /       UNAVOIDABLE WHEN USING THE MORE CONVENTIONAL FILE LAYOUT.]
 7623   
 7624                /       END OF APPENDIX L.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 153

 7626                /       APPENDIX M.
 7627   
 7628                /       USAGE OF THE P?S/8 OPTIONAL PARAMETER AREA.
 7629   
 7630                /       DURING NORMAL EXECUTION, VARIOUS VALUES CAN BE PLACED ANYWHERE IN THE REST OF
 7631                /       THE PAGE FROM 07756-07757.  VARIOUS ASSIGNMENTS HAVE BEEN MADE SPECIFIC TO
 7632                /       THE RELEVANT EXECUTION MODES.
 7633   
 7634                /       SYSTEM EQUALS PARAMETER LOCATION.
 7635   
 7636                /       IN MOST EXECUTABLE CIRCUMSTANCES, THE LATEST COMMAND LINE GENERALLY ALLOWS A
 7637                /       NUMERIC PARAMETER SPECIFIED AS =XXXX [WHERE XXXX IS ANY ONE THROUGH FOUR
 7638                /       DIGIT VALUE FROM 0000 TO 7776].
 7639   
 7640                /       THIS PARAMETER IS STORED IN "SEQUALS" [07756] AND AVAILABLE TO ALL SYSTEM
 7641                /       PROGRAMS IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM AND ALSO THE SHELL OVERLAY CONFIGURATION.
 7642   
 7643                /       THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM ALLOWED THIS PARAMETER [LOADED INTO THE SAME LOCATION]
 7644                /       TO HAVE ANY VALUE FROM 0000-7777; IN P?S/8, 7777 IS EXPLICITLY NOT ALLOWED.
 7645                /       HOWEVER, THE VALUE 7777 WILL BE PASSED IF THERE IS NO PARAMETER USED ON THE
 7646                /       COMMAND LINE.  AS SUCH, THE EXECUTABLE PROGRAM CAN OPTIONALLY ASSIGN A
 7647                /       DEFAULT VALUE [DETERMINED BY INTERNAL PROGRAM LOGIC].
 7648   
 7649                /       FOR EXAMPLE, THE P?S/8 "BIN" UTILITY ALLOWS VARIOUS BINARY LOADER OPTIONS WITH
 7650                /       OPTIONAL STARTING INFORMATION.  IF THE =XXXX PARAMETER IS OMITTED, AFTER THE
 7651                /       PROGRAM IS FULLY LOADED, THE ADDRESS OF A SAFE "HLT" [7402] INSTRUCTION WILL
 7652                /       BE PASSED TO THE LOADER; PRESSING "CONTINUE" ON THE FRONT PANEL WILL JUST
 7653                /       LOOP BACK TO THE SAFE HLT INSTRUCTION.
 7654   
 7655                /       NOTE:  IN CERTAIN P?S/8 RELEASES, THE KEYBOARD MONITOR CAN BE SET TO PASS
 7656                /       VALUES OBTAINED FROM THE COMMAND LINE IN DECIMAL RADIX.  HOWEVER, THE INTERNAL
 7657                /       VALUE IS STILL LIMITED TO THE IDENTICAL RANGE; EXPRESSED IN DECIMAL, THIS IS
 7658                /       0000 THROUGH 4094 WITH A DEFAULT OF 4095 IF THE COMMAND LINE PARAMETER IS NOT
 7659                /       USED.
 7660   
 7661                /       PASSED FILE PARAMETERS AREA.
 7662   
 7663                /       THE P?S/8 "ODT" [OCTAL DEBUGGING TECHNIQUE] UTILITY PLACES VARIOUS SMALL
 7664                /       ROUTINES IN THE OPTIONAL PARAMETER AREA STARTING AT "SFILES" [07757] AND
 7665                /       EXTENDING TO 07777.  UNDER P?S/8 ODT, NO FILE PARAMETERS ARE PRESERVED; IN
 7666                /       CERTAIN RELEASES THESE ROUTINES MAY RESERVE LOCATION 07756 THUS PREVENTING
 7667                /       PROGRAM ACCESS TO THE =XXXX PARAMETER NORMALLY STORED THERE AS WELL.
 7668   
 7669                /       MORE RECENT IMPLEMENTATIONS OF P?S/8 SUPPORT MANY OTHER PARAMETERS WITHIN
 7670                /       THE SYSTEM KERNEL; AS SUCH, 07756 IS SUPPORTED AS THE =XXXX PARAMETER AS IS
 7671                /       THE CASE WITH ALL OTHER PROGRAMS.  HOWEVER, THE FILE AREA IS ALWAYS DESTROYED
 7672                /       BY ODT BREAKPOINT MECHANISMS.  ODT INSERTS TRAP MECHANISMS INTO THE CURRENT
 7673                /       PROGRAM BEING DEBUGGED; WHEN A BREAKPOINT TRAP IS TRIGGERED, THE CONTENTS OF
 7674                /       FIELD 0 IS SAVED IN THE SYSTEM SCRATCH AREA [BLOCKS 0020-0057] AND ODT IS
 7675                /       RELOADED IN BREAKPOINT DISPLAY MODE FOR FURTHER DEBUGGING.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 154

 7677                /       IN ALL OTHER BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS, ALL PARAMETERS ARE SUPPORTED FULLY
 7678                /       WITHOUT RESTRICTIONS; THE P?S/8 "START" COMMAND CAN BE USED TO CREATE A
 7679                /       COMPATIBLE ENVIRONMENT WITHOUT ACTUALLY PLACING THE PROGRAM ENTRY IN THE
 7680                /       SYSTEM PROGRAM DIRECTORY.  HOWEVER, THE =XXXX PARAMETER MUST BE USED TO
 7681                /       SPECIFY THE 12-BIT STARTING ADDRESS FOR THE PROGRAM; LEAVING THE EXPLICIT
 7682                /       PARAMETER OUT OF THE COMMAND LINE WILL INTERPRET THE PASSED VALUE OF 7777 TO
 7683                /       MEAN AN EXPLICIT START IN LOCATION 00200.  ADDITIONALLY, THE OPTION SWITCHES
 7684                /       "/0" THROUGH "/7" ARE RESERVED; THE LOWEST EXPLICITLY PASSED SWITCH BECOMES
 7685                /       THE STARTING FIELD FOR THE PROGRAM.  [NOTE: FUTURE RELEASES OF THE START
 7686                /       COMMAND WILL DISALLOW MULTIPLE OPTION SWITCHES IN THE RANGE OF /0 THROUGH /7
 7687                /       AS A STARTUP COMMAND ERROR; AS SUCH, IT WILL NOT BE POSSIBLE TO USE HIGHER
 7688                /       NUMERICAL OPTION SWITCHES AS PROGRAM PARAMETERS WHEN USED WITH THE START
 7689                /       COMMAND.]
 7690   
 7691                /       IF NONE OF THE SWITCHES IS PASSED, FIELD 0 IS USED BY DEFAULT [AS WELL AS IF
 7692                /       THE PASSED =XXXX VALUE IS 7777; FIELD 0 MUST BE FORCED FOR THIS SITUATION AS
 7693                /       WELL].  OTHER THAN THESE FEW RESTRICTIONS, ALL OTHER OPTIONS ARE AVAILABLE TO
 7694                /       THE PROGRAM BEING STARTED: A) ALL OTHER COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES "/A"
 7695                /       THROUGH "/Z" AND "/8" THROUGH "/9".  INPUT AND OUTPUT FILES MAY BE FREELY
 7696                /       PASSED.  OUTPUT FILES [IF ANY] MUST PRECEDE INPUT FILES [IF ANY]. THE COUNT OF
 7697                /       PASSED OUTPUT FILES [WHICH RANGES FROM 0000-0017] IS AVAILABLE IN "SOUTCT"
 7698                /       [07607] BITS[7-11].  [NOTE: ALL OUTPUT FILES WILL HAVE BEEN CREATED BY THE
 7699                /       TIME THE PROGRAM IS LOADED; IN CERTAIN RELEASES OF P?S/8, THE KEYBOARD MONITOR
 7700                /       WILL CREATE THE OUTPUT FILES IF NECESSARY; EARLIER RELEASES WILL ISSUE ERROR
 7701                /       MESSAGES FOR NON-EXISTENT OUTPUT FILES.]
 7702   
 7703                /       ALL PASSED FILES ARE CREATED IN A LIST STARTING AT "SFILES" [07757] EXTENDING
 7704                /       AS FAR AS NECESSARY.  THE MAXIMUM FILE COUNT IS 17.  A SENTINEL VALUE OF 0000
 7705                /       IS PLACED AFTER THE LAST PASSED FILE WITH ONE EXCEPTION:
 7706   
 7707                /       MAXIMUM LENGTH LISTS [17 FILES PASSED] ARE SELF-TERMINATING BECAUSE THE
 7708                /       DESIGNATED METHOD OF ACCESSING THE FILES ALWAYS USES A PRESCRIBED METHOD
 7709                /       SIMILAR TO WHAT FOLLOWS:
 7710   
 7711                /       ISZ     FILPTR                  /BUMP TO NEXT FILE [WAS SET TO SFILES-1].
 7712                /       TAD I   FILPTR                  /GET LATEST FILE [OR 0000 IF AT END].
 7713                /       SNA CLA                         /SKIP IF ACTUALLY A FILE HANDLE.
 7714                /       JMP     ENDFILES                /JUMP IF NO MORE FILES.
 7715   
 7716                /       IF A SENTINEL 0000 VALUE TERMINATES THE LIST, ALL OF THE ABOVE INSTRUCTIONS
 7717                /       EXECUTE.  IF THE LIST IS MAXIMUM LENGTH, THE "ISZ FILPTR" INSTRUCTION SKIPS
 7718                /       THE NEXT INSTRUCTION THUS THE AC IS 0000 FOR THIS SPECIAL CASE LEADING TO THE
 7719                /       END OF FILE PROCESSING.  [NOTE: WE THANK RICHARD LARY FOR THIS PROGRAMMING
 7720                /       GEM THAT ONLY APPLIES TO SITUATIONS SUCH AS R-L MONITOR AND P?S/8 FILE LIST
 7721                /       PROCESSING; THERE ARE FEW APPLICATIONS FOR LISTS LOCATED AT THE TOP END OF
 7722                /       MEMORY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 155

 7724                /       ADDITIONAL USAGE OF THE PASSED FILE PARAMETER AREA [IN MEMORY].
 7725   
 7726                /       WHILE ALL BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS [OTHER THAN ODT] POTENTIALLY PROCESS
 7727                /       PASSED FILES BY READING THE CONTENTS OF THE PASSED FILE AREA, SOME PROGRAMS
 7728                /       ARE KNOWN TO LOAD OVER THIS AREA IN SPECIFIC CIRCUMSTANCES.  AS EXPLAINED
 7729                /       ELSEWHERE IN THIS DOCUMENT, THE "SLURP" LOADER [WHICH PROCESSES BINARY INPUT
 7730                /       FILE HANDLES PLACED IN THE PASSED FILE AREA] MAY BE SELECTIVELY "DESTROYED" BY
 7731                /       THE LAST "FILE" PASSED TO THE SLURP LOADER; THIS IS ACTUALLY A RESERVED BLOCK
 7732                /       ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE [BLOCK 0060] CONTAINING CODE DESIGNED TO REPLACE THE
 7733                /       SLURP LOADER WITH THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.
 7734   
 7735                /       DURING THIS PROCESS, THE CONTENTS OF THE MEMORY LOCATIONS WITHIN THE PASSED
 7736                /       FILE PARAMETER AREA ARE REPLACED WITH CODE BEING LOADED INTO MEMORY FOR THE
 7737                /       PURPOSE OF ALTERING THE NORMAL SLURP LOADING PROCESS.  [NOTE: SINCE THE
 7738                /       PASSED "FILE" CAUSING THIS OPERATION IS ALWAYS THE LAST INPUT FILE PROCESSED,
 7739                /       THE ENTIRE PASSED FILE PARAMETER AREA IS NO LONGER NEEDED; THERE IS NO FURTHER
 7740                /       USE FOR THE FILE INFORMATION PASSED BY THE OPERATING SYSTEM; AS SUCH, THESE
 7741                /       WORDS ARE AVAILABLE FOR OVERLAY WITH ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS AND DATA TO
 7742                /       CARRY OUT THE OVERLAY PROCESS.]
 7743   
 7744                /       AFTER THIS CODE IS LOADED, THE LAST OPERATION INITIATED BY THIS "FILE" CAUSES
 7745                /       THE [SOMEWHAT MODIFIED] SLURP LOADER TO JUMP INTO THE JUST-LOADED CODE; THE
 7746                /       CONTENTS OF NEARLY ALL LOCATIONS IN THE RANGE OF 07600-07777 IS CHANGED TO THE
 7747                /       CORRESPONDING CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000; THIS CAUSES ALL NECESSARY LOCATIONS
 7748                /       WITHIN THE SYSTEM KERNEL TO BE RESTORED.  THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND SYSTEM
 7749                /       RESTART AT 07600 ARE FUNCTIONAL AS THE USER BINARY PROGRAM IS STARTED.  [NOTE:
 7750                /       THE PROGRAM STARTUP INFORMATION IS STORED IN MEMORY THAT IS NOT OVERLAID BY
 7751                /       RELOADING THE PARTIAL CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000; THIS INCLUDES THE SAFE HALT
 7752                /       LOCATION USED AS THE DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS; THESE LOCATIONS ARE PLACED IN
 7753                /       AN AREA OF MEMORY THAT SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER OPERATIONS WILL EVENTUALLY STORE
 7754                /       INTO; THE PRIOR CONTENTS DO NOT AFFECT EITHER THE SYSTEM HANDLER OR THE SYSTEM
 7755                /       RESTART AT 07600.  IT IS CRUCIAL THAT THERE ARE NO CONFLICTS BETWEEN THE
 7756                /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND THE SLURP LOADER IN THIS REGARD IN ORDER FOR THE
 7757                /       RELOAD PROCESS TO SUCCEED; EACH CONFIGURATION SUPPORTING ALL THREE RELEVANT
 7758                /       COMPONENTS MUST BE VERY CAREFULLY DESIGNED.]  [NOTE: CERTAIN CONFIGURATIONS DO
 7759                /       NOT SUPPORT A SLURP LOADER DUE TO HARDWARE RESTRICTIONS; BINARY FILE LOADING
 7760                /       BY THE VIRTUAL LOADER IS FORCED; AS SUCH, THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF PASSED INPUT
 7761                /       FILES INCREASES BY ONE; NO RELOADER "FILE" IS DEFINED ON THESE SYSTEMS.]
 7762   
 7763                /       ADDITIONAL USAGE OF THE PASSED FILE PARAMETER AREA [STORED IN BLOCK 0000].
 7764   
 7765                /       THE PERMANENT CONTENTS OF THE CORRESPONDING WORDS OF BLOCK 0000 [RELATIVE
 7766                /       156-177] MAY ALSO BE LOADED WITH CERTAIN VALUES NEEDED AS PART OF THE BOOTUP
 7767                /       PROCESS; THIS ONLY APPLIES TO CERTAIN RELEASES OF PARTICULAR P?S/8 SYSTEM
 7768                /       DEVICE HANDLERS, ESPECIALLY SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS THAT REQUIRE A SYSTEM DEVICE
 7769                /       HANDLER EXTENDED MEMORY EXTENSION SUCH AS THE RK8E, RX8E ETC.
 7770   
 7771                /       CERTAIN BOOTSTRAP CONVENTIONS MAY REQUIRE THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 TO BE
 7772                /       LOADED WITH AN UNUSUAL OFFSET [SUCH AS THE RX SERIES OF DISKETTE CONTROLLERS].
 7773                /       AS SUCH, THE PERMANENT CONTENTS OF THESE WORDS MAY BE USED DURING THE
 7774                /       EARLIEST STAGES OF A MULTI-TIERED BOOTSTRAP PROCEDURE DESIGNED TO BRING
 7775                /       ANOTHER DEDICATED SYSTEM BLOCK INTO MEMORY TO FINALIZE THE OVERALL PROCESS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 156

 7777                /       IN THE CASE OF EXTENDED DEVICE HANDLERS, THE SPACE IS OFTEN USED TO LOAD IN
 7778                /       THE DESIGNATED "HELPER" BLOCKS OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER TO DETERMINE
 7779                /       EXACTLY HOW MUCH EXTENDED MEMORY IS AVAILABLE; THE REST OF THE HANDLER IS THEN
 7780                /       LOADED INTO THE HIGHEST MEMORY FIELD.  AS NECESSARY, FIELD RELOCATION IS
 7781                /       PERFORMED TO INITIATE OPERATION OF THE HANDLER AFTER THE CODE IS MOVED [NOTE:
 7782                /       ONLY RELEVANT ON 12K OR LARGER MACHINES].
 7783   
 7784                /       IN PERVERSE SITUATIONS, LARGER SECTIONS OF THE INTENDED FIELD 0 DEVICE HANDLER
 7785                /       CODE MAY NOT BE LOADED WHEN STANDARD BOOSTRAP METHODS ARE USED; IN SUCH CASES,
 7786                /       THE PERMANENT CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 MAY INCLUDE ADDITIONAL INTERMEDIARY
 7787                /       BOOTUP CODE.  THE ADDITIONAL CODE IS USED FOR THE PURPOSE OF LOADING IN
 7788                /       DESIGNATED HELPER BLOCKS WHICH THEN ALSO PROVIDE THE PROPER CONTENTS OF THE
 7789                /       DISPLACED WORDS.  IN THE MOST COMPLEX CASES, IT MAY BE NECESSARY FOR THE
 7790                /       EXTENDED DEVICE HANDLER CODE TO INCLUDE A PORTION OF THIS "FIX-UP" CODE TO
 7791                /       AVOID THE OVERHEAD OF ACCESSING OTHER BLOCKS OF THE ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 7792                /       EVERY TIME THE SYSTEM IS RESTARTED AT 07600.
 7793   
 7794                /       IN SOME CASES, ATTEMPTS TO WRITE LOGICAL BLOCK ZERO FROM THE CONTENTS OF
 7795                /       LOCATIONS 07600-07777 MAY HAVE TO BE INTERCEPTED TO ENSURE THE CONTENTS OF THE
 7796                /       BLOCK ARE AS REQUIRED BY THE BOOTSTRAP CONVENTION [WHICH WILL DIFFER FROM THE
 7797                /       RUNNING CODE IN MEMORY.]  THE ACTUAL UPDATED CONTENTS OF LOGICAL BLOCK 0000
 7798                /       WILL BE A COMPOSITE OF THE DYNAMIC LOCATIONS WITHIN THE SYSTEM KERNEL THAT
 7799                /       REQUIRE UPDATING [SUCH AS THE STORED COPY OF THE CURRENT DATE] WHILE OTHER
 7800                /       LOCATIONS IN BLOCK 0000 ARE TAKEN FROM THE ACTUAL BLOCK 0000 CODE WHICH IS
 7801                /       THEN AUGMENTED ONLY BY THE UPDATABLE MEMORY LOCATIONS WITHIN THE HANDLER.
 7802   
 7803                /       A WORST-CASE SCENARIO MIGHT REQUIRE SPLITTING OFF SOME OF THESE WORDS INTO AN
 7804                /       ALTERNATE BLOCK [PART OF ONE OF THE HELPER BLOCKS] TO STORE UPDATED WORDS THAT
 7805                /       CANNOT BE STORED IN BLOCK 0000 DIRECTLY.  [NOTE: TO DATE THIS ABSOLUTE
 7806                /       WORST-CASE SCENARIO HASN'T BEEN REQUIRED; HOWEVER, ASSUMING THE REST OF THE
 7807                /       CODE CAN FIT WITHIN THE DEVICE HANDLER EXTENSION, EVEN THIS CASE CAN BE
 7808                /       HANDLED.  AS OF THIS WRITING, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER FOR THE DECMATE II-III
 7809                /       SERIES RX50 DISKETTES HAS NOT BEEN FULLY DESIGNED; DUE TO VARIOUS CONFLICTING
 7810                /       REQUIREMENTS OF A BOOTABLE DECMATE RX50, SOME OF THESE PROBLEMS ARE LIKELY TO
 7811                /       ARISE.  IT SHOULD BE NOTED THAT OS/278 V2, THE ONLY OS/8 FAMILY OPERATING
 7812                /       SYSTEM FOR THE DECMATES, IS INCAPABLE OF WRITING ON LOGICAL RECORD 0000; TO
 7813                /       ATTEMPT TO DO SO CAUSES THE DISKETTE TO BECOME UNBOOTABLE.  SPECIAL UTILITIES
 7814                /       ARE REQUIRED TO CREATE THESE DISKETTES FROM DESIGNATED DIAGNOSTIC UTILITY
 7815                /       DISKETTES; THE NORMAL METHODS OF SYSTEM COPY AND UPDATE USED IN OS/8 CANNOT BE
 7816                /       USED IN OS/278.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 157

 7818                /       IN P?S/8, ANY OF THESE SCENARIOS CAN BE SUCCESSFULLY IMPLEMENTED IN MOST
 7819                /       EXTENDED DEVICE HANDLERS DUE TO THE LARGE CODE SPACE [6000-7777] ALLOCATED.
 7820                /       FOR MOST HANDLERS, THE CODE TO ACCOMPLISH THIS WOULD LIKELY BE LESS THAN ONE
 7821                /       PAGE [128 WORDS], INCLUDING A SMALL ROUTINE USED TO MOVE THE CODE TO FIELD 0.
 7822                /       [NOTE: THERE ARE TECHNIQUES TO IMPLEMENT SELF-MODIFYING CODE WITHOUT USING
 7823                /       INDIRECT ADDRESSING;  IF THIS IS FEASIBLE, IT WOULD NOT BE NECESSARY TO BE
 7824                /       AWARE OF THE FIELD THE FIX-UP CODE RESIDES IN.  SHOULD INDIRECT ADDRESSING BE
 7825                /       REQUIRED, APPROPRIATE "CDF" INSTRUCTIONS CAN BE PLACED WITHIN THE CODE AS
 7826                /       NEEDED; THE CORRECT FIELD FOR THE HANDLER EXTENSION CAN BE OBTAINED
 7827                /       DYNAMICALLY.  THIS FORM OF RELOCATION RESULTS IN SLIGHTLY LONGER MOVE
 7828                /       ROUTINES.  THE HANDLER EXTENSION HELPER BLOCKS ARE NOT MEANT TO BE PART OF THE
 7829                /       HANDLER EXTENSION; RATHER, THEIR ONLY PURPOSE IS TO CAUSE THE EXTENSION TO BE
 7830                /       FUNCTIONAL IN THE SMALLEST AMOUNT OF RESIDENT CODE.  FOR MORE INFORMATION AND
 7831                /       PRACTICAL EXAMPLES, REFER TO VARIOUS SPECIFIC RELOCATION ROUTINES IN THE
 7832                /       VARIOUS EXTENDED SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER FOR THE RK8E/RK05, RX8E/RX01, ETC.]
 7833   
 7834                /       [NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS
 7835                /       DOCUMENT DOES NOT USE THE PERMANENT CONTENTS OF THE PASSED FILE AREA AS
 7836                /       DEFINED HERE; HOWEVER, FUTURE RELEASES OF THIS DEVICE HANDLER MAY ADD
 7837                /       ADDITIONAL CODE ONLY USED DURING THE INITIAL STARTUP PHASE OF A P?S/8 BOOTUP
 7838                /       PROCEDURE.  THIS AREA COULD ALSO CONTAIN GENERIC CODE TO ASSIST IN OTHER
 7839                /       ASPECTS OF THE BOOTUP PROCESS; AS SUCH, IT WOULD APPLY TO ALL HANDLER VARIANTS
 7840                /       ASSOCIATED WITH A PARTICULAR P?S/8 RELEASE.  THE PERMANENT CONTENTS OF THESE
 7841                /       WORDS IS ONLY MEANINGFUL DURING THE EARLY STAGES OF THE BOOTUP PROCESS; ONCE
 7842                /       LOADED, THE MEMORY LOCATIONS ARE USED AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE IN THIS
 7843                /       DOCUMENT.]
 7844   
 7845                /       ADDITIONAL USAGE OF THE PASSED FILE PARAMETER AREA BY THE P?S/8 SHELL OVERLAY.
 7846   
 7847                /       WITHIN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT, ALL RELEVANT EXTENDED MEMORY IS COMPLETELY
 7848                /       PHYSICAL WHILE ALL OF FIELD 0 IS ENTIRELY VIRTUAL UNTIL AN EXECUTABLE PROGRAM
 7849                /       IS STARTED.  THIS ALLOWS THE SHELL TO OPERATE AS AN INDEPENDENT 4K SUBSYSTEM
 7850                /       WITH ITS OWN OVERLAY STRUCTURES AS DEFINED IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM.  AS
 7851                /       NECESSARY, INTERCOMMUNICATION ELEMENTS WILL BE PLACED IN THE SYSTEM FILE LIST
 7852                /       AREA UNTIL AN EXECUTION-CLASS COMMAND IS INVOKED.
 7853   
 7854                /       WHEN THE SHELL OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT, VARIOUS ROUTINES ARE PLACED INTO THE
 7855                /       PASSED FILE PARAMETER AREA [WHICH IS NOT SUPPORTED FOR THE ORIGINAL PURPOSE AS
 7856                /       IS THE CASE IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM].  THIS ALLOWS THE SHELL TO EXECUTE
 7857                /       PROGRAMS WITH POTENTIAL STARTING ADDRESSES ANYWHERE IN 32K, AS WELL AS
 7858                /       SUPPORTING THE ABILITY FOR A PROGRAM TO SPECIFY A MEMORY SAVE OPERATION WHEN
 7859                /       LOCATION 07600 IS BRANCHED TO; THE DEFAULT IS TO ONLY PERFORM THE READ-ONLY
 7860                /       BOOTSTRAP ASSOCIATED WITH THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM.  [NOTE: THE BASIC P?S/8
 7861                /       SYSTEM HAS SEVERAL EXPLICIT WAYS TO HAVE MEMORY SAVED BEFORE THE READ-ONLY
 7862                /       RESTART OPERATION; HOWEVER, NONE OF THESE TECHNIQUES APPLY TO THE SHELL
 7863                /       OVERLAY CONFIGURATION.  INSTEAD, EVERY SHELL-BASED PROGRAM CAN POTENTIALLY
 7864                /       REQUEST AN EXPLICIT MEMORY SAVE; HOWEVER, THE DEFAULT ACTION IS TO NOT SAVE
 7865                /       MEMORY WHICH IS THE FAR MORE LIKELY SCENARIO.  THIS LEADS TO LOWERING SYSTEM
 7866                /       OVERHEAD WHILE INCREASING SYSTEM RELIABILITY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 158

 7868                /       FOR THE SELDOM-USED MEMORY-SAVING OPERATIONS, THE BOOTSTRAP IS MODIFIED TO
 7869                /       FIRST SAVE ALL OF FIELD 0 IN THE DEFINED SCRATCH AREA [BLOCKS 0020-0057], THEN
 7870                /       PERFORM THE NORMAL READ-ONLY BOOTUP PROCEDURE.  THIS IS ACCOMPLISHED BY
 7871                /       MODIFYING LOCATION 07600 TO A "JMP" INSTRUCTION INTO THE SYSTEM FILE LIST AREA
 7872                /       TO THE MEMORY SAVE ROUTINES FOLLOWED BY ANOTHER "JMP" INSTRUCTION BACK TO
 7873                /       07600.  [NOTE: THE INSTRUCTION NORMALLY EXECUTED AT 07600 WILL BE SAVED AND
 7874                /       LATER RESTORED BEFORE THE SYSTEM RESTART; AS LONG AS THIS WORD IS RESTORED,
 7875                /       THE NORMAL DEVICE HANDLER REBOOT FUNCTION WILL WORK PROPERLY.]
 7876   
 7877                /       ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS ARE REQUIRED TO FUNCTION FOR PURPOSES OTHER
 7878                /       THAN SYSTEM RESTART WHILE THE NORMAL CONTENTS OF 07600 ARE DISPLACED.  AS
 7879                /       SUCH, LOCATION 07600 MUST NOT BE OTHERWISE REFERENCED BY THE DEVICE HANDLER TO
 7880                /       ALLOW PROPER FUNCTIONALITY.  AS A RESULT OF THIS MECHANISM, THERE IS NO NEED
 7881                /       FOR AN ALTERNATE NON-STANDARD RESTART ADDRESS SUCH AS WAS UNSUCCESSFULLY
 7882                /       ATTEMPTED WITHIN OS/8.  THE UNIVERSAL PROGRAM EXIT/SYSTEM RESTART ADDRESS OF
 7883                /       07600 ALWAYS APPLIES IN ALL OPERATING MODES.
 7884   
 7885                /       THE SHELL DEBUGGING FACILITY ALSO OPERATES IN THE PASSED FILE PARAMETER AREA
 7886                /       IN A MANNER SIMILAR TO THE P?S/8 BASIC ODT BREAKPOINT MECHANISM.  HOWEVER, THE
 7887                /       SHELL ENVIRONMENT PASSES FILES AND OTHER PARAMETERS IN AN INDEPENDENT AREA
 7888                /       ONLY MEANINGFUL IN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT [THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA];
 7889                /       AS SUCH, DEBUGGING SHELL PROGRAMS WITH THE USE OF BREAKPOINT TRAPS DOES NOT
 7890                /       INTERFERE WITH SHELL PROGRAM COMPATIBILITY IN ANY WAY.
 7891   
 7892                /       LOCATIONS 07776-07777 HAVE SPECIFIC MEANING TO SHELL PROGRAMS.  LOCATION 07776
 7893                /       IS ALWAYS A "CIF CDF" INSTRUCTION [62X3] TO THE PROGRAM STARTING FIELD;
 7894                /       LOCATION 07777 IS A "JMP I" INSTRUCTION THAT WILL ALLOW INSPECTION OF THE
 7895                /       STARTING ADDRESS [WHICH MAY BE IMPLEMENTED AS THE CONTENTS OF 07637 FOR ALL
 7896                /       SHELL EXECUTION MODES] BY EXAMINING THE CORRESPONDING ADDRESS.  AS SUCH, P?S/8
 7897                /       SHELL PROGRAMS WILL HAVE THE ABILITY TO BE AWARE OF THEIR OWN STARTING ADDRESS
 7898                /       AND FIELD; IT IS POSSIBLE TO WRITE A UTILITY THAT FUNCTIONS DIFFERENTLY BASED
 7899                /       ON PARAMETERS SUCH AS THE STARTING ADDRESS OR EXECUTABLE FILE NAME.  THIS CAN
 7900                /       BE USEFUL WHEN PROGRAMS WITH ANALOGOUS OPERATING MODES AND THE USE OF PASSED
 7901                /       PARAMETERS MAY ONLY DIFFER IN SOME INTERNAL OPERATING MANNER; IT WOULDN'T BE
 7902                /       NECESSARY TO CREATE SEVERAL SIMILAR UTILITIES IF ONE MASTER UTILITY CAN
 7903                /       INSPECT ITS OWN STARTUP INFORMATION AND SELF-MODIFY APPROPRIATELY.
 7904   
 7905                /       END OF APPENDIX M.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 159

 7907                /       APPENDIX N.
 7908   
 7909                /       TAKING ADVANTAGE OF FORWARD INITIAL SEARCH WITHIN THE TC01/TC08 SLURP LOADER.
 7910   
 7911                /       UNLIKE THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, THE CURRENT IMPLEMENTATION OF THE
 7912                /       SLURP LOADER [TAKEN FROM A 1976 RELEASE OF P?S/8] ALWAYS INITIATES NEW FILE
 7913                /       SEARCHES IN THE FORWARD DIRECTION.  CONVENTIONAL REASONING MIGHT DICTATE THIS
 7914                /       TO BE UNFORTUNATE IN ALL CASES IN TERMS OF OVERALL DECTAPE MOTION WHILE
 7915                /       TRANSFERRING BINARY FILES; HOWEVER, IN THIS PARTICULAR INSTANCE:
 7916   
 7917                /       1) WITH THE EXCEPTION OF THE % AND $ FILES [LOCATED AT BLOCK 0020 AND 0040
 7918                /          RESPECTIVELY], ALL TFS BINARY FILES ARE LOCATED FORWARD OF THE CURRENT TAPE
 7919                /          POSITION WHEN THE P?S/8 "BIN" UTILITY HAS JUST BEEN READ.  [THE SLURP
 7920                /          LOADER IS AN INTERNAL COMPONENT OF P?S/8 BIN; ONCE INITIALIZED, THE SLURP
 7921                /          LOADER AND STAND-ALONE BOOTSTRAP AT 07600 DISPLACE THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 7922                /          HANDLER.  THE FIRST OPERATION INITIATES A FORWARD SEARCH FOR THE FIRST
 7923                /          PASSED INPUT FILE].
 7924   
 7925                /       2) ALL ADDITIONAL BLOCKS WITHIN FILES ARE ALWAYS FORWARD OF THE PREVIOUS BLOCK
 7926                /          JUST PROCESSED; THIS IS THE INTENDED FUNCTION.
 7927   
 7928                /       3) RARELY ARE ADDITIONAL FILES ACTUALLY PASSED IN TYPICAL USAGE.  [THE MAXIMUM
 7929                /          FILE COUNT IS 17 ASSUMING THE SYSTEM HANDLER RELOADER IS NOT IN USE.]
 7930   
 7931                /          HOWEVER, IF MULTIPLE PASSED BINARY FILES ARE LOCATED CONTIGUOUSLY ON THE
 7932                /          DECTAPE, THIS COULD CAUSE UNDESIRABLE DOUBLE TAPE TURNAROUNDS FOR EACH FILE
 7933                /          PROCESSED [DEPENDING ON RELATIVE TAPE POSITION BETWEEN SEQUENTIALLY-USED
 7934                /          FILES].
 7935   
 7936                /       THE PROBLEM DISCUSSED IN 3) ABOVE CAN BE ELIMINATED BY PLACING UNRELATED FILES
 7937                /       BETWEEN FILES TO BE LOADED SEQUENTIALLY; ALL FORWARD SEARCHES WILL ALWAYS
 7938                /       LOCATE THE STARTING BLOCK OF EVERY FILE WITHOUT TURNING AROUND.  [NOTE: TFS
 7939                /       FILES ARE PLACES FURTHER APART THAN THE BLOCK SPACING REQUIRED TO RESTART THE
 7940                /       DECTAPE SEARCH ONCE THE MOTION BIT IS TURNED OFF.]  AN EXAMPLE OF THIS FORM
 7941                /       OF PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION IS AS FOLLOWS:
 7942   
 7943                /       .CATALOG:0
 7944   
 7945                /       BFILE1  TEXT07  BFILE2  TEXT08
 7946                /       BFILE3  TEXT3A  BFILE4  TEXT3R
 7947                /       BFILE5  TXTHLP  BFILE6  MYPROG
 7948   
 7949                /       .BIN BFILE1,BFILE2,BFILE3,BFILE4,BFILE5,BFILE6=200


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 160

 7951                /       AFTER THE OPERATING SYSTEM LOADS THE P?S/8 BIN UTILITY [WHICH IN TURN LOADS
 7952                /       THE SLURP LOADER], A FORWARD SEARCH WILL FIND "BFILE1" WITHOUT TURNING THE
 7953                /       DECTAPE AROUND.  SINCE EACH TFS FILE IS 16 BLOCKS LONG, THERE IS A 16 BLOCK
 7954                /       SPACING BETWEEN THE BINARY FILES PROVIDED BY THE UNRELATED FILES.  THIS
 7955                /       EXCEEDS THE DECTAPE COASTING OFFSET REQUIREMENT WHEN SETTING TAPE MOTION.
 7956                /       NOTE: TAPE MOTION IS CLEARED AFTER EACH FILE IS PROCESSED.  THIS IS NORMAL
 7957                /       PRACTICE AS EACH FILE IS AN INDEPENDENT SET OF BLOCKS TO PROCESS; THE NEXT
 7958                /       INPUT FILE COULD BE LOCATED ON A DIFFERENT DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT; THE PREVIOUS
 7959                /       TAPE UNIT MUST BE STOPPED BEFORE THE NEXT DRIVE UNIT IS STARTED.
 7960   
 7961                /       ADJACENT FILES REQUIRE SUBSEQUENT FILES BE SEARCHED INITIALLY IN REVERSE DUE
 7962                /       TO INERTIAL ROLL AFTER THE CONTROLLER CLEARS THE MOTION BIT; IN THE CURRENT
 7963                /       IMPLEMENTATION OF THE SLURP LOADER THIS IS NOT AVAILABLE.  BY UTILIZING FILE
 7964                /       INTERLEAVING AS DESCRIBED ABOVE, THE ENTIRE OPERATION LOADS ALL FILES WITHOUT
 7965                /       TAPE TURNAROUND.  [NOTE: THE TAPE SEARCH ROUTINE WITHIN THE SLURP LOADER IS
 7966                /       CAPABLE OF REVERSE SEARCH IF NECESSARY; THE SPECIFIC CONSIDERATION DISCUSSED
 7967                /       HERE ONLY REFERS TO THE INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION FOR EACH FILE.]
 7968   
 7969                /       THE ONLY REQUIREMENT IS THE INTERLEAVED PLACEMENT OF FILES NOT PART OF THE
 7970                /       BINARY FILE STREAM AT HAND; THE OTHER FILES CAN BE USED FOR ANY ALTERNATE
 7971                /       PURPOSE, INCLUDING THE STREAM OF BINARY FILES FOR ANOTHER PROGRAM OR SOURCE
 7972                /       FILES FOR ANY PROGRAM [INCLUDING THE SOURCE CODE OF THE CURRENT BINARY FILE
 7973                /       STREAM].
 7974   
 7975                /       P?S/8 TFS FILE NAMES CAN BE ENTERED DIRECTLY WITH KEYBOARD MONITOR COMMANDS;
 7976                /       THE FILE LOCATIONS ARE PERMANENT UNLESS MODIFED WITH DIRECTORY-ORIENTED
 7977                /       MAINTENANCE COMMANDS.  MOST USERS CAN QUICKLY ADAPT TO THIS METHOD USING ONLY
 7978                /       THE DE[LETE] AND [EN]TER COMMANDS AS REQUIRED.  ADVANCED P?S/8 USERS SETUP
 7979                /       NEARLY ALL TFS DIRECTORY SLOTS IN ANTICIPATION OF PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENT.
 7980   
 7981                /       [NOTE: MOVING A TFS DIRECTORY ENTRY DESTROYS THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN THE
 7982                /       FILE NAME AND THE FORMER CONTENTS; REVERTING FILE NAMES CAN RESTORE THE
 7983                /       ABILITY TO ACCESS EXISTING FILES AS THE CONTENTS OF THE FILE BLOCKS ARE NOT
 7984                /       MODIFED WHEN THE TFS DIRECTORY IS UPDATED.  AS SUCH, FILE PLACEMENT
 7985                /       OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES SHOULD BE PERFORMED BEFORE THE FILES ARE ACTUALLY USED
 7986                /       FOR NORMAL CONTENT PURPOSES.]
 7987   
 7988                /       SINCE SOURCE FILE PLACEMENT CAN ALSO BE BENEFICIAL IN TERMS OF LOWERING
 7989                /       ASSEMBLY TIMES USING A SIMILAR POSITIONING SCHEME, THE TWO METHODS CAN BE
 7990                /       COMPLEMENTARY.  [NOTE: THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WILL ALWAYS SET THE CORRECT
 7991                /       INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION.  BY FILE PLACEMENT AS DESCRIBED, THE SEARCH WILL BE
 7992                /       CORRECTLY SET TO INITIALLY FORWARD FOR BOTH PURPOSES.]
 7993   
 7994                /       END OF APPENDIX N.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 161

 7996                /       APPENDIX O.
 7997   
 7998                /       DETAILED DESCRIPTION OF SLURP FORMAT P?S/8 BINARY FILES.
 7999   
 8000                /       EVERY BLOCK OF A SLURP FILE IS INDEPENDENT OF ALL OTHER BLOCKS OTHER THAN ANY
 8001                /       CONSIDERATION OF CURRENT LOADING ORIGIN AND CURRENT LOADING FIELD INHERITED
 8002                /       FROM THE PREVIOUS BLOCK PROCESSED.  TO ENSURE PROPER LOADING, EVERY SLURP FILE
 8003                /       CONTAINS AN INITIAL ORIGIN TO LOCATION 0200 FOLLOWED BY AN APPROPRIATE FIELD
 8004                /       SETTING, GENERALLY TO FIELD 0.  THIS ALLOWS SYSTEM PROGRAMS TO DETERMINE IF A
 8005                /       MIXED STREAM OF PASSED INPUT FILES CONTAINS SLURP BINARY FILES FOR
 8006                /       PROGRAM-SPECIFIC PURPOSE.
 8007   
 8008                /       THERE ARE 18 GROUPS OF SEVEN WORDS WITHIN EACH SLURP BINARY FILE BLOCK.  ANY
 8009                /       ADDITIONAL WORDS WIHIN THE BLOCK ARE IGNORED.
 8010   
 8011                /       ALL DECTAPES HAVE THREE ADDITIONAL WORDS SINCE PDP-8 DECTAPES ARE BASED ON
 8012                /       129 WORDS PER BLOCK; MOST SUPPORTED DEVICES ARE BASED ON 128 WORDS PER BLOCK.
 8013                /       BY IGNORING ALL ADDITIONAL WORDS AS NECESSARY, ALL IMAGED TFS BINARY FILES ARE
 8014                /       COMPATIBLE WHEN COPIED TO ALTERNATE DEVICES.  [NOTE: INDIVIDUAL SLURP LOADERS
 8015                /       ARE HARDWARE-SPECIFIC WITH THE EXCEPTION OF THE P?S/8 VIRTUAL SLURP LOADER
 8016                /       AVAILABLE ON ALL P?S/8 SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS.  SLURP FORMAT IS SURPRISINGLY
 8017                /       UNIVERSAL; IT IS USED IN CERTAIN OTHER PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS GENERALLY
 8018                /       WITHOUT DOCUMENTATION.]
 8019   
 8020                /       EACH OF THE 18 GROUPS IS PROCESSED IN SEQUENCE.  THE CONTROL WORD FOR THE
 8021                /       NEXT SIX DATA WORDS IS PROCESSED FIRST TO DETERMINE THE DISPOSITION OF EACH
 8022                /       WORD.
 8023   
 8024                /       EACH CONTROL WORD CONTAINS SIX PAIRS OF CONTROL BITS, EACH FOR THE
 8025                /       CORRESPONDING DATA WORD.  [BIT PAIRS ARE RECKONED LEFT TO RIGHT.]
 8026   
 8027                /       WITHIN EACH BIT PAIR, THE FOLLOWING DEFINITIONS APPLY:
 8028   
 8029                /       00 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS DATA TO BE LOADED INTO THE LOCATION POINTED TO
 8030                /            BY THE LOADING ORIGIN; THE LOADING ORIGIN WILL BE INCREMENTED AFTERWARDS.
 8031   
 8032                /       10 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS AN ORIGIN SETTING; THE LOADING ORIGIN WILL BE
 8033                /            UPDATED TO THE DATA VALUE.
 8034   
 8035                /       11 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS A FIELD SETTING EXPRESSED AS A "CDF X" [62X1]
 8036                /            INSTRUCTION TO THE NEW LOADING FIELD X; THE VALUE WILL BE STORED IN-LINE
 8037                /            WITHIN THE SLURP LOADER; NEW DATA [IF ANY] WILL BE LOADED INTO THIS FIELD
 8038                /            AS LOADING CONTINUES.  [NOTE: IN THEORY, BINARY FILES COULD END WITH A
 8039                /            FIELD SETTING.]
 8040   
 8041                /       01 - END OF FILE HAS BEEN DETECTED; IGNORE ALL ADDITIONAL DATA WORDS IN THIS
 8042                /            DATA BLOCK AND ANY ADDITIONAL BLOCKS IN THE CURRENT FILE.
 8043   
 8044                /       IT IS THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE LOADER TO PROCESS MULTIPLE FILES IF REQUIRED.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 162

 8046                /       SLURP FORMAT BINARY FILE LENGTH CONSIDERATIONS.
 8047   
 8048                /       P?S/8 TFS FILES CONSIST OF EXACTLY SIXTEEN CONTIGUOUS BLOCKS; THIS WOULD
 8049                /       GENERALLY MEAN THAT P?S/8 BINARY FILES WOULD BE REQUIRED TO BE EXACTLY
 8050                /       SIXTEEN BLOCKS.  WHILE THIS IS GENERALLY THE CASE, VARIOUS EXPERIMENTAL
 8051                /       DIRECTORIES HAVE BEEN DEVELOPED FOR USE WITH VARIOUS P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS
 8052                /       THAT ALLOW FOR VARIABLE LENGTH FILES.  [THE PRIMARY PURPOSE OF THESE EFFORTS
 8053                /       HAS BEEN TO SUPPORT VARIABLE-LENGTH TEXT FILES WITHOUT LINE NUMBERS; HOWEVER,
 8054                /       THERE IS NO INHERENT REASON THAT SUPPORT MUST BE LIMITED TO A SINGLE FILE
 8055                /       FORMAT.]
 8056   
 8057                /       SINCE SLURP FORMAT IS HANDLED ONE BLOCK AT A TIME, THERE IS NO INHERENT LENGTH
 8058                /       LIMITATION; SPECIAL-PURPOSE UTILITIES COULD BE CREATED TO SUPPORT LONGER FILES
 8059                /       AS REQUIRED.  THIS IS THE INTENDED METHOD TO BE USED WITHIN THE P?S/8 SHELL
 8060                /       OVERLAY.
 8061   
 8062                /       COMPARING SLURP FORMAT TO OTHER BINARY FILE FORMATS.
 8063   
 8064                /       IN CERTAIN OPERATING SYSTEMS, BINARY FILE FORMAT IS BASED ON THE 8-BIT FRAMES
 8065                /       OF BINARY PAPER-TAPES.  AT FIRST GLANCE, THIS WOULD APPEAR TO BE A REASONABLE
 8066                /       DESIGN AS BINARY LOADING IS A DESCENDANT OF THE ORIGINAL PAPER-TAPE BIN FORMAT
 8067                /       LOADER.
 8068   
 8069                /       HOWEVER, THIS IS SIMPLY SHORT-SIGHTED.  BINARY PAPER-TAPE FORMAT IS A VERY
 8070                /       INEFFICIENT FORMAT; THERE IS INHERENT OVERHEAD IN "MASSAGING" THE DATA INTO A
 8071                /       FORMAT GEARED TO LOADING 12-BIT WORDS DIRECTLY INTO MEMORY WHEN THE UNDERLYING
 8072                /       STORAGE DEVICE IS 12-BIT ORIENTED; NO ADVANTAGE IS BEING TAKEN OF THIS
 8073                /       IMPORTANT FACT AND WHY THIS WAS REJECTED IN THE DESIGN OF THE R-L MONITOR AND
 8074                /       P?S/8 [WHICH IS PARTIALLY BASED ON THIS FORMER SYSTEM].  PAPER-TAPE BINARY
 8075                /       FORMAT HAS CONSIDERATIONS BEYOND A STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM SUCH AS THE
 8076                /       NOTION OF SECTION HEADERS REQUIRING LEADER/TRAILER FRAMES.  NOT ALL BIT
 8077                /       COMBINATIONS ARE DEFINED IN PAPER-TAPE FRAMES AS IT WAS DEEMED SUFFICIENT TO
 8078                /       ACHIEVE A PRACTICAL LOADER REGARDLESS OF PROGRAM SIZE; ONLY THE CASES THAT
 8079                /       MATTER TO THE BIN LOADER ARE CONSIDERED, INCLUDING THE GENERATION OF A
 8080                /       CHECKSUM DURING THE LOADING TO ENSURE DATA VALIDITY.
 8081   
 8082                /       WHEN BINARY DATA IS AVAILABLE ON A STORAGE DEVICE, THE DATA VALIDITY IS
 8083                /       CHECKED MORE STRINGENTLY BY THE CONTROLLER DEVICE [SUCH AS THE TC01/TC08
 8084                /       DECTAPE CONTROLLER] AFFECTING ALL OPERATIONS, NOT MERELY THOSE RELATED TO
 8085                /       PROCESSING AN IMAGE OF A PAPER-TAPE THROUGH A LOADER THAT IS STORAGE-BASED.
 8086   
 8087                /       AS SUCH, IT IS PERFECTLY REASONABLE TO LIMIT THE FORMAT TO THE EFFICIENT
 8088                /       PURPOSE OF RAPID AND RELIABLE 12-BIT BINARY LOADING.  THE SLURP LOADER IS THE
 8089                /       BEST POSSIBLE IMPLEMENTATION OF A LOADER FOR THIS PURPOSE.  DESPITE THE
 8090                /       ABILITY TO LOAD MEMORY TOTALLY AT RANDOM [UNLIKE CORE-IMAGE BINARY WHICH MUST
 8091                /       BE EXPRESSED IN UNIFORM CONTIGUOUSLY LOADING BLOCKS], SLURP FORMAT IS JUST
 8092                /       UNDER 6/7 THE EFFICIENCY OF CORE-IMAGE BINARY.
 8093   
 8094                /       WHERE NEEDED, PAPER-TAPE COMPATIBILITY CAN ALSO BE ACHIEVED THROUGH THE USE OF
 8095                /       APPROPRIATE CONVERSION PROGRAMS.  ALL SUCH UTILITIES ARE AVAILABLE AS OPTIONS
 8096                /       OF THE P?S/8 BIN PROGRAM BY INVOKING APPROPRIATE OPTION SWITCHES.  [REFER TO
 8097                /       THE FULL DOCUMENTATION OF THE P?S/8 BIN PROGRAM FOR MORE INFORMATION.]
 8098   
 8099                /       END OF APPENDIX O.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 163

 8101                /       GLOSSARY OF TERMS USED IN THIS DOCUMENT.
 8102   
 8103                / 01) %
 8104   
 8105                /     "%" IS THE SYMBOLIC SYNTAX USED IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM FOR THE FILE
 8106                /     LOCATED AT BLOCKS 0020-0037 ON ANY SYSTEM DEVICE LOGICAL UNIT.  IT IS USED
 8107                /     AS A SCRATCH AREA COMPONENT FOR A VARIETY OF TEMPORARY PURPOSES, SOME OF
 8108                /     WHICH ARE INCOMPATIBLE WITH EACH OTHER.  THIS IS ALSO THE FIRST HALF
 8109                /     [00000-03777] OF THE DESIGNATED SCRATCH AREA FOR SAVING ALL OF FIELD 0.
 8110                /     THIS NOTATION IS NOT AVAILABLE IN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT; HOWEVER, THE
 8111                /     SYSTEM SAVE AREA IS USED IDENTICALLY IN BOTH SYSTEM ENVIRONMENTS.
 8112   
 8113                / 02) $
 8114   
 8115                /     "$" IS THE SYMBOLIC SYNTAX USED IN THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM FOR THE FILE
 8116                /     LOCATED AT BLOCKS 0040-0057 ON ANY SYSTEM DEVICE LOGICAL UNIT.  IT IS USED
 8117                /     AS A SCRATCH AREA COMPONENT FOR A VARIETY OF TEMPORARY PURPOSES, SOME OF
 8118                /     WHICH ARE INCOMPATIBLE WITH EACH OTHER.  THIS IS ALSO THE SECOND HALF
 8119                /     [04000-07777] OF THE DESIGNATED SCRATCH AREA FOR SAVING ALL OF FIELD 0.
 8120                /     THIS NOTATION IS NOT AVAILABLE IN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT; HOWEVER, THE
 8121                /     SYSTEM SAVE AREA IS USED IDENTICALLY IN BOTH SYSTEM ENVIRONMENTS.
 8122   
 8123                / 03) JACK BURNESS
 8124   
 8125                /     JACK IS A MEMBER OF THE WCFMPG AND CLOSE COLLEAGUE OF RICHARD LARY, FAMOUS
 8126                /     FOR VARIOUS PDP-8 PROJECTS INCLUDING THE SINGLE-HANDLED CREATION OF
 8127                /     LAP6-DIAL AND DIAL-MS FOR THE PDP-12.  JACK IS THE AUTHOR OF THE 8K
 8128                /     VARIANT OF LENNY ELEKMAN'S BASIC FOR THE PDP-8 [AKA LAB-8/E BASIC].  HE
 8129                /     IS ALSO THE AUTHOR OF THE LEGENDARY MOONLANDER PROGRAM FOR THE GT-40 BASED
 8130                /     ON AN OBSCURE PRIOR DEMO PROGRAM HE WROTE FOR SPECIFIC CONFIGURATIONS OF
 8131                /     THE PDP-12.  IT WOULD APPEAR THAT NONE OF HIS WORK IS ORIENTED TOWARDS
 8132                /     OS/8.  WITHOUT SOURCE CODE MODIFICATIONS, 8K BASIC CANNOT SAFELY BE USED
 8133                /     FROM OS/8 DUE TO LOADING COMPLICATIONS AND LIMITATIONS OF OS/8 THAT DO
 8134                /     NOT APPLY TO NEARLY ALL OTHER PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS.
 8135   
 8136                / 04) COS 300/310 [AND DIBOL-8]
 8137   
 8138                /     THE COMMERCIAL OPERATING SYSTEM [COS] FOR THE PDP-8 AND DECMATE SERIES IS
 8139                /     A DEDICATED ENVIRONMENT TO RUN THE DIBOL LANGUAGE DIALECT AS IMPLEMENTED
 8140                /     ON THE PDP-8.  THE ORIGINAL VERSION WAS KNOWN AS DIBOL-8; INTERNALLY, IT
 8141                /     IS A HACKED UP VERSION OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM WITH CERTAIN KEYBOARD
 8142                /     MONITOR COMMANDS CHANGED [A TRIVIAL CHANGE TO THE OPERATING SYSTEM].  THE
 8143                /     COMPILER WAS WRITTEN ORIGINALLY BY HANK MAURER.
 8144   
 8145                /     TWO MAJOR RELEASE FAMILIES EXIST KNOWN AS COS-300 AND COS-310.  BOTH ARE
 8146                /     BASED ON OS/8 WITH A CHANGED BASE DATE YEAR [AS A BAND-AID ON THE GENERAL
 8147                /     DATE PROBLEM PLAGUING OS/8]. [NOTE: THIS ACTUALLY CREATES ADDITIONAL
 8148                /     PROBLEMS WHILE SOLVING NONE OF THE UNDERLYING DATE PROBLEMS OF OS/8.]
 8149                /     COS-310 RUNS ON THE DECMATES [AT LEAST THE DECMATE II AND III+ WITH THE
 8150                /     HARD DISK OPTION].  THE OS/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR IS REPLACED BY AN INTERFACE
 8151                /     LIFTED FROM THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM; THE UNDERLYING FILE SYSTEM IS
 8152                /     COMPATIBLE WITH OS/8 TO WITHIN THE DATE OFFSET.  CERTAIN PROGRAMS CAN RUN
 8153                /     UNDER EITHER SYSTEM, SOMETIMES BY IMPLEMENTING INTERNAL KLUDGES TO ATTEMPT
 8154                /     TO DETERMINE WHICH SYSTEM IS HOSTING THE PROGRAM. [NOTE: THIS IS NOT
 8155                /     ALWAYS SUCCESSFUL LEADING TO NEGATIVE CONSEQUENCES.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 164

 8157                /     A NOTABLE FEATURE OF COS IS THE USE OF COMMANDS THAT ARE EXPRESSED AS
 8158                /     UNIQUE TWO CHARACTER NAMES OPTIONALLY FOLLOWED BY ADDITIONAL IGNORED
 8159                /     CHARACTERS.  THESE COMMANDS ARE CHOSEN TO AVOID THE NAMES OF OTHER
 8160                /     PROGRAMS THAT MUST BE EXPRESSED AS LONGER COMMANDS UP TO SIX CHARACTERS IN
 8161                /     LENGTH.  [NOTE: FOR ANY SIX CHARACTER NAME, ADDITIONAL CHARACTERS WILL BE
 8162                /     IGNORED; HOWEVER, SHORTER NAMES UP TO FIVE CHARACTERS MUST BE UNIQUELY
 8163                /     GIVEN.]  AS SUCH, THE ORIGINAL COMMANDS OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM IN
 8164                /     CONFLICT WITH THIS SCHEME WERE MODIFIED IN DIBOL-8.  THE ENTIRE MECHANISM
 8165                /     WAS TRANSFERRED TO THE COS INTERFACE THAT REPLACES THE OS/8 KEYBOARD
 8166                /     MONITOR.
 8167   
 8168                /     WHERE RELEVANT, IT IS NOT NECESSARY TO INVOKE THESE PROGRAMS VIA AN
 8169                /     EXPLICIT EXECUTION-CLASS COMMAND.  INSTEAD THE NAME ITSELF IMPLIES
 8170                /     EXECUTION; THIS SUPERFICIALLY RESEMBLES OS/8 CCL.  FOR EXAMPLE, THE
 8171                /     SE[QUENCE] COMMAND [TO HAVE AUTOMATICALLY GENERATED LINE NUMBERS WITHIN
 8172                /     THE BUILT-IN EDITOR] WAS CHANGED TO LN[UMBER].  THIS ALLOWS THE "SET"
 8173                /     PROGRAM TO BE INVOKED DIRECTLY AS A COMMAND.
 8174   
 8175                /     CLEARLY, THERE IS AN "INCESTUOUS" RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN THE R-L MONITOR AND
 8176                /     THE VARIOUS COS IMPLEMENTATIONS.  TO MAKE MATTERS SOMEWHAT MORE
 8177                /     COMPLICATED, THE IDENTICAL EXECUTION SCHEME WAS FIRST IMPLEMENTED IN
 8178                /     P?S/8, OFTEN WITH THE SAME NAMES OF UTILITY PROGRAMS FOR ANALOGOUS
 8179                /     PURPOSES [SUCH AS "SET", FORMERLY KNOWN AS "VARY"].
 8180   
 8181                /     EARLY VERSIONS OF P?S/8 ATTEMPT TO BE AS CLOSE TO THE R-L MONITOR AS
 8182                /     POSSIBLE IN TERMS OF IMPLEMENTING NOT ONLY THE SAME FUNCTIONALITY BUT ALSO
 8183                /     THE SAME COMMAND NAMES.  MORE RECENT VERSIONS ARE TOTALLY COMPATIBLE WITH
 8184                /     COS.  THUS, USERS OF THE VARIOUS COS SYSTEMS ARE ALREADY SOMEWHAT FAMILIAR
 8185                /     WITH P?S/8 EVEN IF THEY HAVE NEVER USED IT. [NOTE: THESE SIMILARITIES ARE
 8186                /     NOT A CONINCIDENCE.  THERE HAS ALWAYS BEEN A FLUID RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN
 8187                /     MEMBERS OF THE WCFMPG AND P?S, THUS CERTAIN INDIVIDUALS CLAIMING DUAL
 8188                /     MEMBERSHIP IN BOTH ORGANIZATIONS.]
 8189   
 8190                /     DIBOL-RELATED COMMANDS IN COS 300/310 ARE IMPLEMENTED IN THE SAME MANNER
 8191                /     AS THE ANALOGOUS COMMANDS IN P?S/8.  HOWEVER, ALL OTHER OS/8 OPERATIONS
 8192                /     RUNNING FROM COS ARE IMPLEMENTED WITH ALL OF THE OVERHEAD THAT APPLIES TO
 8193                /     OS/8 IN GENERAL.  IN P?S/8, THIS IS NOT ACTUALLY A CCL IMPLEMENTATION.
 8194                /     AS SUCH, THE OVERHEAD OF CCL AS A LAYER OVER AN OPERATING SYSTEM DOES NOT
 8195                /     APPLY.  HOWEVER, MANY COMMANDS ARE ENTERED IN QUITE SIMILAR WAYS IN ALL
 8196                /     THREE SYSTEMS WHERE APPLICABLE.
 8197   
 8198                /     IF THERE IS SUFFICIENT INTEREST, A COS-LIKE OVERLAY TO THE P?S/8 SHELL MAY
 8199                /     BE IMPLEMENTED TO ALLOW ONE UNIFIED SYSTEM TO REPLACE ALL OF THESE
 8200                /     OPERATING SYSTEMS.  IT IS ARGUABLE THAT COS SHOULD NEVER HAVE BEEN
 8201                /     IMPLEMENTED AS A SEPARATE OPERATING SYSTEM THAT OVERWHELMED THE PDP-8
 8202                /     DEVELOPMENT GROUPS WITHIN DEC.  HOWEVER, THIS WAS A MANAGEMENT DECISION;
 8203                /     MANY MANAGEMENT DECISIONS WERE EVENTUALLY PROVEN TO BE POOR JUDGEMENT.


