 8511   
 8512                / 16) P?S/8
 8513   
 8514                /     P?S/8 IS THE NAME OF AN OPERATING SYSTEM FOR THE PDP-8.  IT IS BASED, IN
 8515                /     PART, ON THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM.  ALL OF THE DEVELOPERS OF P?S/8 ARE
 8516                /     EXPERIENCED PROGRAMMERS WHO STARTED THEIR PDP-8 PROGRAMMING CAREERS ON THE
 8517                /     R-L MONITOR SYSTEM AND ARE THOROUGHLY FAMILIAR WITH IT INTERNALLY AS WELL
 8518                /     AS EXPERT USERS OF THAT PIONEERING SYSTEM.  BY KNOWING ITS WEAKNESSES AND
 8519                /     OVERCOMING THEM IN NEW PROGRAMMING, A BETTER OVERALL SYSTEM WAS CREATED.
 8520   
 8521                /     THE REGULAR USER INTERFACE TO P?S/8 IS BASED ON USER EXPERIENCE WITH
 8522                /     ORIGINAL IMPLEMENTATIONS OF THE BASIC LANGUAGE AS PRACTICED ON VARIOUS
 8523                /     GE-235 TIME-SHARING SYSTEMS.  FILE COMMANDS ARE VERY MINIMAL AND GEARED
 8524                /     TOWARDS BEING AS INOBSTRUSIVE AS POSSIBLE.  MOST USERS INTUITIVELY
 8525                /     UNDERSTAND THIS INTERFACE WITHIN MOMENTS OF FIRST CONTACT WITH P?S/8.
 8526   
 8527                /     WHILE MAINTAINING THE ORIGINAL STYLE AND SUBSTANCE OF THE R-L MONITOR, THE
 8528                /     OUTGROWTH OF P?S/8 IS TOTALLY ALONG THE LINES OF OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS
 8529                /     FROM DEC FOR OTHER COMPUTERS.  HOWEVER, UNLIKE OS/8, THE EMPHASIS HAS BEEN
 8530                /     ON EFFICIENCY OF RESOURCES AND SPEED OF PERFORMANCE, ESPECIALLY ON SLOWER
 8531                /     DEVICES SUCH AS DECTAPES AND LINCTAPES.  MOST USERS, GIVEN AN INFORMED
 8532                /     CHOICE, WILL PREFER P?S/8 TO OS/8 FOR A VARIETY OF REASONS.
 8533   
 8534                /     P?S/8 HAS CERTAIN LIMITATIONS, NOT SO MUCH AS TO IMPLEMENTATION, BUT
 8535                /     TO THE DELIBERATE ATTEMPT TO MAXIMIZE PERFORMANCE WHILE PROVIDING ADEQUATE
 8536                /     PRODUCTIVITY FEATURES; THIS IS NOT TO EVERYONE'S LIKING.  FOR THOSE WHO
 8537                /     PREFER A LARGER FEATURE SET, P?S/8 IS BEING EXPANDED TO INCLUDE AN
 8538                /     OPTIONAL SHELL OVERLAY.   THIS WILL OPERATE UNDER AN ENTIRELY DIFFERENT
 8539                /     SET OF TRADE-OFFS GENERALLY REQUIRING MORE MEMORY.
 8540   
 8541                /     FOR MOST USERS, THE OPTIONAL SHELL WILL NOT BE NEEDED.  HOWEVER, "POWER
 8542                /     USERS" WILL PREFER THE P?S/8 SHELL OVER THE BASIC SYSTEM [AS WELL AS OS/8]
 8543                /     FOR GENERAL USE WITHOUT RESTRICTIONS.  WHILE THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT WILL
 8544                /     REQUIRE MORE RESOURCES THAN THE BASIC ENVIRONMENT, PERFORMANCE WILL STILL
 8545                /     EXCEED THAT OF OS/8; ALL ATTEMPTS WILL BE MADE TO OBTAIN MAXIMUM
 8546                /     PERFORMANCE FROM THE RESOURCES AVAILABLE.  HOWEVER, THE BASIC SYSTEM IS
 8547                /     4K-ORIENTED. [FOR REFERENCE, OS/8 IS 8K-ORIENTED.] THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT
 8548                /     IS ORIENTED TOWARDS LARGER CONFIGURATIONS; CERTAIN FEATURES WILL WORK BEST
 8549                /     IN AS MUCH AS 24K OR MORE; A FEW WILL REQUIRE 32K TO WORK AS BEST AS
 8550                /     POSSIBLE; A SMALL FEW FEATURES WILL NOT BE AVAILABLE IN A 4K ENVIRONMENT,
 8551                /     GENERALLY LEADING TO GRACEFUL ERROR MESSAGES.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 172

 8553                /     BY ADJUSTING CERTAIN CONFIGURATION PARAMETERS, THE SCOPE OF FUNCTIONALITY
 8554                /     CAN BE ROUGHLY THE SAME AS AN EQUIVALENT OS/8 SYSTEM GIVEN THE IDENTICAL
 8555                /     HARDWARE.  HOWEVER, PERFORMANCE WILL BE BETTER IN THE P?S/8 SHELL IN MANY
 8556                /     WAYS.  SINCE THE CONFIGURATION IS QUITE FLEXIBLE, THE USER CAN DETERMINE
 8557                /     WHAT IS BEST FOR THE PRESENT USAGE.  FOR THE MOST SOPHISTICATED USERS,
 8558                /     SEVERAL VARIATIONS USED JUDICIOUSLY MAY BE THE BEST OVERALL SOLUTION
 8559                /     UNLESS 32K IS AVAILABLE. [NOTE: CERTAIN SYSTEM CONFIGURATION PARAMETERS
 8560                /     CAN BE ADJUSTED TO OBTAIN TRADEOFFS WITH DIFFERENT OUTCOMES.]  DUE TO THE
 8561                /     VARIOUS FEATURES OF P?S/8 THAT ARE IMPLEMENTED IN A FAR MORE COMPREHENSIVE
 8562                /     WAY THAN OS/8, COUPLED WITH OPTIONAL FEATURES THAT HAVE NO OS/8
 8563                /     EQUIVALENT, IT MAY BE DIFFICULT TO COMPARE PERFORMANCE OF THE TWO SYSTEMS
 8564                /     DIRECTLY. [NOTE: OS/8 DOES NOT SUPPORT ALTERNATE CONSOLE DEVICES OR
 8565                /     MODERN TERMINAL FLOW-CONTROL OTHER THAN WHEN USED WITH A SPECIFIC OUTPUT
 8566                /     HANDLER [KNOWN AS THE KL8E HANDLER].  UNFORTUNATELY, MOST CONSOLE OUTPUT
 8567                /     IS PERFORMED DIRECTLY BY EACH PROGRAM.  THUS, COMPARING THE ERRATIC
 8568                /     PERFORMANCE OF OS/8 PROGRAMS TO THE SYSTEM-WIDE APPROACH OF P?S/8 WHERE
 8569                /     ALL CONFORMING PROGRAMS ARE FULLY COMPLIANT WITH RESPECT TO BOTH TERMINAL
 8570                /     HANDLING AND LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY INTERFACE IS NOT EASILY GAUGED.  MANY
 8571                /     USERS WITH TERMINALS SUCH AS THE VT100 FAMILY HAVE COMPROMISED THEIR
 8572                /     SYSTEMS TO LIMIT THE AMOUNT OF FLAWED OUTPUT FROM OS/8; P?S/8 CAN RUN
 8573                /     AT ANY CONNECTION RATE WITHOUT DROPPING A SINGLE CHARACTER.]
 8574   
 8575                /     NOTE: P?S/8 WAS DEMONSTRATED TO DEC PERSONNEL WITH THE INTENTION OF GIVING
 8576                /     THE SOFTWARE TO DEC IN EXCHANGE FOR HARDWARE DONATION TO THE BROOKLYN
 8577                /     POLYTECHNIC INSTITUTE, WHERE ALL MEMBERS OF THE WCFMPG AND P?S ONCE MET
 8578                /     [NOT NECESSARILY AT THE SAME TIME].  THIS WAS ACTUALLY ATEMPTED TWICE.
 8579                /     HOWEVER, DEC MANAGEMENT REJECTED P?S/8 FOR REASONS THAT DEFY RATIONAL
 8580                /     EXPLANATION DESPITE ACTIVE PARTICIPATION BY SOME OF THE INDIVIDUALS
 8581                /     MENTIONED IN THIS DOCUMENT AS WELL AS OTHER MEMBERS OF THE DEC PDP-8
 8582                /     PROGRAMMING STAFF LOBBYING FOR IT.
 8583   
 8584                /     EVENTUALLY, A LIMITED EDITION OF P?S/8 [RENAMED IFDOS] WAS LICENSED TO
 8585                /     INTERSIL CORPORATION FOR USE ON THEIR INTERCEPT I, A PDP-8-COMPATIBLE
 8586                /     MACHINE BASED ON THE IM6100 AND SIMILAR TO AN INFERIOR OFFERING FROM DEC
 8587                /     KNOWN AS THE VT78, THE SLOWEST SYSTEM CONFIGURATION SUPPORTED BY P?S/8.
 8588                /     FOR SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CONSIDERATIONS ALONE, P?S/8 RUNS 13 TIMES AS
 8589                /     FAST AS OS/8 ON THIS MACHINE.  SINCE THE INTERCEPT I IS 20 PERCENT FASTER
 8590                /     THAN THE VT78, OS/8 PERFORMANCE IS MODESTLY BETTER ON THIS MACHINE.  ON
 8591                /     EITHER MACHINE, PERFORMANCE COMPARISONS SHOW A DRAMATIC DIFFERENCE BETWEEN
 8592                /     THE TWO OPERATING SYSTEMS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 173

 8594                /     [NOTE: P?S/8 ACTUALLY RUNS AT THE SAME SPEED ON THE RX01/RX02 DISKETTE
 8595                /     DRIVES REGARDLESS OF THE SPEED OF THE HOST MACHINE.  HOWEVER, OS/8 RUNS
 8596                /     13 TIMES AS SLOW ON THE VT78 DUE TO HANDLER INEFFICIENCY THAT CANNOT BE
 8597                /     OVERCOME DUE TO DESIGN LIMITATIONS OF OS/8.  THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE
 8598                /     HANDLER REQUIRES EXTENDED MEMORY AS DOES OS/8; HOWEVER, P?S/8 EXTENDED
 8599                /     DEVICE HANDLER DESIGN ALLOWS MUCH MORE CODE SPACE ALLOWING
 8600                /     HIGH-PERFORMANCE HANDLING OF RX01/RX02 DRIVES BY USING MORE SOPHISTICATED
 8601                /     METHODS.  EVEN ON A SYSTEM AS SLOW AS THE VT78 THE PERFORMANCE IS
 8602                /     VIRTUALLY THE SAME AS ON MUCH FASTER HARDWARE.  OS/8 ONLY ALLOWS ENOUGH
 8603                /     CODE SPACE TO IMPLEMENT A SEVERELY COMPROMISED DEVICE HANDLER UNABLE TO
 8604                /     KEEP UP WITH THE REQUIRED 2:1 INTERLEAVE.  AS SUCH, THE HIGHEST
 8605                /     TRACK/SECTOR CALCULATIONS FAIL TO MAINTAIN TRANSFER SPEED ON ALL MODELS;
 8606                /     ON VERY SLOW MACHINES SUCH AS THE VT78, THIS LOSS OF PERFORMANCE APPLIES
 8607                /     TO VIRTUALLY EVERY DISKETTE SECTOR.  MOST USERS OF THE RX01/RX02 DISKETTE
 8608                /     DRIVE, WHEN CONNECTED TO FASTER MACHINES SUCH AS THE PDP-8/E, ARE UNAWARE
 8609                /     OF THIS PROBLEM SINCE ONLY THE HIGHEST SECTORS ARE AFFECTED; MOST
 8610                /     OPERATIONS ADDRESS LOWER SECTORS WHERE THE PERFORMANCE LOSS DOESN'T SET
 8611                /     IN; ON THE VT78 ALL SECTORS ARE AFFECTED WITH THE POSSIBLE EXCEPTION OF A
 8612                /     PORTION OF TRACK 1.  OS/8 USES A MINIMAL SIZE DIVISION ALGORITHM
 8613                /     IMPLEMENTED BY THE USE OF REPETITIVE SUBTRACTION; ALL TRACK/SECTOR DATA
 8614                /     MUST BE CALCULATED EACH AND EVERY TIME.  P?S/8 RX01/RX02 HANDLERS USE A
 8615                /     METHOD DERIVED BY STRIPPING DOWN AN EAE SIMULATOR TO A DIRECT
 8616                /     IMPLEMENTATION OF THE REQUIRED DIVISION.  HOWEVER, THIS IS ONLY USED ONCE
 8617                /     PER CALL TO ESTABLISH THE INITIAL TRACK/SECTOR DATA.  ALL ADDITIONAL
 8618                /     SECTORS ARE INSTANTLY CALCULATED BY A PREDICTOR/CORRECTOR ROUTINE.  THE
 8619                /     LATEST TRACK AND SECTOR ARE DERIVED FROM THE PREVIOUS VALUES.  THE AMOUNT
 8620                /     OF CODE SPACE TO IMPLEMENT THIS TWO-STAGE METHOD IS FAR BEYOND THE UPPER
 8621                /     LIMIT OF OS/8 HANDLER DESIGN.]
 8622   
 8623                / 17) R-L MONITOR SYSTEM
 8624   
 8625                /     THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM IS THE PRECURSOR TO P?S/8 WRITTEN BY RICHARD LARY
 8626                /     AND LENNY ELEKMAN.  THE ENTIRE OPERATING SYSTEM WAS HAND ASSEMBLED AND
 8627                /     ENTERED DIRECTLY INTO PDP-8 MEMORY MANUALLY ON THE FRONT PANEL OF AN
 8628                /     ORIGINAL PDP-8 COMPUTER LOCATED IN THE BROOKLYN POLYTECHNIC INSTITUTE
 8629                /     ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING COMPLEX.  ALL OF THE PROGRAMMERS MENTIONED HERE
 8630                /     EXTENSIVELY USED THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM AS IT WAS A MAJOR ADVANCE OVER
 8631                /     PREVIOUS SYSTEMS FOR THE SAME HARDWARE.
 8632   
 8633                /     [NOTE: THE MANUAL ENTRY OF SO MANY THOUSANDS OF R-L MONITOR INSTRUCTIONS
 8634                /     ON THIS COMPUTER CAUSED PREMATURE FAILURE OF THE FRONT PANEL.  CHARLES
 8635                /     LASNER HAD THE DUBIOUS DISTINCTION OF MANUALLY CHANGING EVERY ONE OF THE
 8636                /     FRONT PANEL SWITCHES TO RESTORE FUNCTIONALITY.  IT IS NOT ADVISABLE TO
 8637                /     MENTION THE SWITCH MANUFACTURER'S NAME ["STACKPOLE"] IN HIS PRESENCE.]
 8638   
 8639                /     THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM ONLY SUPPORTS A SINGLE TU55/TU56 DECTAPE DRIVE AND
 8640                /     REQUIRES EXTENSIVE MODIFICATION TO SUPPORT ANY ALTERNATE DEVICE.  MEMBERS
 8641                /     OF THE P?S INTENSELY STUDIED THE INTERNAL WORKINGS OF THE R-L MONITOR
 8642                /     SYSTEM AND BODILY REPLACED VARIOUS SECTIONS OVER THE COURSE OF MANY YEARS,
 8643                /     EVENTUALLY PRODUCING THE P?S/8 REPLACEMENT SYSTEM AS DESCRIBED ELSEWHERE
 8644                /     IN THIS DOCUMENT.


