/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 175

 8698                / 19) SLURP LOADER
 8699   
 8700                /     RICHARD LARY IMPLEMENTED THE FIRST "SLURP" LOADER [THE TERM ATTEMPTS TO
 8701                /     DESCRIBE SOME ASPECTS OF THE PROCESS] AS THE ONLY BINARY FILE LOADER ON
 8702                /     THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM.  ALL P?S/8 SYSTEMS PROVIDE A VIRTUAL LOADER THAT
 8703                /     PRODUCES SUBSTANTIALLY THE SAME RESULT; WHERE FEASIBLE, THE SLURP LOADER
 8704                /     IS PROVIDED AS AN OPTION.  MOST, BUT NOT ALL SUPPORTED DEVICES CAN
 8705                /     IMPLEMENT A SLURP LOADER.  EXCEPTIONS INCLUDE THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK
 8706                /     DEVICES THAT ARE TOO FAST [RF08] AND OTHER DMA DEVICES THAT CANNOT BE
 8707                /     ACCESSED VIA SINGLE WORD TRANSFERS [RK08/RK01, RK8E/RK05, ETC.].  ALL
 8708                /     ASYNCHRONOUS PROGRAM TRANSFER DEVICES SHOULD SUPPORT A SLURP LOADER.
 8709   
 8710                /     SLURP LOADING IS BASED ON AN EFFICIENT LOADING FORMAT THAT IS NEARLY 6/7
 8711                /     AS EFFICIENT AS CORE-IMAGE FORMAT [AND FAR MORE EFFICIENT THAN ANY
 8712                /     PAPER-TAPE LOADING FORMAT].  THE FORMAT CONSISTS OF A 12-BIT CONTROL WORD
 8713                /     CONTAINING SIX TWO-BIT CONTROL FIELDS FOLLOWED BY SIX 12-BIT DATA WORDS.
 8714                /     EACH DATA WORD IS EITHER A) A LOADING ADDRESS FOR SUBSEQUENT LOADING, B) A
 8715                /     WORD TO BE LOADED INTO THE LATEST ADDRESS; IMPLICITLY INCREMENT THE
 8716                /     LOADING ADDRESS, C) A FIELD SETTING [FIELD 0 IS THE DEFAULT], D) END OF
 8717                /     FILE.  THE SEVEN-WORD SEQUENCE IS REPEATED 18 TIMES IN ANY SINGLE BLOCK;
 8718                /     THE LAST TWO WORDS OF EACH 128-WORD BLOCK ARE IGNORED.
 8719   
 8720                /     OTHER THAN THE PREVAILING LOADING ORIGIN AND CURRENT DATA FIELD SETTING,
 8721                /     ALL DATA BLOCKS ARE INDEPENDENT. [NOTE: SLURP FORMAT FILES CAN
 8722                /     THEORETICALLY BE CREATED PROVISIONALLY THAT ARE SIGNIFICANTLY LONGER THAN
 8723                /     TFS FILES.]
 8724   
 8725                /     A PRACTICAL SLURP LOADER WORKS WITHOUT A BLOCK-ORIENTED BUFFER, PROCESSING
 8726                /     THE WORDS AS THEY ARE READ IN DIRECTLY.  IN THE CASE OF THE TC01/TC08
 8727                /     DECTAPE, THE WORD-COUNT REGISTER IS SET TO 0000; THE CURRENT ADDRESS IS
 8728                /     SET TO ONE LESS THAN THE ADDRESS OF A ONE-WORD BUFFER; JIM ROTH PROVED
 8729                /     THAT THE CURRENT ADDRESS REGISTER IS A PERFECTLY ACCEPTABLE BUFFER.  THE
 8730                /     PROGRAM WAITS FOR THE WORD-COUNT REGISTER TO CHANGE FROM 0000 -> 0001.  AT
 8731                /     THAT POINT, THE LATEST FILE WORD IS IN THE CURRENT ADDRESS REGISTER.  EACH
 8732                /     WORD IS PROCESSED AS DESCRIBED ABOVE.
 8733   
 8734                /     WHEN ALL FILES HAVE BEEN LOADED, THE LOADED PROGRAM IS STARTED AT AN
 8735                /     ADDRESS DETERMINED BY THE P?S/8 BIN PROGRAM THAT PLACES THE SLURP LOADER,
 8736                /     IN CONJUNCTION WITH USER-SUPPLIED OPTION SWITCHES.  ALL SLURP LOADERS
 8737                /     INCLUDE AN INTERNAL ADDRESS OF A HLT INSTRUCTION [7402] TO BE USED AS THE
 8738                /     DEFAULT ADDRESS IF THE USER DOESN'T SUPPLY ONE; STARTING ADDRESS AND
 8739                /     INITIAL STARTING FIELD ARE PROCESSED AT THE END OF FILE LOADING.
 8740   
 8741                /     A MAJOR ADVANTAGE OF THE SLURP LOADER IS THAT THE DEVICE CAN BE
 8742                /     WRITE-PROTECTED AS ALL LOADING IS READ-ONLY.  [NOTE: IF THE VIRTUAL LOADER
 8743                /     IS CHOSEN, THE SYSTEM DEVICE MUST BE WRITE-ENABLED AS THE VIRTUAL AREA IS
 8744                /     USED TO PROTECT LOADING OVER THE VIRTUAL LOADER INCLUDING ITS BUFFER USED
 8745                /     TO READ IN PORTIONS OF BINARY FILES ETC.] THERE IS A NOTICABLE PERFORMANCE
 8746                /     IMPROVEMENT BETWEEN THE TWO LOADER VARIANTS; USE OF THE SLURP LOADER IS
 8747                /     ALWAYS RECOMMENDED FOR NORMAL USAGE [IF AVAILABLE].
 8748   
 8749                /     THE SLURP LOADER IS INCLUDED IN THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM, POLY BASIC
 8750                /     [EDU-SYSTEM 30] AS WELL AS P?S/8.  [NOTE: ONLY THE P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATION
 8751                /     SUPPORTS EXTENDED MEMORY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 176

 8753                / 20) SYSTEM SAVE AREA
 8754   
 8755                /     THE SYSTEM SAVE AREA REFERS TO THE DEDICATED BLOCKS ON THE P?S/8 SYSTEM
 8756                /     DEVICE [BLOCKS 0020-0057] USED TO PRESERVE THE ENTIRE USER AREA CONTENTS
 8757                /     OF FIELD 0 [0000-7577].  THIS IS ACCESSED BY VARIOUS MEMORY-ORIENTED
 8758                /     COMPONENTS OF BOTH THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM AND THE OPTIONAL SHELL
 8759                /     ENVIRONMENT.
 8760   
 8761                /     IN THE BASIC SYSTEM, THE SYSTEM SAVE AREA CAN ALSO BE ADDRESSED BY TWO TFS
 8762                /     FILE NAMES "%" AND "$"; EACH TFS FILE IS 2K LONG.  "%" STORES LOCATIONS
 8763                /     00000-03777]; "$" STORES LOCATIONS 04000-07777]. [NOTE: THE LAST PAGE OF
 8764                /     MEMORY WHERE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS RESIDENT IS NEVER OVERWRITTEN;
 8765                /     HOWEVER, CERTAIN PROGRAMS MAY WRITE OUT AN IMAGE OF THE DEVICE HANDLER IN
 8766                /     CONJUNCTION WITH SAVING THE REST OF FIELD 0.]
 8767   
 8768                / 21) P?S/8 SHELL
 8769   
 8770                /     THE P?S/8 SHELL IS AN OPTIONAL SYSTEM OVERLAY THAT DISPLACES THE NORMAL
 8771                /     KEYBOARD MONITOR IN FAVOR OF AN ALTERNATE INTERFACE AND FILE SYSTEM.  THE
 8772                /     SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS USED ONLY AS AN INTERNAL MECHANISM TO BIND ALL OF
 8773                /     THE SHELL COMPONENTS TOGETHER.  THIS INCLUDES THE SYSTEM LOADER WHICH,
 8774                /     UNLIKE THE OS/8 ANALOGOUS SEGMENT, IS SELF-SUFFICIENT AND CANNOT FAIL DUE
 8775                /     TO INTERNAL ERROR [UNRECOVERABLE HALT].  IT IS INTENDED TO BE USED WITH
 8776                /     THE OPTIONAL LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY; AS SUCH, ALL ERRORS ON THE SYSTEM
 8777                /     DEVICE WILL BE ELEGANTLY HANDLED BY THE OPERATING SYSTEM; PROGRAMS DO NOT
 8778                /     PARTICIPATE IN THE ERROR RECOVERY PROCESS FOR ANY OPERATION ON THE SYSTEM
 8779                /     DEVICE. [NOTE: THE P?S/8 SHELL CANNOT DIRECTLY ACCESS THE TFS DIRECTORY ON
 8780                /     THE SYSTEM DEVICE; SPECIFIC UTILITIES CAN BE WRITTEN TO ACCOMPLISH THIS.
 8781                /     THIS WILL FACILITATE FILE CONVERSION BETWEEN THE TWO P?S/8 OPERATIONAL
 8782                /     VARIANTS AND/OR OS/8.]
 8783   
 8784                /     NO OTHER BASIC P?S/8 FEATURE IS USED IN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT.  AS SUCH,
 8785                /     IT IS FEASIBLE TO STRIP OUT MOST OF THE BASIC SYSTEM TO MINIMIZE THE
 8786                /     OVERALL SYSTEM REQUIREMENT IF DESIRED.  BY SUITABLE CONFIGURATION, THE
 8787                /     FIRST [UP TO] 4096 BLOCKS CAN BE RESERVED FOR THE SYSTEM DEVICE, WHICH
 8788                /     CORRESPONDS TO THE LARGEST POSSIBLE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE STORAGE.  IF
 8789                /     BOTH SYSTEM VARIANTS ARE TO BE PRESENT ON ANY DEVICE, THE RESERVED SIZE
 8790                /     CAN BE ADJUSTED ACCORDINGLY.  HOWEVER, THE MINIMUM VIABLE SIZE FOR A
 8791                /     SYSTEM DEVICE GEARED TOWARDS ROUTINELY RUNNING THE SHELL ON A DAILY BASIS
 8792                /     CAN BE SET TO APPROXIMATELY 200 BLOCKS; THIS IS COMPARABLE TO THE
 8793                /     DIFFERENCE BETWEEN AN OS/8 SYSTEM DEVICE AND AN OS/8 NON-SYSTEM DEVICE ON
 8794                /     THE SAME MEDIA.  AS NECESSARY, ANY ADDITIONAL STORAGE CAN BE ACCESSED AS A
 8795                /     NON-SYSTEM DEVICE; P?S/8 INCLUDES A TABLE OF NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS THAT
 8796                /     SPECIFIES PROTECTION OFFSETS TO DEAL WITH MIXED CONFIGURATIONS. [NOTE: THE
 8797                /     BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM IS ORIENTED TOWARDS THE SMALLER MAXIMUM STORAGE SPACE
 8798                /     ADDRESSED BY THE SYSTEM HANDLER.  NON-SYSTEM DEVICES CAN ADDRESS AS MUCH
 8799                /     AS 4096 TIMES AS MUCH SPACE.  ALL NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CALLS ARE THUS
 8800                /     POTENTIALLY SUBJECT TO AN ADJUSTMENT ON DEVICES WITH DUAL STRUCTURES
 8801                /     PRESENT.]  [NOTE: THE NOTION OF MIXED CONFIGURATIONS IS STILL BEING
 8802                /     RESEARCHED AS OF THIS WRITING; ACTUAL IMPLEMENTATION MAY VARY GREATLY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 177

 8804                /     THE SHELL PROCESSES COMMANDS THAT REFERENCE A FILE SYSTEM FAR MORE
 8805                /     POWERFUL THAN THE OS/8 STRUCTURE.  FILE NAMES ARE UP TO 12 CHARACTERS WITH
 8806                /     UP TO FOUR CHARACTER FILE EXTENSIONS WITH CASE-RETENTION. [OS/8 FILE NAMES
 8807                /     ARE UP TO SIX CHARACTERS WITH UP TO TWO CHARACTER FILE EXTENSIONS IN
 8808                /     UPPER-CASE ONLY.]  SINCE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS DESIGNED FOR EITHER
 8809                /     THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM OR SHELL INTER-COMPONENT COMMUNICATION, IT PLAYS NO
 8810                /     DIRECT ROLE IN THE SHELL FILE ACCESS.  POWERFUL NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS ARE
 8811                /     USED FOR ALL OPERATIONS WHICH CAN ACCESS FAR MORE STORAGE THAN EITHER THE
 8812                /     BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM OR OS/8.
 8813   
 8814                /     THE FOLLOWING IS A REALISTIC COMPARISON: THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM CAN
 8815                /     ADDRESS .5 MWORDS OF DATA PER LOGICAL UNIT.  [NOTE: 1.0 MWORDS IS 1048576
 8816                /     12-BIT WORDS OF STORAGE.]  ASSUMING EIGHT LOGICAL UNITS PER SYSTEM DEVICE,
 8817                /     THIS IS A TOTAL ADDRESSABLE SPACE OF 4.0 MWORDS PER SYSTEM DEVICE GROUP.
 8818                /     OS/8 HANDLERS CAN ADDRESS AT MOST 1.0 MWORDS OF STORAGE PER HANDLER.
 8819                /     THUS, OS/8 CAN ADDRESS WITH ANY ONE HANDLER TWICE AS MUCH STORAGE SPACE AS
 8820                /     ANY ONE P?S/8 LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT WHILE THE ENTIRE P?S/8 DEVICE GROUP CAN
 8821                /     ACCESS FOUR TIMES AS MUCH STORAGE AS OS/8'S SYSTEM HANDLER.
 8822   
 8823                /     THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT USES NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS THAT ARE RADICALLY
 8824                /     DIFFERENT FROM EITHER THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM OR OS/8.  THE MAXIMUM
 8825                /     ADDRESSABLE SPACE IS 2048 MWORDS PER LOGICAL UNIT FOR A TOTAL ADDRESSABLE
 8826                /     STORAGE OF 16384 MWORDS PER NON-SYSTEM DEVICE GROUP.  THIS IS 2048 TIMES
 8827                /     AS LARGE AS ANY OS/8 HANDLER CAN ACCESS AND 4096 TIMES AS LARGE AS ANY
 8828                /     P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER PER LOGICAL UNIT.
 8829   
 8830                /     A FURTHER CONSIDERATION OF STORAGE SPACE IS THE ALLOWED NUMBER OF LOGICAL
 8831                /     DEVICES.  OS/8 ALLOWS AT MOST 15 LOGICAL DEVICES.  [NOTE: INTERNAL DEVICE
 8832                /     INFORMATION IS HELD IN 4-BIT FIELDS; THE CASE OF 0000 IS NEEDED TO
 8833                /     INDICATE A LACK OF EXPLICIT DEVICE TO ALLOW INTERPRETATION AT ANOTHER
 8834                /     LEVEL.] AS A PRACTICAL MATTER [FOR COMPARISON PURPOSES] THE NUMBER EIGHT
 8835                /     WILL BE USED. [NOTE: ONE OF THE DEVICES IS DSK:, A LOGICAL STAND-IN FOR
 8836                /     ONE OF THE OTHER DEVICES; SEVERAL ADDITIONAL DEVICES ARE USED FOR
 8837                /     NON-STORAGE PURPOSES SUCH AS THE CONSOLE DEVICE, HIGH-SPEED READER/PUNCH
 8838                /     IF APPLICABLE AND SYSTEM LINEPRINTER.] WHILE THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF
 8839                /     DEVICES AVAILABLE TO THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT HAS NOT BEEN DETERMINED, THE
 8840                /     MAXIMUM NUMBER IS NOT LIMITED TO A SUBSET OF 16; BETTER OVERALL DESIGN
 8841                /     ALLOWS FOR FAR MORE DEVICE HANDLER ENTRIES IN A PRACTICAL SYSTEM.  FOR
 8842                /     COMPARISON PURPOSES THE NUMBER 32 WILL BE USED [IT COULD EASILY BE TWICE
 8843                /     AS MUCH].
 8844   
 8845                /     MORE MODERN STORAGE DEVICES TEND TO BE LARGER HARD DISKS PARTITIONED INTO
 8846                /     SMALLER DIVISIONS AS NECESSARY.  THIS SUGGESTS THE LARGEST ADDRESSABLE
 8847                /     SPACE ON A SUITABLY PARTITIONED DISK WOULD BE 512 MWORDS.  MANY MORE
 8848                /     RECENT DEVICES USE SIX-BIT BYTES IN THE UNDERLYING STORAGE. [NOTE:
 8849                /     EXAMPLES INCLUDE RX-SERIES DISKETTES AS TYPICALLY OPERATED IN 12-BIT MODE,
 8850                /     RD50 HARD DISK SUBSYSTEM ON CERTAIN DECMATES, CESI MDC8 ETC.  ALL SHARE
 8851                /     THE COMMON TRAIT OF USING 3/4 OF THE ACTUAL STORAGE OF 8-BIT BYTES.] THUS,
 8852                /     ANOTHER WAY TO STATE CAPACITY IS 4 GB PER LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT, 32 GB PER
 8853                /     LOGICAL DEVICE GROUP AND 1.0 TB PER SYSTEM WITHIN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT.
 8854                /     OS/8 ONLY SUPPORTS 2.0 MB MAXIMUM ON ANY DEVICE AND 32 MB ON THE LARGEST
 8855                /     SUPPORTABLE SYSTEM.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 178

 8857                /     THERE IS AN ADDITIONAL IMPORTANT TRADEOFF: OS/8 RECORD SIZE IS 256 WORDS
 8858                /     WHILE ALL P?S/8 HANDLERS ADDRESS STORAGE IN 128 WORD BLOCKS.  DEPENDING ON
 8859                /     PROGRAM LOGISTICS, OS/8'S LARGER MINIMUM BUFFER REQUIREMENT CAN CAUSE
 8860                /     PROGRAM SIZE TO BE NEEDLESSLY LARGE AND UNWIELDY.  OFTEN A SMALL CONTROL
 8861                /     FILE IS NEEDED AND THE SMALLEST FILE BUFFER WOULD BE APPROPRIATE.  IN ANY
 8862                /     P?S/8 SITUATION THIS WOULD BE A ONE PAGE BUFFER WHILE OS/8 WOULD DEMAND A
 8863                /     TWO PAGE BUFFER; THE OVERALL REQUIREMENT QUICKLY BUILDS WHEN MULTIPLE
 8864                /     FILES ARE OPEN SIMULTANEOUSLY, EACH NEEDING ITS OWN BUFFER.
 8865   
 8866                /     EVEN WHEN BETTER OVERALL THROUGHPUT IS DESIRABLE, OS/8 STILL PROVIDES AN
 8867                /     INFERIOR SOLUTION: DUE TO THE OS/8 PROHIBITION ON THE USE OF THE LAST PAGE
 8868                /     OF EVERY MEMORY FIELD, THE MAXIMUM GENERALLY ALLOWED TRANSFER WOULD BE 15
 8869                /     RECORDS [30 PAGES].  ONLY EVEN VALUES FROM TWO THROUGH 30 CAN BE SPECIFIED
 8870                /     IN TERMS OF ALLOWABLE PAGE COUNT [DIVIDE BY TWO TO GET RECORD COUNT].  IN
 8871                /     ALL P?S/8 HANDLER CALLS, ANY VALUE IS ALLOWED IN THE RANGE OF ONE THROUGH
 8872                /     32.  P?S/8 HAS BOTH THE LARGEST MAXIMUM BUFFER SIZE AND THE SMALLEST
 8873                /     MINIMUM BUFFER SIZE AND ALL CASES IN-BETWEEN WHILE OS/8 CANNOT ALLOW ANY
 8874                /     ODD SIZES OR THE MAXIMUM SIZE OF 32. [NOTE: CERTAIN EXISTENT HARDWARE
 8875                /     DEVICES CANNOT RUN OS/8.  OS/8 DEMANDS A DESIGN QUIRK: THE DEVICE MUST BE
 8876                /     INHERENTLY PAGE-ORIENTED [AS IS IDEAL FOR ALL P?S/8 IMPLEMENTATIONS] OR,
 8877                /     IF THE DEVICE IS DOUBLE-PAGE RECORD-ORIENTED, THERE MUST BE A WAY TO
 8878                /     READ/WRITE THE FIRST 128 WORDS ONLY.  MOREOVER, THIS HAS TO BE
 8879                /     IMPLEMENTED WITHIN A DEVICE HANDLER THAT OCCUPIES AT MOST SOMEWHAT LESS
 8880                /     THAN TWO PAGES OF CODE SPACE.
 8881   
 8882                /     SINCE P?S/8 ALLOWS JUST UNDER NINE PAGES OF CODE SPACE, THIS CAN GENERALLY
 8883                /     BE ACCOMPLISHED WITH EASE.  FOR EXAMPLE: P?S/8 ON THE RK8E/RK05 ADDRESSES
 8884                /     EACH 256-WORD RK05 RECORD TO THE NEAREST HALF SECTOR.  WHEN NECESSARY, AN
 8885                /     INTERNAL BUFFER WITHIN THE HANDLER IS USED TO IMPLEMENT FIXUPS FOR ALL
 8886                /     SPECIAL CASE CONSIDERATIONS SUCH AS WRITING ONLY ON THE SECOND HALF OF A
 8887                /     SECTOR; FOR ALL CASES ANALOGOUS TO WHAT OS/8 ALLOWS, THE BUFFERING SCHEME
 8888                /     IS NOT USED.
 8889   
 8890                /     THE CESI MDC8 WITH 256 WORD RECORDS IS THE DESIGNATED STORAGE HARDWARE FOR
 8891                /     OMNI-8, A DEDICATED BOOTABLE APPLICATION SYSTEM THAT IS EXPLICITLY
 8892                /     DESIGNED TO MAXIMIZE PERFORMANCE ON THAT DEVICE, OFTEN WITH A NET STORAGE
 8893                /     SPACE OF 32 MWORDS PER HARD DISK.  OS/8 IS ONE OF THE *EMULATED* OPERATING
 8894                /     SYSTEMS SUPPORTED; HOWEVER, OS/8 CANNOT RUN ON THE MACHINE.  P?S/8 CAN RUN
 8895                /     ON THIS MACHINE IF 8K AVAILABLE; THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT CAN ADDRESS
 8896                /     THE ENTIRE MDC8 ENVIRONMENT OF TWO COMPLETE HARD DISKS WITH EASE.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 179

 8898                /     THE FULL CAPABILITY OF THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DATE WILL BE AVAILABLE TO THE
 8899                /     SHELL ENVIRONMENT.  ALL FILES WILL BE CAPABLE OF A UNIQUE DATE IN THE
 8900                /     RANGE OF 01-JAN-1900 THROUGH 31-DEC-2411.  THIS IS THE ONLY OPERATING
 8901                /     SYSTEM FOR A DEC-MADE PRODUCT WITH THAT WIDE A DATE RANGE.  AS SUCH, THERE
 8902                /     SHOULD NOT BE ANY FILE THAT REQUIRES ANY FORM OF DATE SPOOFING FROM THE
 8903                /     DAWN OF THE TWENTIETH CENTURY FORWARD.  THE LARGE STORAGE CAPABILITY WILL ALLOW
 8904                /     THE ENTIRE HISTORICAL RECORD OF THE PDP-8 TO BE STORED ON A SINGLE DEVICE THAT
 8905                /     CAN BE SHARED WITH ALL USERS FOR THE FORESEEABLE FUTURE.  THIS INCLUDES TEXT
 8906                /     FILES WRITTEN BY SEYMOUR CRAY IN 1960 DEFINING 12-BIT COMPUTING AND LATER
 8907                /     ARTICLES FROM MIT LINCOLN LABORATORIES IN SUPPORT OF THE LINC PROJECT WITH
 8908                /     ACCURATE ORIGINAL FILE DATES.  THE PDP-8 SHARES A HERITAGE OF DESIGN WITH THESE
 8909                /     OTHER TWO PREDECESSOR SYSTEMS; SUPPORTING DOCUMENTS SHOULD BE PART OF ANY PROPER
 8910                /     MASTER FILE STORAGE COLLECTION.
 8911   
 8912                /     SHELL COMMANDS WILL BE TOTALLY IN LINE WITH DEC STANDARDS FOR OPERATING
 8913                /     SYSTEMS NEWER THAN OS/8.  FROM THE STANDPOINT OF AN OS/8 USER, THIS
 8914                /     ESSENTIALLY MEANS CCL-LIKE IMPLEMENTATION IN ALL SITUATIONS, WITHOUT THE
 8915                /     OVERHEAD OF OS/8'S IMPLEMENTATION OF CCL. [NOTE: OS/8 CCL IS SO
 8916                /     OVERHEAD-RIDDEN, DEC MANAGEMENT MADE OFFICIAL RECOMMENDATIONS NOT TO
 8917                /     ENABLE CCL ON SYSTEMS BASED ON SLOWER DEVICES SUCH AS DECTAPES AND
 8918                /     LINCTAPES.]
 8919   
 8920                /     UNLIKE OS/8, PROGRAMS CAN DETERMINE THE EXACT MEANING OF DEFAULT DEVICES.
 8921                /     [NOTE: OS/8 ONLY SUPPORTS ONE DEFAULT DEVICE AT A TIME; THE SHELL ALLOWS
 8922                /     PROGRAMS TO SUPPLY UNIQUE POSITIONAL DEFAULT DEVICES WITHIN THE COMMAND.]
 8923                /     BY FORCING DEFAULTS TO BE DETERMINED FROM A SMALL SYSTEM CACHE OF UNIQUE
 8924                /     DEFAULT DEVICES, IT IS POSSIBLE TO OPEN MORE THAN ONE OUTPUT STREAM AT A
 8925                /     TIME.
 8926   
 8927                /     FOR EXAMPLE, THE SHELL PDP-8 ASSEMBLER [TENTATIVELY TO BE NAMED XPAL] WILL
 8928                /     BE ABLE TO PERFORM ALL OPERATIONS IN TWO PASSES, NOT THREE AS IS DONE IN
 8929                /     OS/8. [NOTE: THE P?S/8 "PAL" ASSEMBLER NEARLY DOES THIS CURRENTLY, WITH
 8930                /     THE LONE EXCEPTION BEING THE LINEPRINTER OUTPUT MUST BE DIRECTED TO THE
 8931                /     PHYSICAL DEVICE 66 LINEPRINTER OR THE LOGICAL LINEPRINTER IF THE LOGICAL
 8932                /     CONSOLE OVERLAY IS ENABLED.]  THE ONLY REQUIREMENT IS THAT THE TWO OUTPUT
 8933                /     DEVICES ARE DIFFERENT FROM EACH OTHER.  WHILE THE USER CAN CREATE A
 8934                /     COMMAND ATTEMPTING TO VIOLATE THIS, THE SHELL WILL REJECT THIS AS
 8935                /     IMPOSSIBLE.  HOWEVER, BY DEFAULT, THE DEVICES WILL ALREADY BE UNIQUE.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 180

 8937                /     THE ONLY CONFIGURATION THAT WOULD PREVENT SUCH OPERATION ENTIRELY WOULD BE
 8938                /     IF THE SYSTEM IS CONFIGURED TO ONLY EXIST AS BLOCKS ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 8939                /     WITH AN OFFSET AND THERE ARE NO OTHER LOGICAL DEVICES AVAILABLE.  IN THIS
 8940                /     CONTRIVED EXAMPLE, XPAL WOULD HAVE TO OPERATE IN A MANNER ANALOGOUS TO
 8941                /     OS/8 PAL8 USING TWO ASSEMBLY OPERATIONS: ONE TO PRODUCE BINARY AND ANOTHER
 8942                /     TO PRODUCE A LISTING FILE.  EXECUTABLE PROGRAMS CAN DEMAND AS MANY AS
 8943                /     THREE POSITIONAL DEFAULT DEVICES, ALL UNIQUE.  [NOTE: THIS IS SUBJECT TO
 8944                /     CHANGE; HOWEVER, IT IS HOPED THAT THIS SHOULD COVER ALL KNOWN CASES.]
 8945                /     XPAL WILL REJECT ATTEMPTS TO USE THE SECOND DEVICE IN A DEFAULT SITUATION
 8946                /     SHOULD THIS DEFAULT DEVICE NOT BE DEFINED.  FOR MOST CONFIGURATIONS, TWO
 8947                /     OR MORE DEVICE/LOGICAL UNITS WILL BE AVAILABLE TO ALLOW MAXIMUM
 8948                /     FUNCTIONALITY.  [CONTRAST THIS WITH OS/8 WHERE THIS FEATURE DOES NOT
 8949                /     EXIST; OS/8 DOES NOT ALLOW MULTIPLE OUTPUT FILES TO BE OPEN FOR PAL8 EVEN
 8950                /     IF LOCATED ON DIFFERENT DEVICES.  THUS, ONLY DEFICIENT CONFIGURATIONS WILL
 8951                /     BE REQUIRED TO OPERATE IN A LIKE MANNER TO OS/8 WITH REGARD TO ASSEMBLER
 8952                /     PASS CONSIDERATIONS.]
 8953   
 8954                /     FOR RETRO-COMPATIBILITY ONLY, A COMMAND DECODE OPTION MAY BE MADE
 8955                /     AVAILABLE.  ALL SYSTEM PROGRAMS WILL BE DESIGNED TO NOT REQUIRE IT,
 8956                /     INSTEAD DEPENDING UPON THE SHELL TO PRE-PARSE THE ENTIRE COMMAND AND OPEN
 8957                /     ALL EXISTING AND TENTATIVE FILES.
 8958   
 8959                /     GIVEN THIS LEVEL OF OPERATING SYSTEM SUPPORT, THE INTERNAL INTERFACE
 8960                /     PORTION OF MOST OS/8 PROGRAMS CAN EASILY BE REWRITTEN ALLOWING ALL USEFUL
 8961                /     APPLICATIONS TO BE MIGRATED TO THE P?S/8 SHELL.
 8962   
 8963                /     THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE SHELL WILL TEND TO CHANGE CERTAIN SYSTEM
 8964                /     CONFIGURATIONS SUCH AS THE RK8E.  WHEN THE RK8E SYSTEM HANDLER WAS
 8965                /     ORIGINALLY DESIGNED, IT WAS NOT CONTEMPLATED THERE WOULD BE A NEED FOR
 8966                /     MORE LOGICAL BLOCKS PER DEVICE THAN THE BASIC P?S/8 UPPER LIMIT OF 4096
 8967                /     LOGICAL BLOCKS; BLOCKS BEYOND THIS RANGE CAN ONLY BE ADDRESSED IF THE
 8968                /     DEVICE MAPPING CONTINUES THE NEXT PHYSICAL BLOCK AS THE LOWEST BLOCK ON
 8969                /     THE NEXT LOGICAL UNIT.  THE P?S/8 SHELL CHANGES THE DESIGN AS THE
 8970                /     NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS HAVE A LARGER SCOPE OF BLOCK ADDRESSIBILITY.
 8971   
 8972                /     AS SUCH, INSTEAD OF SUPPORTING LOGICAL UNITS 0 THROUGH 3 ON A SINGLE DISK
 8973                /     CARTRIDGE, LOGICAL UNIT 0 COULD ADDRESS AN ENTIRE RK05 PACK WHICH IS
 8974                /     BEYOND THE CAPABILITY OF OS/8 [WHICH REQUIRES TWO HANDLERS PER PACK].  BY
 8975                /     DEFINING THE SYSTEM CONFIGURATION TO INCLUDE TWO PHYSICAL PACKS, EACH
 8976                /     LOGICAL UNIT OF AN RK8E NON-SYSTEM HANDLER COULD ADDRESS AN ENTIRE RK05F
 8977                /     FIXED PACK OR A PAIR OF RK05/RK05J CARTRIDGE PACKS ARRANGED AS PHYSICAL
 8978                /     DRIVE 0 AND 1, 2 AND 3, ETC. [NOTE: A CONTROLLER MODIFICATION EXISTS KNOWN
 8979                /     AS THE RKS8E THAT ALLOWS UP TO 8 PACKS SIMILAR TO THE WAY RK05 PACKS ARE
 8980                /     DEFINED ON PDP-11 SYSTEMS.]
 8981   
 8982                /     IN OS/8 TERMS, EACH RK05 PACK CONTAINS TWO DEVICES OF 3248 256-WORD
 8983                /     RECORDS WHERE THE MAXIMUM POSSIBLE IS 4096 RECORDS].  SINCE THE ENTIRE
 8984                /     PACK IS MORE THAN ONE OS/8 DEVICE, TWO DEVICES ARE REQUIRED.  BY
 8985                /     COMPARISON, THE ORIGINAL P?S/8 SYSTEM SUPPORTS FOUR LOGICAL UNITS PER PACK
 8986                /     EACH 3248 128-WORD SIZE BLOCKS.  THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT CAN UTILIZE AN
 8987                /     ENTIRE RK05 PACK THAT ADDRESSES 12992 128-WORD BLOCKS [25984 FOR RK05F
 8988                /     DEVICES OR A PAIR OF RK05J PACKS] WHERE THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE ADDRESSES
 8989                /     UP TO THE FIRST 4096 BLOCKS. [NOTE: THE SHELL DIRECTORY STRUCTURE PROVIDES
 8990                /     ENTRIES TO PROTECT THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM AREA; INTERNAL FILE ENTRIES WILL
 8991                /     BE AVAILABLE AS HIDDEN UNMOVABLE DIRECTORY ENTRIES FOR THIS PURPOSE, ETC.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 181

 8993                /     ASSUMING MODEST USAGE OF THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM AREA WITH ONLY A FEW TFS
 8994                /     FILES ALLOWED, ONLY THE FIRST 400 BLOCKS SHOULD BE REQUIRED FOR FULL
 8995                /     COMPATIBILITY WITH THE BASIC SYSTEM.  AS SUCH, OVER 12000 BLOCKS WOULD BE
 8996                /     AVAILABLE TO THE SHELL SYSTEM.  [COMPARE THIS WITH OS/8 REQUIRING TWO
 8997                /     DEVICE HANDLERS EACH ADDRESSING AROUND HALF OF THIS.  AND THIS SYSTEM
 8998                /     COULD BE CONFIGURED FOR LARGER DEVICE SIZE SUCH AS REQUIRING EITHER TWO OR
 8999                /     FOUR RK05 PACKS.]  BECAUSE OF THE SUPERIOR DIRECTORY DESIGN OF THE P?S/8
 9000                /     SHELL, THIS WOULD CREATE A FAR MORE POWERFUL ENVIRONMENT FOR ANY USAGE
 9001                /     RANGING FROM MANY SMALL FILES [WHERE OS/8 EASILY RUNS OUT OF DIRECTORY
 9002                /     SPACE] TO HANDLING FAR LARGER FILES THAN OS/8 CAN ADDRESS IN ANY
 9003                /     CONFIGURATION.  YET DESPITE THESE CONFIGURATIONAL DIFFERENCES, THE P?S/8
 9004                /     SHELL NON-SYSTEM DRIVERS CAN ADDRESS THE ENTIRE DISK USING SMALLER READ
 9005                /     AND WRITE BUFFERS [FOR A MINIMUM CONFIGURATION], OR LARGER READ AND WRITE
 9006                /     BUFFERS [DUE TO LIFTING RESTRICTIONS ON FILE BUFFERING THAT ONLY APPLY IN
 9007                /     OS/8 DUE TO VARIOUS LIMITATIONS.  OS/8 CAN ONLY ADDRESS FILES BY TWO-PAGE
 9008                /     BUFFERS OR MULTIPLES THEREOF UP TO A MAXIMUM SIZE OF 30.  P?S/8 PROGRAMS
 9009                /     CAN ALWAYS ADDRESS ANY FILE USING A SINGLE-PAGE BUFFER OR MULTIPLES
 9010                /     THEREOF UP TO A MAXIMUM OF 32.
 9011   
 9012                /     AS SUCH, CHANGING THE SYSTEM CONFIGURATION ON THE RK8E WILL BETTER TAKE
 9013                /     ADVANTAGE OF THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT. [NOTE: THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM SUPPORTS
 9014                /     THE CONVRT PROGRAM TO TRANSFER TEXT FILES BETWEEN OS/8 AND P?S/8 IN BOTH
 9015                /     DIRECTIONS.  THIS PROGRAM REQUIRES THAT OS/8 DEVICES CAN BE ADDRESSED IN
 9016                /     THE SAME BOUNDARY CONDITIONS ON A DEFINABLE LOGICAL UNIT.  THE ORIGINAL
 9017                /     RK8E HANDLER WOULD THUS ALLOW CONVRT TO WORK ON LOGICAL UNITS 2, 4 AND 6
 9018                /     ASSUMING TWO CARTRIDGES.  IN THE NEW CONFIGURATION LOGICAL UNIT 1 WILL
 9019                /     WORK IDENTICALLY TO THE ORIGINAL SYSTEM USING UNIT 4.  THIS CONSIDERATION
 9020                /     WOULD BE CONSIDERED AN INTERIM STEP AS THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT CAN SUPPORT
 9021                /     AN INDEPENDENT UTILITY TO BE KNOWN AS PIPOS8 TO TRANSFER FILES BETWEEN THE
 9022                /     SHELL ENVIRONMENT AND AN OS/8 DEVICE MOUNTED ON A P?S/8 ADDRESSABLE
 9023                /     DEVICE.  THIS WILL ALLOW FILE TRANSFERS ON AN EQUAL BASIS IN BOTH
 9024                /     DIRECTIONS.] 
 9025   
 9026                / 22) TFS FILE SYSTEM
 9027   
 9028                /     THE TINY FILE SYSTEM [TFS] IS THE PRIMARY FILE SYSTEM OF THE BASIC P?S/8
 9029                /     OPERATING SYSTEM.  IT IS A DIRECT DESCENDANT OF THE R-L MONITOR FILE
 9030                /     SYSTEM WITH WHICH IT IS PARTIALLY COMPATIBLE.
 9031   
 9032                /     TFS FILES ARE FIXED SIZE INDEPENDENT FILES OF 2048 12-BIT WORDS.  ACTUAL
 9033                /     FILE CONTENTS IS CONTEXT-DEPENDENT; THERE ARE NO RULES OTHER THAN THE
 9034                /     FILE SIZE.  ENTRIES CAN BE MADE IN THE TFS DIRECTORY OVER OTHERWISE
 9035                /     UNDEFINED STORAGE SPACE IN ANTICIPATION OF FUTURE USE.  DEPENDING ON
 9036                /     SPECIFIC CIRCUMSTANCES, PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENT CAN BE ACHIEVED BY CLEVER
 9037                /     FILE NAME PLACEMENT WITHIN THE DIRECTORY, ESPECIALLY ON SLOWER DEVICES
 9038                /     BASED ON DECTAPES OR LINCTAPES.
 9039   
 9040                /     THE FILE STRUCTURE ONLY SUPPORTS AN UP TO SIX CHARACTER FILE NAME WITHOUT
 9041                /     EXTENSION; THERE IS NO DATE SUPPORT FOR TFS FILES.  THIS CAN BECOME EITHER
 9042                /     ITS STRONGEST POINT OR ITS WEAKEST POINT DEPENDING ON ACTUAL USAGE.  THE
 9043                /     TFS FIXED FILE CONCEPT IS NOT TO THE LIKING OF ALL USERS; HOWEVER, ONCE
 9044                /     ACCEPTED, IT GENERALLY PROVIDES GREATER PERFORMANCE UNLESS THE SIZE OF A
 9045                /     PROJECT OVERWHELMS THE TFS DIRECTORY CAPABILITY IN TERMS OF UNWIELDINESS.


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 182

 9047                /     FOR SMALL TO MEDIUM SOURCE CODE PROJECTS, EDITING A STRING OF TFS FILES
 9048                /     CAUSES FAR LESS OVERHEAD THAN MORE CONVENTIONAL FILE SYSTEMS [SUCH AS IS
 9049                /     USED IN THE OPTIONAL P?S/8 SHELL OR OS/8].  USERS EMBRACING THE TFS
 9050                /     DIRECTORY WILL QUICKLY DEVELOP A SENSE OF HOW TO ACCESS THE ACTIVE
 9051                /     PORTION OF A SOURCE CODE STREAM CONSISTING OF THE CONCATENATION OF SEVERAL
 9052                /     TFS FILES.  IT IS NOT NECESSARY TO MODIFY UNRELATED TFS FILES TO APPLY
 9053                /     EDITS TO ONE PORTION OF A PROGRAM; MERELY OPEN THE AFFECTED FILE, MAKE
 9054                /     CHANGES AND WRITE THE FILE BACK TO EXACTLY THE SAME EXACT FILE BLOCKS.  NO
 9055                /     BACKUP FILES ARE WRITTEN [UNLESS THE USER CREATES THEM EXPLICITLY] AND NO
 9056                /     OPERATIONS ARE PERFORMED TO UPDATE THE TFS DIRECTORY UNLESS ABSOLUTELY
 9057                /     NECESSARY. [NOTE: THE BASIC P?S/8 KEYBOARD EDITOR AND MONITOR OPERATES
 9058                /     WITH THE ENTIRE TFS DIRECTORY CACHED AT ALL TIMES.  BACKUP FILES CAN BE
 9059                /     CREATED AHEAD OF TIME AND FILE COPIES CREATED INSTANTLY WRITING ONLY TO
 9060                /     THE AFFECTED FILE BLOCKS ON THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE LOGICAL UNIT; THE
 9061                /     TFS DIRECTORY WILL NEVER BE UPDATED NEEDLESSLY.]
 9062   
 9063                /     A TYPICAL EXAMPLE OF CONVENTIONAL VARIABLE-LENGTH FILE EDITING VERSUS TFS
 9064                /     FILE EDITING FOLLOWS:
 9065   
 9066                /     ASSUME A SINGLE FILE IN A CONVENTIONAL VARIABLE SIZE ENVIRONMENT IS 128
 9067                /     BLOCKS LONG AND IN A TFS ENVIRONMENT THE SAME SOURCE TEXT EXISTS IN EIGHT
 9068                /     TFS FILES TO BE USED AS A CONCATENATED STREAM WHEN ASSEMBLED.  EDITING IN
 9069                /     THE CONVENTIONAL ENVIRONMENT REQUIRES OPENING A FREE SPACE ON THE DEVICE
 9070                /     THAT IS AT LEAST 128 BLOCKS LONG; EACH SYSTEM MAY PROVIDE A WAY TO OBTAIN
 9071                /     A SUITABLE SECTION, OFTEN THE LARGEST CONTIGUOUS FREE SPACE CURRENTLY
 9072                /     AVAILABLE.  [NOTE: THE EDIT CANNOT EVEN BE PERFORMED IF SUCH SPACE IS NOT
 9073                /     PRESENTLY AVAILABLE; THE USER MAY BE FORCED TO PUT OFF THE EDIT AND DEAL
 9074                /     WITH COMPRESSING EMPTY SPACE TOGETHER BEFORE THE EDIT CAN BE PERFORMED.]
 9075                /     ONCE THE EDITING SESSION COMMENCES, THERE ARE NOW TWO AREAS THE SIZE OF
 9076                /     THE ENTIRE FILE IN PLAY SIMULTANEOUSLY.  CLOSING THE FILE LEADS TO THE
 9077                /     DECOMMITMENT OF THE ORIGINAL FILE BLOCKS; THIS IN TURN TENDS TO FRAGMENT
 9078                /     THE STORAGE DEVICE LEADING TO ADDITIONAL NEED TO COMPRESS OUT EMPTY SPACE
 9079                /     THE NEXT TIME, ETC.  THE NEW FILE ALSO TENDS TO LEAD TO FRAGMENTATION AS
 9080                /     THE FINAL FILE SIZE WILL TEND TO BE LESS THAN THE TEMPORARY ALLOCATION OF
 9081                /     STORAGE SPACE REQUESTED.  UNLESS THE STORAGE SYSTEM IS ONLY LIGHTLY USED,
 9082                /     DEFRAGMENTATION OF EMPTY SPACE WILL TEND TO BE NEEDED OFTEN.  [NOTE: THE
 9083                /     P?S/8 SHELL CANNOT HELP BUT SUFFER FROM THIS PROBLEM IN PRACTICAL USAGE;
 9084                /     HOWEVER, THERE ARE SOME WAYS TO MITIGATE THE PROBLEM SOMEWHAT AS COMPARED
 9085                /     TO OS/8'S RAW IMPLEMENTATION OF A VARIABLE SIZE FILE SYSTEM THAT WORKS
 9086                /     EXACTLY AS DESCRIBED IN THIS EXAMPLE.  THIS WILL INCLUDE SUCH AS FILE
 9087                /     LOCALIZATION TECHNIQUES AND THE ABILITY TO DEFINE TIERS OF SPACE
 9088                /     ALLOCATION; EDITING PROGRAMS CAN SPECIFY REQUESTING THE NEAREST EMPTY
 9089                /     SPACE ON THE DEVICE AS WELL AS ACCEPTABLE LIMITS ON THE ACTUAL SIZE OF THE
 9090                /     REQUESTED TENTATIVE FILE SPACE INSTEAD OF ALWAYS BEING PROVIDED THE
 9091                /     LARGEST AVAILABLE EMPTY SPACE ANYWHERE ON THE DEVICE.  SUCH TECHNIQUES
 9092                /     WILL TEND TO SOMEWHAT LIMIT FRAGMENTATION.] [NOTE: DETAILS OF THE OVERHEAD
 9093                /     OF CARRYING OUT THESE OPERATIONS HAS NOT BEEN EXAMINED; IT IS DEPENDENT ON
 9094                /     SPECIFIC IMPLEMENTATION; NO VARIABLE-LENGTH FILE SYSTEM ACCOMPLISHES THESE
 9095                /     OPERATIONS INSTANTANEOUSLY.]


/    P?S/8 TC01/TC08 SYSTEM HANDLER  P?S PAL V08I    18-AUG-14  PAGE 183

 9097                /     ACCOMPLISHING THE EQUIVALENT EDIT IN THE TFS DIRECTORY INVOLVES MERELY
 9098                /     LOCATING THE ONE FILE WHERE THE EDITS MUST BE PERFORMED.  THE FILE IS
 9099                /     LOADED INTO THE EDITING BUFFER, EDITED IN PLACE AND WRITTEN BACK TO THE
 9100                /     SAME BLOCKS AS BEFORE THE EDIT. [NOTE: THE USER MAY OPTIONALLY LOAD THE
 9101                /     FILE, SAVE TO A PRE-ALLOCATED DESIGNATED BACKUP FILE, EDIT THE FILE AND
 9102                /     THEN WRITE IT BACK TO THE ORIGINAL STORAGE BLOCKS.]  BECAUSE THE TFS
 9103                /     DIRECTORY STRUCTURE IS 100 PERCENT CACHED, THERE IS *NO* OVERHEAD
 9104                /     ASSOCIATED WITH THE EDIT!
 9105   
 9106                /     THE LARGER THE FILE, THE INHERENTLY MORE DRAMATIC THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN
 9107                /     THE TWO METHODS. [NOTE: THERE ARE TRADE-OFFS RELATED TO SUCH AS WHEN A
 9108                /     TFS FILE BECOMES TOO FULL INVOLVING SPLITING THE FILE INTO TWO FILES; THIS
 9109                /     MUST BE PERFORMED AS NEEDED.  AS LONG AS THERE IS ENOUGH ADDITIONAL SPACE
 9110                /     ON THE STORAGE DEVICE TO ALLOCATE ONE MORE TFS FILE, THIS CAN ALWAYS BE
 9111                /     ACHIEVED; CONTRAST THIS WITH THE NEED FOR ADDITIONALLY AT LEAST THE
 9112                /     STORAGE OF THE ENTIRETY OF THE CURRENT FILE SIZE IN A CONVENTIONAL
 9113                /     EDITING ENVIRONMENT. THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM PROVIDES ADEQUATE UTILITIES TO
 9114                /     MANAGE EVERY ASPECT OF THIS PROCESS.]
 9115   
 9116                /     ULTIMATELY, VERY LARGE PROJECTS CANNOT BE HANDLED BY THE TFS DIRECTORY
 9117                /     BECAUSE OF RESOURCE LIMITATIONS OF P?S/8. [NOTE: THERE ARE PROVISIONAL
 9118                /     WAYS AROUND MOST OF THE LIMITATIONS.] THERE IS A HARD UPPER LIMIT ON
 9119                /     PASSING TFS FILE HANDLES TO PROGRAMS OF 17 MAXIMUM, INCLUDING ANY REQUIRED
 9120                /     OUTPUT FILES.  FOR EXAMPLE, A PROGRAM MAY BE CREATED IN 14 TFS FILES THAT
 9121                /     CREATES THREE BINARY FILES.  DUE TO P?S/8 BATCH SUPPORT BEING PRESENT IN
 9122                /     THE OPERATING SYSTEM, THE USER CAN TYPE UP SHORT ["MAKE"-LIKE] COMMAND
 9123                /     FILES TO BE USED REPEATEDLY TO AVOID THE TEDIUM OF MANUALLY ENTERING LONG
 9124                /     COMMAND LINES.  FOR TRULY LARGE SOURCE CODE PROJECTS, A LARGER SYSTEM WILL
 9125                /     GENERALLY BE REQUIRED; THE P?S/8 SHELL WILL BE AN EXCELLENT WAY TO HANDLE
 9126                /     LARGER PROJECTS ASSUMING SUFFICIENT RESOURCES ARE AVAILABLE [SUCH AS MORE
 9127                /     MEMORY AND LARGER STORAGE DEVICES].
 9128   
 9129                / 23) .XOR.
 9130   
 9131                /     MANY OPERATIONS WITHIN THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ARE CARRIED OUT
 9132                /     BY A BIT-WISE EXCLUSIVE-OR OPERATION BETWEEN THE DECTAPE CONTROLLER STATUS
 9133                /     REGISTER "A" AND THE PDP-8 ACCUMULATOR [AC].  THE ".XOR." NOTATION IS USED
 9134                /     AS SHORT-HAND WHEN DESCRIBING THESE OPERATIONS.
 9135   
 9136                /     A SUBTLE ISSUE COMPLICATES DESCRIBING THESE OPERATIONS: ONLY THE TEN
 9137                /     HIGHEST BITS ARE TRANSFERRED BY THE EXCLUSIVE-OR OPERATION; THE TWO LOWEST
 9138                /     BITS OF STATUS REGISTER "A" ARE USED TO PRESERVE CORRESPONDING CONTROLLER
 9139                /     FLAGS DIRECTLY IF SET.  ALL BITS OF THE AC ARE APPLIED TO STATUS REGISTER
 9140                /     "A" SIMULTANEOUSLY AS DESCRIBED ABOVE.
 9141   
 9142                        $                               /THAT'S ALL, FOLK!


